        createDestination(ap, "Talakona Waterfall", "talakona-waterfall", "Tirupati District",
                "The highest waterfall in Andhra Pradesh, cascading 270 feet amidst the biodiverse biosphere of Sri Venkateswara National Park.",
                "Talakona drops dramatically through a vertical rock canyon into a crystalline forest pool reputed to possess therapeutic medicinal minerals from surrounding native flora. The protected reserve boasts dense forest trails, a 240-meter canopy rope walkway, and rich sanctuary wildlife including endangered slender lorises and flying lizards.",
                "Waterfall", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Talakona_waterfall.jpg",
                "September to January (post-monsoon waterfalls are at maximum cascade volume)", "3 - 5 hours", "'Talakona' in Telugu signifies 'head of the hills,' designating the starting point of the sacred Seshachalam mountain range; home to the ancient Siddheswara Swamy Temple founded in the 14th century.", "270-foot Waterfall cascade, Canopy Rope Walk, Lord Siddheswara Swamy Temple, Nature Trek trails, and Jungle Safari.",
                "The waterfall requires a 2-kilometer forest hike from the parking area, so wear sturdy grip shoes; stay cautious around slippery rock faces near the plunge pool; watch out for mischievous monkeys.", "07:00 AM - 05:00 PM daily (entry into forest allowed until 04:00 PM)", "Forest Entry: ₹50 per person; Vehicle entry: ₹50 for cars; Canopy Walk: ₹50 per person",
                new BigDecimal("13.8058"), new BigDecimal("79.2173"), 16, 2024, 480000L, "TOTAL",
                "Andhra Pradesh Tourism (APTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Talakona_waterfall.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(ap, "Vijayawada Kanaka Durga Temple", "vijayawada-kanaka-durga-temple", "NTR District",
                "Legendary hillside temple dedicated to Goddess Kanaka Durga presiding atop the Indrakeeladri hill overlooking the Krishna River.",
                "One of Andhra Pradesh's most visited sacred shrines, Kanaka Durga Temple is an ancient Shakti pilgrimage center where the Goddess is venerated as Mahishasuramardini. Perched majestically on the Indrakeeladri hillock adjacent to the Prakasam Barrage, the temple dazzles with gold-leaf gopurams and attracts millions during the grand Navaratri festival.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bezawada_Kanaka_Durga_temple.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 - 3 hours", "Mentioned in the Mahabharata and ancient Kalika Purana, Arjuna is said to have performed penance to Lord Shiva here to acquire the divine Pasupatastra weapon; expanded under the Badami Chalukyas and Vijayanagara kings.", "Golden Gopuram sanctum, Malleswara Swamy shrine, Indrakeeladri Ghat road panoramic viewpoints, Prakasam Barrage views, and Bhavani Island nearby.",
                "Ghat road buses and elevators/lifts are available for seniors to ascend the hill; traditional dress is mandatory; visit early in the morning or during sunset to witness the evening aarti over the river.", "04:00 AM - 09:00 PM daily (except during afternoon pooja intervals)", "Free (Dharma Darshan); Special Darshan tickets: ₹100, ₹300, and ₹500 (VIP queue)",
                new BigDecimal("16.5161"), new BigDecimal("80.6053"), 17, 2024, 5600000L, "TOTAL",
                "Andhra Pradesh Tourism (APTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bezawada_Kanaka_Durga_temple.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(ap, "Undavalli Caves", "undavalli-caves", "Guntur District",
                "Magnificent 4th to 7th-century CE four-storied rock-cut cave monument featuring an iconic 5-meter colossal statue of reclining Vishnu (Anantasayana).",
                "Carved into the face of a solid sandstone hillside just across the Krishna River from Vijayawada, Undavalli Caves showcase a seamless fusion of Buddhist rock architecture and classical Hindu iconography. The impressive multi-tiered facade opens into grand pillared halls decorated with sculptures of deities, mythical beasts, and a massive monolithic sculpture of Lord Vishnu reclining on the multi-headed serpent Adishesha.",
                "Caves", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/A_view_of_Undavalli_Caves.jpg",
                "October to March (cooler weather makes exploring the upper stone tiers pleasant)", "1.5 - 2.5 hours", "Constructed during the Vishnukundina dynasty rule (4th–5th century CE) with later modifications under the Eastern Chalukyas; originally served as Buddhist monastic cells before being dedicated to Hindu deities.", "Colossal monolithic Reclining Vishnu (Anantasayana Padmanabha), 4-storey rock-cut pillared facade, sculpted friezes of Brahma and Shiva, and panoramic hilltop vistas of the Krishna River and lush green paddies.",
                "Wear comfortable shoes as there are multiple flights of stone steps to reach the upper tiers; bring a camera to capture the fine architectural carvings and scenic river valley; well-maintained lawns surround the caves.", "09:00 AM - 05:30 PM daily", "₹25 for Indian Citizens, SAARC and BIMSTEC visitors; ₹300 for Foreign Tourists; Free for children under 15",
                new BigDecimal("16.4967"), new BigDecimal("80.5796"), 18, 2024, 580000L, "TOTAL",
                "Andhra Pradesh Tourism (APTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_view_of_Undavalli_Caves.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(ap, "Nagarjuna Sagar", "nagarjuna-sagar", "Palnadu District",
                "The world's tallest and largest masonry dam built across the Krishna River, featuring a scenic reservoir and the historic island museum of Nagarjunakonda.",
                "Completed in 1967 as a flagship post-independence engineering achievement, Nagarjuna Sagar Dam stands 124 meters high with 26 massive radial spillway crest gates. The expansive reservoir lake submerges ancient valleys, with ferry boats shuttling visitors to the Nagarjunakonda island museum preserving excavated Buddhist relics and stupas relocated before the lake filled.",
                "Dam", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nagarjuna_Sagar_dam.jpg",
                "August to February", "Half day to 1 Full Day", "Named after the revered 2nd-century CE Buddhist philosopher Acharya Nagarjuna who founded the Madhyamaka school of Buddhism here; the island museum features prehistoric artifacts, Buddhist stupas, and Satavahana inscriptions rescued during archaeological salvage operations.", "Nagarjuna Sagar Dam viewpoints, 26 Radial Spillway Gates, Nagarjunakonda Island Museum (reached by launch boat), Ethipothala Waterfall (11 km away), and Launch Boat Station.",
                "Take the morning ferry ride to Nagarjunakonda Island to have ample time to tour the museum; check water level reports before visiting to catch the dam's gates in full release during peak monsoon.", "Dam viewpoints: 06:00 AM - 06:00 PM", "Dam viewpoint entry is Free; Ferry boat to Nagarjunakonda: ~₹150 - ₹200 per adult; Museum entry: ₹20",
                new BigDecimal("16.5764"), new BigDecimal("79.313"), 19, 2024, 920000L, "TOTAL",
                "Andhra Pradesh Tourism (APTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nagarjuna_Sagar_dam.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(ap, "Mypadu Beach", "mypadu-beach", "Sri Potti Sriramulu Nellore District",
                "Serene and unspoiled golden sand beach along the Bay of Bengal, fringed by whispering casuarina and lush palm groves.",
                "Located about 25 km from Nellore city, Mypadu Beach is famous for its clean, flat shoreline, gentle gradients, and uncrowded coastal atmosphere. Developed with an APTDC Haritha beach resort and promenades, it is a favored weekend retreat for peaceful morning sunrise strolls, seaside picnics, and fresh coastal seafood.",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mypadu_Beach.jpg",
                "October to March (pleasant coastal weather, gentle sea breezes, and cool evenings)", "2 - 4 hours", "Long an active coastal village for traditional seafaring fishermen and trade, Mypadu's coastline played a role in the maritime commerce of ancient dynasties of the Nellore region like the Cholas and Pallavas.", "Long golden sand shoreline, Casuarina plantations, sunrise views over the Bay of Bengal, and APTDC Haritha beach resort.",
                "The waters are relatively calm compared to urban beaches, but exercise caution during high tide; enjoy fresh tender coconut and local snacks from beachside stalls; visit during early morning to witness vibrant fishing boats returning at dawn.", "Open 24 hours daily", "Free entry (no ticket required)",
                new BigDecimal("14.5065"), new BigDecimal("80.1783"), 20, 2024, 410000L, "TOTAL",
                "Andhra Pradesh Tourism (APTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mypadu_Beach.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        // --- 2. ARUNACHAL PRADESH ---
        State arunachal = createState("Arunachal Pradesh", "arunachal-pradesh", "STATE", "North-Eastern", "Itanagar",
                "Land of the Dawn-Lit Mountains and Eastern Himalayan biodiversity.",
                "Arunachal Pradesh is India's easternmost state, offering snow-capped peaks, ancient Buddhist monasteries, serene lakes, and vibrant tribal traditions.",
                "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/TawangMonastery-ArunachalPradesh-1.jpg",
                "October to April");
        createDestination(arunachal, "Tawang Monastery", "tawang-monastery", "Tawang, Tawang District",
                "Perched at an altitude of approximately 3,000 meters, Tawang Monastery is the largest monastery in India and the second largest in the world after the Potala Palace.",
                "Founded by Merak Lama Lodre Gyamtso in 1680–1681 following the wishes of the 5th Dalai Lama, it belongs to the Gelug school of Tibetan Buddhism. The complex accommodates over 400 monks and houses an imposing 8-meter gilded statue of Lord Buddha, historic manuscripts, and vibrant thangkas.",
                "Monastery", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/TawangMonastery-ArunachalPradesh-1.jpg",
                "March to October (and January/February during Torgya and Losar festivals)", "2 to 3 hours", "Established in the 17th century, the monastery is linked to the 6th Dalai Lama (Tsangyang Gyatso), who was born in the Tawang valley, and has served as a cultural anchor for Monpa Buddhists.", "Dukhang (Main Assembly Hall),26-foot gilded Buddha statue,Ancient manuscript library (Kangyur and Tengyur),Monastery Museum & Craft Centre,Kakaling entrance gate",
                "Remove shoes before entering sanctums; hire an authorized guide to explain the rich murals; photography inside the inner shrine may be restricted.", "07:00 AM – 07:00 PM daily", "Free (donations welcome)",
                new BigDecimal("27.5861"), new BigDecimal("91.8683"), 1, 2024, 320000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TawangMonastery-ArunachalPradesh-1.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Sela Pass", "sela-pass", "Tawang & West Kameng border",
                "Situated at an altitude of 4,170 meters (13,700 ft), Sela Pass is a breathtaking Himalayan mountain pass connecting Tawang to the rest of India.",
                "The pass is enveloped by alpine vegetation and is accompanied by the sacred, frozen Sela Lake (Paradise Lake). It holds deep strategic importance for the Indian Army, marked additionally by the historic Sela Tunnel built below it.",
                "Mountain Pass", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sela_Pass%2C_Arunachal_Pradesh.jpg",
                "March to November (scenic clear skies) or December to February (snowscapes)", "45 minutes to 1.5 hours", "Named after Sela, a local Monpa tribal woman who assisted Indian Army soldier Jaswant Singh Rawat during the 1962 Sino-Indian War before making the supreme sacrifice.", "Sela Lake (Paradise Lake),Prayer flag decorated summit gate,Snow-clad Eastern Himalayan panorama,Jaswant Garh War Memorial nearby",
                "High-altitude winds are biting cold so dress in multi-layered thermals; acute mountain sickness (AMS) can happen, so carry AMS medication and acclimatize.", "Open 24 hours (daylight crossing recommended: 06:00 AM – 04:00 PM)", "Free (Inner Line Permit / PAP mandatory)",
                new BigDecimal("27.5034"), new BigDecimal("92.1037"), 2, 2024, 280000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sela_Pass,_Arunachal_Pradesh.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Ziro Valley", "ziro-valley", "Ziro, Lower Subansiri District",
                "Ziro Valley is an idyllic, pine-clad plateau renowned for its unique terraced paddy-cum-pisciculture farming and the indigenous Apatani tribe.",
                "Situated in the Lower Subansiri district, the valley boasts tranquil rolling hills, pristine pine groves, and UNESCO-tentative-listed cultural landscapes. It also hosts the internationally acclaimed Ziro Festival of Music every autumn.",
                "Valley", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ziro_Valley_View.jpg",
                "March to October (September is ideal for golden paddies and the music festival)", "2 to 3 days", "Ancestral home of the Apatani civilization, recognized globally for their sustainable natural resource management, unique bamboo architecture, and distinctive heritage.", "Apatani tribal villages (Hong, Hari, Bamin Michi),Kardo Shiva Lingam,Meghna Cave Temple,Tarin High-Altitude Fish Farm,Pine Grove & Siikhe Lake",
                "Always ask permission before photographing elderly villagers; pre-book homestays months ahead if attending the Ziro Festival of Music.", "Open all day (village walks 08:00 AM – 05:00 PM)", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("27.545"), new BigDecimal("93.834"), 3, 2024, 210000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ziro_Valley_View.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Namdapha National Park", "namdapha-national-park", "Miao / Deban, Changlang District",
                "Namdapha is India’s easternmost biodiversity hotspot, harboring the world's northernmost lowland evergreen rainforests.",
                "Spanning nearly 1,985 square kilometers in Changlang district, the park encompasses elevations from 200m to over 4,500m. It is renowned for harboring all four big cats of the feline family—tiger, leopard, clouded leopard, and snow leopard—alongside the endemic Namdapha flying squirrel and red panda.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Forest_snow_Namdapha_IMG_3373_04.jpg",
                "October to April", "2 to 4 days (wilderness trekking)", "Declared a Wildlife Sanctuary in 1972, then elevated to a National Park and Tiger Reserve in 1983, safeguarding primary tropical dipterocarp rainforests untouched for centuries.", "Deban campsite and river valley,Noa-Dihing River,Hornbill Camp & Firmbase trek,Hoolock gibbon sightings,Dense rainforest canopy trails",
                "Obtain entry permits from the Field Director’s office in Miao; bring leech guards, waterproof trekking gear, and water purifiers.", "06:00 AM – 05:00 PM daily", "₹50 for Indian nationals, ₹500 for foreigners; vehicle fee ₹100; camera fees apply",
                new BigDecimal("27.491"), new BigDecimal("96.383"), 4, 2024, 45000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Forest_snow_Namdapha_IMG_3373_04.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Mechuka Valley", "mechuka-valley", "Mechuka, Shi Yomi District",
                "Often hailed as the 'Switzerland of the East', Mechuka is a stunning high-altitude valley surrounded by snow-capped peaks and the serpentine Yargyap Chhu river.",
                "Situated just 29 kilometers from the Indo-Tibet border at an elevation of 1,829 meters, Mechuka is home to the Memba, Ramo, Bokar, and Libo tribes. The valley combines ancient Mahayana Buddhist heritage with picturesque wooden settlements and sweeping pine meadows.",
                "Valley", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mechuka_village.jpg",
                "October to April", "2 to 3 days", "Home to the 400-year-old Samten Yongcha Monastery of the Gelugpa sect, which predates Tawang Monastery and houses treasured Buddhist relics.", "Samten Yongcha Monastery,Yargyap Chhu (Siyom River),Hanuman Camp natural rock face,Dorjeeling village & hanging wooden bridges,Scenic snow peaks and pine forests",
                "The road journey from Aalo takes 6-8 hours; carry sufficient cash as connectivity and ATMs are limited; nights can be freezing.", "Open all day (Monastery 08:00 AM – 05:00 PM)", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("28.6019"), new BigDecimal("94.1354"), 5, 2024, 95000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mechuka_village.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Bomdila Monastery", "bomdila-monastery", "Bomdila, West Kameng District",
                "Established in 1965, Bomdila Monastery is a key center of Mahayana Buddhism and the residence of the 12th reincarnated Tsona Gontse Rinpoche.",
                "Situated on a quiet hillside above Bomdila town, the monastery is an exact replica of the Tsona Gontse Monastery in southern Tibet. It comprises three separate wings—Upper, Middle, and Lower Gompas—complete with traditional Tibetan architecture, painted murals, and prayer wheels.",
                "Monastery", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bomdila_Monastery%2C_Arunachal_Pradesh.jpg",
                "September to April", "1 to 2 hours", "Established in 1965 to preserve Tibetan and Monpa Buddhist religious traditions following the Tibetan diaspora.", "Upper Gompa prayer hall,Vibrant Buddhist murals and mandalas,Panoramic viewpoint over Bomdila town,Monastic school and library",
                "Visit during early morning or late afternoon monk prayer sessions; the viewpoint offers striking sights of Kangto and Gorichen snow peaks on clear days.", "06:00 AM – 06:00 PM daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("27.2644"), new BigDecimal("92.4188"), 6, 2024, 160000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bomdila_Monastery,_Arunachal_Pradesh.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Dirang", "dirang", "Dirang, West Kameng District",
                "Dirang is a picturesque valley town on the banks of the Kameng River, famed for apple orchards, kiwifruit farms, and historic Monpa forts.",
                "Located halfway between Bomdila and Tawang at 1,560 meters elevation, Dirang offers mild weather, lush terraced hills, and geothermal hot water springs. The town bridges centuries of history with the 500-year-old Dirang Dzong fortress and modern Buddhist landmarks like Thupsung Dhargye Ling monastery.",
                "Valley", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dirang_Valley_01.jpg",
                "October to April", "1 to 2 days", "Features the historic Dirang Dzong, built in 1831 (with foundations dating back earlier centuries) serving as a fortified community haven and administrative seat.", "Dirang Dzong (Historic stone fort),Thupsung Dhargye Ling Monastery,Dirang Hot Water Spring,National Research Centre on Yak (NRCY),Kiwi and apple orchards",
                "An excellent stop for acclimatization before ascending to Sela Pass; try local organic kiwi wine and take a dip in the natural sulfur springs.", "Open all day", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("27.357"), new BigDecimal("92.235"), 7, 2024, 140000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirang_Valley_01.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Sangti Valley", "sangti-valley", "Near Dirang, West Kameng District",
                "Sangti Valley is a peaceful, emerald-green hidden valley known as the winter nesting retreat for the endangered black-necked cranes.",
                "Situated just 15 kilometers from Dirang, the Sangti River ripples through rolling meadows, traditional Monpa stone cottages, and pine-clad hills. Far away from tourist bustle, it remains one of Arunachal Pradesh's most idyllic rural hideaways.",
                "Valley", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sangti_river.jpg",
                "November to March (for wintering black-necked cranes) or April to May (fruit orchards in bloom)", "Half-day to 1 day", "A sacred valley for the local Monpa people, who revere the black-necked cranes (Tung Tung Karmo) as harbingers of peace and bountiful harvests.", "Sangti Riverbanks and suspension bridge,Black-necked crane wintering grounds,Fruit orchards (persimmon, kiwi, and apple),Riverside camping and village walks",
                "Carry binoculars for birdwatching and maintain silence to avoid startling the endangered cranes.", "Open all day", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("27.424"), new BigDecimal("92.275"), 8, 2024, 75000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sangti_river.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Bum La Pass", "bum-la-pass", "Indo-China Border, Tawang District",
                "Standing at an altitude of 4,630 meters (15,200 ft), Bum La Pass is an iconic international border pass marking the Line of Actual Control between India and China.",
                "Located 37 kilometers north of Tawang, the pass is heavily guarded by the Indian Army and serves as an official Border Personnel Meeting (BPM) point. It remains snowbound throughout most of the year, surrounded by frozen high-altitude lakes and windswept peaks.",
                "Mountain Pass", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bumla_Pass.jpg",
                "May to October (pass frequently closed in winter due to heavy snow)", "1 to 2 hours", "The historic passage where the 14th Dalai Lama entered India in 1959 from Tibet; also the scene of fierce combat during the 1962 Sino-Indian War.", "Line of Actual Control (Indo-China border),Rock of Peace (Heap of Stones),Indian Army border hut & briefing area,Stunning high-altitude alpine landscape",
                "Special DC permit from Tawang and Indian Army approval are required; accessible only via authorized 4x4 vehicles driven by local operators.", "09:00 AM – 03:00 PM (subject to army and weather clearance)", "Free permits (vehicle hire approximately ₹5,000–₹6,000)",
                new BigDecimal("27.7289"), new BigDecimal("91.8906"), 9, 2024, 110000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bumla_Pass.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Madhuri Lake", "madhuri-lake", "Tawang District",
                "Sangetsar Lake, popularly known as Madhuri Lake, is a dramatic glacial lake created by a post-earthquake flash flood that inundated a pine forest.",
                "Located at 3,708 meters altitude on the road between Tawang and Bum La Pass, the lake is distinguished by ghostly dead tree trunks protruding eerily from its pristine turquoise waters. It gained national fame after featuring in the Bollywood film 'Koyla' starring Madhuri Dixit.",
                "Lake", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sangetsar_Lake%2C_Tawang%2C_Arunachal_Pradesh.jpg",
                "April to October", "1 to 2 hours", "Formed during the catastrophic 1950 Assam-Tibet earthquake when an entire forest was submerged beneath diverted glacial streams.", "Submerged pine tree trunks rising from water,Turquoise glacial waters and mountain reflections,Wooden boardwalk trail,Army-run lakeside cafeteria",
                "Combine this visit with Bum La Pass as they share the same route and permit zone; wear warm layers due to strong winds.", "08:00 AM – 04:00 PM daily", "Free (covered under Tawang DC/Army permit)",
                new BigDecimal("27.7186"), new BigDecimal("91.7583"), 10, 2024, 135000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sangetsar_Lake,_Tawang,_Arunachal_Pradesh.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Itanagar Fort", "itanagar-fort", "Itanagar, Papum Pare District",
                "Ita Fort ('Fort of Bricks') is an ancient 14th–15th century fortress located in the heart of Arunachal Pradesh's capital, giving Itanagar its name.",
                "Built using over 8 million kiln-baked bricks with an irregular polygonal layout adhering to the natural hill ridge, it represents an impressive feat of pre-modern engineering. Attributed to the Chutiya or Jitari dynasties of early Assam-Arunachal history, it features distinct eastern, western, and southern ramparts.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ita_Fort_01.JPG",
                "October to April", "1 to 1.5 hours", "Constructed between the 14th and 15th centuries CE, it is one of the earliest known brick architectural fortifications in the Eastern Himalayas.", "Ancient brick walls and ramparts,Eastern and southern gateways,Hilltop gardens with views over Itanagar,Archaeological excavation markers",
                "Wear walking shoes for ascending the paved pathways; visit the nearby Jawaharlal Nehru State Museum to see excavated relics.", "08:00 AM – 05:00 PM (Closed Mondays)", "₹10 for Indian nationals, ₹50 for foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("27.0903"), new BigDecimal("93.6264"), 11, 2024, 190000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ita_Fort_01.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Ganga Lake", "ganga-lake", "Itanagar, Papum Pare District",
                "Ganga Lake, locally known as Gekar Sinying ('Confined Lake'), is a secluded emerald lake enclosed by dense subtropical rainforest and primeval rock boulders.",
                "Located roughly 6 kilometers from central Itanagar, the lake has still, deep-green waters devoid of any visible river connection. Surrounded by giant ferns, orchids, and evergreen groves, it is a favored relaxation and boating spot for locals and nature lovers.",
                "Lake", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gyakar_Sinyi_or_Ganga_Lake.jpg",
                "October to April", "1.5 to 2.5 hours", "A sacred and storied water body in local Nyishi folklore, believed to hold mystical natural balance.", "Deep emerald lake waters,Paddle boating facilities,Subtropical botanical trail and orchid foliage,Picnic groves and birdwatching",
                "Morning visits offer calm reflections and abundant birdlife; bring insect repellent for the forest path.", "09:00 AM – 05:00 PM daily", "₹20 per person; boating ₹50–₹100 extra",
                new BigDecimal("27.086"), new BigDecimal("93.585"), 12, 2024, 175000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gyakar_Sinyi_or_Ganga_Lake.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Pasighat", "pasighat", "East Siang District",
                "Founded in 1911 by the British Raj, Pasighat is Arunachal Pradesh's oldest town and the proud 'Gateway to Arunachal,' resting beside the mighty Siang River.",
                "Situated where the ferocious Himalayan Siang empties onto the fertile Assam plains to form the Brahmaputra, Pasighat is rich in indigenous Adi tribal culture. The town is flanked by cascading waterfalls, expansive riverbeds, tea gardens, and the Daying Ering Wildlife Sanctuary.",
                "Nature", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Pasighat_Town_-_May2019.jpg",
                "October to April", "1 to 2 days", "Established in 1911 by British administrator Noel Williamson as the primary headquarters for the frontier tract following the Anglo-Abor conflicts.", "Siang River banks and river beaches,Daying Ering Wildlife Sanctuary,Ranaghat suspension bridge,Sirki Waterfall,Pasighat local market",
                "Ideal base for Siang white-water rafting expeditions; explore the local craft stalls for Adi woven 'gale' textiles and cane craft.", "Open all day", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("28.0667"), new BigDecimal("95.3333"), 13, 2024, 125000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pasighat_Town_-_May2019.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Roing", "roing", "Lower Dibang Valley District",
                "Roing is a scenic valley town in the Lower Dibang Valley, flanked by rushing rivers, forested hills, and the ancestral culture of the Idu Mishmi tribe.",
                "Nestled between the foothills of the Mishmi Hills and the sprawling Dibang river basin, Roing serves as the primary gateway to Mayudia Pass and the deep Dibang Valley. The area boasts historic sites like Bhismaknagar Fort along with placid lakes like Sally Lake and Mehao Lake.",
                "Nature", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Roing_Entrance.jpg",
                "October to April", "1 to 2 days", "Home to ancient archeological heritage including the 8th-century terracotta fortress of Bhismaknagar associated with the early Chutiya Kingdom.", "Sally Lake,Mehao Wildlife Sanctuary & Mehao Lake,Bhismaknagar Fort ruins,RIWATCH Museum,Dibang River bank",
                "Visit the RIWATCH museum to learn about the intricate animistic and medicinal heritage of the Mishmi tribes.", "Open all day", "Free (RIWATCH museum ₹20)",
                new BigDecimal("28.1404"), new BigDecimal("95.8361"), 14, 2024, 115000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roing_Entrance.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Mayudia Pass", "mayudia-pass", "Lower Dibang Valley & Dibang Valley border",
                "Perched at 2,655 meters amidst the rugged Mishmi Hills, Mayudia Pass is famous for receiving dense winter snowfall and spectacular views of alpine landscapes.",
                "Located 56 kilometers from Roing on the highway towards Anini, Mayudia is a tranquil Himalayan pass covered in sub-alpine forests of oak and rhododendron. During winter months from December to February, it turns into a snow playground attracting travelers from all over northeast India.",
                "Mountain Pass", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mayodia_Pass_1.jpg",
                "December to March (snowfall) or April to October (rhododendrons and birding)", "2 to 4 hours", "Historically an arduous high mountain foot pass crossing the Mishmi ridge; named in memory of Maya, a local girl who perished in the snow.", "Snow-covered mountain ridges,Mayudia Pass viewpoint,Rare birding (Mishmi wren-babbler, tragopans),Lush sub-alpine oak-rhododendron forest",
                "The road can be icy during peak winter; travel during daylight hours with experienced drivers; stop at the Mayudia Coffee House.", "Open all day (travel 07:00 AM – 04:00 PM recommended)", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("28.232"), new BigDecimal("95.918"), 15, 2024, 85000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mayodia_Pass_1.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Namsai Golden Pagoda", "namsai-golden-pagoda", "Tengapani, Namsai District",
                "Kongmu Kham, or the Golden Pagoda of Tengapani, is an exquisite Burmese-style Buddhist pagoda set across a sprawling 20-hectare landscaped compound.",
                "Inaugurated in 2010 in Namsai district, this monument represents the rich Theravada Buddhist culture of the Tai Khamti tribe. Built with magnificent golden spires, octagonal shrines, and tranquil meditation halls, it preserves holy relics of Lord Buddha brought from Thailand and Myanmar.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Golden_Pagoda_(Kongmu_Kham).jpg",
                "September to March (especially during Poy-Leng and Kathina festivals in November)", "2 to 3 hours", "A modern centerpiece of Theravada Buddhism in India, establishing Namsai as a major spiritual and architectural hub for the Tai Khamti community.", "Central Golden Pagoda (Kongmu Kham),Gilded Buddha statues & relics,Sacred Bodhi Tree sapling grove,Surrounding ornamental water pond & gardens",
                "Dress modestly (shoulders and knees covered); sunset visits are magical as the gold leaf reflects against the evening lights and water.", "06:00 AM – 06:30 PM daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("27.7816"), new BigDecimal("95.9602"), 16, 2024, 240000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_Pagoda_(Kongmu_Kham).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Parshuram Kund", "parshuram-kund", "Near Tezu, Lohit District",
                "Parshuram Kund is a revered Hindu pilgrimage shrine nestled in the lower reaches of the Lohit River, drawing thousands of devotees each year during Makar Sankranti.",
                "Surrounded by towering forested mountains where the Lohit River breaks through the gorge, this holy basin is steeped in Puranic lore. Devotees take a ceremonial dip in the sacred waters to wash away their sins, followed by temple prayers at the hilltop shrine.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Parshuram_Kund.jpg",
                "November to February (Makar Sankranti Fair in mid-January)", "2 to 3 hours", "Mentioned in the Kalika Purana as the spot where sage Parshuram bathed in the Lohit River to atone for matricide, causing his axe to finally detach from his hand.", "Sacred river bathing pool,Parshuram Temple,Lohit River gorge and mountain scenery,Lohit river suspension bridge",
                "The descent down to the river involves several hundred steep stone steps; watch your footing and obey safety signs near the river currents.", "05:00 AM – 06:00 PM daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("27.8761"), new BigDecimal("96.3533"), 17, 2024, 290000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parshuram_Kund.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Dong Valley", "dong-valley", "Walong Circle, Anjaw District",
                "Tucked near the easternmost tri-junction of India, China, and Myanmar, Dong Valley is celebrated as the enchanted land where India greets its first dawn.",
                "Located along the rugged gorges of the Lohit River near Walong at an elevation of 1,240 meters, Dong is a remote village in Anjaw district. Adventurers embark on midnight treks through pine forests to reach the Dong ridge by 4:00 AM to witness the nation's earliest rays of sunrise illuminating snow peaks.",
                "Valley", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Hanging_Bridge,_Kahoo,_Arunachal_Pradesh,_India.jpg",
                "October to April", "1 to 2 days", "Geographically celebrated as India's easternmost inhabited valley receiving the country's first sunrise; situated in the historic 1962 Walong sector.", "Dong Peak First Sunrise viewpoint,Confluence of the Lohit and Sati rivers,Hanging bridges across mountain streams,Walong War Memorial nearby,Natural hot water springs at Tilam",
                "NOTE: No genuine, verified photograph of Dong Valley currently exists on Wikimedia Commons. Trekking to the peak requires hiring a local guide from Walong and bringing reliable headlamps and warm thermal layers.", "Open all day (pre-dawn hike begins ~02:30 AM)", "Free (local guide fee ~₹1,000–₹1,500 recommended)",
                new BigDecimal("28.243"), new BigDecimal("97.045"), 18, 2024, 55000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://tourism.gov.in", "UNRESOLVED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Anini", "anini", "Dibang Valley District",
                "Anini is the remote, cloud-kissed headquarters of Dibang Valley district, famous for dramatic green plateaus and rolling mist reminiscent of the Scottish Highlands.",
                "Perched atop a high mountain plateau flanked by the Dri and Mathun rivers, Anini is the heartland of the indigenous Idu Mishmi tribe. Recently made famous by viral road-trip travelogues, the region features emerald meadows, pristine waterfalls, and alpine hiking paths towards the Seven Lakes.",
                "Hills", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Anini_Panoramia.jpg",
                "October to April", "2 to 4 days", "The heartland of the Idu Mishmi people, preserving animistic rituals, intricate cane-bamboo craft, and ancestral shamanic traditions.", "Panoramic views of Anini town and Dri River,Seven Lakes Alpine Trek base,Mipi and Acheso villages,Traditional Idu Mishmi wooden stilt houses,Dembu plateau",
                "The scenic mountain drive from Roing takes 6-8 hours; prepare for erratic network connectivity and carry extra fuel and emergency vehicle essentials.", "Open all day", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("28.79"), new BigDecimal("95.905"), 19, 2024, 40000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anini_Panoramia.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(arunachal, "Pakke Tiger Reserve", "pakke-tiger-reserve", "Seijosa, Pakke-Kessang District",
                "Spanning 862 square kilometers in the foothills of the Eastern Himalayas, Pakke Tiger Reserve is an award-winning sanctuary famed for its community-driven hornbill conservation.",
                "Bounded by the Kameng, Pakke, and Bhareli rivers, this semi-evergreen and subtropical forest is home to four hornbill species (Great, Wreathed, Oriental Pied, and Rufous-necked), Bengal tigers, Asian elephants, and clouded leopards. Its world-renowned Hornbill Nest Adoption Program unites local Nyishi villagers with wildlife biologists.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Landscape_Kameng_River_Pakke_Tiger_Reserve.jpg",
                "November to April", "2 to 3 days", "Recipient of the India Biodiversity Award in 2016 for community conservation led by the Ghora Aabhe Society and Nature Conservation Foundation.", "Hornbill roosting sites & canopy birding,Kameng and Pakke River banks,Seijosa forest walking trails,Ghora Aabhe Nyishi community eco-camps,Rich butterfly biodiversity",
                "Obtain entry permits in advance from the DFO office at Seijosa; nature safaris are conducted strictly with trained local guides.", "06:00 AM – 05:00 PM daily", "₹50 for Indians, ₹500 for foreigners; vehicle fee ₹100; camera fees apply",
                new BigDecimal("27.05"), new BigDecimal("92.93"), 20, 2024, 35000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Arunachal Pradesh Tourism Department", "Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Landscape_Kameng_River_Pakke_Tiger_Reserve.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        // --- 3. ASSAM ---
        State assam = createState("Assam", "assam", "STATE", "North-Eastern", "Dispur", "Land of the Red River and Blue Hills, famous for tea gardens and one-horned rhinos.",
                "Assam is the gateway to North-East India, world-renowned for Kaziranga National Park, the mighty Brahmaputra River, and Kamakhya Temple.",
                "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/One_horn_Rhino_in_Kaziranga_national_park.jpg", "November to April");
        createDestination(assam, "Kaziranga National Park", "kaziranga-national-park", "Golaghat and Nagaon districts",
                "Kaziranga National Park is a world-renowned UNESCO World Heritage Site famous for hosting two-thirds of the world's great one-horned rhinoceros population.",
                "Situated in the floodplains of the Brahmaputra River, Kaziranga features dense elephant grass, marshlands, and tropical broadleaf forests. In addition to rhinoceroses, it boasts a high tiger density and sustains large populations of Asian elephants, wild water buffalo, and swamp deer.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/One_horn_Rhino_in_Kaziranga_national_park.jpg",
                "November to April", "1 to 2 days", "Established as a proposed reserve forest in 1905 at the behest of Lady Mary Curzon, Kaziranga was declared a national park in 1974 and inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1985.", "One-horned Rhinoceros safaris, Central (Kohora) Range, Western (Bagori) Range, Kaziranga National Orchid and Biodiversity Park, Elephant and Jeep Safaris.",
                "Book elephant and jeep safaris well in advance; carry binoculars and sun protection; the park is closed during the monsoon season (May to October).", "05:30 AM – 05:00 PM (Safaris operate in morning and afternoon slots)", "Indian nationals: ~₹100 entrance fee + vehicle/safari charges (jeep safari approx ₹2,000–₹2,500); Foreign nationals: ~₹650 + safari charges.",
                new BigDecimal("26.5775"), new BigDecimal("93.1711"), 1, 2024, 325000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:One_horn_Rhino_in_Kaziranga_national_park.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(assam, "Kamakhya Temple", "kamakhya-temple", "Guwahati, Kamrup Metropolitan",
                "Perched atop Nilachal Hill in Guwahati, Kamakhya Temple is one of the oldest and most venerated Shakti Peethas in India.",
                "Dedicated to the goddess Kamakhya, the shrine celebrates divine feminine power and fertility, housing a natural rock fissure washed by an underground spring instead of an idol. The temple architecture features a distinctive beehive-shaped shikhara (Nilachal type) rebuilt by King Naranarayana of the Koch dynasty in the 16th century.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kamakhya_Temple,_Guwahati.jpg",
                "October to March (Ambubachi Mela occurs in late June during monsoon)", "2 to 4 hours", "One of 51 Shakti Peethas, where the yoni of goddess Sati is believed to have fallen; rebuilt in 1565 CE after destruction during the Koch dynasty era.", "Garbhagriha (inner sanctum rock spring), 10 Mahavidya shrines on Nilachal Hill, scenic viewpoints of the Brahmaputra River, Ambubachi Mela festival.",
                "Dress conservatively; VIP/special darshan passes are available to bypass lengthy general queues; be prepared for stone steps and crowds during festivals.", "05:30 AM – 01:00 PM & 02:30 PM – 05:30 PM (Sanctum closes for bhog in the early afternoon)", "Free entry for general queue; special darshan ticket costs ₹501 per person.",
                new BigDecimal("26.1664"), new BigDecimal("91.7056"), 2, 2024, 4800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kamakhya_Temple,_Guwahati.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(assam, "Majuli Island", "majuli-island", "Majuli district",
                "Majuli is the world's largest river island, recognized as the epicentre of Neo-Vaishnavite culture and Satriya traditions in Assam.",
                "Formed by the Brahmaputra River and its tributaries, Majuli is celebrated for its monastic Satras established by Srimanta Sankardev and Madhavdev. Visitors can witness traditional bamboo mask-making, classical Sattriya dance, Mishing tribal village life, and scenic wetland birds.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Majuli_Island.jpg",
                "October to March (Raas Mahotsav in November is a major highlight)", "1 to 2 days", "Cultural cradle of the Neo-Vaishnavite movement since the 15th-16th century; headquarters of over 30 historic Satras (monasteries).", "Kamalabari Satra, Uttar Kamalabari Satra, Samaguri Satra (traditional mask-making), Dakhinpat Satra, Auniati Satra, Mishing tribal villages.",
                "Reach Majuli via regular government ferry from Nimati Ghat (Jorhat); renting bicycles or scooters on the island is the best way to explore at a relaxed pace.", "Satras are typically open 08:00 AM – 06:00 PM; ferries operate roughly 08:00 AM – 04:00 PM.", "Free entry to the island and Satras (donations welcome); ferry ticket costs ~₹15–₹30 per passenger.",
                new BigDecimal("26.95"), new BigDecimal("94.2167"), 3, 2024, 450000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Majuli_Island.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(assam, "Umananda Island", "umananda-island", "Guwahati, Kamrup Metropolitan",
                "Umananda Island, also known as Peacock Island, is the world's smallest inhabited river island, nestled in the middle of the mighty Brahmaputra River.",
                "Home to the 17th-century Shiva temple commissioned by Ahom king Gadadhar Singha, the island is enveloped in mythological lore where Lord Shiva is believed to have burnt Kamadeva to ashes. The rocky forested islet is also known as a habitat for the endangered golden langur.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Umananda_Island_from_banks_of_Brahmaputra.jpg",
                "October to April", "2 to 3 hours", "Features the Umananda Temple built in 1694 by Bar Phukan Garhganya Handique on the orders of King Supaatphaa (Gadadhar Singha).", "Umananda Shiva Temple, scenic ferry crossing on the Brahmaputra River, sightings of golden langurs, Guwahati riverfront ropeway views.",
                "Accessible via motorboats/ferries from Uzanbazar ghat or Sukreswar ghat; you can also take the Guwahati passenger ropeway overhead to North Guwahati.", "05:30 AM – 05:00 PM daily (Ferries operate till 04:30 PM)", "Free temple entry; government ferry costs ~₹20 per passenger each way (private boats charge ₹100–₹200).",
                new BigDecimal("26.1925"), new BigDecimal("91.7456"), 4, 2024, 580000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umananda_Island_from_banks_of_Brahmaputra.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(assam, "Sivasagar Sivadol", "sivasagar-sivadol", "Sivasagar district",
                "Sivasagar Sivadol is an imposing 18th-century temple dedicated to Lord Shiva, towering 104 feet high on the banks of the historic Borpukhuri tank.",
                "Constructed in 1734 by Queen Ambika, consort of Ahom King Shiva Singha, it is believed to be the tallest Shiva temple in Northeast India. The complex also features two accompanying temples—Vishnudol and Devidol—crowned by a pure gold dome (kalasha) that gleams against the skyline.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Siva_Dol.JPG",
                "October to March (Maha Shivaratri in February/March is celebrated on an immense scale)", "1 to 2 hours", "Monumental peak of Ahom temple architecture constructed in 1734 CE alongside the artificial Sivasagar tank (Borpukhuri).", "Main Sivadol shikhara and 8-foot golden kalasha, Vishnudol, Devidol, Borpukhuri lake promenade, Tai Ahom Museum nearby.",
                "Remove footwear at the temple complex entrance; photography inside the inner sanctum is prohibited; combine your visit with Rang Ghar and Talatal Ghar.", "06:00 AM – 06:00 PM daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("26.988"), new BigDecimal("94.636"), 5, 2024, 620000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siva_Dol.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(assam, "Rang Ghar", "rang-ghar", "Sivasagar district",
                "Rang Ghar is a two-storied royal amphitheatre often hailed as Asia’s oldest surviving sports pavilion, constructed by the Ahom dynasty.",
                "Built by King Pramatta Singha in 1744–1746 CE, the pavilion served as an observation platform where Ahom monarchs and nobles watched traditional sports such as buffalo fights, elephant fights, and wrestling during Rongali Bihu. Its architectural signature is its parabolic, inverted boat-shaped roof topped with a pair of ornamental Ahom dragons.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Rang_ghar.JPG",
                "October to March", "1 hour", "Symbol of Assam's royal architectural legacy built by Swargadeo Pramatta Singha; inspired the logo of the 33rd National Games held in Assam.", "Ahom boat-style curved roof, royal viewing chambers, arched brick hallways, landscaped archaeological gardens.",
                "ASI monument with QR code ticketing; best visited in morning or late afternoon for golden hour lighting and architectural photography.", "09:00 AM – 05:00 PM daily", "Indian citizens: ₹25; SAARC/BIMSTEC visitors: ₹25; Foreign tourists: ₹300; Children under 15: Free.",
                new BigDecimal("26.9697"), new BigDecimal("94.6225"), 6, 2024, 710000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rang_ghar.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(assam, "Talatal Ghar", "talatal-ghar", "Sivasagar district",
                "Talatal Ghar is the largest and grandest Tai-Ahom palace complex, renowned for its secret subterranean military chambers and tunnels.",
                "Commissioned by King Rudra Singha in 1698 and expanded by King Rajeswar Singha, this seven-storied brick fort consists of four upper stories and three underground levels. Built primarily as an army base, the subterranean corridors included secret escape tunnels connecting to the Dikhow River and Garhgaon Palace.",
                "Palace", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Talatal_Ghar.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 hours", "Strategic royal fortress and military headquarters of the Ahom capital Rangpur, constructed using indigenous mortar made of sticky rice and duck eggs.", "Seven-story royal structure, subterranean vaulted rooms, royal stables, open courtyards, historical cannons (bor-toop) displayed on grounds.",
                "The underground levels are sealed for visitor safety, but the ground and upper floors offer fascinating structural insights; wear comfortable walking shoes.", "09:00 AM – 05:00 PM daily", "Indian citizens: ₹25; Foreign tourists: ₹300; Children under 15: Free.",
                new BigDecimal("26.9589"), new BigDecimal("94.6339"), 7, 2024, 680000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Talatal_Ghar.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(assam, "Manas National Park", "manas-national-park", "Baksa and Chirang districts",
                "Manas National Park is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, Project Tiger Reserve, and biosphere reserve lying at the scenic foothills of the Bhutan Himalayas.",
                "Contiguous with Royal Manas National Park in Bhutan, Manas is renowned for its exceptional biodiversity, crystal-clear Manas River, and pristine forested landscapes. It is home to rare and endemic species including the pygmy hog, hispid hare, golden langur, Bengal tiger, and wild water buffalo.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Spectacular_view_of_the_mountains_from_Manas_National_Park.jpg",
                "November to April", "2 days (weekend getaway)", "Declared a Sanctuary in 1928, a Project Tiger Reserve in 1973, a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1985, and elevated to National Park in 1990.", "Manas River river rafting, Mathanguri forest lodge view, wildlife jeep and elephant safaris, golden langur habitat, Bhutan border crossing at Mathanguri.",
                "Stay overnight at Mathanguri or eco-lodges near the Bansbari gate; book safaris and forest permits in advance via the forest department counter.", "05:30 AM – 04:30 PM (Safaris timed at dawn and early afternoon)", "Indian nationals: ~₹100 entry fee + vehicle/safari charges (jeep safari approx ₹3,000–₹4,000); Foreign tourists: ~₹500 + safari charges.",
                new BigDecimal("26.7167"), new BigDecimal("90.9667"), 8, 2024, 140000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spectacular_view_of_the_mountains_from_Manas_National_Park.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(assam, "Pobitora Wildlife Sanctuary", "pobitora-wildlife-sanctuary", "Morigaon district",
                "Pobitora Wildlife Sanctuary holds the highest ecological density of the greater one-horned rhinoceros in the entire world.",
                "Located just 48 kilometers east of Guwahati, this compact 38.8-square-kilometer wetland grassland sanctuary guarantees rhino sightings within minutes of a safari. The sanctuary is also an Important Bird Area harboring migratory waterfowl, wild boars, and Asiatic buffaloes.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Pobitora_Wildlife_Sanctuary,_Assam.jpg",
                "November to March", "Half day (3 to 5 hours)", "Declared a reserve forest in 1971 and upgraded to a wildlife sanctuary in 1987 to protect rhinos in the Brahmaputra floodplain.", "Close-range rhino sightings, morning elephant safaris, open jeep safaris, birdwatching in Haduk Beel, Haduk hanging bridge.",
                "Easily done as a morning day-trip from Guwahati; morning elephant safaris (06:30 AM / 07:30 AM) offer the closest encounters with rhinos.", "06:30 AM – 04:00 PM (Safaris operate seasonally from Oct/Nov to April/May)", "Indian nationals: ~₹50 entry fee + jeep safari (~₹1,500–₹2,000) / elephant safari (~₹500–₹1,000); Foreigners: ~₹500 entry + safari fees.",
                new BigDecimal("26.2167"), new BigDecimal("92.05"), 9, 2024, 190000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pobitora_Wildlife_Sanctuary,_Assam.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(assam, "Haflong", "haflong", "Dima Hasao district",
                "Haflong is Assam's only hill station, celebrated for its emerald valleys, misty hills, vibrant tribal heritage, and colonial charm.",
                "Known affectionately as the 'Switzerland of the East' or 'White Ant Hillock', Haflong features the picturesque Haflong Lake at its centre spanned by a vintage hanging bridge. The town serves as the cultural hub of the Dimasa and other indigenous tribes, surrounded by tea gardens and cloud-kissed peaks.",
                "Hills", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Haflong_Lake.jpg",
                "October to April", "1 to 2 days", "Administrative headquarters of North Cachar Hills established during British colonial rule in the late 19th century; famous for heritage mountain railway routes.", "Haflong Lake and hanging bridge, Haflong View Point, Synod View Point, Abraham View Point, Maibang stone ruins (45 km away), heritage train rides via Vistadome coach.",
                "Take the Vistadome tourist train from Guwahati for spectacular views through the Borail hills; carry light woolens even during autumn and spring evenings.", "Public viewpoints and lake open all day (boating usually 09:00 AM – 05:00 PM)", "Free entry to the town and lake promenade; boating at Haflong Lake charges ~₹50–₹100 per boat.",
                new BigDecimal("25.1667"), new BigDecimal("93.0167"), 10, 2024, 210000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haflong_Lake.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(assam, "Hajo", "hajo", "Kamrup Rural district",
                "Hajo is a revered ancient pilgrimage centre on the northern bank of the Brahmaputra, representing harmonious confluence of Hinduism, Buddhism, and Islam.",
                "Situated around 30 km northwest of Guwahati, Hajo is acclaimed for the 16th-century Hayagriva Madhava Temple on Monikut Hill, revered by Hindus as a Vishnu shrine and by Buddhists who believe Gautama Buddha attained Parinirvana here. It is also home to Poa Mecca, an Islamic shrine built on Garurachal Hill believed to confer one-fourth the religious merit of Mecca.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Hayagriva_Madhava_Temple,_Hajo.jpg",
                "October to March", "3 to 4 hours", "Historic religious synthesis centre; the Hayagriva Madhava Temple was rebuilt in 1583 CE by Koch King Raghudeva Narayan.", "Hayagriva Madhava Temple, Madan Kamdev Temple ruins nearby, Poa Mecca (mosque built by Mir Lutfullah-i-Shiraji in 1657), Kedar Temple, traditional brass metal craft bell workshops.",
                "Wear modest attire covering shoulders and knees; remove shoes before entering temple and mosque premises; easily accessible by car or state bus from Guwahati.", "06:00 AM – 06:00 PM daily", "Free entry to both Hayagriva Madhava Temple and Poa Mecca.",
                new BigDecimal("26.2483"), new BigDecimal("91.5272"), 11, 2024, 340000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hayagriva_Madhava_Temple,_Hajo.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(assam, "Charaideo Maidams", "charaideo-maidams", "Charaideo district",
                "Charaideo Maidams is a UNESCO World Heritage Site comprising the royal burial mounds and sacred necropolis of the Ahom dynasty kings.",
                "Inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage list in July 2024, Charaideo was the first capital established in 1253 CE by Chaolung Sukaphaa, founder of the Ahom kingdom. The maidams, colloquially known as the 'Pyramids of Assam', are hemispherical earthen mounds over vaulted brick tombs that house royal remains and funerary treasures.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Charaideo_Maidam.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "The primary sacred resting ground of Ahom Swargadeos for over 600 years; designated India's 43rd UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2024.", "Royal Maidam mounds (Maidam No. 38, Maidam No. 89), excavated burial chambers, Archaeological Survey of India interpretive museum, serene hillock gardens.",
                "Located approximately 30 km from Sivasagar and 45 km from Sonari; hire a private taxi from Sivasagar; photography allowed in outdoor gardens.", "09:00 AM – 05:00 PM daily", "Indian citizens: ₹25; Foreign tourists: ₹300; Children under 15: Free.",
                new BigDecimal("26.936"), new BigDecimal("94.908"), 12, 2024, 280000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charaideo_Maidam.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(assam, "Assam State Museum", "assam-state-museum", "Guwahati, Kamrup Metropolitan",
                "Located in the heart of Guwahati near Dighalipukhuri, the Assam State Museum is Northeast India's premier institution for history, archaeology, and ethnography.",
                "Founded in 1940 by the Kamarupa Anusandhan Samiti and housed in an imposing British-era colonial building, the museum showcases rare stone and bronze sculptures dating from the 5th to 12th centuries CE. Its extensive galleries exhibit Ahom weaponry, traditional musical instruments, tribal ethnography, manuscripts, and the historic royal pleasure boat (Mayurpokhyi Khel-Nao).",
                "Museum", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Assam_State_Museum_main_gate_in_Guwahati_01.jpg",
                "Throughout the year (October to March is most comfortable for sightseeing)", "2 to 3 hours", "One of the oldest multipurpose museums in India, inaugurated in 1940 by Undivided Assam Governor Sir Robert Reid.", "Epigraphy and archaeology galleries, Ahom dynasty cannons and armor, Mayurpokhyi royal boat, ethnic tribal lifestyle dioramas, Dighalipukhuri lake opposite.",
                "Closed on Mondays and 2nd/4th Saturdays; nominal camera fees apply; conveniently located near Guwahati Railway Station and Ambari.", "10:00 AM – 05:00 PM (Closed on Mondays and Government Holidays", "Adults: ₹20; Children: ₹10; Foreign tourists: ₹100; Photography fee: ~₹50.",
                new BigDecimal("26.1867"), new BigDecimal("91.7508"), 13, 2024, 310000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Assam_State_Museum_main_gate_in_Guwahati_01.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(assam, "Srimanta Sankardev Kalakshetra", "srimanta-sankardev-kalakshetra", "Guwahati, Kamrup Metropolitan",
                "Srimanta Sankardev Kalakshetra is the largest cultural complex in Northeast India, celebrating Assam’s vibrant arts, literature, and ethnic heritage.",
                "Spanning over a sprawling hillside campus in Panjabari, Guwahati, the Kalakshetra was established in the 1990s as a result of the Assam Accord to preserve regional identity. It houses the Purvajjyotika Central Museum, Dr. Bhupen Hazarika Memorial, Lalit Kala Bhawan art gallery, open-air amphitheatres, and life-size ethnic village exhibits.",
                "Cultural", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Front_view_of_Srimanta_Sankardev_Kalakhetra,_Guwahati.jpg",
                "October to April", "3 to 4 hours", "Created under Clause VI of the Assam Accord to honor 15th-century saint-reformer Srimanta Sankardev and safeguard Assamese cultural legacy.", "Purvajjyotika Central Museum, Dr. Bhupen Hazarika Museum, Rang Ghar-inspired grand entrance gate, open-air amphitheatre, Children’s Park, cable-car/battery buggy tours.",
                "The campus is extensive; battery-operated golf carts are available for elderly visitors; evening cultural programs and laser light shows are held on weekends.", "10:00 AM – 07:00 PM daily (Museum galleries close by 06:30 PM)", "Adults: ₹30; Children: ₹10; Senior citizens: ₹20; Foreign nationals: ₹100.",
                new BigDecimal("26.1306"), new BigDecimal("91.8197"), 14, 2024, 850000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Front_view_of_Srimanta_Sankardev_Kalakhetra,_Guwahati.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(assam, "Deepor Beel", "deepor-beel", "Kamrup Metropolitan district",
                "Deepor Beel is a permanent freshwater lake and Assam’s only Ramsar Convention wetland of international importance.",
                "Located on the southwestern outskirts of Guwahati in a former channel of the Brahmaputra River, Deepor Beel supports exceptional aquatic biodiversity and serves as an elephant corridor connecting Rani and Garbhanga reserve forests. It is an Important Bird Area that attracts over 150 species of resident and migratory birds, including the critically endangered greater adjutant stork.",
                "Nature", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Deepor_beel.jpg",
                "November to March (peak migratory bird season)", "2 to 3 hours", "Designated as Assam’s first Ramsar Site in November 2002; historically served as an important water defence channel for the kingdom of Kamarupa.", "Birdwatching from the watchtower, greater adjutant stork nesting sites, herds of wild Asian elephants foraging at water’s edge, sunset views over the wetland.",
                "Bring telephoto lenses or binoculars for birdwatching; early morning or sunset provides the best lighting and wildlife sightings; combine with a visit to the nearby Assam Engineering College hill.", "06:00 AM – 06:00 PM daily", "Free access to lakeside viewpoints; watchtower entry ~₹20 per person.",
                new BigDecimal("26.125"), new BigDecimal("91.6583"), 15, 2024, 160000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deepor_beel.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(assam, "Kakochang Waterfall", "kakochang-waterfall", "Golaghat and Karbi Anglong border",
                "Kakochang Waterfall (locally Kaipholangso) is a spectacular cascade tucked inside lush forests and tea plantations near Bokakhat and Kaziranga.",
                "Falling from a height of approximately 40 meters into a clear natural pool, the waterfall offers an invigorating trek through rubber estates and tropical hillsides. It is a favored excursion for travelers exploring Kaziranga National Park and the historic ruins of Deopahar.",
                "Waterfall", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Heritage_amid_Sengsua_Tea_Garden,_Jorhat,_Assam.jpg",
                "October to March (post-monsoon water volume is robust and trekking trails are accessible)", "3 to 4 hours (including trek)", "A cherished natural landmark located close to ancient 10th-11th century archaeological ruins at Numaligarh and Deopahar.", "40-meter cascading falls, natural plunge pool for wading, trek through rubber and coffee plantations, scenic picnic areas.",
                "Requires a moderate 2.5–3 km trek from the nearest motorable road; wear non-slip shoes and carry drinking water; visit during daylight hours.", "06:00 AM – 05:00 PM (Recommended to start before 02:00 PM)", "Free entry (local parking and village maintenance fee of ~₹20–₹50 per vehicle may apply).",
                new BigDecimal("26.5417"), new BigDecimal("93.5333"), 16, 2024, 110000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://tourism.gov.in", "UNRESOLVED");

        createDestination(assam, "Jatinga", "jatinga", "Dima Hasao district",
                "Jatinga is a tranquil scenic ridge village in the Borail Range, internationally famous for the mysterious seasonal 'bird suicide' phenomenon.",
                "Located just 9 kilometers south of Haflong, Jatinga is primarily inhabited by the Jaintia (Pnars) and Dimasa communities amidst orange orchards and misty green ridges. On foggy, moonless nights between September and November, high-velocity winds disorient birds which dive towards village light sources, a zoological curiosity documented by ornithologists worldwide.",
                "Nature", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Overhead_view_of_Jatinga_village,_Assam.jpg",
                "September to November for the bird phenomenon; October to March for pleasant hill weather", "Half day (3 to 4 hours) or overnight stay", "Settled by the Jaintia community in the early 20th century; gained global ornithological fame following studies by naturalist E. P. Gee in the 1950s.", "Jatinga Bird Watch Tower, scenic views of the Borail mountain range, orange and pineapple groves, tribal village handicrafts and culture.",
                "The bird phenomenon only occurs during foggy, moonless autumn nights with specific wind conditions; easily reachable by taxi from Haflong.", "Open all day; bird watch tower typically accessible 06:00 AM – 06:00 PM", "Free village access; watchtower fee ~₹10–₹20 per visitor.",
                new BigDecimal("25.12"), new BigDecimal("93.0333"), 17, 2024, 95000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Overhead_view_of_Jatinga_village,_Assam.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(assam, "Orang National Park", "orang-national-park", "Darrang and Sonitpur districts",
                "Often called the 'Mini Kaziranga', Orang National Park is a critical tiger reserve and one-horned rhino refuge on the northern bank of the Brahmaputra.",
                "Spanning 79 square kilometers of alluvial floodplains, grassland savannah, and tropical moist deciduous forests, Orang mirrors Kaziranga's landscape and ecological richness. The park shelters the royal Bengal tiger with one of India's highest tiger densities, along with elephants, pygmy hogs, and the endangered Bengal florican.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Greater_one-horned_rhinoceros.jpg",
                "November to April", "Half day to 1 full day", "Originally established as a game reserve in 1919, declared a wildlife sanctuary in 1985, upgraded to a National Park in 1999, and notified as a Tiger Reserve in 2016.", "Greater one-horned rhino safari, Bengal tiger tracking, river dolphins in the Brahmaputra, birdwatching (Bengal florican and pelicans), morning elephant safaris.",
                "Located roughly 110 km from Tezpur and 140 km from Guwahati; less crowded than Kaziranga, offering peaceful safari experiences; hire a jeep from Silbori gate.", "06:00 AM – 04:30 PM (Morning safari: 06:30–09:30 AM; Afternoon safari: 01:30–04:00 PM)", "Indian nationals: ~₹50 entry fee + vehicle/jeep safari (~₹1,500–₹2,000); Foreigners: ~₹500 + safari charges.",
                new BigDecimal("26.55"), new BigDecimal("92.3333"), 18, 2024, 65000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greater_one-horned_rhinoceros.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(assam, "Tocklai Tea Research Institute", "tocklai-tea-research-institute", "Jorhat district",
                "Established in 1911, Tocklai Tea Research Institute is the oldest and largest tea research station in the world.",
                "Operated by the Tea Research Association (TRA), Tocklai pioneered modern global tea science, developing renowned clone varieties, pest management practices, and manufacturing equipment like the CTC tea machine. Situated in Jorhat—the tea capital of Assam—its leafy campus houses heritage British colonial laboratories, tea experimental plots, and a specialized tea tasting unit.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tocklai_Experimental_Station.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 hours", "Founded in 1911 by the Indian Tea Association on the banks of the Tocklai river; revolutionized global tea cultivation and processing technology.", "Historic colonial administrative building and laboratories, experimental tea gardens and germplasm bank, Tea Museum, model miniature tea manufacturing plant.",
                "Prior permission or visitor appointment is recommended on working days for laboratory tours and tea tasting sessions; located in central Jorhat city.", "09:30 AM – 05:00 PM (Monday to Friday; closed on weekends and national holidays)", "Free entry (prior official appointment or permission required for educational/academic tours).",
                new BigDecimal("26.7569"), new BigDecimal("94.2217"), 19, 2024, 80000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tocklai_Experimental_Station.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(assam, "Bogamati", "bogamati", "Baksa district",
                "Bogamati is a picturesque eco-tourism retreat along the pristine Bornadi river, situated at the border foothills of Assam and Bhutan.",
                "Named 'Bogamati' (meaning 'White Soil' in Assamese) after the white deposits found along the riverbed, this scenic hotspot is surrounded by the lush Bornadi Wildlife Sanctuary and the Bhutan hills. It has transformed into a bustling destination for white-water river rafting, zip-lining, trekking, and riverside picnics.",
                "Nature", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bogamati_view.jpg",
                "November to April", "Half day to 1 full day", "Once an unstable border territory, Bogamati was developed into an eco-tourism and peace destination by the Bodoland Territorial Council and local community initiatives.", "Bornadi river rafting, river zip-lining, Buddha statue atop the hillock, panoramic viewpoints of the Bhutan mountain ranges, picnic grounds.",
                "Carry a change of clothes if doing water rafting; bring cash as digital network connectivity can be spotty in the foothills; reach via Baihata Chariali/Goreswar.", "07:00 AM – 05:00 PM daily", "Free entry to the riverfront area; nominal vehicle parking fee (~₹50); adventure activities priced separately (rafting ~₹200–₹500 per person).",
                new BigDecimal("26.8378"), new BigDecimal("91.5647"), 20, 2024, 120000L, "TOTAL",
                "Directorate of Tourism, Government of Assam", "Assam Tourism Statistics Record 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bogamati_view.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        // --- 4. BIHAR ---
        State bihar = createState("Bihar", "bihar", "STATE", "Eastern", "Patna", "Cradle of Buddhism and Jainism with ancient heritage.",
                "Bihar is the birthplace of Buddhism and Jainism, home to the ancient Nalanda University and Bodh Gaya where Lord Buddha attained enlightenment.",
                "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mahabodhitemple.jpg", "October to March");

        createDestination(bihar, "Mahabodhi Temple, Bodh Gaya", "mahabodhi-temple-bodh-gaya", "Gaya",
                "An ancient UNESCO World Heritage Buddhist temple marking the sacred site where Siddhartha Gautama attained supreme enlightenment under the Bodhi Tree.",
                "The Mahabodhi Temple Complex is one of the four holy sites related to the life of the Lord Buddha, featuring a towering 55-metre grand pyramidal spire built of brick. It houses the sacred Bodhi Tree, the diamond throne (Vajrasana), and seven meditative reflection spots preserving more than two millennia of Buddhist monastic tradition.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mahabodhitemple.jpg",
                "October to March", "2-3 hours", "Built initially by Emperor Ashoka in the 3rd century BCE with the present grand brick temple dating back to the 5th-6th century CE Gupta era; site of Buddha's enlightenment in 531 BCE.", "Sacred Bodhi Tree, Vajrasana (Diamond Throne), Animesh Lochan Chaitya, Chankramana, Muchalinda Lake",
                "Dress respectfully covering shoulders and knees; mobile phones and electronic devices are prohibited inside the inner complex; early morning visits offer the most peaceful atmosphere.", "05:00 AM - 09:00 PM daily", "Free entry (Still camera: ₹100, Video camera: ₹300)",
                new BigDecimal("24.6959"), new BigDecimal("84.9913"), 1, 2024, 2500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahabodhitemple.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Nalanda Mahavihara", "nalanda-mahavihara", "Nalanda",
                "The excavated ruins of an acclaimed ancient Buddhist residential university that flourished from the 5th to the 12th century CE.",
                "Nalanda Mahavihara was the ancient world's premier residential university, attracting over 10,000 scholars and monks from across Asia, including Xuanzang and Yijing. The UNESCO-listed archaeological complex spans monumental brick stupas, viharas (monasteries), prayer halls, and an extensive drainage system showcasing sophisticated ancient Indian architecture.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Temple_No.-_3%2C_Nalanda_Archaeological_Site.jpg",
                "October to March", "2-4 hours", "Founded under Gupta Emperor Kumaragupta I in the 5th century CE and patronized by Emperor Harsha and Pala kings until sacked in 1193 CE; designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2016.", "Sariputta Stupa (Temple No. 3), Monastery Complex No. 1, Excavated Chaityas and Viharas, Nalanda Archaeological Museum, Xuanzang Memorial Hall",
                "Hire an ASI-certified guide to appreciate the intricate layout; carry drinking water, sunglasses, and an umbrella as the exposed brick complex provides little shade.", "09:00 AM - 05:00 PM daily", "Indian citizens: ₹40, Foreign tourists: ₹600, Children below 15: Free",
                new BigDecimal("25.1357"), new BigDecimal("85.4449"), 2, 2024, 1400000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_No.-_3,_Nalanda_Archaeological_Site.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Rajgir", "rajgir", "Nalanda",
                "An ancient historic valley surrounded by seven rugged hills, celebrated as the first capital of the powerful Magadha Empire.",
                "Nestled amidst lush green hills, Rajgir (ancient Rajagriha) was the venerable capital where King Bimbisara ruled and where Gautama Buddha delivered foundational discourses on Vulture Peak. The destination boasts the 40-kilometre pre-Mauryan Cyclopean Wall, therapeutic sulfur hot springs (Brahmakund), Venuvana bamboo grove, and ancient chariot tracks cut into bedrock.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Cyclopean_Wall_of_Rajgir.jpg",
                "October to March", "1-2 days", "Pre-Mauryan capital of the Magadha kingdom under the Haryanka dynasty, setting of the First Buddhist Council in 483 BCE at Saptaparni Cave, and revered by Mahavira.", "Cyclopean Wall of Rajgir, Gridhrakuta (Vulture Peak), Venuvana Bamboo Grove, Saptaparni Cave, Brahmakund Hot Springs, Bimbisara Jail",
                "Combine your visit with Nalanda; use battery-operated rickshaws or tongas for intra-city transit; early morning visits to hot springs and hills avoid crowds.", "06:00 AM - 06:00 PM daily", "Free to nominal (ASI monuments: ₹25-₹40)",
                new BigDecimal("25.0267"), new BigDecimal("85.4194"), 3, 2024, 1850000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cyclopean_Wall_of_Rajgir.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Vaishali", "vaishali", "Vaishali",
                "A historic archaeological destination revered as one of the world's earliest known democratic republics and a seminal pilgrimage centre for Buddhists and Jains.",
                "Vaishali was the thriving ancient capital of the Licchavi republic where Lord Buddha preached his last sermon and announced his approaching Mahaparinirvana. The expansive archaeological park features the pristine monolithic Lion Pillar of Ashoka, the Ananda Stupa, the relic stupa that yielded Buddha's ashes, and the sacred Abhishek Pushkarini coronation pond.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ananda_Stupa_with_Ashok_lion_pillar_at_vaishali%2C_Bihar_02.jpg",
                "October to March", "2-3 hours", "Capital of the Vajji republic, considered the world's first republic; site of the Second Buddhist Council in 383 BCE; birthplace of Lord Mahavira (Kundalpur).", "Ashokan Lion Pillar at Kolhua, Ananda Stupa, Buddha Relic Stupa, Abhishek Pushkarini, Raja Vishal Ka Garh, Vaishali Museum",
                "Visit the Vaishali Archaeological Museum situated nearby; best explored during cooler morning hours; private taxis from Patna are convenient.", "06:00 AM - 06:00 PM daily", "Indian citizens: ₹25, Foreign tourists: ₹300, Children under 15: Free",
                new BigDecimal("25.9868"), new BigDecimal("85.1278"), 4, 2024, 850000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ananda_Stupa_with_Ashok_lion_pillar_at_vaishali,_Bihar_02.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Vikramshila University Ruins", "vikramshila-university-ruins", "Bhagalpur",
                "The monumental archaeological remains of the ancient Mahavihara founded during the Pala Empire, once renowned across Asia for Buddhist philosophy and Tantric scholarship.",
                "Established by King Dharmapala in the late 8th century CE, Vikramshila was one of the two most illustrious seats of learning in ancient India alongside Nalanda. The excavated complex features a colossal cruciform central stupa adorned with terracotta plaques, an expansive quadrangular monastery with over 200 residential cells, and an on-site archaeological museum.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Vikramashila_University_Landscape_front_view.jpg",
                "October to March", "2-3 hours", "Pala dynasty centre of intellectual excellence and Vajrayana Buddhism, produced renowned master scholar Atisha Dipankara; destroyed in 1193 CE.", "Central Cruciform Stupa, Monastic Quadrangle and Meditation Cells, Terracotta Relief Panels, Vikramshila Archaeological Museum",
                "Located roughly 50 km from Bhagalpur; hire local transport in advance as amenities near the excavation site are minimal.", "09:00 AM - 05:00 PM (Museum closed on Fridays)", "Indian citizens: ₹25, Foreign tourists: ₹300, Children under 15: Free",
                new BigDecimal("25.3283"), new BigDecimal("87.2889"), 5, 2024, 320000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vikramashila_University_Landscape_front_view.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Patna Sahib Gurudwara", "patna-sahib-gurudwara", "Patna",
                "One of Sikhism's five holy Takhts, built to commemorate the birthplace of the tenth Sikh Guru, Guru Gobind Singh.",
                "Located in the historic old quarters of Patna near the banks of the Ganges, Takht Sri Harmandir Ji Patna Sahib is an ornate white marble gurdwara first built by Maharaja Ranjit Singh. The sacred shrine enshrines rare personal relics of Guru Gobind Singh, including golden footstools, an inscribed wooden comb, and ancient handwritten copies of scriptures.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Takht_Sri_Patna_Sahib_01.jpg",
                "October to March (especially during Prakash Parv celebrations)", "1-2 hours", "Birthplace of Guru Gobind Singh Ji in 1666 CE; visited by Guru Nanak Dev Ji and Guru Tegh Bahadur Ji; established as one of the Five Takhts of Sikh authority.", "Main Darbar Sahib, Guru Gobind Singh Ji Relics Museum, Guru Ka Langar, Historic Sacred Well, Ganga Ghats",
                "Remove footwear, wash hands/feet, and cover head with a scarf before entering; partake in the community langar; respect photography restrictions in the sanctum.", "Open 24 hours daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("25.5925"), new BigDecimal("85.2281"), 6, 2024, 2200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Takht_Sri_Patna_Sahib_01.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Golghar", "golghar", "Patna",
                "An iconic beehive-shaped British-era granary in central Patna, built in 1786 following the devastating Bengal famine of 1770.",
                "Designed by Captain John Garstin for the British East India Company, Golghar is a pillarless domed granary capable of holding over 137,000 tons of grain. It features dual spiraling exterior staircases with 145 steps wrapping around the dome, which lead to a summit viewing deck offering sweeping panoramic views of Patna city and the meandering Ganges River.",
                "Monument", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Golghar_patna_Bihar_India.jpg",
                "October to March (late afternoons for sunset)", "45 mins - 1.5 hours", "Commissioned by Governor-General Warren Hastings in 1786 as a strategic grain reservoir against recurring famines; pioneering pillarless brick masonry structure.", "Beehive-shaped Granary Dome, Twin Spiral Staircases, Summit Panoramic River Viewpoint, Surrounding Landscaped Gardens",
                "Climb the steps carefully as stairs can be steep; visit in late afternoon to enjoy cool breezes and picturesque river sunset views.", "10:00 AM - 05:00 PM (Tuesday to Sunday, closed Mondays)", "₹10 per person; Children under 5: Free",
                new BigDecimal("25.619"), new BigDecimal("85.1432"), 7, 2024, 950000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golghar_patna_Bihar_India.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Barabar Caves", "barabar-caves", "Jehanabad",
                "The oldest surviving rock-cut caves in India, carved into solid granite hills with mirror-like internal polish during the Mauryan Empire.",
                "Dating to the reign of Emperor Ashoka in the 3rd century BCE, the Barabar Caves served as an ascetic sanctuary for monks of the ancient Ajivika sect. The four primary caves—Lomas Rishi, Sudama, Karan Chaupar, and Visva Zopri—feature sophisticated horseshoe-shaped chaitya arches and glass-smooth polished granite interiors that produce phenomenal reverberating acoustic resonance.",
                "Caves", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Lomas_Rishi_entrance.jpg",
                "October to March", "2-3 hours", "Oldest rock-cut caves in India (circa 260 BCE), commissioned by Ashoka and grandson Dasharatha; inspiration for the 'Marabar Caves' in E.M. Forster's A Passage to India.", "Lomas Rishi Cave (with elephant frieze facade), Sudama Cave, Karan Chaupar Cave, Visva Zopri Cave, Nagarjuni Caves",
                "Carry a good flashlight to examine the mirror polish and inscriptions inside; test the acoustic echo with a low vocal chant; wear sturdy shoes.", "06:00 AM - 06:00 PM daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("25.0069"), new BigDecimal("85.0625"), 8, 2024, 210000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lomas_Rishi_entrance.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Pawapuri Jal Mandir", "pawapuri-jal-mandir", "Nalanda",
                "A sacred white marble Jain temple floating serenely in the centre of a large lotus pond, marking the cremation place of Lord Mahavira.",
                "Known as Apapuri ('town free of sins'), Pawapuri is the revered site where Lord Mahavira, the 24th and last Jain Tirthankara, attained Nirvana in 527 BCE. The Jal Mandir is reached via a 600-foot-long red stone bridge spanning a blooming water-lily tank; according to legend, the pond was formed by pilgrims scooping up soil mixed with Mahavira's holy ashes.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Pawapuri_-_001_Temple_marking_Mahavira%27s_Passing_(9243092471).jpg",
                "October to March (especially during Diwali for Nirvana Mahotsav)", "1-2 hours", "Mahaparinirvana and cremation site of Lord Mahavira in 527 BCE; the current marble sanctum was built by King Nandivardhan, the elder brother of Mahavira.", "Jal Mandir Water Temple, Kamal Sarovar (Lotus Pond), Red Sandstone Causeways, Charan Paduka, Samosharan Temple",
                "Dress modestly in accordance with Jain temple guidelines; maintain quiet reverence on the causeway; early mornings or sunsets offer photogenic reflections.", "06:00 AM - 08:00 PM daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("25.0934"), new BigDecimal("85.5269"), 9, 2024, 680000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pawapuri_-_001_Temple_marking_Mahavira%27s_Passing_(9243092471).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Kesaria Stupa", "kesaria-stupa", "East Champaran",
                "One of the tallest and grandest ancient Buddhist stupas in the world, standing majestically in East Champaran.",
                "Excavated by the Archaeological Survey of India in 1998, Kesaria Stupa rises over 104 feet (originally around 123 feet before the 1934 earthquake) with a circumference of roughly 1,400 feet, surpassing Indonesia's Borobudur in original scale. The cylindrical multi-terraced brick edifice is lined with circular prayer cells and niches that house life-sized stone and terracotta images of the seated Buddha.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kesariya.jpg",
                "October to March", "1-2 hours", "Constructed by Mauryan and later Gupta rulers; marks the spot where Buddha gave his begging bowl to the Lichhavis of Vaishali before his final journey to Kushinagar.", "Multi-tiered Circular Stupa, Terracotta and Stone Buddha Niches, Excavated Brick Terraces, Surrounding Archaeological Park",
                "Located about 110 km northwest of Patna and 55 km from Motihari; wear comfortable walking shoes and bring sun protection.", "06:00 AM - 06:00 PM daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("26.3503"), new BigDecimal("84.8736"), 10, 2024, 280000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kesariya.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Valmiki Tiger Reserve", "valmiki-tiger-reserve", "West Champaran",
                "Bihar's only national park and tiger reserve, nestled along the foothills of the Himalayas and the picturesque banks of the Gandak River.",
                "Encompassing nearly 900 square kilometres on the India-Nepal border contiguous with Chitwan National Park, Valmiki Tiger Reserve hosts diverse terai-bhabar sal forests, riverine grasslands, and hills. The reserve protects thriving populations of Royal Bengal tigers, leopards, Indian rhinoceroses, flying foxes, and diverse Himalayan avifauna.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Himalayan_Foot_Hills%2C_Valmikinagar%2C_Bihar%2C_India.jpg",
                "November to April", "1-2 days", "Ancient hermitage site of sage Valmiki where Sita took refuge and gave birth to Lava and Kusha in the Ramayana; designated Project Tiger Reserve in 1990.", "Jungle Jeep Safari, Gandak River Boating, Valmiki Ashram, Triveni Sangam, Cane Brakes and Bhediyari Watchtower",
                "Pre-book safari permits and forest rest house accommodation through Bihar Tourism or Forest Department portal; carry warm clothing during winter morning safaris.", "06:00 AM - 05:30 PM (safari batches)", "Entry fee: ₹100 per person; Jeep safari: ₹2,500 - ₹3,500 per vehicle",
                new BigDecimal("27.3516"), new BigDecimal("84.2155"), 11, 2024, 120000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Himalayan_Foot_Hills,_Valmikinagar,_Bihar,_India.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Rohtasgarh Fort", "rohtasgarh-fort", "Rohtas",
                "A colossal hill fort perched high atop the Kaimur plateau, ranking among the largest fortress complexes in medieval India.",
                "Spanning an immense perimeter of nearly 45 kilometres atop an isolated 1,500-foot-high cliff overlooking the Son River valley, Rohtasgarh was traditionally founded by Rohitashva, son of mythical King Harishchandra. The citadel contains majestic Mughal-era palaces, carved elephant gates (Hathi Pol), the Aina Mahal palace of mirrors, the Jami Masjid, and scenic bastions fortified by Sher Shah Suri and Raja Man Singh.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Rohtasgarh_Fort_Entrance.jpg",
                "October to March", "4-6 hours", "Ancient Hindu stronghold captured by Sher Shah Suri in 1538 using a legendary ruse of armed soldiers in palanquins; later served as regional headquarters for Raja Man Singh.", "Hathi Pol (Elephant Gate), Aina Mahal, Baradari, Shahi Jami Masjid, Habsh Khan Tomb, Son River Plateau Vista",
                "Moderate uphill trek or 4WD vehicle required; carry sufficient water, packed food, and sun protection as basic amenities atop the plateau are sparse.", "06:00 AM - 05:00 PM daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("24.63"), new BigDecimal("83.9167"), 12, 2024, 180000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rohtasgarh_Fort_Entrance.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Sher Shah Suri Tomb", "sher-shah-suri-tomb", "Rohtas",
                "A magnificent Indo-Islamic mausoleum standing in the centre of a large artificial lake, often hailed as the second Taj Mahal of India.",
                "Built between 1540 and 1545 CE by architect Mir Muhammad Aliwal Khan, this majestic red sandstone octagonal tomb rises 122 feet over a stepped square plinth surrounded by water. The mausoleum features a colossal 22-metre-wide dome, elegant chattris (domed pavilions), domed kiosks, and a wide causeway bridge connecting the island tomb to the northern gateway.",
                "Monument", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sher_Shah_Suri_Tomb.jpg",
                "October to March", "1-2 hours", "Final resting place of Sultan Sher Shah Suri, founder of the Suri Empire who rebuilt the Grand Trunk Road and introduced the Rupiya currency; influenced later Mughal architecture.", "Central Octagonal Water Tomb, Grand Reflection Lake, Arched Stone Causeway Bridge, Step-access Water Ghats, Carved Sandstone Jalis",
                "Sasaram is well-connected on the Grand Chord railway line; photograph the tomb in early morning or golden hour light for stunning reflections on the lake.", "06:00 AM - 06:00 PM daily", "Indian citizens: ₹25, Foreign tourists: ₹300, Children under 15: Free",
                new BigDecimal("24.9458"), new BigDecimal("84.0153"), 13, 2024, 450000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sher_Shah_Suri_Tomb.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Mundeshwari Temple", "mundeshwari-temple", "Kaimur",
                "Widely recognized as one of the oldest functional Hindu temples in the world, dedicated to Shiva and Shakti.",
                "Perched atop the Mundeshwari Hill in Kaimur at an elevation of 600 feet, this unique octagonal stone temple contains inscriptions dated to 625 CE (or earlier Saka era 108 CE). Inside the ancient sanctum are a four-faced stone Chaturmukha Shiva Lingam and a revered idol of Goddess Mundeshwari (a form of Durga riding a buffalo), where worship has continued unbroken for millennia.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Maa_Mundeshwari_Devi.jpg",
                "October to March (and during Navratri)", "1-2 hours", "Documented by the ASI as one of the oldest active temples in India dating back to the Gupta period with continuous ritual worship.", "Rare Nagara Octagonal Sanctum Plan, Chaturmukha Shiva Lingam, Maa Mundeshwari Devi Idol, Ancient Stone Carvings, Scenic Hilltop Panoramas",
                "Accessible by road with stone stairs and motorable road to summit; special non-violent animal sacrifice ritual (ahimsa pratha) is unique here.", "06:00 AM - 07:00 PM daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("25.0211"), new BigDecimal("83.5683"), 14, 2024, 750000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maa_Mundeshwari_Devi.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Kanwar Lake Bird Sanctuary", "kanwar-lake-bird-sanctuary", "Begusarai",
                "Asia's largest freshwater oxbow lake and Bihar's first Ramsar wetland site, serving as a critical haven for migratory birds.",
                "Formed by the meandering geological course of the Gandak River, Kanwar Taal (Kabartal) spans over 67 square kilometres of open water, marshes, and reed beds. In winter months, it plays host to over 106 species of resident and migratory waterbirds travelling along the Central Asian Flyway, including the critically endangered oriental white-backed vulture, long-billed vulture, and greater spotted eagle.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kanwar_Lake_Begusarai_by_Ziddi_Photowala.jpg",
                "November to February", "2-4 hours", "Designated as Bihar's first Ramsar Wetland of International Importance in 2020; formed naturally as an ancient oxbow lake of the Burhi Gandak River.", "Migratory Bird Watching, Oxbow Wetland Ecology, Country Boat Rides, Jaimangalgarh Temple Island, Diverse Aquatic Flora",
                "Bring high-magnification binoculars or telephoto lenses; hire a local boatman early in the morning for bird sightings; carry warm clothing during winter mornings.", "06:00 AM - 05:30 PM daily", "Free entry (boat hire extra)",
                new BigDecimal("25.6292"), new BigDecimal("86.1472"), 15, 2024, 95000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kanwar_Lake_Begusarai_by_Ziddi_Photowala.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Kakolat Waterfall", "kakolat-waterfall", "Nawada",
                "A picturesque cascade tumbling from a height of about 150 feet down the Kakolat hill into a crystal-clear natural plunge pool.",
                "Situated on the border of Bihar and Jharkhand amidst dense deciduous forests of the Kakolat hill range, this perennial waterfall is celebrated for its refreshing and pleasantly cool waters throughout the year. The pool at the base is a favorite spot for bathing, picnics, and cultural gatherings, steeped in mythological lore involving the Pandavas and a cursed king.",
                "Waterfall", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kakolat_waterfall.jpg",
                "October to March (or during Baisakhi festival in April)", "2-3 hours", "Associated with Treta and Dvapara Yuga legends; Hindu myth recounts that an ancient king cursed to live as a python was liberated by the Pandavas during their exile here.", "150-foot Cascading Waterfall, Natural Plunge Pool (Kund), Forested Hill Surroundings, Eco-tourism Pathways, Annual Baisakhi Fair Site",
                "Slippery rocks require cautious walking; carry spare clothes and towels if you plan to swim in the pool; facilities include eco-tourism pathways and changing rooms.", "06:00 AM - 05:00 PM daily", "Nominal entry fee (approx ₹10 - ₹20)",
                new BigDecimal("24.6869"), new BigDecimal("85.6178"), 16, 2024, 520000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kakolat_waterfall.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Vishwa Shanti Stupa, Rajgir", "vishwa-shanti-stupa-rajgir", "Nalanda",
                "A dazzling white Peace Pagoda perched atop the Ratnagiri Hill in Rajgir, symbolizing global peace and non-violence.",
                "Conceptualized by Japanese Buddhist monk Nichidatsu Fujii (Guruji) and designed by Minoru Ohoka, this 400-metre-high stupa was consecrated in 1969 by President V.V. Giri. The marble monument features four golden statues of the Buddha depicting his birth, enlightenment, first sermon, and parinirvana, and is accessible via an iconic aerial chairlift ropeway.",
                "Monument", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Rajgir_-_046_Side_View_(9242059259).jpg",
                "October to March", "2-3 hours", "Consecrated in 1969 to commemorate the 2500th anniversary of Buddha's Parinirvana; built atop Ratnagiri hill near Gridhrakuta where the Buddha preached the Lotus Sutra.", "White Marble Peace Pagoda, Four Golden Buddha Sculptures, Aerial Single-Chair Ropeway, Gridhrakuta Pathway, Japanese Nipponzan Myohoji Temple",
                "Take the chairlift ropeway for scenic views going up and walk down via the trail connecting to Gridhrakuta; early morning visits have shorter ropeway queues.", "09:00 AM - 05:00 PM daily", "Stupa entry free; Aerial ropeway approx ₹100 - ₹120 round-trip",
                new BigDecimal("25.0069"), new BigDecimal("85.4458"), 17, 2024, 1600000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rajgir_-_046_Side_View_(9242059259).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Bihar Museum", "bihar-museum", "Patna",
                "A world-class cultural institution and architectural marvel showcasing Bihar's millennial heritage and ancient civilizations.",
                "Designed by Japanese architectural firm Maki and Associates, the sprawling 13.9-acre museum campus seamlessly integrates Corten steel pavilions, sunken courtyards, and tranquil water bodies. Its state-of-the-art galleries feature priceless masterworks of antiquity—including the world-famous Didarganj Yakshi, Mauryan and Gupta stone sculptures, Nalanda bronzes, and immersive regional folk art installations.",
                "Museum", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bihar_Museum.jpg",
                "October to March", "3-5 hours", "Conceived as an international showcase of Bihar's civilizational contributions; houses the priceless Didarganj Yakshi (3rd century BCE polished Mauryan masterpiece) and artifacts spanning 3,000 years.", "Didarganj Yakshi Masterpiece, Children's Discovery Gallery, History Galleries A to D, Visible Storage and Conservation Lab, Corten Steel Architecture",
                "Dedicate at least half a day; photography is permitted in non-flash mode; audio guides are available at the reception desk; Monday is the weekly closing day.", "10:30 AM - 05:00 PM (Tuesday to Sunday; closed on Mondays)", "Indian adults: ₹100, Children: ₹50, Foreign tourists: ₹500",
                new BigDecimal("25.6111"), new BigDecimal("85.1147"), 18, 2024, 820000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bihar_Museum.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Maner Sharif", "maner-sharif", "Patna",
                "An ancient Sufi pilgrimage town famous for the magnificent 17th-century Mughal mausoleum known as Chhoti Dargah.",
                "Situated about 30 km west of Patna, Maner Sharif is renowned for its two celebrated Sufi shrines: the Badi Dargah (tomb of 13th-century Sufi saint Makhdoom Yahya Maneri) and the Chhoti Dargah (tomb of Makhdoom Shah Daulat, built in 1616 by Ibrahim Khan). The Chhoti Dargah is widely admired as one of the finest Mughal monuments in eastern India, featuring delicate sandstone lattice screens (jalis), a high fluted dome, and ceiling panels inscribed with Quranic calligraphy.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tomb_of_Shah_Makhadum_Daulat_Maneri_and_Ibrahim_Khan.jpg",
                "October to March (especially during annual Urs festival)", "1-2 hours", "Flourished as a major Sufi intellectual center from the 12th century; Chhoti Dargah represents the highest achievement of Jahangir-era Mughal sandstone architecture in Bihar.", "Chhoti Dargah (Makhdoom Shah Daulat Tomb), Badi Dargah, Historic Stepped Water Tank, Carved Sandstone Jalis, Traditional Maner ka Laddu",
                "Dress respectfully as it is an active religious shrine; remove shoes before entering the sanctum; don't miss tasting the authentic gram-flour 'Maner ka Laddu'.", "06:00 AM - 07:00 PM daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("25.6483"), new BigDecimal("84.8778"), 19, 2024, 310000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Shah_Makhadum_Daulat_Maneri_and_Ibrahim_Khan.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(bihar, "Lauria Nandangarh", "lauria-nandangarh", "West Champaran",
                "A renowned archaeological site featuring a complete monolithic Ashoka pillar and massive ancient stupa and burial mounds.",
                "Located in West Champaran near the Burhi Gandak River, Lauriya Nandangarh features a single intact 35-foot-tall sandstone Ashoka Pillar erected in 249 BCE, complete with its circular bell-shaped capital and seated single lion. Adjacent to the pillar lies the colossal Nandangarh mound, a multi-terraced Buddhist brick stupa soaring over 80 feet, alongside ancient earthen mounds believed to be pre-Mauryan royal burials.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Lauria_Nandangarh_pillar_of_Ashoka_side_view.jpg",
                "October to March", "1-2 hours", "Monolithic pillar bearing Emperor Ashoka's six major pillar edicts in pristine Brahmi script; huge terraced stupa mound excavated by N.G. Majumdar.", "Intact Ashoka Pillar with Lion Capital, Emperor Ashoka's Brahmi Inscriptions, Nandangarh Brick Stupa Mound, Ancient Vedic Burial Mounds",
                "Located about 28 km north of Bettiah; hiring a private vehicle from Bettiah is recommended; carry drinking water and sun protection.", "06:00 AM - 06:00 PM daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("26.9983"), new BigDecimal("84.4039"), 20, 2024, 85000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Bihar", "Bihar State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lauria_Nandangarh_pillar_of_Ashoka_side_view.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        // --- 5. CHHATTISGARH ---
        State chhattisgarh = createState("Chhattisgarh", "chhattisgarh", "STATE", "Central", "Raipur", "Heartland of tribal art, dense forests, and magnificent waterfalls.",
                "Chhattisgarh is rich in eco-tourism, ancient temples, and waterfalls often called the 'Niagara of India'.",
                "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chitrakote_Falls.jpg", "October to March");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Chitrakote Waterfall", "chitrakote-waterfall", "Bastar",
                "Chitrakote Waterfall is the widest waterfall in India, earning it the popular title 'Niagara of India'.",
                "Formed by the Indravati River plunging nearly 30 meters over a massive horseshoe cliff, Chitrakote expands to a width of up to 300 meters during the monsoons. The misty spray, roaring torrents, and vivid rainbows make it the foremost natural spectacle of Bastar.",
                "Waterfall", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chitrakote_Falls.jpg",
                "July to February (monsoon for grandeur, winter for tranquil waters)", "2 to 4 hours", "Celebrated in ancient Bastar folklore and tribal epics; documented extensively in British colonial surveys of central India.", "Horseshoe waterfall viewpoints, Boat ride near spray mist, Evening floodlighting, Bastar tribal handicraft stalls",
                "Wear shoes with firm grip as cliff edges and stairs can be slippery. Visit at sunset for golden reflections across the spray.", "6:00 AM – 7:00 PM (illumination runs until 8:30 PM)", "Free entry; boating charges approx. ₹50–₹100 per person",
                new BigDecimal("19.2008"), new BigDecimal("81.7061"), 1, 2024, 650000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chitrakote_Falls.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Tirathgarh Waterfall", "tirathgarh-waterfall", "Bastar",
                "Tirathgarh Waterfall is a scenic, multi-tiered block waterfall nestled inside the Kanger Valley National Park.",
                "Fed by the Mugabahar River, Tirathgarh drops over 91 meters in stepped white cascades across ancient rock faces. The waterfall is surrounded by dense sal trees and features an ancient Shiva temple embedded directly on the craggy ledges.",
                "Waterfall", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tirathgarh_waterfall.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Houses an ancient rock-cut Shiva shrine revered by indigenous forest tribes and pilgrims for centuries.", "Multi-tiered cascading waterfall, Ancient Shiva temple on the rocky ledge, Kanger Valley forest nature trails",
                "Descend the stone staircases carefully. Swimming is strictly discouraged near steep cascade drop-offs.", "8:00 AM – 5:00 PM", "₹25 per person park entry; parking charges extra",
                new BigDecimal("18.9142"), new BigDecimal("81.8653"), 2, 2024, 480000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tirathgarh_waterfall.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Kanger Valley National Park", "kanger-valley-national-park", "Bastar",
                "Kanger Valley National Park is a prominent biodiversity hotspot celebrated for limestone caves, sal forests, and rare fauna.",
                "Declared a national park in 1982, the 200-square-kilometer reserve encompasses diverse ecosystems spanning deep gorges, subterranean caverns, and perennial streams. It is home to the state bird of Chhattisgarh—the Bastar hill myna—as well as leopards, barking deer, flying squirrels, and mugger crocodiles.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kanger_valley_National_park.jpg",
                "November to April", "4 to 6 hours", "One of India's earliest identified biosphere reserves, preserving primordial flora, fauna, and indigenous Bastar culture.", "Kotumsar and Kailash limestone caves, Tirathgarh Waterfall, Bhaisa Darha crocodile sanctuary, Gypsy safari",
                "Hire an official forest guide at the gate. The national park remains closed to visitors during the core monsoon season.", "8:00 AM – 4:00 PM (Closed July to October)", "₹50 per person; ₹150–₹250 per vehicle entry; guide charges extra",
                new BigDecimal("18.9056"), new BigDecimal("81.9961"), 3, 2024, 320000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kanger_valley_National_park.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Barnawapara Wildlife Sanctuary", "barnawapara-wildlife-sanctuary", "Baloda Bazar",
                "Barnawapara Wildlife Sanctuary is a pristine forest sanctuary known for frequent sightings of Indian gaur, leopards, and diverse birdlife.",
                "Encompassing roughly 245 square kilometers of tropical dry deciduous forests and teak woodlands, Barnawapara is drained by tributaries of the Mahanadi and Balamdehi rivers. Visitors frequently spot Indian bison (gaur), leopards, sambar, nilgai, wild boars, and more than 150 bird species.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Barnawapara_Wildlife_Sanctuary_Chhattisgarh_India.jpg",
                "November to June", "3 to 5 hours", "Named after the historic revenue forest settlements of Bar and Nawapara, preserving an ancient central Indian forest corridor.", "Jeep wildlife safari, Indian bison (gaur) herds, Pakshi Vihar watchtower, Pakdhi and Nawapara reservoirs",
                "Opt for dawn or late afternoon safari slots for higher chances of big cat and gaur sightings. Book forest rest houses in advance.", "6:30 AM – 10:30 AM and 2:30 PM – 5:30 PM (Closed July to October)", "₹50 entry per person; gypsy safari & guide ₹1,200–₹2,000",
                new BigDecimal("21.4011"), new BigDecimal("82.4278"), 4, 2024, 240000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barnawapara_Wildlife_Sanctuary_Chhattisgarh_India.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Sirpur Archaeological Site", "sirpur-archaeological-site", "Mahasamund",
                "Sirpur is an internationally acclaimed archaeological complex preserving 5th–8th century Hindu, Buddhist, and Jain monastic ruins.",
                "Historically known as Shripur ('City of Wealth'), the ancient capital of the Somavamshi kings along the Mahanadi River contains over 40 excavated monument complexes. Its crowning jewel is the 7th-century brick Laxman Temple, universally lauded for its intricate terracotta carvings and architectural ingenuity.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Laxman_Temple,_Sirpur,_Chhattisgarh.jpg",
                "October to March", "3 to 5 hours", "Described by Chinese traveler Xuanzang in 639 CE; represents an unparalleled center of syncretic harmony between Shaivism, Vaishnavism, and Mahayana Buddhism.", "Laxman Brick Temple, Teevardev Buddhist Monastery, Surang Tila tiered temple, Gandheshwar Mahadev Temple, Archaeological Museum",
                "Hire an ASI-certified guide to appreciate the detailed iconography. Carry drinking water and wear comfortable walking shoes.", "6:00 AM – 6:00 PM", "₹25 for Indians, ₹300 for foreigners (combined ASI monument ticket)",
                new BigDecimal("21.3444"), new BigDecimal("82.1794"), 5, 2024, 210000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laxman_Temple,_Sirpur,_Chhattisgarh.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Bhoramdeo Temple", "bhoramdeo-temple", "Kabirdham",
                "Bhoramdeo Temple is an intricately sculpted 11th-century Nagara-style stone temple known as the 'Khajuraho of Chhattisgarh'.",
                "Constructed by the Nagavanshi dynasty against the backdrop of the Maikal mountain range, Bhoramdeo overlooks a calm lake. The exterior and interior walls feature dense stone friezes depicting Hindu divinities, erotic motifs, musicians, courtly dancers, and mythological legends.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bhoramdeo_Temple.JPG",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "The religious nucleus of the Phaniganga Nagavanshi rulers, synthesizing aesthetic traditions of Khajuraho and Konark temples.", "Main Bhoramdeo Shiva Temple, Madwa Mahal, Chherki Mahal, Archaeological museum and lakefront gardens",
                "Combine with a trip up the scenic Maikal hills and Chilfi Ghati. Early morning photography reveals magnificent stone relief details.", "6:00 AM – 7:00 PM", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("22.1206"), new BigDecimal("81.1611"), 6, 2024, 380000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bhoramdeo_Temple.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Dongargarh", "dongargarh", "Rajnandgaon",
                "Dongargarh is a prominent hilltop pilgrimage center celebrated for the hilltop Maa Bamleshwari Devi Temple and ropeway.",
                "Crowning a 1,600-foot-high hill, the temple of Badi Bamleshwari draws millions of devotees each year. Visitors reach the summit via a cable-car ropeway or an ascent of over 1,000 steps, enjoying commanding panoramic vistas of the surrounding plains and serene water bodies below.",
                "Pilgrimage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dongargarh.jpg",
                "October to March (Navratri festivals are especially vibrant)", "2 to 4 hours", "Linked in local lore to 2nd-century BCE ruler Raja Veersen; one of Chhattisgarh's most venerated Shakta pilgrimage shrines.", "Maa Badi Bamleshwari hilltop temple, Maa Chhoti Bamleshwari lower shrine, Aerial passenger ropeway, Pragya Giri Buddha hill",
                "The ropeway is ideal for seniors and families, though queues can be lengthy during Navratri. Dress respectfully.", "4:00 AM – 1:00 PM and 2:00 PM – 10:00 PM (open 24 hours during Navratri)", "Free temple entry; ropeway round trip approx. ₹60–₹100",
                new BigDecimal("21.1906"), new BigDecimal("80.76"), 7, 2024, 1800000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dongargarh.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Danteshwari Temple", "danteshwari-temple", "Dantewada",
                "Danteshwari Temple in Dantewada is one of the 52 sacred Shakti Peethas and the spiritual heart of the Bastar tribal culture.",
                "Situated at the sacred confluence of the Shankhani and Dankhani rivers, this 14th-century temple enshrines Goddess Danteshwari, the patron deity of the Bastar Kakatiya kings and regional tribes. The shrine displays classical Nagara architecture, multi-pillared mandapas, and historic brass idols.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Danteswari_Temple_0034.jpg",
                "October to March (and during Bastar Dussehra)", "1 to 2 hours", "Recognized as a Shakti Peetha where Sati's tooth (daant) is believed to have fallen; forms the religious core of the legendary 75-day Bastar Dussehra.", "Sacred Shakti Peetha sanctum, Shankhani-Dankhani river confluence ghat, Carved wooden ceiling panels, Garuda sthambha",
                "Traditional attire is required (dhoti/veshti for men entering the sanctum). Photography inside the inner sanctuary is prohibited.", "6:00 AM – 8:00 PM", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("18.895"), new BigDecimal("81.3508"), 8, 2024, 1200000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Danteswari_Temple_0034.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Bastar Palace", "bastar-palace", "Bastar",
                "Bastar Palace in Jagdalpur is the historical royal residence of the Kakatiya rulers who governed the Bastar kingdom.",
                "Built after the royal capital was transferred from Barsur to Jagdalpur, the palace features an eclectic mix of indigenous central Indian and colonial architecture. The sprawling grounds house historic royal relics, a Danteshwari temple, and the ceremonial courtyard where Bastar Dussehra festivities begin.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jagdalpur_Palace.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 hours", "Sovereign seat of the Kakatiya dynasty tracing lineage from King Prataparudra of Warangal, ruling Bastar until democratic integration.", "Royal palace façade and ornate wooden gateways, Palace Danteshwari shrine, Bastar Dussehra rath construction ground",
                "Part of the palace remains a private royal residence; respect restricted zones. Plan your visit during Bastar Dussehra to see the giant wooden chariots.", "9:00 AM – 6:00 PM", "Free entry (accessible sections)",
                new BigDecimal("19.0744"), new BigDecimal("82.0306"), 9, 2024, 160000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jagdalpur_Palace.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Kutumsar Caves", "kutumsar-caves", "Bastar",
                "Kutumsar Caves is an underground limestone karst cave system known for dramatic stalactites, stalagmites, and blind cavefish.",
                "Descended via a narrow vertical shaft into total subterranean darkness, Kutumsar extends roughly 330 meters through chambers adorned with calcite dripstones. The cave is renowned among speleologists for its subterranean fauna, including the endemic blind cavefish (Nemacheilus ssp.).",
                "Caves", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kutumsar_Caves.jpg",
                "November to May (inaccessible during monsoons due to standing water)", "1 to 2 hours", "Scientifically discovered and mapped in the 1950s by Indian geographer Prof. Shankar Tiwari, making it one of India's earliest documented karst cave systems.", "Calcite stalactites and stalagmites, Naturally formed stalagmite Shiva lingam, Endemic blind cavefish habitat",
                "Always explore with a registered forest guide carrying high-beam lamps. Not recommended for visitors with severe asthma or claustrophobia.", "9:00 AM – 4:00 PM (Closed June 15 to October 31)", "₹25–₹50 per person (plus national park gate pass); guide fees ₹100–₹200",
                new BigDecimal("18.8822"), new BigDecimal("81.9367"), 10, 2024, 290000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kutumsar_Caves.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Kailash Caves", "kailash-caves", "Bastar",
                "Kailash Caves is a deep underground limestone cave system in Kanger Valley featuring massive acoustic stalactite pillars.",
                "Discovered in 1993 on a hillside within Kanger Valley National Park, Kailash Caves extends over 250 meters into the earth. The cavern chambers house towering stalagmite formations resembling Shiva lingams and acoustic limestone pillars that produce hollow musical resonances when tapped.",
                "Caves", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kutumsar_Caves.jpg",
                "November to April", "1.5 to 2 hours", "A modern speleological discovery regarded with deep religious reverence by regional tribes as an underground temple of Lord Shiva.", "Acoustic resonant limestone columns, Stalagmite Shiva lingam formations, Spacious subterranean cavern halls",
                "Carry torches and hire an authorized guide. The cave is poorly lit and requires stepping over uneven natural stone floors.", "9:00 AM – 4:00 PM (Closed during monsoon season)", "₹25–₹50 per person (plus national park vehicle entry)",
                new BigDecimal("18.8683"), new BigDecimal("81.9481"), 11, 2024, 110000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://tourism.gov.in", "UNRESOLVED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Mainpat", "mainpat", "Surguja",
                "Mainpat is a tranquil hill station and plateau often referred to as the 'Shimla of Chhattisgarh' and 'Mini Tibet'.",
                "Situated at an altitude exceeding 1,000 meters in northern Chhattisgarh, Mainpat boasts lush rolling meadows, pine woods, waterfalls, and a vibrant Tibetan refugee community. Attractions range from serene Tibetan Buddhist monasteries to geological wonders like the trembling, bouncing earth at Jaljali.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/MAINPAT_LANDSCAPE.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 days", "Home to a Tibetan settlement established in 1962 following the Dalai Lama's arrival in India, preserving authentic Tibetan carpet weaving and spiritual traditions.", "Dhakpo Shedrupling Tibetan Buddhist Monastery, Tiger Point and Fish Point waterfalls, Jaljali (bouncing earth), Mehta Point",
                "Try authentic steamed momos and thukpa in the Tibetan settlements. Experience the unique sensation of jumping on the elastic bog ground at Jaljali.", "Open 24 hours (monasteries and viewpoints generally 6:00 AM – 6:00 PM)", "Free entry; nominal ₹10–₹20 parking fees at select viewpoints",
                new BigDecimal("22.8167"), new BigDecimal("83.2833"), 12, 2024, 350000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MAINPAT_LANDSCAPE.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Malhar Archaeological Site", "malhar-archaeological-site", "Bilaspur",
                "Malhar is an ancient archaeological treasure trove containing structural remains of Hindu, Buddhist, and Jain civilizations dating from 1000 BCE.",
                "Positioned strategically along historical trade routes connecting northern India and Kalinga, Malhar flourished under the Mauryas, Satavahanas, Somavamshis, and Kalachuris. The complex houses the partially subterranean 12th-century Pataleshwar Mahadev Temple, the Didneshwari shrine, and an ASI museum of excavated sculptures.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Temple_Carvings_in_Malhar_Bilaspur_Chhattisgarh_2009.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Mentioned as Sarabhapura and Mallari in copper-plate inscriptions; exhibits continuous archaeological layers spanning over two millennia.", "Pataleshwar Mahadev Temple, Didneshwari Devi Temple, Bhima Kichak Temple ruins, ASI Sculpture Shed & Museum",
                "Take time to explore the ASI museum, which houses one of the earliest four-armed stone Vishnu statues ever unearthed in India.", "9:00 AM – 5:00 PM", "Free temple access; ₹5–₹10 for the local museum",
                new BigDecimal("21.8961"), new BigDecimal("82.2803"), 13, 2024, 140000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_Carvings_in_Malhar_Bilaspur_Chhattisgarh_2009.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Madku Dweep", "madku-dweep", "Bilaspur",
                "Madku Dweep is a tranquil river island in the Shivnath River famous for excavated Kalachuri-era temple complexes and Vedic sage lore.",
                "Spanning around 35 hectares where the Shivnath River bifurcates, Madku Dweep houses 19 excavated stone temples dating back to the 10th and 11th centuries. According to regional tradition, Sage Mandukya composed the Mandukya Upanishad on this secluded river hermitage.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Madku_island_Hindu_temples_and_Mandukya_rishi_memorial,_Chhattisgarh_-_7.jpg",
                "November to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Archaeological excavations confirmed an extensive Kalachuri temple cluster, ancient Shaivite inscriptions, and sacred sacrificial altars.", "Excavated Kalachuri stone temple ruins, Mandukya Rishi memorial ashram, Panoramic Shivnath river sandbars",
                "Access via the causeway can be submerged during peak monsoon season; visit in the dry winter months for easy crossing.", "6:00 AM – 6:00 PM", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("21.6575"), new BigDecimal("81.7611"), 14, 2024, 190000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madku_island_Hindu_temples_and_Mandukya_rishi_memorial,_Chhattisgarh_-_7.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Rajim", "rajim", "Gariaband",
                "Rajim is the sacred 'Prayag of Chhattisgarh', situated at the holy confluence of the Mahanadi, Pairi, and Sondhur rivers.",
                "Famed for the 7th-century Rajiv Lochan Temple dedicated to Lord Vishnu, Rajim is the state's foremost spiritual center. The riverbanks host historic ghats, island temples like Kuleshwar Mahadev, and the annual fortnight-long Rajim Kumbh Mela that draws thousands of saints and pilgrims.",
                "Pilgrimage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Rajiv_Lochan_Temple.JPG",
                "October to March (especially during Magh Purnima for the Kumbh Mela)", "2 to 4 hours", "A pivotal religious capital under the Nala and Panduvamshi dynasties; contains masterworks of early post-Gupta temple architecture.", "Rajiv Lochan Temple complex, Kuleshwar Mahadev Temple on river island, Triveni Sangam confluence ghats",
                "Footwear must be deposited before entering the temple precincts. Cross the pedestrian causeway to reach Kuleshwar Mahadev in the middle of the river.", "6:00 AM – 8:30 PM", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("20.9631"), new BigDecimal("81.8797"), 15, 2024, 850000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rajiv_Lochan_Temple.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Achanakmar Tiger Reserve", "achanakmar-tiger-reserve", "Mungeli",
                "Achanakmar Tiger Reserve is a dense tropical sal and bamboo reserve forming the ecological core of the Achanakmar-Amarkantak Biosphere.",
                "Covering more than 550 square kilometers, Achanakmar forms a crucial wildlife corridor connecting Kanha and Bandhavgarh national parks. The reserve supports healthy populations of Royal Bengal tigers, leopards, Indian gaur, sloth bears, chital, and four-horned antelopes along the Maniyari River.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Achanakmar_Tiger_Reserve.jpg",
                "November to May", "4 to 6 hours", "Established as a wildlife sanctuary in 1975 and declared a Project Tiger reserve in 2009; designated a UNESCO Biosphere Reserve.", "Guided open gypsy wildlife safari, Sihawal Sagar watchtower, Maniyari River forest valleys",
                "Pre-book safari slots via the Chhattisgarh Forest Department portal. Carry warm layers for brisk morning drives.", "6:00 AM – 10:00 AM and 3:00 PM – 6:00 PM (Closed July to October)", "₹50 per person; safari vehicle & guide charges ₹1,500–₹2,500",
                new BigDecimal("22.5028"), new BigDecimal("81.7628"), 16, 2024, 130000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Achanakmar_Tiger_Reserve.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Gangrel Dam", "gangrel-dam", "Dhamtari",
                "Gangrel Dam (Ravishankar Sagar) is the longest dam in Chhattisgarh, impounding the Mahanadi River and serving as a prime water sports hub.",
                "Built near Dhamtari across the Mahanadi, this massive reservoir provides essential irrigation, industrial water, and hydroelectric power to central Chhattisgarh. The reservoir shores feature landscaped gardens, an island resort, and water sports including speedboating, jet skiing, and water surfing.",
                "Dam", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/RAVISHANKAR_SAGAR_PROJECT_GANGREL.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 4 hours", "Commissioned in 1978 as the Pandit Ravishankar Sagar Project, driving agricultural prosperity across the Mahanadi delta basin.", "Angaarmoti Temple on reservoir bank, Water sports complex, Gangrel Dam embankment promenade, Bardia island eco-resort",
                "Sunset over the expansive waters of the reservoir is exceptionally picturesque. Can get crowded on weekends with day-trippers from Raipur.", "6:00 AM – 7:00 PM", "Free general entry; water sports charged individually (₹150–₹500)",
                new BigDecimal("20.6133"), new BigDecimal("81.5647"), 17, 2024, 550000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RAVISHANKAR_SAGAR_PROJECT_GANGREL.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Hasdeo Bango Dam", "hasdeo-bango-dam", "Korba",
                "Hasdeo Bango Dam is the tallest dam in Chhattisgarh, creating a massive, picturesque reservoir in the forested hills of Korba.",
                "Standing 87 meters high across the Hasdeo River near Machadoli, this multipurpose dam supplies cooling water to major power complexes as well as extensive canal irrigation. The vast catchment forms island-studded backwaters, notably the scenic Buka eco-tourism zone known for boating and camping.",
                "Dam", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Hasdeo_Bango_reservoir,_Korba_district,_Chhattisgarh,_India.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Dedicated in 1990 as the Minimata Bango Project in tribute to Minimata, Chhattisgarh's first female Member of Parliament.", "Dam crest and spillway viewpoint, Buka eco-tourism hub and backwater boating, Hydroelectric power complex",
                "Prior permission from local irrigation authorities may be required to walk onto the dam crest. Visit the Buka zone for water activities.", "8:00 AM – 6:00 PM", "Free entry; boating at Buka costs approx. ₹100–₹300",
                new BigDecimal("22.6106"), new BigDecimal("82.5978"), 18, 2024, 280000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hasdeo_Bango_reservoir,_Korba_district,_Chhattisgarh,_India.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Chaiturgarh Fort", "chaiturgarh-fort", "Korba",
                "Chaiturgarh (Lafagarh) is an ancient mountaintop fortress on a 3,060-foot plateau, often called the 'Shivaji Fort of Chhattisgarh'.",
                "Built by the Kalachuri king Raja Prithvideva I in the 10th–11th century, Chaiturgarh is considered one of India's strongest natural fortresses. The flat plateau is protected by sheer cliffs and monumental stone gates, housing the sacred Mahishasurmardini Durga Temple and historic mountain ponds.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chaiturgarh_temple_Korba,_Chhattisgarh.jpg",
                "October to March", "3 to 5 hours", "Prime stronghold of the Ratanpur Kalachuris with three massive fortified gates (Menaka, Humkara, Simhadwar); praised by British chroniclers for near-invincibility.", "Mahishasurmardini Temple, Ancient fortified stone gates, Panoramic plateau cliff vistas, Shankar Talab spring reservoir",
                "Wear sturdy trekking shoes to explore the ramparts. The ghat road up the mountain is narrow with sharp hairpins; drive carefully.", "6:00 AM – 6:00 PM", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("22.45"), new BigDecimal("82.35"), 19, 2024, 175000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chaiturgarh_temple_Korba,_Chhattisgarh.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(chhattisgarh, "Amrit Dhara Waterfall", "amrit-dhara-waterfall", "MCB District",
                "Amrit Dhara Waterfall is a majestic natural cascade on the Hasdeo River plunging over steep rocky ledges in dense forest.",
                "Dropping roughly 27 meters (90 feet) with a breadth of around 15 feet, Amrit Dhara forms a roaring curtain of white water and perpetual mist. The site is also an ancient religious destination featuring a historic Shiva temple founded by the Maharaja of Koriya, drawing thousands during Shivratri.",
                "Waterfall", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Amritdhara1.jpg",
                "July to February (peak monsoon to winter)", "2 to 3 hours", "Site of the annual Amritdhara Mahotsav, an enduring cultural fair celebrated since 1936 under royal patronage.", "Waterfall viewpoint galleries, Ancient Shiva Temple, Amritdhara mela grounds, Hasdeo rocky river gorge",
                "The concrete steps leading down to the base can become slippery from spray. Avoid entering deep plunge pools.", "6:00 AM – 6:00 PM", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("23.3325"), new BigDecimal("82.3275"), 20, 2024, 220000L, "DOMESTIC",
                "Chhattisgarh Tourism Board (CTB)", "Chhattisgarh State Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amritdhara1.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        // --- 6. GOA ---
        State goa = createState("Goa", "goa", "STATE", "Western", "Panaji", "India's beach capital, famous for sun, sand, Portuguese heritage, and vibrant nightlife.",
                "Goa is India's premier coastal vacation state, celebrated for its golden palm-fringed beaches, UNESCO-listed churches, spice plantations, and seafood.",
                "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Basilica_of_Bom_Jesus,_old_goa_(cropped).JPG", "November to February");

        createDestination(goa, "Basilica of Bom Jesus", "basilica-of-bom-jesus", "Old Goa, North Goa",
                "A UNESCO World Heritage Site in Old Goa, the Basilica of Bom Jesus is one of India's most venerated Catholic pilgrimage centers.",
                "Constructed between 1594 and 1605, this historic landmark is world-renowned for housing the mortal remains of St. Francis Xavier in an ornate silver casket. Its unplastered Baroque facade and exquisite gilded altars represent the pinnacle of Portuguese colonial architecture in India.",
                "Church", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Basilica_of_Bom_Jesus,_old_goa_(cropped).JPG",
                "November to February", "1 - 1.5 hours", "Consecrated in 1605, it is the first minor basilica in India and holds profound significance for global Catholicism as the resting place of patron saint St. Francis Xavier.", "Mortal remains of St. Francis Xavier, Gilded Baroque high altar, Carved wooden pulpit, Modern art gallery, Unplastered laterite facade",
                "Dress modestly covering shoulders and knees as it is an active sacred shrine. Photography is restricted inside the relic chapel.", "Monday to Saturday: 9:00 AM – 6:30 PM; Sunday: 10:30 AM – 6:30 PM", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("15.5009"), new BigDecimal("73.9116"), 1, 2024, 3400000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilica_of_Bom_Jesus,_old_goa_(cropped).JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Se Cathedral", "se-cathedral", "Old Goa, North Goa",
                "One of the largest churches in Asia, Se Cathedral stands as an imposing monument to the Portuguese victory over an invading Muslim army in 1510.",
                "Dedicated to St. Catherine of Alexandria, this grand cathedral exemplifies Portuguese-Manueline architecture with a majestic Tuscan exterior and classical Corinthian interior. It famously houses the legendary 'Golden Bell' (Sino de Ouro), noted for its rich and melodic chime heard for miles.",
                "Church", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Old_Goa,_Se_Cathedral,_Panoramic_view.jpg",
                "November to March", "45 mins - 1 hour", "Commissioned under Governor George Cabral in 1562 and consecrated in 1640, it served as the ecclesiastical seat of the Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Goa and Daman.", "The Golden Bell, Main altar dedicated to St. Catherine, Chapel of the Miraculous Cross, Manueline entrance",
                "Maintain silence inside the cathedral nave. Conservative attire required.", "7:30 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("15.5036"), new BigDecimal("73.9122"), 2, 2024, 2800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Goa,_Se_Cathedral,_Panoramic_view.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Fort Aguada", "fort-aguada", "Sinquerim, Candolim, North Goa",
                "Perched atop a headland at the mouth of the Mandovi River, Fort Aguada is a formidable 17th-century Portuguese fortress overlooking the Arabian Sea.",
                "Built between 1609 and 1612 to protect Goa from Maratha forces and Dutch armadas, the fort derives its name from its freshwater spring that watered visiting ships. The fort complex features solid laterite ramparts and a four-tiered historic lighthouse, one of the earliest constructed in Asia.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Aguada_Fort,_India_Goa.jpg",
                "October to March (late afternoons for sunset)", "1.5 - 2 hours", "Served as the premier maritime defense sentinel of Portuguese Goa, featuring an immense subterranean freshwater cistern holding 2.37 million gallons.", "Four-tiered 19th-century lighthouse, Subterranean freshwater reservoir, Panoramic views of Sinquerim Beach, Historic gun embrasures",
                "Wear comfortable walking shoes for cobblestones. Sunset hours offer the most picturesque lighting.", "9:30 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "₹25 for Indians, ₹300 for foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("15.492"), new BigDecimal("73.7737"), 3, 2024, 3100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aguada_Fort,_India_Goa.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Chapora Fort", "chapora-fort", "Chapora, Bardez, North Goa",
                "Elevated high above the Chapora River estuary, Chapora Fort is an iconic red laterite ruin celebrated for its sweeping coastline panoramas.",
                "Originally built by the Adil Shahi rulers of Bijapur and extensively reconstructed by the Portuguese in 1717, the fort witnessed fierce battles between Portuguese and Maratha forces. In contemporary culture, it gained legendary fame across India as the 'Dil Chahta Hai' fort, drawing travelers for spectacular sunset views over Vagator Beach.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chapora_fort,_Goa,India.JPG",
                "November to March (especially at golden hour/sunset)", "1 - 1.5 hours", "Strategic military outpost that changed hands between the Adil Shahi Sultanate, the Marathas under Sambhaji, and the Portuguese Crown over two centuries.", "Panoramic views of Vagator Beach and Chapora River mouth, Rampart stone walls and bastions, Iconic cinematic vantage points",
                "Requires a moderate 10-15 minute uphill trek from the parking base. Wear shoes with good traction as loose laterite gravel can be slippery.", "Open 24 hours (Best visited 6:00 AM – 6:30 PM)", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("15.6058"), new BigDecimal("73.7366"), 4, 2024, 2600000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chapora_fort,_Goa,India.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Dudhsagar Waterfalls", "dudhsagar-waterfalls", "Sonaulim, South Goa",
                "Cascading down four tiers from a height of 310 meters, Dudhsagar is one of India's tallest and most spectacular waterfalls.",
                "Nestled deep in the Western Ghats along the Mandovi River inside Bhagwan Mahavir Wildlife Sanctuary, its name translates to 'Sea of Milk' due to its frothy white plunge. The dramatic railway viaduct traversing directly across the midpoint of the roaring falls forms one of the country's most photographed spectacles.",
                "Waterfall", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dudhsagar_Falls,_Goa.jpg",
                "October to February (post-monsoon for jeep safaris; monsoon for viewing from trains)", "4 - 5 hours", "Ancient mountain pass connecting coastal Goa with the Deccan plateau, woven with local folklore of a bathing princess whose sweetened milk turned the cascade white.", "Four-tiered 310-meter cascade, Iconic railway bridge arch, Natural plunge pool, Jungle jeep safari through Mollem streams",
                "Book official Goa Forest Department jeep safari vouchers early morning at Kulem. Life jackets are mandatory for entering the water.", "6:00 AM – 5:00 PM (Jeep safaris: 8:30 AM – 3:30 PM)", "Sanctuary entry ₹100 per person, Jeep safari approx. ₹500–₹600 per passenger",
                new BigDecimal("15.3144"), new BigDecimal("74.3143"), 5, 2024, 950000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dudhsagar_Falls,_Goa.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Palolem Beach", "palolem-beach", "Canacona, South Goa",
                "A picturesque crescent-shaped bay fringed by coconut palms and gentle waters in southernmost Goa.",
                "Flanked by rocky headlands that buffer ocean currents, Palolem offers safe, calm swimming conditions and an easygoing bohemian vibe. The beach is famous for colorful wooden beach huts, sunset boat excursions to spot playful dolphins, and unique open-air silent noise parties.",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Palolem_beach.jpg",
                "November to March", "Half day to full day", "A traditional Konkani fishing haven transformed into a favored sanctuary for backpackers seeking pristine nature away from crowded northern shores.", "Crescent white sand bay, Monkey Island at northern headland, Dolphin spotting boat tours, Silent disco events",
                "Rent a kayak during low tide to explore the calm northern lagoon. Swim inside demarcated lifeguard zones.", "Open 24 hours", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("15.01"), new BigDecimal("74.0232"), 6, 2024, 2200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palolem_beach.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Baga Beach", "baga-beach", "Baga, Bardez, North Goa",
                "Goa's most vibrant and bustling coastal strip, famous for pulsating nightlife, thrilling watersports, and lively beach shacks.",
                "Forming a continuous coastline with Calangute, Baga is the epicenter of Goa's commercial tourist activity. From jet skiing and parasailing by day to the throbbing music of Tito's Lane and seaside candlelit dining at night, it offers non-stop entertainment for energetic holidaymakers.",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/India_-_Goa_-_010_-_Touristy_Baga_Beach.jpg",
                "October to April", "3 - 5 hours", "Became the heart of the 1960s and 70s international hippie trail, subsequently evolving into Goa's premier commercial tourism and hospitality hub.", "Tito's Lane clubs, Water sports (parasailing, jet ski, banana boat), Baga Creek estuary, Lively beach shacks",
                "Traffic gets congested on Tito's Lane in late evenings. Always negotiate water sport rates with registered operators.", "Open 24 hours", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("15.5553"), new BigDecimal("73.7517"), 7, 2024, 6500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:India_-_Goa_-_010_-_Touristy_Baga_Beach.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Calangute Beach", "calangute-beach", "Calangute, Bardez, North Goa",
                "Universally known as the 'Queen of Beaches', Calangute is the largest and most frequented coastal destination in North Goa.",
                "Spanning several kilometers of golden sands, Calangute attracts families and tourists from across the world with its boundless rows of sun loungers, handicraft stalls, and multi-cuisine restaurants. It serves as the primary gateway for beachside watersports, souvenir shopping, and coastal excursions.",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Calangute_beach_Goa.jpg",
                "November to February", "2 - 4 hours", "The original epicenter of tourist discovery in Goa during the mid-20th century, growing from a sleepy coastal village to a world-renowned vacation hotspot.", "Vast golden sand beachfront, Calangute Market Square and Tibetan Market, Beachfront dining, Parasailing and windsurfing",
                "Swimming should strictly follow lifeguard flags due to unpredictable undercurrents. Visit early morning for peaceful walks.", "Open 24 hours", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("15.5442"), new BigDecimal("73.7553"), 8, 2024, 7800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calangute_beach_Goa.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Anjuna Beach", "anjuna-beach", "Anjuna, Bardez, North Goa",
                "Celebrated for its rugged laterite rock formations, legendary weekly flea market, and bohemian psychedelic trance heritage.",
                "Anjuna presents a striking visual contrast where volcanic-like black laterite rocks meet the rolling turquoise Arabian Sea. Home to legendary beachside institutions like Curlies and Shiva Valley, it retains an alternative, artistic ambiance complemented by world-famous Wednesday flea markets and sunset gatherings.",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Anjuna_beach_Goa.jpg",
                "November to March (especially Wednesdays for flea market)", "3 - 4 hours", "The iconic cradle of Goa's counter-culture movement in the 1960s, giving birth to Goa Trance music and global bohemian traveler communities.", "Anjuna Wednesday Flea Market, Volcanic laterite rock promontories, Iconic shacks (Curlies, Café Lilliput), Sunset viewpoints",
                "Do not miss the Wednesday flea market for spices, handicrafts, and apparel. Be cautious walking on wet laterite rocks.", "Open 24 hours", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("15.5833"), new BigDecimal("73.7428"), 9, 2024, 3200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anjuna_beach_Goa.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Colva Beach", "colva-beach", "Colva, Salcete, South Goa",
                "The crown jewel of South Goa's coastline, featuring a sprawling 2.4-kilometer stretch of powdery white sand lined with swaying palms.",
                "Colva is one of the oldest and most developed beach resort towns in South Goa, favored by families and domestic travelers for its wide expanse and peaceful ambiance compared to the North. The nearby historic Our Lady of Mercy Church adds cultural depth to this idyllic seaside sanctuary.",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Activities_at_Colva_Beach.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 - 3 hours", "Long favored by Portuguese high society and colonial nobility who built aristocratic seaside mansions (quintas) along its shores.", "Powdery white sand shoreline, Our Lady of Mercy Church, Local seafood shacks, Sunset strolls and jet skiing",
                "Walk south along the shore for quieter, secluded sections away from the main entrance. Try fresh Goan fish curry at local shacks.", "Open 24 hours", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("15.2792"), new BigDecimal("73.9103"), 10, 2024, 2400000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Activities_at_Colva_Beach.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Cabo de Rama Fort", "cabo-de-rama-fort", "Canacona, South Goa",
                "An ancient clifftop fortress steeped in mythological lore, commanding dramatic views over the deep blue waters of Cabo de Rama Beach.",
                "According to Hindu legend, Lord Rama, Sita, and Lakshmana took shelter here during their 14-year exile. Later occupied by Hindu rulers, the Soonda rajas, and captured by the Portuguese in 1763, the fort features ancient cannon ramparts and the charming whitewashed Church of St. Anthony, which remains operational to this day.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Cabo_de_Rama_Entrance.jpg",
                "October to March (late afternoon)", "1.5 - 2 hours", "Predates European arrival by centuries, served as a military fortress for the Soonda kings and Portuguese rulers, and was later converted into a government prison until 1955.", "Historic arched entrance bridge and moat, Active Church of St. Anthony, Ancient iron cannons, Dramatic sheer cliff views",
                "Bring water and sturdy footwear; the fort grounds are vast and rugged. There are no safety railings along the high cliffs.", "9:00 AM – 5:30 PM daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("15.0898"), new BigDecimal("73.9213"), 11, 2024, 580000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cabo_de_Rama_Entrance.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Fontainhas", "fontainhas", "Panaji, North Goa",
                "The picturesque Latin Quarter of Panaji, renowned for narrow winding lanes lined with brightly colored Portuguese colonial villas.",
                "Established in the late 18th century at the foot of Altinho hill, Fontainhas has successfully preserved its authentic Mediterranean architectural character with vivid ochre, pastel green, and indigo facades. Walking along its cobblestone pathways reveals traditional wooden balconies, wrought-iron railings, art galleries, and historic bakeries.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Streets_of_Fontainhas,_Panaji_(07).jpg",
                "November to March (early morning or late afternoon for photography)", "2 - 3 hours", "Declared a UNESCO recognized heritage zone, it was developed by Goan expatriate Antonio Joao de Sequeira and became the administrative hub of Portuguese Goa.", "St. Sebastian Chapel, Vibrant Portuguese-style heritage homes and azulejos, Historic bakeries (Confeitaria 31 de Janeiro), Art galleries",
                "Explore on foot as lanes are too narrow for vehicles. Respect local residents' privacy and avoid posing on private doorsteps.", "Open 24 hours", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("15.4989"), new BigDecimal("73.8315"), 12, 2024, 1100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Streets_of_Fontainhas,_Panaji_(07).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Reis Magos Fort", "reis-magos-fort", "Reis Magos, Bardez, North Goa",
                "A beautifully restored 16th-century fortress situated on the northern bank of the Mandovi River facing Panaji.",
                "Built by the Portuguese in 1551 over an older outpost of the Adil Shahi Sultanate, the fort defended the narrow estuary of the Mandovi River. Extensively restored by INTACH and the Goa Government, it now serves as a vibrant cultural center and museum displaying historical armaments and works by renowned cartoonist Mario Miranda.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Reis_Magos_Fort_in_Goa.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 - 2 hours", "One of the earliest Portuguese defensive structures in Goa, later used as a prison and military hospital before its meticulous restoration as a heritage museum.", "Mario Miranda permanent cartoon gallery, Restored bastions and vintage cannons, Sweeping views across Mandovi River, Military artifacts exhibition",
                "Closed on Mondays. Carry a camera for scenic views of the river and capital city skyline.", "9:30 AM – 5:00 PM (Closed on Mondays)", "₹50 for adults, ₹25 for students, ₹10 for children",
                new BigDecimal("15.4975"), new BigDecimal("73.8087"), 13, 2024, 4200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reis_Magos_Fort_in_Goa.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Tambdi Surla Temple", "tambdi-surla-temple", "Dharbandora, South Goa",
                "A 12th-century Shaivite temple crafted from grey-black basalt, standing as the oldest surviving temple in Goa.",
                "Dedicated to Lord Shiva (Mahadev), this architectural marvel was built during the Kadamba dynasty in Kadamba-Yadava style. Hidden deep within the lush jungle of the Western Ghats, its remote jungle location spared it from destruction during both the Muslim invasions and the Portuguese Inquisition.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tambdi_Surla_Mahadev_temple.JPG",
                "October to March", "1 - 1.5 hours", "The sole intact structural specimen of Kadamba temple architecture surviving in Goa, protected as a National Monument by the ASI.", "Intricately carved basalt pillars and ceiling rosettes, Shiva Linga sanctuary, Headless Nandi statue, River Surla flowing beside complex",
                "Located in a secluded forest area; private transport is essential. Remove shoes before entering temple sanctum.", "8:30 AM – 5:30 PM daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("15.4384"), new BigDecimal("74.2547"), 14, 2024, 260000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tambdi_Surla_Mahadev_temple.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Divar Island", "divar-island", "Tiswadi, North Goa",
                "A serene, unspoiled riverine island in the Mandovi River offering a nostalgic glimpse into traditional Goan village life.",
                "Accessible only by government ferry boats, Divar Island is a tranquil sanctuary untouched by commercialization, characterized by emerald paddy fields, ancient church spires, and stately Indo-Portuguese mansions. It is famous for hosting the colorful annual Bonderam flag festival in August and the historic hilltop Church of Our Lady of Compassion.",
                "Island", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The_Church_of_Our_Lady_of_Compassion_is_located_in_the_village_of_Piedade;_in_Divar_island.jpg",
                "November to February", "2 - 3 hours", "Historically one of the most sacred Hindu pilgrimage centers in Goa housing ancient Saptakoteshwar temple before residents migrated in the 16th century.", "Church of Our Lady of Compassion on hilltop, Ancient Saptakoteshwar Temple tank ruins, Picturesque village lanes, Bonderam Festival",
                "Take the free vehicle and passenger ferry from Old Goa, Ribandar, or Narva. Renting a bicycle or scooter is ideal for exploring.", "Open 24 hours (Ferry runs 6:00 AM to midnight)", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("15.5167"), new BigDecimal("73.8967"), 15, 2024, 310000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Church_of_Our_Lady_of_Compassion_is_located_in_the_village_of_Piedade;_in_Divar_island.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Salim Ali Bird Sanctuary", "salim-ali-bird-sanctuary", "Chorão Island, North Goa",
                "A protected estuarine mangrove habitat on Chorão Island along the Mandovi River, named after India's legendary ornithologist.",
                "Covering 178 hectares of dense mangrove swamp, this ecological reserve is a haven for coastal and migratory birds including kingfishers, herons, pintails, and curlews. Visitors can navigate the network of tidal channels via a paved mangrove boardwalk or guided boat tours through the tranquil waterways.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Salim_ali_sanctuary_walkway.jpg",
                "November to February", "2 - 3 hours", "Established in 1988 to safeguard the delicate estuarine mangrove ecosystem and named in honor of the revered 'Birdman of India', Dr. Sálim Ali.", "Elevated paved walkway through dense mangrove forest, Three-story bird observation watchtower, Boat safari along tidal waterways",
                "Visit at low tide early in the morning when birds congregate on mudflats. Take the regular ferry from Ribandar jetty to Chorão Island.", "6:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "₹20 for Indians, ₹100 for foreign visitors; boat rides ₹75–₹150",
                new BigDecimal("15.5133"), new BigDecimal("73.8647"), 16, 2024, 140000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salim_ali_sanctuary_walkway.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Grande Island", "grande-island", "Mormugao, South Goa",
                "Goa's premier offshore diving and snorkeling destination, located in the Arabian Sea off the coast of Vasco da Gama.",
                "Also known as Ilha Grande, this rocky offshore island is renowned for its vibrant underwater biodiversity, coral gardens, and historic marine shipwrecks. Organized boat trips departing from Sinquerim, Dona Paula, and Baina offer dolphin sighting safaris, snorkeling in sheltered bays, and scuba diving at famous sites like Suzy's Wreck.",
                "Island", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Grande_Island,_Vasco-da-Gama.jpg",
                "October to May", "5 - 7 hours", "A crucial maritime landmark for centuries, its surrounding submerged reefs hold sunken cargo ships from World War II and the colonial era.", "Scuba diving and snorkeling at Suzy's Wreck, Dolphin spotting on boat cruise, Monkey Beach barbecue, Rich coral reefs",
                "Book boat packages in advance through certified PADI diving operators. Overnight stays on the island are prohibited.", "Tours operate typically 7:00 AM – 4:00 PM daily", "Boat tour with snorkeling ₹1,200–₹1,800; Scuba diving packages ₹2,500–₹4,500",
                new BigDecimal("15.3528"), new BigDecimal("73.7667"), 17, 2024, 380000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grande_Island,_Vasco-da-Gama.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Miramar Beach", "miramar-beach", "Panaji, North Goa",
                "Panaji's city beach, situated where the Mandovi River meets the Arabian Sea, famous for evening strolls and sunset views.",
                "Originally named 'Porta de Gaspar Dias' during the Portuguese era, Miramar is a broad, palm-lined urban beach known for its soft silvery sands and views across the river mouth toward Fort Aguada. It serves as a favored evening promenade for locals and travelers alike, dotted with street food vendors serving Goan snacks.",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Miramar_beach_goa.JPG",
                "November to March", "1 - 2 hours", "Site of the historic 16th-century Gaspar Dias Fort that once guarded the entrance to the Mandovi River opposite Fort Aguada.", "Sunset views across Mandovi estuary and Aguada lighthouse, Miramar promenade and gardens, Dayanand Bandodkar memorial",
                "Swimming is not recommended here due to deceptive river currents. Ideal for relaxed evening walking and photography.", "Open 24 hours", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("15.4839"), new BigDecimal("73.808"), 18, 2024, 2900000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miramar_beach_goa.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Bhagwan Mahavir Wildlife Sanctuary", "bhagwan-mahavir-wildlife-sanctuary", "Mollem, South Goa",
                "Goa's largest protected wildlife sanctuary, covering 240 square kilometers of pristine Western Ghats tropical evergreen forests.",
                "Forming the core of Goa's richest biodiversity hotspot, this sprawling sanctuary encompasses Mollem National Park, Dudhsagar Falls, and the ancient Tambdi Surla temple. It provides a vital ecological corridor for leopards, Bengal tigers, barking deer, King cobras, and over 120 species of indigenous birds.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/NH748_Mollem_Goa.jpg",
                "October to March", "Half day to full day", "Originally designated as Mollem Game Sanctuary in 1969, it was upgraded and renamed in 1982 to preserve Western Ghats biodiversity.", "Nature Interpretation Centre at Mollem, Devil's Canyon rock formation, Sunset Point overlooking canopy hills, Jungle trekking trails",
                "Obtain vehicle permits at the checkpost. Hire an authorized forest guide for tracking wildlife safely.", "8:00 AM – 5:30 PM daily", "₹20 for adults, ₹10 for children; ₹150 for cars; camera fee ₹30",
                new BigDecimal("15.3789"), new BigDecimal("74.2439"), 19, 2024, 350000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NH748_Mollem_Goa.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(goa, "Dona Paula", "dona-paula", "Panaji, North Goa",
                "A romantic rocky headland jutting out into the confluence of the Zuari and Mandovi rivers with the Arabian Sea.",
                "Famed as the 'Lovers' Paradise', Dona Paula is named after Dona Paula de Menezes, a legendary 17th-century Portuguese noblewoman associated with romantic Goan folklore. The windswept promontory offers magnificent views of Mormugao Harbor and features the iconic white sculpture 'Image of India' by Baroness Yrsa von Leistner.",
                "Viewpoint", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dona_Paula,_Goa.jpg",
                "October to March (especially at sunset)", "1 - 1.5 hours", "Named after historical figure Paula Amaral Antonio de Souto Maior, daughter of the Portuguese Governor, celebrated for philanthropy.", "Hilltop gazebo overlooking Mormugao port, 'Image of India' white modern sculpture atop rocks, Water sports and souvenir stalls",
                "Visit in late afternoon to enjoy cool ocean breezes and sunset views. Wear comfortable shoes to navigate the paved stairs.", "6:00 AM – 10:00 PM daily", "₹50 entry fee for the renovated jetty promenade",
                new BigDecimal("15.4533"), new BigDecimal("73.8033"), 20, 2024, 2700000L, "TOTAL",
                "Department of Tourism, Government of Goa", "Goa Annual Tourism Statistics Digest 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dona_Paula,_Goa.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        // --- 7. GUJARAT ---
        State gujarat = createState("Gujarat", "gujarat", "STATE", "Western", "Gandhinagar", "Land of legends, home to the Statue of Unity, Asiatic lions, and white Rann salt desert.",
                "Gujarat offers diverse attractions from Mahatma Gandhi's birthplace and Gir National Park to the majestic Statue of Unity and white desert of Kutch.",
                "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Statue_of_Unity.jpg", "October to March");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Statue of Unity", "statue-of-unity", "Kevadia",
                "World's tallest statue standing at 182 meters (597 feet), honoring Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel.",
                "Located on Sadhu Bet island facing the Narmada Dam, the monument includes a high-speed elevator to a viewing gallery at 153 meters inside the statue's chest.",
                "Monument", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Statue_of_Unity.jpg",
                "October to March", "Full Day", "Inaugurated on October 31, 2018, by PM Narendra Modi.", "Viewing Gallery, Valley of Flowers, Laser Light Show, Cactus Garden",
                "Book tickets online well in advance. Mondays closed for maintenance.", "8:00 AM - 6:00 PM (Closed Mon)", "Rs 150 Adults (Basic), Rs 380 (Viewing Gallery)",
                new BigDecimal("21.8380"), new BigDecimal("73.7191"), 1, 2024, 5200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Statue of Unity Tourism Authority (SUTA)", "SUTA Annual Report 2024", "https://statueofunity.in", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Rann of Kutch", "rann-of-kutch", "Kutch",
                "Vast white salt desert famous for the Rann Utsav cultural festival.",
                "One of the world's largest salt marshes, transforming into a brilliant white expanse under full moon nights.",
                "Nature", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The_White_Desert_in_Kutch,_the_great_rann_of_kutch.jpg",
                "November to February", "1-2 Days", "Ancient saline marsh formation.", "Kala Dungar, Dhordo Tent City, Kutchi Handicrafts",
                "Obtain Kutch Permit at Bhirandiyara checkpoint or online.", "Open All Day", "Permit Rs 100/person",
                new BigDecimal("23.8293"), new BigDecimal("69.8398"), 2, 2024, 2100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Gujarat Tourism Board", "Rann Utsav Visitor Report", "https://gujarattourism.com", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Somnath Temple", "somnath-jyotirlinga-temple", "Gir Somnath",
                "First of the twelve sacred Shiva Jyotirlingas of India, standing majestically in Chalukya stone architecture on the Arabian Sea shore.",
                "Overlooking the roaring waves of the Arabian Sea at Prabhas Patan, Shree Somnath Jyotirlinga Temple is an eternal symbol of spiritual resilience. Reconstructed in the Kailash Mahameru Prasad (Maru-Gurjara) style, its 155-foot shikhar is crowned by a 10-ton kalash, while the seaside Arrow Pillar (Baan Stambh) points along an unobstructed line of ocean all the way to the South Pole.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Shree_Somnath_Temple.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Reconstructed beginning in 1947 under the initiative of Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel and consecrated by President Dr. Rajendra Prasad in May 1951.", "Jyotirlinga Sanctum, Baan Stambh (South Pole Arrow Pillar), Evening Sound & Light Show, Triveni Sangam Ghat, Seafront Promenade",
                "Mobile phones, cameras, and leather items must be deposited at the cloakroom before security screening; stay for the 8:00 PM light show.", "6:00 AM – 10:00 PM daily", "Free entry (Light Show ₹50)",
                new BigDecimal("20.8880"), new BigDecimal("70.4012"), 3, 2024, 4800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tourism Corporation of Gujarat Limited (TCGL)", "Gujarat Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shree_Somnath_Temple.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Gir National Park", "gir-national-park", "Junagadh",
                "The sole natural habitat of the majestic Asiatic Lion (Panthera leo persica) in the dry deciduous teak forests of Saurashtra.",
                "Spread across 1,412 sq km of rugged hills, teak and acacia woodlands, and seven perennial rivers centered at Sasan Gir, Gir National Park and Wildlife Sanctuary is one of the world's greatest conservation success stories. Alongside over 670 Asiatic lions, the park supports Indian leopards, striped hyenas, marsh crocodiles at Kamleshwar Dam, and over 300 bird species.",
                "National Park", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Portrait_of_an_Asiatic_lion_at_Gir_National_Park_02.jpg",
                "December to March (Open Oct 16 – Jun 15)", "Half Day to 2 Days", "Protected first by the Nawab of Junagadh in 1900 when fewer than two dozen lions remained; declared a Sanctuary in 1965 and National Park in 1975.", "Sasan Gir Open Gypsy Jungle Safari, Devalia Safari Park (Gir Interpretation Zone), Kamleshwar Dam Crocodile Point, Crocodile Breeding Centre",
                "Book official e-permits at girlion.gujarat.gov.in at least 60 days ahead; the park is closed during monsoon (June 16 to October 15).", "6:00 AM – 9:00 AM, 9:00 AM – 12:00 PM, 3:00 PM – 6:00 PM", "Permit ₹800–₹1,000 + Gypsy & Guide charges",
                new BigDecimal("21.1243"), new BigDecimal("70.8242"), 4, 2024, 850000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tourism Corporation of Gujarat Limited (TCGL)", "Gujarat Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_an_Asiatic_lion_at_Gir_National_Park_02.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Dwarkadhish Temple", "dwarkadhish-temple", "Devbhumi Dwarka",
                "2,000-year-old five-storey limestone Jagat Mandir dedicated to Lord Krishna at the confluence of the Gomti River and Arabian Sea, one of India's Char Dham.",
                "Soaring 78 meters (256 feet) and supported by 72 intricately carved pillars, the Dwarkadhish Temple (Jagat Mandir) is both one of the four cardinal Char Dham pilgrimage sites established by Adi Shankaracharya and one of the Sapta Puri (seven sacred cities). Five times a day, a 52-yard multicolored ध्वज (flag) is hoisted atop its pinnacle against the sea breeze.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/N-GJ-126_Dwarkadhish_Temple,_Dwarka_Flag_Hoisting.jpg",
                "October to March (Janmashtami)", "2 to 3 hours", "Current Chalukya-style structure dates to the 15th–16th centuries over ancient foundations attributed in tradition to Krishna's great-grandson Vajranabha.", "72-Pillar Five-Storey Sabha Mandap, Daily 52-Yard Flag Hoisting Ceremony, Swargadwar & Mokshadwar Gates, Sudama Setu Suspension Bridge, Gomti Ghat",
                "Cross the Sudama Setu pedestrian bridge at sunset for panoramic views of the temple spire above Gomti Ghat.", "6:30 AM – 1:00 PM, 5:00 PM – 9:30 PM daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("22.2376"), new BigDecimal("68.9675"), 5, 2024, 4200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tourism Corporation of Gujarat Limited (TCGL)", "Gujarat Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N-GJ-126_Dwarkadhish_Temple,_Dwarka_Flag_Hoisting.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Rani ki Vav (Queen’s Stepwell)", "rani-ki-vav-patan", "Patan",
                "11th-century UNESCO World Heritage subterranean stepwell in Patan, designed as an inverted temple with over 1,500 exquisite sculptures.",
                "Commissioned around 1063 CE by Queen Udayamati in memory of Solanki ruler Bhima I, Rani ki Vav descends seven terraced storeys (27 meters deep) into the earth. Built in the Maru-Gurjara architectural style to sanctify groundwater, its stepped corridors are lined with over 500 principal sculptures and 1,000 minor carvings of Dashavatara Vishnu, Apsaras, and geometric Patola motifs.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Rani_ki_vav_-_Patan_-_Gujarat_-_Wall_Decorations.jpg",
                "October to March", "1.5 to 2 hours", "Silted over by the Saraswati River for centuries and excavated by the Archaeological Survey of India in the 1960s–1980s; inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2014 and featured on the ₹100 banknote.", "Seven Subterranean Sculpted Terraces, Sheshashayi Vishnu Carving in the Deepest Well Shaft, Patan Patola Silk Weaving Museum nearby",
                "Visit between 9:30 AM and 11:30 AM when angled sunlight illuminates the deepest western shaft carvings.", "8:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "₹40 Indian nationals; ₹600 Foreign nationals",
                new BigDecimal("23.8589"), new BigDecimal("72.1016"), 6, 2024, 690000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tourism Corporation of Gujarat Limited (TCGL)", "Gujarat Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rani_ki_vav_-_Patan_-_Gujarat_-_Wall_Decorations.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Sabarmati Ashram", "sabarmati-ashram-ahmedabad", "Ahmedabad",
                "Tranquil riverside residence of Mahatma Gandhi along the Sabarmati River from 1917 to 1930 and launchpad of the historic Dandi Salt March.",
                "Shaded by neem and peepal trees on the quiet western bank of the Sabarmati River in Ahmedabad, Satyagraha Ashram was Mahatma Gandhi's home and headquarters for India's non-violent freedom struggle. The campus preserves Hriday Kunj (Gandhi and Kasturba's cottage), Vinoba-Mira Kutir, Upasana Mandir prayer ground, and the Charles Correa-designed Gandhi Smarak Sangrahalaya museum.",
                "Historical", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Statue_of_Gandhi_at_Sabarmati_Ashram.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 hours", "Established on the Sabarmati riverbank on June 17, 1917; on March 12, 1930, Gandhi set out from here with 78 followers on the 241-mile Dandi Salt Satyagraha.", "Hriday Kunj Cottage & Charkha, Charles Correa Museum Galleries, My Life is My Message Exhibition, Sabarmati Riverfront Walk",
                "Try spinning cotton thread on a traditional Charkha at the ashram workshop and browse the bookstore for affordable editions of Gandhi's writings.", "8:30 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("23.0605"), new BigDecimal("72.5808"), 7, 2024, 1450000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tourism Corporation of Gujarat Limited (TCGL)", "Gujarat Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Gandhi_at_Sabarmati_Ashram.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Sun Temple, Modhera", "sun-temple-modhera", "Mehsana",
                "11th-century Solanki sandstone masterpiece dedicated to Surya (the Sun God), featuring a 52-pillar Sabha Mandap and stepped Surya Kund tank.",
                "Built in 1026–1027 CE during the reign of King Bhima I of the Chaulukya (Solanki) dynasty on the Tropic of Cancer, the Sun Temple at Modhera comprises three axially aligned components: the stepped Surya Kund (Rama Kund) lined with 108 miniature shrines, the open 52-pillar Sabha Mandapa representing the 52 weeks of the solar year, and the Gudha Mandapa sanctum designed to catch the first rays of the equinox sun.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sun_Temple,_Modhera_-_Sabha_Mandap_01.jpg",
                "October to March (Uttarardh Dance Festival in Jan)", "2 hours", "Constructed in 1026 CE without lime mortar using interlocking golden-yellow sandstone; Modhera became India's first 24x7 solar-powered heritage village in 2022.", "Surya Kund with 108 Stepped Shrines, 52-Pillar Sabha Mandapa, Solar Museum & 3D Projection Mapping Light Show",
                "Combine with Rani ki Vav in Patan (35 km away) and stay until 7:00 PM for the solar-powered 3D projection show.", "7:00 AM – 8:00 PM daily", "₹25 Indian nationals; ₹300 Foreign nationals",
                new BigDecimal("23.5835"), new BigDecimal("72.1332"), 8, 2024, 620000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tourism Corporation of Gujarat Limited (TCGL)", "Gujarat Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sun_Temple,_Modhera_-_Sabha_Mandap_01.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Laxmi Vilas Palace", "laxmi-vilas-palace-vadodara", "Vadodara",
                "Colossal Indo-Saracenic royal residence of the Gaekwad dynasty of Baroda, four times the size of Buckingham Palace.",
                "Commissioned by Maharaja Sayajirao Gaekwad III and completed in 1890 by British architect Major Charles Mant, Laxmi Vilas Palace spans 500 acres of landscaped parkland in Vadodara. Its ornate façade blends Hindu, Mughal, and Venetian Gothic domes, while the interior boasts the Venetian mosaic Durbar Hall, stained glass by Fell & Floyd, and the Maharaja Fateh Singh Museum.",
                "Palace", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Laxmi_Vilas_Palace_-_Vadodara.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Built at a cost of £180,000 in 1890 with modern elevators and internal telephone systems; remains the residence of the royal family of Baroda.", "Venetian Mosaic Durbar Hall, Royal Armoury & Bronze Sculptures, Maharaja Fateh Singh Museum (Raja Ravi Varma Original Paintings), Golf Course Grounds",
                "The ticket price includes an excellent multilingual audio guide that narrates the history of each hall.", "9:30 AM – 5:00 PM (Closed Mondays)", "₹250 Palace Entry (with Audio Guide); ₹150 Museum",
                new BigDecimal("22.2936"), new BigDecimal("73.1919"), 9, 2024, 540000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tourism Corporation of Gujarat Limited (TCGL)", "Gujarat Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laxmi_Vilas_Palace_-_Vadodara.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Champaner-Pavagadh Archaeological Park", "champaner-pavagadh-archaeological-park", "Panchmahal",
                "UNESCO World Heritage pre-Mughal walled city at the foot of volcanic Pavagadh Hill, celebrated for the Indo-Islamic Jami Masjid and Kalika Mata Temple.",
                "Situated 50 km northeast of Vadodara, Champaner-Pavagadh Archaeological Park preserves the only complete and unchanged Islamic pre-Mughal 16th-century capital in India alongside ancient Hindu and Jain fortress temples crowning the 800-meter volcanic crag of Pavagadh. Its architectural jewel is the Jami Masjid (1513 CE), which fuses Hindu stone jali latticework and lotus domes with soaring minarets.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jami_Masjid_-_Champaner-Pavagadh_Archaeological_Park_-_Gujarat_-_DSC027.jpg",
                "October to March", "Half Day", "Founded in the 8th century by Vanraj Chavda and rebuilt as Mahmudabad Champaner by Sultan Mahmud Begada in 1484; inscribed by UNESCO in 2004.", "Jami Masjid, Kevada Masjid & Nagina Masjid, Helical Stepwell, Saat Kaman (Seven Arches), Kalika Mata Temple & Ropeway on Pavagadh Hill",
                "Start early at the foot-hill mosques (Jami, Kevada, and Sahar ki Masjid) before taking the ropeway up Pavagadh Hill.", "8:30 AM – 5:00 PM daily", "₹40 Indian nationals; ₹600 Foreign nationals",
                new BigDecimal("22.4847"), new BigDecimal("73.5367"), 10, 2024, 780000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tourism Corporation of Gujarat Limited (TCGL)", "Gujarat Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jami_Masjid_-_Champaner-Pavagadh_Archaeological_Park_-_Gujarat_-_DSC027.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Dholavira UNESCO Harappan Site", "dholavira-harappan-site", "Kutch",
                "4,500-year-old UNESCO World Heritage Indus Valley metropolis on Khadir Bet island in Kutch, famous for giant rock-cut water reservoirs.",
                "Reached across the dramatic Road to Heaven causeway cutting through the white Rann of Kutch to Khadir Bet island, Dholavira (Kotada Timba) was occupied from ~3000 BCE to 1500 BCE. Unlike brick-built Mohenjo-daro and Harappa, Dholavira was constructed in dressed stone and features a three-tiered urban plan (Citadel, Middle Town, Lower Town), the world's earliest rock-cut water conservation reservoirs, and a 10-character giant Harappan signboard.",
                "Archaeological", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dholavira_gujarat.jpg",
                "November to February", "3 to 4 hours", "Discovered by J.P. Joshi in 1967–68 and inscribed as India's 40th UNESCO World Heritage Site in July 2021.", "Eastern & Southern Rock-Cut Reservoirs, Citadel Bailey & Ceremonial Stadium, 10-Symbol Harappan Signboard Replica, Dholavira Site Museum, Fossil Park nearby",
                "Drive the scenic Khavda-to-Dholavira \"Road to Heaven\" highway across the Rann lakebed during daylight hours.", "6:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily (Museum 9:00 AM – 5:00 PM)", "₹25 Entry",
                new BigDecimal("23.8866"), new BigDecimal("70.2122"), 11, 2024, 310000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tourism Corporation of Gujarat Limited (TCGL)", "Gujarat Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dholavira_gujarat.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Palitana Jain Temples (Shatrunjaya Hill)", "palitana-shatrunjaya-temples", "Bhavnagar",
                "Sacred hilltop city of over 860 marble-carved Svetambara Jain temples crowning twin peaks of Shatrunjaya Hill above the Shetrunji River.",
                "Ascended via 3,800 stone steps in Bhavnagar district, the Shatrunjaya Hill temples of Palitana form one of the holiest pilgrimage tirthas in Jainism, where the first Tirthankara, Lord Adinath (Rishabhadeva), meditated. Built over 900 years starting in the 11th century and clustered into nine fortified enclosures (Tunks), the gleaming white marble temples are considered an abode of the gods—no person, including priests, stays overnight on the summit.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Palitana_temples_01.jpg",
                "October to March", "Half Day (4 to 6 hours climb)", "Constructed across nine centuries by generations of Jain patrons, with the main Adinath temple restored in the 16th century.", "Shri Adishwar Main Temple, Nine Fortified Tunks (Enclosures), Chaumukhji Four-Faced Shrine, 3,800-Step Pilgrim Ascent, Shetrunji River Panorama",
                "Begin the climb at 5:30 AM before sunrise; dress modestly, carry drinking water, and note that food and leather items are not allowed on the sacred hill.", "5:30 AM – 5:30 PM daily (Summit vacated before dusk)", "Free entry (Doli palanquin available for seniors)",
                new BigDecimal("21.5039"), new BigDecimal("71.8108"), 12, 2024, 490000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tourism Corporation of Gujarat Limited (TCGL)", "Gujarat Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palitana_temples_01.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Adalaj Stepwell (Adalaj ni Vav)", "adalaj-stepwell-gandhinagar", "Gandhinagar",
                "Five-storey 15th-century octagonal sandstone stepwell near Ahmedabad and Gandhinagar, blending Solanki Hindu pillars with Indo-Islamic floral domes.",
                "Built in 1499 CE by Queen Rudabai, wife of Vaghela chief Veer Singh, Adalaj ni Vav is a subterranean architectural marvel entered from three cardinal staircases that converge on the first underground landing. Its five cool storeys are supported by octagonal shafts, intricately carved balconies, Kalpavriksha (tree of life) motifs, and Navagraha friezes that keep the interior 6°C cooler than the surface.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Adalaj_Stepwell_-_Rudabai_stepwell_-_Ahmedabad_-_Gujarat_-_001.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 1.5 hours", "Completed in 1499 CE under the patronage of Queen Rudabai and Sultan Mahmud Begada, as recorded in a Sanskrit inscription on the upper gallery.", "Three-Way Stepped Entrance, Octagonal Deep Shaft, Navagraha & Kalpavriksha Stone Reliefs, Cool Subterranean Colonades",
                "Only 18 km north of Ahmedabad—visit around midday when vertical sunbeams pierce straight down the octagonal shaft.", "6:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "₹25 Indian nationals; ₹300 Foreign nationals",
                new BigDecimal("23.1667"), new BigDecimal("72.5801"), 13, 2024, 720000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tourism Corporation of Gujarat Limited (TCGL)", "Gujarat Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adalaj_Stepwell_-_Rudabai_stepwell_-_Ahmedabad_-_Gujarat_-_001.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Swaminarayan Akshardham, Gandhinagar", "akshardham-temple-gandhinagar", "Gandhinagar",
                "Grand 23-acre pink sandstone monument dedicated to Bhagwan Swaminarayan in Gujarat's capital, famed for its Sat-Chit-Anand Water Show.",
                "Constructed from 6,000 metric tons of pink sandstone from Rajasthan without a single piece of structural steel, Swaminarayan Akshardham in Gandhinagar rises 108 feet high with 97 carved pillars, 17 domes, and 264 sculpted figures. Surrounding the central mandir are lush Sahajanand Van gardens, cultural exhibition halls, and an amphitheater hosting the multimedia Sat-Chit-Anand Water Show.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Akshardham_Gandhinagar_Gujarat.jpg",
                "October to March", "3 to 4 hours", "Inspired by Pramukh Swami Maharaj and inaugurated on November 2, 1992, by BAPS Swaminarayan Sanstha.", "Pink Sandstone Central Mandir & 7-Foot Gold-Leaf Murti, Sat-Chit-Anand Multimedia Water Show, Sahajanand Van Gardens, Audio-Animatronic Exhibition Halls",
                "Arrive by 3:30 PM to tour the monument and gardens before attending the 6:45 PM Sat-Chit-Anand Water Show.", "9:30 AM – 7:30 PM (Closed Mondays)", "Mandir Entry Free; Water Show & Exhibitions ₹150–₹250",
                new BigDecimal("23.2292"), new BigDecimal("72.6742"), 14, 2024, 1650000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tourism Corporation of Gujarat Limited (TCGL)", "Gujarat Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akshardham_Gandhinagar_Gujarat.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Saputara Hill Station", "saputara-hill-station", "Dang",
                "Gujarat's premier Sahyadri hill station perched at 1,000 meters in the forested Dang district, overlooking Saputara Lake and Gira Waterfalls.",
                "Set on a misty plateau in the Western Ghats (Sahyadri range) along the Gujarat-Maharashtra border, Saputara (\"Abode of Serpents\") is surrounded by bamboo forests, Dangi tribal villages, and cascading monsoon waterfalls. Visitors enjoy boating on Saputara Lake, riding the aerial ropeway to Sunset Point, and exploring the Vansda National Park and Gira Falls nearby.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Saputara,_Gujarat,_India_-_panoramio.jpg",
                "July to March (Monsoon Festival Aug–Sep)", "1 to 2 Days", "Inhabited by indigenous Dangi Bhil, Kunbi, and Warli tribes who worship the sacred serpent deity along the Sarpaganga River on Holi.", "Saputara Lake & Boat Club, Sunset Point Ropeway, Governor's Hill & Table Point, Saputara Tribal Museum, Gira Waterfalls (Waghai)",
                "Visit during the Saputara Monsoon Festival (August–September) for lush green hills, cultural dances, and roaring waterfalls.", "Open 24 Hours (Boating & Ropeway 8:30 AM – 6:00 PM)", "Free town entry (Boating ₹50; Ropeway ₹120)",
                new BigDecimal("20.5761"), new BigDecimal("73.7486"), 15, 2024, 920000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tourism Corporation of Gujarat Limited (TCGL)", "Gujarat Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saputara,_Gujarat,_India_-_panoramio.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Girnar Hills & Jain Temples", "girnar-hills-junagadh", "Junagadh",
                "Gujarat's highest mountain massif (1,117 m) rising above Junagadh, crowned by 12th-century Jain temples, Ambaji Temple, and Asia's longest temple ropeway.",
                "Older than the Himalayas and sacred to both Hindus and Jains, Mount Girnar (Revatak Parvat) rises in five dramatic volcanic peaks east of Junagadh. Traditionally climbed via 9,999 stone steps starting from Bhavnath Taleti, the mountain is now also served by the 2.3-km Girnar Ropeway to the Ambaji peak, near the intricately carved 12th-century Neminath Jain temple cluster and the highest Gorakhnath and Dattatreya summits.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Girnar_1.jpg",
                "October to March", "Half Day (Climb or Ropeway)", "At the foot of Girnar stands Emperor Ashoka's 3rd-century BCE Rock Edicts (coupled with Rudradaman and Skandagupta Sanskrit inscriptions).", "Girnar Aerial Ropeway, 12th-Century Lord Neminath Jain Temple Complex, Ambaji & Gorakhnath Peaks, Ashoka Rock Edicts at the Foothills",
                "Take the morning ropeway slot to Ambaji Peak or begin the traditional stair climb before 5:00 AM with a walking stick.", "6:00 AM – 5:00 PM (Ropeway operating hours)", "Free trail climb (Girnar Ropeway ₹700 two-way)",
                new BigDecimal("21.5269"), new BigDecimal("70.5289"), 16, 2024, 1100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tourism Corporation of Gujarat Limited (TCGL)", "Gujarat Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Girnar_1.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Vijay Vilas Palace, Mandvi", "vijay-vilas-palace-mandvi", "Kutch",
                "1929 red sandstone seaside summer palace of the Jadeja rulers of Kutch on Mandvi Beach, famous for its Rajput dome and film heritage.",
                "Built in 1929 by Rao Vijayrajji as a summer retreat for the Maharao of Kutch, Vijay Vilas Palace sits amidst 450 acres of manicured gardens and a private 2-km stretch of Mandvi Beach. Crafted by Jaipur artisans in red sandstone, it blends Rajput jharokha balconies, a high central Bengal dome modelled on Orchha and Datia palaces, coloured glass windows, and breezy rooftop chhatris overlooking the Arabian Sea.",
                "Palace", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Magnificent_Vijay_Vilas_Palace-Mandvi,_Gujarat.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 hours", "Designed in 1920–1929 during the reign of Maharao Khengarji III for Yuvraj Vijayarajji; featured in iconic Indian films including Hum Dil De Chuke Sanam and Lagaan.", "Rooftop Chhatri & Central Dome Terrace, Royal Museum Galleries, Mandvi Private Beachfront, Traditional Dhow Shipbuilding Yard in Mandvi town",
                "Climb to the upper terrace for sweeping coastal views and stop by the 400-year-old wooden dhow shipbuilding yard on the Rukmavati River.", "9:00 AM – 1:00 PM, 3:00 PM – 6:00 PM daily", "₹50 Palace Entry; ₹20 Vehicle",
                new BigDecimal("22.8339"), new BigDecimal("69.3081"), 17, 2024, 430000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tourism Corporation of Gujarat Limited (TCGL)", "Gujarat Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Magnificent_Vijay_Vilas_Palace-Mandvi,_Gujarat.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Uparkot Fort & Mahabat Maqbara", "uparkot-fort-junagadh", "Junagadh",
                "2,300-year-old Mauryan citadel in Junagadh paired with the fairy-tale Indo-Gothic minarets of Mahabat Maqbara.",
                "Towering over historic Junagadh, Uparkot Fort was founded in 319 BCE by Chandragupta Maurya and besieged 16 times over two millennia. Inside its 20-meter ramparts lie 2nd-century Buddhist rock-cut caves, the deep rock-hewn Adi Kadi Vav and Navghan Kuvo stepwells, and the massive Neelam and Manek cannons. Nearby in the old city stands Mahabat Maqbara (1892), famous for its silver portals and spiral exterior staircases wrapping around four minarets.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mahabat_Maqbara_-_Junagadh_-_Gujarat_-_005.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Uparkot served as the stronghold of Maurya, Gupta, Chudasama, Muzaffarid, and Babi Nawab rulers; recently restored under Gujarat Tourism.", "Mahabat Maqbara & Bahauddin Maqbara Spiral Minarets, Adi Kadi Vav & Navghan Kuvo Rock-Cut Stepwells, Uparkot Buddhist Caves, Neelam & Manek Cannons",
                "Start at Mahabat Maqbara in the morning light, then spend 2 hours exploring the newly restored walkways of Uparkot Fort.", "8:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "₹50 Uparkot Fort Entry; Mahabat Maqbara Exterior Free",
                new BigDecimal("21.5247"), new BigDecimal("70.4675"), 18, 2024, 460000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tourism Corporation of Gujarat Limited (TCGL)", "Gujarat Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahabat_Maqbara_-_Junagadh_-_Gujarat_-_005.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Shivrajpur Blue Flag Beach", "shivrajpur-beach-dwarka", "Devbhumi Dwarka",
                "Internationally certified Blue Flag white-sand cove 12 km north of Dwarka, known for clear turquoise waters, scuba diving, and coastal trails.",
                "Curving between rocky headlands and the Kachchhigadh Lighthouse between Dwarka and Okha, Shivrajpur Beach was awarded the prestigious international Blue Flag eco-label for water quality, safety, and environmental management. Its gentle gradient and clear Arabian Sea waters make it Gujarat's premier destination for snorkeling, scuba diving, dolphin spotting, and family beach recreation.",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Guj21_Dwarka_Lighthouse_and_clear_Arabian_sea.jpg",
                "October to April", "2 to 3 hours", "Awarded Blue Flag Certification by the Foundation for Environmental Education (FEE), Denmark, in October 2020.", "Blue Flag Certified White-Sand Bay, Scuba Diving & Coral Snorkeling, Kachchhigadh Lighthouse Viewpoint, Solar-Lit Coastal Promenade",
                "Located right along the Dwarka–Bet Dwarka highway, making it an easy afternoon stop after morning darshan at Dwarkadhish Temple.", "6:00 AM – 7:00 PM daily", "₹30 Beach Facility Entry",
                new BigDecimal("22.3328"), new BigDecimal("68.9547"), 19, 2024, 580000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tourism Corporation of Gujarat Limited (TCGL)", "Gujarat Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guj21_Dwarka_Lighthouse_and_clear_Arabian_sea.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(gujarat, "Lothal Archaeological Dockyard", "lothal-harappan-port", "Ahmedabad District",
                "4,400-year-old Indus Valley Civilization maritime port city featuring the world's earliest known brick-built tidal dockyard.",
                "Situated 80 km southwest of Ahmedabad near the Gulf of Khambhat, Lothal (\"Mound of the Dead\") flourished around 2400 BCE as the premier seaport and bead-making factory of the Harappan Civilization. Its trapezoidal kiln-fired brick basin (214 m x 36 m) connected via an ancient channel of the Sabarmati River to the Arabian Sea, enabling maritime trade with Mesopotamia and Egypt.",
                "Archaeological", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dockyard2.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 hours", "Excavated by ASI archaeologist S.R. Rao between 1955 and 1962; currently being developed alongside India's National Maritime Heritage Complex.", "Harappan Brick Tidal Dockyard & Spillway Lock-Gate, Acropolis & Underground Drainage System, Bead Factory Ruins, ASI Archaeological Museum",
                "Visit the on-site ASI Archaeological Museum first (closed Fridays) to see the original carnelian beads, steatite seals, and ivory scales before walking the mounds.", "8:00 AM – 6:00 PM (Museum 10:00 AM – 5:00 PM, Closed Fridays)", "₹15 Entry",
                new BigDecimal("22.5214"), new BigDecimal("72.2494"), 20, 2024, 295000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tourism Corporation of Gujarat Limited (TCGL)", "Gujarat Tourism Footfall Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dockyard2.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        // --- 8. HARYANA ---
        State haryana = createState("Haryana", "haryana", "STATE", "Northern", "Chandigarh", "Land of Kurukshetra, highway tourism, and ancient Vedic roots.",
                "Surrounding Delhi on three sides, Haryana is known for Kurukshetra battlefield, Sultanpur Bird Sanctuary, and Kingdom of Dreams.",
                "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Brahma_Sarovar,_Kurukshetra.jpg", "October to March");

        createDestination(haryana, "Kurukshetra Brahma Sarovar", "kurukshetra-brahma-sarovar", "Kurukshetra",
                "Brahma Sarovar is an ancient water tank in Kurukshetra, revered as the cradle of civilization where Lord Brahma performed his cosmic sacrifice.",
                "Mentioned in the Mahabharata and the travelogues of Al-Beruni, this vast water body stretches over 1,800 meters in length and holds deep spiritual significance. Pilgrims gather here in the millions during solar eclipses and the annual International Gita Mahotsav to take a holy dip in its sacred waters.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Brahma_Sarovar,_Kurukshetra.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 hours", "Associated with Lord Brahma's yajna to commence cosmic creation and referenced in ancient texts including the Mahabharata.", "Sarveshwar Mahadev Temple, colossal bronze Krishna-Arjuna Chariot, evening Maha Aarti, Gita Mahotsav pavilions",
                "Attend the evening Maha Aarti on the ghats for a mesmerizing spiritual ambiance; wear modest attire.", "Open 24 hours daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("29.9592"), new BigDecimal("76.8342"), 1, 2024, 4500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brahma_Sarovar,_Kurukshetra.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Jyotisar", "jyotisar", "Kurukshetra",
                "Jyotisar is the venerated birthplace of the Bhagavad Gita, where Lord Krishna delivered his immortal sermon to Arjuna beneath a sacred banyan tree.",
                "Situated along the Kurukshetra-Pehowa road, this hallowed site features an ancient banyan tree believed to be an offshoot of the original Kalpataru tree witness to the discourse. The complex encompasses marble statues of Krishna and Arjuna in their war chariot, a holy pond, and a world-class sound and light show explaining the core tenets of the Gita.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jyotisar-Kurukshetra-India.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 hours", "The epic epicenter where the celestial song of the Bhagavad Gita was delivered right before the Kurukshetra War.", "Sacred Akshay Vat (banyan tree), marble chariot sculpture, holy sarovar, multimedia light and sound show",
                "Plan to attend the evening light and sound show; hiring an authorized local guide enhances the historical context.", "6:00 AM – 8:00 PM daily", "Free entry (Light & Sound show: ₹50 per person)",
                new BigDecimal("29.9678"), new BigDecimal("76.7644"), 2, 2024, 1800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jyotisar-Kurukshetra-India.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Sultanpur National Park", "sultanpur-national-park", "Gurugram",
                "Sultanpur National Park is a prominent Ramsar wetland sanctuary famous for sheltering hundreds of migratory and resident bird species in winter.",
                "Located along the Gurugram-Jhajjar highway, the park transforms into a vibrant avian haven between November and February when winter migrants from Siberia, Central Asia, and Europe arrive. It features well-maintained walking trails, four watchtowers, and an educational interpretation centre named after Dr. Salim Ali.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sultanpur_Bird_Sanctuary,_Gurgaon.jpg",
                "November to February", "3 to 4 hours", "Established as a bird sanctuary in 1972 through the tireless advocacy of Peter Jackson and Dr. Salim Ali; designated a Ramsar Site in 2021.", "Migratory bird observation, watchtowers, Dr. Salim Ali Interpretation Centre, freshwater lake",
                "Carry high-power binoculars and zoom cameras; the park is closed on Tuesdays, so plan your visit on other days.", "7:00 AM – 4:30 PM (Closed on Tuesdays)", "₹5 per person (Indians), ₹40 (Foreigners); Camera fee extra",
                new BigDecimal("28.4623"), new BigDecimal("76.8929"), 3, 2024, 420000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultanpur_Bird_Sanctuary,_Gurgaon.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Morni Hills", "morni-hills", "Panchkula",
                "Morni Hills is the sole hill station in Haryana, perched at 1,220 meters amidst lush pine forests and serene twin lakes in the Shivalik foothills.",
                "Set around 45 kilometers from Chandigarh, Morni Hills offers panoramic vistas, trekking trails, and rich biodiversity including barking deer and leopards. The scenic twin water bodies of Tikkar Taal are a focal point for boating, picnics, and adventure sports.",
                "Nature", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Morni_Hills.jpg",
                "September to March", "Half day to full day", "Ruled by local Thakur chieftains for centuries; home to the 17th-century Morni Fort overlooking the valley.", "Tikkar Taal twin lakes, Morni Fort, herbal nature park, adventure sports camp",
                "Drive carefully on winding hill roads; book Haryana Tourism's Mountain Quail resort in advance for weekend stays.", "Open 24 hours daily", "Free entry (Boating and adventure activities chargeable)",
                new BigDecimal("30.6975"), new BigDecimal("77.0862"), 4, 2024, 650000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morni_Hills.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Pinjore Gardens", "pinjore-gardens", "Panchkula",
                "Pinjore Gardens is a magnificent 17th-century terraced Mughal garden complex renowned for its cascading fountains, water pavilions, and manicured lawns.",
                "Constructed during the reign of Emperor Aurangzeb by architect Nawab Fidai Khan, the garden descends over seven stepped terraces in the Charbagh pattern. Beautiful palaces including Shish Mahal, Rang Mahal, and Jal Mahal line the central waterway, which is magically illuminated after dusk.",
                "Gardens", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Pinjore_Garden_Panchkula.jpg",
                "October to April", "2 to 3 hours", "A masterpiece of Mughal landscape architecture designed by Nawab Fidai Khan in the 17th century and restored by the Patiala rulers.", "Shish Mahal, Rang Mahal, Jal Mahal, seven descending terraces, illuminated fountains",
                "Visit in the late afternoon to witness the day views and stay through the night when fountains are illuminated.", "7:00 AM – 10:00 PM daily", "₹25 per person",
                new BigDecimal("30.7944"), new BigDecimal("76.915"), 5, 2024, 1600000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pinjore_Garden_Panchkula.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Sheikh Chilli's Tomb", "sheikh-chillis-tomb", "Kurukshetra",
                "Sheikh Chilli's Tomb is an exquisite Mughal architectural complex featuring a gleaming white marble pear-shaped dome, madrasa, and lush gardens.",
                "Built in memory of Sufi master Abd-ur-Rahim, popularly revered as Sheikh Chilli and the spiritual preceptor of Mughal Prince Dara Shikoh, the mausoleum reflects high Mughal craftsmanship. The elevated complex houses an ASI archaeological museum exhibiting artifacts unearthed from nearby ancient mounds such as Harsh Ka Tila.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tomb_of_Sheikh-Chilli-1.JPG",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 hours", "Mausoleum of the Sufi saint associated with Dara Shikoh; protected ASI Monument of National Importance.", "Marble dome mausoleum, medieval madrasa cloisters, Mughal charbagh garden, ASI Archaeological Museum",
                "Combine with a visit to the adjacent Harsh Ka Tila excavation mound; photography permitted outside.", "9:00 AM – 5:00 PM (Closed on Fridays)", "₹25 (Indians), ₹300 (Foreigners)",
                new BigDecimal("29.9808"), new BigDecimal("76.8222"), 6, 2024, 380000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Sheikh-Chilli-1.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Surajkund", "surajkund", "Faridabad",
                "Surajkund is a 10th-century semicircular sun pool reservoir built by Tomar king Suraj Pal, renowned for hosting the world-famous International Crafts Mela.",
                "Constructed against the rugged backdrop of the Aravalli hills, this stepped stone amphitheatre was engineered as a sophisticated rainwater harvesting reservoir dedicated to the Sun God. Today, it serves as the vibrant staging ground for the Surajkund International Crafts Mela every February, drawing artisans and performers worldwide.",
                "Archaeological", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Suraj_Kund.jpg",
                "November to February", "2 to 3 hours", "Built in the 10th century by Tomara king Suraj Pal, representing early medieval rainwater harvesting engineering in North India.", "Semicircular stepped reservoir, sun temple ruins, Surajkund International Crafts Mela grounds, Aravalli nature trails",
                "Visiting during the February Crafts Mela requires advance booking to avoid massive weekend ticket queues.", "6:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "Free on normal days; ₹120–₹180 per person during Crafts Mela",
                new BigDecimal("28.4983"), new BigDecimal("77.2831"), 7, 2024, 1950000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suraj_Kund.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Rakhigarhi Archaeological Site", "rakhigarhi-archaeological-site", "Hisar",
                "Rakhigarhi is the largest known metropolis of the Indus Valley Civilisation, sprawling across over 350 hectares of ancient mounds.",
                "Excavations at Rakhigarhi have revealed planned urban streets, sophisticated brick drainage systems, terracotta pottery, lapidary workshops, and Harappan burial grounds dating from 5500 BCE to 1900 BCE. A new state-of-the-art site museum showcases pioneering archaeological and ancient DNA discoveries that revolutionized South Asian history.",
                "Archaeological", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Rakhigarhi_Harappan_civilization.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 4 hours", "The largest excavated Harappan settlement, establishing the indigenous continuity and antiquity of the Saraswati-Indus civilization.", "Excavation mounds (Mound 1-7), mudbrick residential quarters, ancient drainage networks, Rakhigarhi Site Museum",
                "Engage a certified ASI or local guide to properly interpret the excavation trenches; bring sun protection.", "9:00 AM – 5:00 PM (Closed on Mondays)", "Free entry / Nominal museum charges",
                new BigDecimal("29.2894"), new BigDecimal("76.1158"), 8, 2024, 210000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rakhigarhi_Harappan_civilization.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Agroha Dham", "agroha-dham", "Hisar",
                "Agroha Dham is an imposing Hindu temple complex dedicated to Goddess Mahalaxmi and Maharaja Agrasen, the revered ancestor of the Agrawal community.",
                "Spread over a spacious complex divided into three wings dedicated to Mahalaxmi, Maharaja Agrasen, and Saraswati, Agroha Dham features a large holy sarovar and amusement park. Adjacent to the shrine lies the historic Agroha Mound, an ancient trade hub mentioned in classical Sanskrit and Buddhist literature.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Agroha_Temple.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Spiritual headquarters of the Agrawal community and legendary capital of Maharaja Agrasen's ancient Agroha republic.", "Mahalaxmi Temple, Maharaja Agrasen Shrine, Shakti Sarovar, Sheela Mata Temple, Agroha Mound",
                "Enjoy the pure vegetarian meals served at the temple langar; Sharad Purnima and Diwali festivals draw large crowds.", "5:00 AM – 9:00 PM daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("29.3308"), new BigDecimal("75.6175"), 9, 2024, 1400000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agroha_Temple.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Bhindawas Wildlife Sanctuary", "bhindawas-wildlife-sanctuary", "Jhajjar",
                "Bhindawas Wildlife Sanctuary is the largest freshwater wetland in Haryana, designated as a Ramsar Site that hosts over 250 bird species.",
                "Covering an area of over 1,000 acres, this man-made lake and wetland sanctuary features a 12-kilometer peripheral embankment that serves as an exceptional vantage point for birdwatchers. In winter, flocks of migratory waterfowl including bar-headed geese, pelicans, and flamingos populate its tranquil waters.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Saxicoloides_fulicatus_Bhindawas.jpg",
                "November to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Created to store surplus floodwater via the Jawaharlal Nehru Canal; designated a Ramsar Wetland of International Importance in 2021.", "Wetland lake, birdwatching trail along outer bund, viewing towers, resident and migratory waterfowl",
                "Carry spotting scopes or binoculars; best visited in early morning during winter months for optimal bird activity.", "6:00 AM – 5:00 PM daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("28.5392"), new BigDecimal("76.5414"), 10, 2024, 150000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saxicoloides_fulicatus_Bhindawas.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Damdama Lake", "damdama-lake", "Gurugram",
                "Damdama Lake is an expansive natural depression lake nestled at the base of the Aravalli hills, popular for boating and adventure getaways.",
                "Formed in 1947 by a rainwater harvesting bund constructed by the British, Damdama spans nearly 3,000 acres during peak monsoons. The lake serves as a favored weekend retreat offering paddle boating, rowing, rock climbing, and camping against a picturesque hill backdrop.",
                "Lake", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Lakedamd.jpg",
                "October to March", "3 to 5 hours", "Formed originally in 1947 by rainwater harvesting barrages; serves as an important ecological buffer in the southern Aravalli ridge.", "Lake boating, kayaking, camping resorts, rock climbing, Aravalli trekking trails",
                "Ideal for day trips from Delhi NCR; avoid peak summer months when the water level recedes significantly.", "9:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "Free entry (Boating: ₹100–₹300 per boat)",
                new BigDecimal("28.3075"), new BigDecimal("77.07"), 11, 2024, 720000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lakedamd.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Badkhal Lake", "badkhal-lake", "Faridabad",
                "Badkhal Lake is an artificial lake surrounded by the Aravalli hills, established post-independence and currently undergoing major ecological rejuvenation.",
                "Constructed in 1947 by creating an earthen dam between two hills to harvest rainwater for irrigation, Badkhal Lake grew into a thriving tourist and boating hub. While underground mining and drying depleted its basin in past decades, comprehensive smart city rejuvenation projects are restoring its water levels and recreational promenades.",
                "Lake", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dried_Badkhal_lake.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 hours", "Constructed in 1947 shortly after Indian independence to conserve rainwater; historic recreation landmark of Faridabad.", "Aravalli hillside views, heritage bund walk, birdwatching spots, upcoming waterfront promenade",
                "Combine your visit with nearby Surajkund or the Asola Bhatti wildlife sanctuary fringes.", "6:00 AM – 6:30 PM daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("28.4111"), new BigDecimal("77.2847"), 12, 2024, 350000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dried_Badkhal_lake.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Yadavindra Gardens", "yadavindra-gardens", "Panchkula",
                "Yadavindra Gardens is a historic 17th-century stepped royal garden complex in Pinjore, renowned for its heritage palaces, fountains, and night illumination.",
                "Named in honor of Maharaja Yadavindra Singh of Patiala, the garden features classical Mughal pavilions set along descending terraces. Its central water channels, cypress-lined walks, and traditional pavilions like the Rang Mahal and Jal Mahal showcase royal Mughal and Rajasthani-Sikh architectural confluence.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Pinjore_Gardens_at_night.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Commissioned by Nawab Fidai Khan under Mughal Emperor Aurangzeb and later conserved and embellished by Patiala royalty.", "Illuminated waterways, historic Rang Mahal and Shish Mahal, Japanese garden wing, annual Pinjore Mango Festival",
                "Evenings are ideal to experience the lit fountains and cool mountain breezes coming down from the Shivalik hills.", "7:00 AM – 10:00 PM daily", "₹25 per person",
                new BigDecimal("30.7944"), new BigDecimal("76.915"), 13, 2024, 850000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pinjore_Gardens_at_night.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Mata Mansa Devi Temple", "mata-mansa-devi-temple", "Panchkula",
                "Mata Mansa Devi Temple is a venerated Shakti shrine dedicated to the goddess of wish-fulfillment, perched at the foothills of the Shivalik range.",
                "Constructed by Maharaja Gopal Singh of Mani Majra between 1811 and 1815, the temple complex displays exquisite wall frescoes representing the Kangra painting style. Millions of pilgrims gather during the bi-annual Navratri fairs to seek blessings from Mata Mansa Devi, a manifestation of Goddess Shakti.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mansa_Devi_temple_main_shrine.jpg",
                "September to April (especially during Navratri)", "1 to 2 hours", "Built in early 19th-century by the rulers of Mani Majra; renowned for its preserved Pahari and Kangra school wall murals.", "Main sanctum sanctorum, Patiala Temple, heritage wall frescoes, Yagya Shala, panoramic foothill views",
                "Expect heavy pilgrim rush and security queues during Chaitra and Ashvin Navratri; special pass counters available.", "4:00 AM – 10:00 PM daily", "Free entry (VIP darshan queues available during festivals)",
                new BigDecimal("30.7183"), new BigDecimal("76.8486"), 14, 2024, 3200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mansa_Devi_temple_main_shrine.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Farrukhnagar Fort", "farrukhnagar-fort", "Gurugram",
                "Farrukhnagar Fort was an 18th-century bastion town established by Faujdar Khan, featuring the iconic arched Dilli Darwaza and royal palaces.",
                "Founded in 1732 under Mughal Emperor Farrukhsiyar, the fort was shaped in an octagonal design with five grand entrance gates. While much of the ramparts was dismantled during British rule after the 1857 uprising, the majestic Dilli Darwaza (Delhi Gate), Sheesh Mahal, and Baoli Ghaus Ali Shah remain standing as proud testaments to its royal past.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dilli_Darwaza_002.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 hours", "Seat of the Nawabs of Farrukhnagar founded in 1732; prominent center of the 1857 freedom struggle under Nawab Ahmed Ali Khan.", "Dilli Darwaza (Delhi Gate), Sheesh Mahal palace, Baoli Ghaus Ali Shah stepwell, Sethani Ki Chhatri",
                "Combine your tour of Dilli Darwaza with the nearby Sheesh Mahal and stepwell, located within walking distance in the town.", "9:00 AM – 5:00 PM daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("28.4503"), new BigDecimal("76.8242"), 15, 2024, 180000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dilli_Darwaza_002.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Kos Minars", "kos-minars", "Ambala",
                "Kos Minars are solid masonry milestone towers erected along the Grand Trunk Road by Sher Shah Suri and Mughal emperors to mark imperial distances.",
                "Constructed at intervals of one 'kos' (roughly 3.2 kilometers or 2 miles), these conical cylindrical towers rose up to 30 feet tall and served as road markers, postal stations, and rest stops across Haryana. Scores of these preserved medieval pillars dot the GT Road corridor through Ambala, Karnal, Kurukshetra, and Faridabad.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kos_Minar-Ambala-Haryana-DSC1922.jpg",
                "October to March", "30 to 45 minutes", "Introduced by Sher Shah Suri in the 16th century and expanded by Emperors Akbar and Jahangir as an imperial communications network.", "Conical brick-and-lime milestone towers, historical Grand Trunk Road alignment, ASI heritage markers",
                "Easily spotted along the NH-44 highway; good stopovers for history and road-trip enthusiasts.", "Open 24 hours daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("30.3782"), new BigDecimal("76.7767"), 16, 2024, 120000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kos_Minar-Ambala-Haryana-DSC1922.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Raja Nahar Singh Palace", "raja-nahar-singh-palace", "Faridabad",
                "Raja Nahar Singh Palace is an 18th-century royal Jat fort-palace in Ballabhgarh, decorated with arched pavilions, jharokhas, and courtyards.",
                "Constructed between 1739 and 1850 by the ancestors of martyr Raja Nahar Singh, the palace exemplifies Shekhawati and Mughal architectural confluence. Painstakingly restored by Haryana Tourism into a heritage palace, it features elegant sandstone arches, darbar courtyards, and hosts the annual Kartik Cultural Festival.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nahar_Singh_Palace,_Ballabhgarh.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 hours", "Historic fortress of Raja Nahar Singh, a revered martyr who led local forces against the British during the 1857 uprising.", "Darbar Mahal, intricate sandstone jharokhas, central courtyard, heritage suites, Kartik Cultural Festival",
                "Accessible via Ballabhgarh Metro Station on the Violet Line; enjoy dining at the heritage lounge inside.", "9:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "Free entry / Nominal entry fee",
                new BigDecimal("28.3364"), new BigDecimal("77.3242"), 17, 2024, 290000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nahar_Singh_Palace,_Ballabhgarh.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Tilyar Lake", "tilyar-lake", "Rohtak",
                "Tilyar Lake is a popular 132-acre recreation destination in Rohtak featuring a tranquil lake, landscaped gardens, and a mini zoo.",
                "Located 42 kilometers from Delhi border on the Delhi-Fazilka highway, Tilyar Lake is a prime picnic and holiday spot maintained by Haryana Tourism. Visitors can enjoy paddle boating, cycling tracks, toy train rides, and an adjoining mini zoo housing tigers, leopards, and diverse bird species.",
                "Lake", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tilyar_Lake_water_And_greenary.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Developed in the late 20th century as a landmark ecotourism and recreational wetland hub by Haryana Tourism.", "Tilyar Lake boating, Rohtak Mini Zoo, toy train, lush green lawns, children's adventure park",
                "Great family getaway on weekends; paddle boats and speed boats are available for rental at reasonable rates.", "7:00 AM – 7:00 PM daily", "Free entry to lake grounds; Zoo: ₹10 (adults), ₹5 (children)",
                new BigDecimal("28.8756"), new BigDecimal("76.6272"), 18, 2024, 580000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tilyar_Lake_water_And_greenary.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Kalesar National Park", "kalesar-national-park", "Yamunanagar",
                "Kalesar National Park is an 11,570-acre biodiversity hotspot in the Shivalik foothills, known for sal forests, wild elephants, and leopards.",
                "Bordered by the Yamuna River and contiguous with Rajaji National Park in Uttarakhand, Kalesar protects dense sal, semal, and khair woodlands. The sanctuary is a critical wildlife corridor supporting leopards, Asian wild elephants, barking deer, sambar, and rare birds like the red junglefowl.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kot_Trail_in_Kaleshwar_National_Park_Panch_Khola_Haryana.jpg",
                "October to April", "3 to 5 hours", "Named after the historic Kaleshwar (Shiva) temple within its boundary; declared a National Park in December 2003.", "Jungle jeep safaris, Kot Trail, Dakpathar Yamuna riverbed, ancient Kaleshwar Mahadev Temple, birdwatching",
                "Book authorized forest jeep safaris in advance; safari trails may remain closed during the monsoon season (July to September).", "6:00 AM – 5:00 PM (Closed July to September)", "₹50 (Indians), ₹100 (Foreigners); Jeep safari extra",
                new BigDecimal("30.3432"), new BigDecimal("77.4624"), 19, 2024, 160000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kot_Trail_in_Kaleshwar_National_Park_Panch_Khola_Haryana.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(haryana, "Brahma Sarovar Heritage Area", "brahma-sarovar-heritage-area", "Kurukshetra",
                "The Brahma Sarovar Heritage Area encompasses the monumental bronze chariot of Sri Krishna and Arjuna, sacred ghats, and heritage promenades.",
                "Forming the cultural heart of holy Thanesar, this heritage perimeter features landscaped parks, stone pavilions, and the iconic chariot installation depicting the Gita discourse. It hosts the grand crafts exhibitions, international cultural performances, and laser water-screen shows during the annual Gita Jayanti celebrations.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sri_Krishna_Chariot_at_Brahma_Sarovar_Kurukshetra.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Integral to the sacred 48 Kos Kurukshetra Parikrama pilgrimage circuit, steeped in Vedic lore and epic Mahabharata history.", "Monumental Bronze Sri Krishna Chariot, heritage promenades, Arjun Chowk, International Gita Mahotsav cultural arena",
                "Evening walks around the illuminated perimeter offer spectacular reflections over the water; electric carts are available for seniors.", "Open 24 hours daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("29.9595"), new BigDecimal("76.8345"), 20, 2024, 1100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)", "Haryana State Tourism Statistics 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sri_Krishna_Chariot_at_Brahma_Sarovar_Kurukshetra.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        // --- 9. HIMACHAL PRADESH ---
        State himachal = createState("Himachal Pradesh", "himachal-pradesh", "STATE", "Northern", "Shimla", "Land of Gods, snow-capped Himalayan summits, and serene mountain valleys.",
                "Himachal Pradesh is a mountain paradise featuring colonial Shimla, adventure hub Manali, spiritual Dharamshala, and high-desert Spiti Valley.",
                "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Christ_Church,_Shimla.jpg", "March to June & Oct to Feb");

        createDestination(himachal, "Shimla", "shimla", "Shimla District",
                "Shimla, the capital of Himachal Pradesh, is an iconic hill station built along a scenic crescent ridge enveloped by cedar and pine forests.",
                "Once the summer capital of British India, Shimla is renowned for its grand neo-Gothic colonial architecture, vibrant pedestrian Mall Road, and sweeping Himalayan panoramas. The historic Kalka-Shimla Toy Train, a UNESCO World Heritage railway, winds through tunnels and misty slopes to connect the plains to this classic mountain haven.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Christ_Church,_Shimla.jpg",
                "March to June (Summer) and December to February (Snowfall)", "2-3 Days", "Established as the summer capital of the British Raj in 1864, Shimla served as the administrative nerve center of colonial India and hosted landmark events like the Simla Conference of 1945 and the 1972 Simla Agreement.", "The Ridge, Mall Road, Christ Church, Jakhoo Temple, Viceregal Lodge, Kalka-Shimla Toy Train",
                "Vehicular traffic is banned on the Ridge and Mall Road, so wear sturdy walking shoes. Beware of assertive monkeys near Jakhoo Temple. Reserve toy train tickets in advance.", "Open 24 hours (Mall Road shops: 10:00 AM - 9:00 PM)", "Free (Viceregal Lodge: ₹40 for Indians, ₹85 for foreigners)",
                new BigDecimal("31.1048"), new BigDecimal("77.1734"), 1, 2024, 3800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christ_Church,_Shimla.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "Manali", "manali", "Kullu District",
                "Nestled in the Beas River valley at 2,050 meters, Manali is a premier Himalayan resort town renowned for snow-clad peaks, pine groves, and alpine adventure sports.",
                "Named after the legendary sage Manu, Manali seamlessly bridges centuries-old wooden Himalayan village culture with active adventure tourism. Serving as the primary gateway to high-altitude routes via the Atal Tunnel, it attracts backpackers, skiers, and nature lovers year-round.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Manali_City.jpg",
                "October to June (Skiing in Jan-Feb, Pleasant weather in Apr-Jun)", "3-4 Days", "According to Hindu mythology, Manali is named after the lawgiver Manu, who stepped off his ark here to regenerate human life after a devastating great deluge, founding 'Manu-alaya' (the abode of Manu).", "Hidimba Devi Temple, Solang Valley, Old Manali, Jogini Waterfall, Manu Temple, Vashisht Hot Water Springs",
                "Head to Solang Valley early in the morning to beat tourist congestion and secure adventure slots. Use certified operators for adventure activities. Hire 4x4 vehicles during winter.", "Open 24 hours (Temples typically 6:00 AM - 8:00 PM)", "Free (Adventure sports priced individually by operators)",
                new BigDecimal("32.2432"), new BigDecimal("77.1892"), 2, 2024, 3500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manali_City.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "Dharamshala", "dharamshala", "Kangra District",
                "Dharamshala rests on the lower forested slopes of the Dhauladhar range, serving as the winter capital of Himachal Pradesh and a cultural center of Kangra Valley.",
                "Divided into Lower Dharamshala and Upper Dharamshala, the town is globally famous for its scenic HPCA International Cricket Stadium set against snow-capped peaks. It combines verdant cedar forests, tea gardens, and rich Pahari heritage with smooth access to Tibetan Buddhist monasteries.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dharamshala_Cricket_Stadium.jpg",
                "March to June and September to November", "2-3 Days", "Annexed by the British in 1849 as a military cantonment and district headquarters of Kangra, Dharamshala was substantially rebuilt after the catastrophic earthquake of 1905 before rising to global prominence.", "HPCA Cricket Stadium, Kangra Art Museum, War Memorial, Tea Gardens of Dharamshala, Gyuto Monastery, Dharamkot",
                "Tour the HPCA Cricket Stadium on non-match days for photography. Visit the lush tea estates located between Lower Dharamshala and Kangra. Carry light rainwear as sudden showers are frequent.", "Open 24 hours (HPCA Stadium: 9:00 AM - 5:30 PM)", "Free (HPCA Stadium visitor entry: ₹30 per person)",
                new BigDecimal("32.219"), new BigDecimal("76.3234"), 3, 2024, 2100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dharamshala_Cricket_Stadium.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "McLeod Ganj", "mcleod-ganj", "Kangra District",
                "Perched at 2,082 meters above Dharamshala, McLeod Ganj is the vibrant seat of the Central Tibetan Administration and home to the 14th Dalai Lama.",
                "Known worldwide as 'Little Lhasa', McLeod Ganj is a tranquil spiritual sanctuary where Tibetan Buddhist traditions, monks in maroon robes, and bustling handicraft markets meet pine-scented mountain air. It also serves as the launchpad for the iconic Triund ridge trek across the Dhauladhars.",
                "Spiritual", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Prayer_Wheels_at_Tsuglagkhang_Temple,_McLeod_Ganj.jpg",
                "October to June", "2-3 Days", "Named after Sir Donald McLeod, former Lieutenant Governor of Punjab, this former British garrison was revitalized in 1959 when the Government of India granted asylum to the 14th Dalai Lama and thousands of Tibetan refugees.", "Tsuglagkhang Complex (Dalai Lama Temple), Namgyal Monastery, Bhagsunag Waterfall, Triund Trek, St. John in the Wilderness Church",
                "Observe clockwise circumambulation etiquette around Buddhist temples and prayer wheels. Start early morning for the Triund hike. Try authentic Tibetan momos and butter tea.", "Open 24 hours (Tsuglagkhang Complex: 5:00 AM - 8:00 PM)", "Free entry across main temple complex",
                new BigDecimal("32.2426"), new BigDecimal("76.3213"), 4, 2024, 1900000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prayer_Wheels_at_Tsuglagkhang_Temple,_McLeod_Ganj.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "Dalhousie", "dalhousie", "Chamba District",
                "Spread across five forested hills in western Himachal Pradesh, Dalhousie is a serene colonial-era hill retreat framed by oak, pine, and deodar woods.",
                "Established in 1854 as a sanatorium for British officers and named after Lord Dalhousie, this quiet town preserves its vintage allure through Scottish church architecture, Victorian bungalows, and panoramic views of the Ravi River valley and Pir Panjal range.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dalhousie_view.jpg",
                "March to June and October to November", "2-3 Days", "Formed in 1854 after territorial negotiations with the Raja of Chamba, Dalhousie served as a tranquil convalescent haven favored by historic figures, including Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose during his freedom struggle.", "Panchpula, Dainkund Peak, St. John's Church, Subhash Baoli, Bakrota Hills, Satdhara Falls",
                "Hike up to Dainkund Peak for the highest viewpoint and natural singing breeze phenomenon. Stroll along Garam Sadak and Thandi Sadak. Carry woolens as mountain evenings turn brisk.", "Open 24 hours", "Free across the town",
                new BigDecimal("32.5387"), new BigDecimal("75.971"), 5, 2024, 1400000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dalhousie_view.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "Khajjiar", "khajjiar", "Chamba District",
                "Popularly crowned the 'Mini Switzerland of India', Khajjiar is an emerald saucer-shaped alpine meadow cradling a serene lake and ringed by dense deodar forests.",
                "Situated at an altitude of 1,920 meters between Dalhousie and Chamba, Khajjiar's lush glade features a small stream-fed lake with a floating island of weed at its center. The destination offers horse riding, zorbing, and scenic nature trails bordering the Kalatop Wildlife Sanctuary.",
                "Meadow", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Khajjiar_lake.jpg",
                "March to June and September to December", "1 Day", "Home to the 12th-century wooden Khajji Nag Temple dedicated to snake deities, Khajjiar gained international recognition in 1992 when Swiss Ambassador Willy Blazer officially designated it 'Mini Switzerland'.", "Khajjiar Lake, Khajji Nag Temple, Kalatop Khajjiar Sanctuary, Panch Pandav Tree, Golden Devi Temple, Adventure Meadows",
                "Do not litter around the fragile wetland ecosystem of Khajjiar Lake. Pre-negotiate rates for horse riding before starting. Visit early in the morning for mist-free lake reflections.", "Open 24 hours (Daylight visits recommended: 6:00 AM - 6:00 PM)", "Free entry to meadow; Kalatop Wildlife Sanctuary permit ₹250 per car",
                new BigDecimal("32.5556"), new BigDecimal("76.0654"), 6, 2024, 1200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khajjiar_lake.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "Spiti Valley", "spiti-valley", "Lahaul and Spiti District",
                "Spiti Valley is a windswept high-altitude cold desert in the trans-Himalayas, famous for dramatic barren canyons, ancient monasteries, and vibrant Tibetan heritage.",
                "Translating as 'The Middle Land' between Tibet and India, Spiti is carved by the glacial Spiti River at average altitudes above 3,800 meters. The valley features dramatic geological strata, thousand-year-old Buddhist monasteries, and high-perched settlements steeped in deep Himalayan silence.",
                "Valley", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Spiti_river_and_valley.jpg",
                "May to October (Roads through Kunzum and Rohtang open)", "5-7 Days", "Historically part of the Western Tibetan kingdom of Guge and Ladakh, Spiti has safeguarded millennial Buddhist scripture, Tethys Sea marine fossil beds, and age-old village communal traditions.", "Kaza Town, Dhankar Monastery & Lake, Hikkim Highest Post Office, Komic Village, Langza Fossil Village, Pin Valley National Park",
                "Acclimatize for at least 24-48 hours in Kaza before visiting higher settlements above 4,000 meters. Carry ample physical cash as ATMs and internet connectivity are erratic across the valley.", "Open 24 hours", "Free entry to the valley",
                new BigDecimal("32.2461"), new BigDecimal("78.0349"), 7, 2024, 450000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spiti_river_and_valley.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "Key Monastery", "key-monastery", "Lahaul and Spiti District",
                "Key Monastery is a monumental 11th-century Tibetan Buddhist monastery perched fortress-like on a conical hill at 4,166 meters overlooking the Spiti River.",
                "Serving as the largest monastery and religious training center in Spiti Valley, Key Gompa belongs to the Gelugpa (Yellow Hat) sect. Its distinctive defensive fortress design features stacked whitewashed cells, ancient prayer halls, intricate murals, and sacred thangkas housing hundreds of resident monks.",
                "Monastery", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kee_monastery_Spiti_Valley_(edited).jpg",
                "June to October", "2-3 Hours", "Founded in the 11th century by Dromtön, a key disciple of the Buddhist master Atisha, Key Monastery survived repeated attacks by Mongols, Dogras, and Sikhs, evolving its layered multi-tier fort structure.", "Ancient Assembly Hall (Dukhang), Sacred Tengyur Collection, Rooftop Balcony, Murals and Thangkas, Historic Monastery Kitchen",
                "Monks graciously welcome visitors with herbal tea in the monastery kitchen. Remove footwear before stepping inside prayer halls. Dress respectfully covering shoulders and knees.", "6:00 AM - 6:00 PM daily", "Free entry; contributions to monastery preservation fund welcome",
                new BigDecimal("32.2989"), new BigDecimal("78.0125"), 8, 2024, 320000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kee_monastery_Spiti_Valley_(edited).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "Chandratal Lake", "chandratal-lake", "Lahaul and Spiti District",
                "Chandratal, or the 'Moon Lake', is a breathtaking crescent-shaped high-altitude lake resting at 4,300 meters on the Samudra Tapu plateau in Spiti.",
                "Designated as an internationally protected Ramsar wetland, Chandratal's crystal-clear waters shift color from luminous emerald to deep cobalt blue across daylight hours. Surrounded by scree mountains and Chandra Bhaga peaks, it is an iconic stop for trekkers crossing Kunzum Pass.",
                "Lake", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chandra_Tal_Lake.jpg",
                "Late June to mid-September", "Half Day to 1 Day", "According to the Mahabharata epic, Chandratal is the sacred location where Lord Indra's celestial chariot descended to pick up Yudhishthira, the eldest Pandava brother, on his final ascent to heaven.", "Crescent Shoreline, Reflections of Chandra Bhaga Peaks, Samudra Tapu Glacier View, Stargazing, Kunzum Pass Trek",
                "Vehicles must halt at the designated parking area roughly 1-2 km away; prepare for a high-altitude walk. Pitching tents beside the water is prohibited. Carry heavy windproof thermal layers.", "Open 24 hours (Visits recommended between 6:00 AM and 5:00 PM)", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("32.4824"), new BigDecimal("77.6158"), 9, 2024, 210000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chandra_Tal_Lake.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "Rohtang Pass", "rohtang-pass", "Kullu District",
                "Rohtang Pass is an iconic high mountain pass at 3,978 meters on the Pir Panjal range, linking lush Kullu Valley with the barren Lahaul and Spiti valleys.",
                "Celebrated for dramatic year-round snowfields, biting mountain winds, and sweeping Himalayan panoramas, Rohtang is one of India's most famous passes. Historically a hazardous trade crossroads between civilizations, it is now reached via alpine switchbacks from Manali.",
                "Mountain Pass", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Rohtang_La_pass.jpg",
                "May to October (Closed during winter snowfall)", "3-4 Hours", "Meaning 'pile of corpses' in Tibetan/Ladakhi, Rohtang was named after the countless travelers who perished in historic blizzards while trying to cross between the Kullu and Lahaul valleys.", "Snow Viewpoints and Slopes, Source of the Beas River, Panoramas of Geypan Peak and Lahaul Valley, Rani Nallah Glacier, Sledge Slopes",
                "Obtain mandatory National Green Tribunal (NGT) permits online in advance before traveling. Rohtang Pass remains closed for maintenance on Tuesdays. Depart Manali early to avoid congestion.", "6:00 AM - 5:00 PM (Closed on Tuesdays)", "Permit fee ₹500 + Congestion charge ₹50 per vehicle",
                new BigDecimal("32.3716"), new BigDecimal("77.2466"), 10, 2024, 2800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rohtang_La_pass.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "Kullu Valley", "kullu-valley", "Kullu District",
                "Known as the 'Valley of the Gods' (Dev Bhumi), Kullu Valley is a fertile mountain basin shaped by the rushing Beas River, apple orchards, and deodar forests.",
                "Flanked by the Pir Panjal and Great Himalayan ranges, Kullu is famous for its terraced orchards, traditional wooden pagoda temples, and handicraft handlooms. The valley serves as the cultural heartland of regional folk traditions and is renowned for its grand International Dussehra festival.",
                "Valley", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kullu_Valley_near_Manali,_Himachal_Pradesh,_India.jpg",
                "March to June and September to November", "2-3 Days", "Celebrated in ancient epics as Kulanthapitha ('the end of the habitable world'), Kullu was governed by the ancient Pal kings who made Naggar and Sultanpur their royal seats of power.", "Raghunath Temple, Bijli Mahadev Temple, Naggar Castle & Roerich Gallery, Beas River Rafting, Kullu Shawl Weaving Centers",
                "Experience the week-long Kullu Dussehra celebrations at Dhalpur Maidan in October. Look for the authentic Handloom Mark on Kullu shawls. Try river rafting between Pirdi and Jhiri.", "Open 24 hours", "Free (Naggar Castle entry: ₹30 per person)",
                new BigDecimal("31.9579"), new BigDecimal("77.1095"), 11, 2024, 2600000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kullu_Valley_near_Manali,_Himachal_Pradesh,_India.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "Kasol", "kasol", "Kullu District",
                "Kasol is a scenic riverside hamlet in the Parvati Valley, widely celebrated as an international backpacker enclave and trekking hub in Himachal.",
                "Surrounded by pine-blanketed cliffs and granite boulders along the roaring Parvati River, Kasol is famed for its relaxed café culture, Israeli cuisine, and vibrant mountain ambiance. It serves as the primary gateway for treks heading to Kheerganga, Tosh, Chalal, and Malana.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kasol_Parvati_Valley.jpg",
                "March to June and October to November", "2-3 Days", "Formerly a remote Himalayan shepherd settlement, Kasol developed into a major bohemian stopover on the international traveler circuit in the late 20th century.", "Parvati River Banks, Chalal Village Nature Walk, Manikaran Sahib Gurudwara & Hot Springs, Tosh Village, Kheerganga Trek Base",
                "Take the suspension bridge to enjoy peaceful walks to Chalal. Visit the natural hot sulfur springs at nearby Manikaran Sahib (5 km away). Carry physical cash as network reception varies.", "Open 24 hours", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("32.01"), new BigDecimal("77.315"), 12, 2024, 1500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kasol_Parvati_Valley.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "Bir Billing", "bir-billing", "Kangra District",
                "Bir Billing is globally acclaimed as India's paragliding capital, renowned for ideal thermal wind currents and majestic Dhauladhar mountain backdrops.",
                "Set against the Dhauladhar ranges in Kangra, the destination pairs Billing at 2,400 meters as the takeoff ridge with Bir at 1,400 meters as the landing ground. Beyond extreme flying, the region is prized for its serene Tibetan settlements, ecotourism centers, and meditation monasteries.",
                "Adventure", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bir-Billing_Paragliding.jpg",
                "October to November and March to May", "2 Days", "Bir hosted the Paragliding World Cup in 2015, putting Himachal on the global adventure map, while Tibetan refugees established settlements and monasteries here starting in the 1960s under Chokling Rinpoche.", "Billing Paragliding Take-off Site, Bir Landing Ground, Chokling Monastery, Palpung Sherab Ling Monastery, Bir Tea Factory",
                "Ensure your tandem flight pilot is officially certified and registered with HP Tourism. Catch sunset at the Bir landing field. Rent a bicycle to explore peaceful monastery lanes.", "Open 24 hours (Paragliding: 7:00 AM - 5:30 PM, weather permitting)", "Free entry; tandem paragliding flights cost approximately ₹2,000 - ₹3,500",
                new BigDecimal("32.0437"), new BigDecimal("76.717"), 13, 2024, 850000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bir-Billing_Paragliding.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "Tirthan Valley", "tirthan-valley", "Kullu District",
                "Tirthan Valley is an unspoiled eco-tourism haven formed by the crystal-clear Tirthan River flowing out of the Great Himalayan National Park.",
                "Celebrated for cold glacial streams teeming with rainbow and brown trout, Tirthan Valley features quiet wooden homestays, apple orchards, and forest trails. It serves as the primary southern threshold to the untouched wilderness of the Great Himalayan National Park.",
                "Valley", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tirthan_River_Rapids.jpg",
                "March to June and October to November", "2-3 Days", "Named after the sacred alpine spring 'Tirth' at its glacial source, the valley has intentionally resisted large-scale hydro-electric dam construction to safeguard its fragile trout habitat.", "Tirthan River Trout Angling, Gushaini Village, Chhoie Waterfall, Shringi Rishi Temple, Chehni Kothi Fort Tower, Serolsar Lake Trek",
                "Obtain an angling permit from the state fisheries office before fishing for trout. Choose riverside eco-homestays in Gushaini for an authentic village experience. Pack warm clothing.", "Open 24 hours", "Free (Trout fishing license: ~₹100 per day)",
                new BigDecimal("31.6429"), new BigDecimal("77.3467"), 14, 2024, 380000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tirthan_River_Rapids.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "Great Himalayan National Park", "great-himalayan-national-park", "Kullu District",
                "A UNESCO World Heritage site, the Great Himalayan National Park protects pristine alpine ecosystems and endangered Western Himalayan biodiversity in Kullu.",
                "Spanning over 1,171 square kilometers across elevations from 1,500 to 6,000 meters, GHNP safeguards virgin oak forests, glacial valleys, and fragile alpine meadows. The national park provides refuge to rare Himalayan wildlife, including the Western tragopan, snow leopard, and Himalayan brown bear.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The_great_himalayan_national_park.jpg",
                "April to June and September to November", "2-4 Days", "Constituted in 1984 and inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage list in 2014, GHNP preserves an unfragmented biodiversity hotspot protected from road intrusions and commercial exploitation.", "Tirthan and Sainj River Valleys, Rolla Trek, Jiwa Nala Valley, Sainj Wildlife Sanctuary, Nada Alpine Meadow Trek",
                "Trekking inside the core zone requires advance permits from the forest department at Shamshi or Sai Ropa. Carry all camping gear and rations. Engage certified local guides.", "6:00 AM - 6:00 PM (Entry hours; multi-day trekking permitted with permit)", "Indian nationals: ₹100/day; Foreign nationals: ₹400/day",
                new BigDecimal("31.7333"), new BigDecimal("77.55"), 15, 2024, 140000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_great_himalayan_national_park.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "Chitkul", "chitkul", "Kinnaur District",
                "Chitkul is the last inhabited village on the Indian side of the Indo-Tibetan frontier, tucked away at 3,450 meters in the dramatic Baspa Valley.",
                "Perched on the banks of the turquoise Baspa River in Kinnaur, Chitkul is renowned for its traditional wood-and-slate homes, organic potato fields, and crystal-clear mountain air. Beyond this remote settlement lies an uninhabited frontier ridge guarded by the ITBP extending toward Tibet.",
                "Village", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chitkul,_Himachal_Pradesh.jpg",
                "May to October (Inaccessible during winter snow)", "1-2 Days", "Chitkul was an essential high-altitude trade post on the historic Hindustan-Tibet trade route, retaining indigenous Kinnauri animist-Hindu heritage centered on the worship of Goddess Mathi.", "Mathi Devi Temple (500 years old), Baspa River Pebble Banks, Hindustan Ka Aakhri Dhaba, Nagasti ITBP Post View, Baspa Valley Meadows",
                "Dress in heavy windproof woolens as temperatures drop drastically. Leather articles are strictly forbidden in the Mathi temple complex. Drive with care along narrow cliff passes.", "Open 24 hours", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("31.3503"), new BigDecimal("78.4357"), 16, 2024, 260000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chitkul,_Himachal_Pradesh.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "Kalpa", "kalpa", "Kinnaur District",
                "Perched high above the Sutlej River gorge at 2,960 meters, Kalpa offers dramatic front-row views of the sacred Kinnaur Kailash mountain massif.",
                "Framed by sprawling apple orchards and fragrant Chilgoza pine forests, Kalpa is a historic Kinnauri village famous for spectacular sunrise views over the 6,050-meter Kinnaur Kailash peak. It merges ancient Tibetan Buddhist gompas with traditional multi-tiered wood and stone Hindu temples.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kalpa,_Kinnaur_District,_Himachal_Pradesh.JPG",
                "April to October", "1-2 Days", "Formerly called Chini, Kalpa was the historic headquarters of Kinnaur and a favorite summer retreat of Lord Dalhousie, who stayed here while planning the Hindustan-Tibet Road in the 1850s.", "Kinnaur Kailash Sunrise Viewpoint, Suicide Point Cliff, Hu-Bu-Lan-Kar Monastery, Narayan-Nagini Temple Complex, Roghi Village",
                "Rise before dawn to watch the morning sunlight turn the Kinnaur Kailash Shivling from crimson to gold. Stroll through Roghi village for timber architecture. Purchase authentic Kinnauri apples.", "Open 24 hours", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("31.5367"), new BigDecimal("78.2562"), 17, 2024, 310000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalpa,_Kinnaur_District,_Himachal_Pradesh.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "Narkanda", "narkanda", "Shimla District",
                "Situated at 2,708 meters along the Hindustan-Tibet Road, Narkanda is a tranquil hill town renowned for apple and cherry orchards and winter skiing.",
                "Cradled by dense spruce, oak, and deodar woods, Narkanda commands spectacular 360-degree panoramas of the snow-capped Greater Himalayas. It is crowned by the 3,400-meter Hatu Peak, the highest summit in the Shimla hills, and acts as one of Himachal's oldest skiing destinations.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Narkanda.jpg",
                "December to March (Snow) and April to June (Summer)", "1-2 Days", "Narkanda was an important trading stage on the historic Hindustan-Tibet highway; nearby Kotgarh is where Samuel Stokes pioneered commercial apple cultivation in Himachal Pradesh in 1916.", "Hatu Peak & Hatu Mata Temple, Stokes Apple Farm at Kotgarh, Tannu Jubbar Lake, Dhumari Ski Slopes, Mahamaya Temple",
                "The 7 km road to Hatu Peak is steep and narrow; take a small 4x4 or enjoy a scenic hike. HP Tourism organizes skiing courses in January and February. Visit in spring for apple blossoms.", "Open 24 hours", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("31.2587"), new BigDecimal("77.4589"), 18, 2024, 420000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narkanda.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "Jibhi", "jibhi", "Kullu District",
                "Jibhi is a serene mountain hamlet in the Banjar Valley known for traditional kath-kuni wooden cottages, hidden waterfalls, and peaceful pine woodlands.",
                "Tucked away alongside a gushing glacial stream on the route to Jalori Pass, Jibhi has emerged as an eco-conscious mountain getaway. With untouched forest trails, traditional wood and stone craftsmanship, and minimal commercialization, it offers pure Himalayan stillness.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jibhi_Waterfall,_Jibhi,_Himachal_Pradesh.jpg",
                "March to June and September to November", "2-3 Days", "Jibhi was a historic rest stop on the vintage trans-Himalayan trail connecting Shimla and Kullu across the high Jalori Pass, guarded by ancient watchtowers like Chehni Kothi and Raghupur Fort.", "Jibhi Waterfall, Jalori Pass, Serolsar Lake Trek, Chehni Kothi Tower Temple, Raghupur Fort Ruins, Mini Thailand Rock Pools",
                "Cross Jalori Pass with an experienced mountain driver due to steep gradients. Hike the gentle 5 km trail through oak woods to sacred Serolsar Lake. Wear slip-resistant shoes near waterfalls.", "Open 24 hours (Jibhi Waterfall: 6:00 AM - 6:00 PM)", "Jibhi Waterfall entry: ₹20 per person; village areas free",
                new BigDecimal("31.6384"), new BigDecimal("77.3824"), 19, 2024, 360000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jibhi_Waterfall,_Jibhi,_Himachal_Pradesh.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(himachal, "Chamba", "chamba", "Chamba District",
                "Perched on the right bank of the Ravi River, Chamba is an ancient Himalayan capital famed for medieval stone temples, Pahari paintings, and rich handicrafts.",
                "Founded in 920 CE by Raja Sahil Varman, Chamba preserves remarkable stone shikhara temples that escaped medieval invasions thanks to protective mountain gorges. The town centers on the grassy Chaugan promenade and celebrates traditional folk heritage during the historic Minjar Fair.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Laxminarayan_temple_of_Chamba.jpg",
                "March to June and September to November", "1-2 Days", "Chamba was the royal seat of one of India's longest continuous ruling dynasties (over a thousand years), world-famous for GI-tagged Chamba Rumals (embroidered textiles) and exquisite Pahari miniature paintings.", "Lakshmi Narayan Temple Complex, Chaugan Promenade, Bhuri Singh Museum, Akhand Chandi Palace, Chamunda Devi Temple, Rang Mahal",
                "Explore Bhuri Singh Museum to see original Pahari miniature paintings and medieval copper inscriptions. Buy authentic GI-tagged Chamba chappals and rumals from local artisans.", "Open 24 hours (Temples: 6:00 AM - 8:00 PM", "Free for temples and Chaugan; Bhuri Singh Museum: ₹20 for Indians, ₹100 for foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("32.5534"), new BigDecimal("76.1258"), 20, 2024, 750000L, "TOTAL",
                "Himachal Pradesh Tourism Development Corporation (HPTDC)", "HPTDC Annual Tourism Statistical Bulletin 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laxminarayan_temple_of_Chamba.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        // --- 10. KARNATAKA ---
        State karnataka = createState("Karnataka", "karnataka", "STATE", "Southern", "Bengaluru", "One State, Many Worlds — combining tech capitals, UNESCO heritage, and Western Ghats forests.",
                "Karnataka features the magnificent royal palaces of Mysore, UNESCO ruins of Hampi, coastal Gokarna, and coffee hills of Coorg.",
                "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Stone_Chariot_at_the_Vitthala_Temple_in_Hampi.jpg", "October to March");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Hampi", "hampi", "Vijayanagara",
                "Hampi is a UNESCO World Heritage Site famed for the spectacular ruins of the medieval Vijayanagara Empire set amidst a surreal boulder-strewn landscape.",
                "Once the seat of the opulent 14th-century Vijayanagara Empire, Hampi features grand temples, royal pavilions, and ancient bazaars along the banks of the Tungabhadra River. The iconic Stone Chariot in the Vittala Temple complex stands as an enduring masterpiece of Dravidian craftsmanship. Exploring this vast open-air museum offers an immersive journey through South India's glorious architectural heritage.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Stone_Chariot_at_the_Vitthala_Temple_in_Hampi.jpg",
                "October to February", "2 to 3 days", "Serving as the capital of the Vijayanagara Empire from 1336 to 1565 CE, Hampi was one of the largest and wealthiest metropolises in the medieval world before its sack following the Battle of Talikota.", "Vittala Temple and Stone Chariot, Virupaksha Temple, Lotus Mahal, Elephant Stables, Matanga Hill, Hemakuta Hill",
                "Rent a bicycle or scooter to cover the expansive ruins easily; climb Matanga Hill early for sunrise views; carry sun protection and water.", "6:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "₹40 for Indians, SAARC and BIMSTEC visitors; ₹600 for foreign tourists; Free under 15",
                new BigDecimal("15.335"), new BigDecimal("76.46"), 1, 2024, 1800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stone_Chariot_at_the_Vitthala_Temple_in_Hampi.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Mysore Palace", "mysore-palace", "Mysuru",
                "Mysore Palace, or Amba Vilas Palace, is an iconic Indo-Saracenic royal residence known for its ornate halls, stained glass ceilings, and weekend lightings.",
                "Commissioned by the Wadiyar dynasty and completed in 1912 under British architect Henry Irwin, the palace is among India's most visited royal estates. It showcases an intricate fusion of Hindu, Muslim, Rajput, and Gothic styles, centered on magnificent ceremonial durbar halls. Every Sunday evening and during the grand Dasara festivities, the palace dazzles under roughly 100,000 glowing bulbs.",
                "Palace", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mysore_Palace_eve_01.jpg",
                "September to March", "2 to 3 hours", "The official seat of the Wadiyar maharajas who ruled the Kingdom of Mysore from 1399 to 1950, rebuilt after the original wooden palace was gutted in an 1897 fire during a royal wedding.", "Durbar Hall, Kalyana Mantapa (Marriage Pavilion), Golden Royal Throne, Gombe Thotti, Palace Illumination",
                "Visit on Sunday evening or public holidays around 7:00 PM to view the grand illumination; footwear must be deposited outside.", "10:00 AM – 5:30 PM daily (Illumination: 7:00 PM – 7:45 PM Sundays & holidays)", "₹100 for adults; ₹50 for children (10–18 yrs); ₹100 for foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("12.3052"), new BigDecimal("76.6552"), 2, 2024, 3600000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mysore_Palace_eve_01.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Coorg", "coorg", "Kodagu",
                "Known as the Scotland of India, Coorg is a mist-covered hill paradise celebrated for its aromatic coffee plantations, cascading waterfalls, and Kodava culture.",
                "Nestled along the Western Ghats, the district of Kodagu features lush undulating valleys draped in Arabica and Robusta coffee estates, cardamom groves, and pepper vines. Visitors flock to the tranquil peaks of Mandalpatti and Tadiandamol, the serene Abbey and Iruppu Falls, and the vibrant Namdroling Tibetan Monastery. The region is equally famous for its warm hospitality, martial heritage, and rich culinary traditions like Pandi curry.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/View_of_Mandalpatti_in_Coorg.jpg",
                "October to April", "2 to 3 days", "Ruled by the Haleri dynasty from the 17th century until British annexation in 1834, Coorg is the homeland of the martial Kodava community and the origin point of the sacred Kaveri River.", "Mandalpatti Viewpoint, Abbey Falls, Raja's Seat, Namdroling Monastery (Golden Temple), Talakaveri, Dubare Elephant Camp",
                "Hire a 4x4 jeep to reach the summit of Mandalpatti; sample authentic Kodava cuisine and freshly brewed plantation filter coffee.", "Open 24 hours (Attractions operate 6:00 AM – 6:00 PM)", "Free entry to district; Small charges (₹20–₹50) at specific parks and falls",
                new BigDecimal("12.4244"), new BigDecimal("75.7382"), 3, 2024, 1500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Mandalpatti_in_Coorg.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Chikmagalur", "chikmagalur", "Chikkamagaluru",
                "Chikmagalur is a serene hill station famous as the birthplace of coffee in India, crowned by Karnataka's highest summit at Mullayanagiri.",
                "Perched in the foothills of the Mullayanagiri range, Chikmagalur offers endless rolling hills enveloped in fog, pristine wilderness, and sprawling estates. Trekking trails leading up to Mullayanagiri and Baba Budangiri present sweeping panoramic vistas of the Western Ghats. It provides an ideal retreat for coffee connoisseurs, avid trekkers, and nature lovers seeking peaceful homestays amidst emerald greenery.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mullayanagiri_in_Chikmagalur_district_of_Karnataka.jpg",
                "September to March", "2 to 3 days", "Legend holds that the 16th-century Sufi saint Baba Budan smuggled seven raw coffee beans from Mocha, Yemen, and planted them in the Chandradrona Hills, introducing coffee cultivation to the Indian subcontinent.", "Mullayanagiri Peak, Baba Budangiri, Hebbe Falls, Z Point Kemmangundi, Kallathigiri Falls, Coffee Museum",
                "Start treks to Mullayanagiri early in the morning to beat heavy cloud cover and strong winds; drive carefully along winding ghat roads.", "Open 24 hours (Viewpoints accessible 6:00 AM – 6:00 PM)", "Free access to hill trails; Small parking and entry fees at certain waterfalls",
                new BigDecimal("13.3153"), new BigDecimal("75.7754"), 4, 2024, 950000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mullayanagiri_in_Chikmagalur_district_of_Karnataka.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Gokarna", "gokarna", "Uttara Kannada",
                "Gokarna is a laid-back coastal pilgrimage town where sacred Shaivite heritage meets pristine crescent beaches and rugged coastal cliff trails.",
                "Gokarna is famous for housing the sacred Atmalinga at the ancient Mahabaleshwar Temple, attracting pilgrims from across India. Flanked by rocky headlands, its distinctive shoreline features iconic sandy stretches including Om Beach—shaped like the sacred Om symbol—Kudle Beach, and Half Moon Beach. The town has evolved into a beloved bohemian haven for cliff trekking, sunset watching, and tranquil seaside relaxation.",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Om_beach_Gokarna.JPG",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 days", "Mentioned in Hindu epics like the Ramayana and Mahabharata, Gokarna is revered as Dakshina Kashi, where Lord Ganesha outsmarted the demon king Ravana to ground the divine Atmalinga.", "Om Beach, Kudle Beach, Mahabaleshwar Temple, Half Moon Beach, Paradise Beach, Mirjan Fort",
                "Embark on the beach trek connecting Kudle, Om, Half Moon, and Paradise beaches; dress modestly when visiting Mahabaleshwar Temple.", "Beaches: Open 24 hours; Temple: 6:00 AM – 12:30 PM, 5:00 PM – 8:00 PM", "Free entry to all beaches and temple complex",
                new BigDecimal("14.5479"), new BigDecimal("74.3188"), 5, 2024, 1200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Om_beach_Gokarna.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Jog Falls", "jog-falls", "Shivamogga",
                "Jog Falls is one of India's tallest un-tiered plunge waterfalls, dropping 253 meters in four dramatic cascades named Raja, Roarer, Rocket, and Rani.",
                "Fed by the Sharavathi River as it tumbles over the rugged cliffs of the Western Ghats, Jog Falls creates a thunderous spectacle shrouded in mist and rainbows. The falls split into four distinct torrents, each showcasing unique flow dynamics ranging from the majestic Raja to the foaming Rani. During peak monsoon discharges from the Linganamakki Dam, the falls merge into a breathtaking wall of roaring white water.",
                "Waterfall", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jog_falls,_Karnataka.jpg",
                "July to December", "3 to 4 hours", "A long-celebrated geographical wonder documented during the colonial era as Gersoppa Falls, Jog Falls has powered the Mahatma Gandhi Hydroelectric Project since 1948.", "Raja, Rani, Roarer, and Rocket Viewpoints, Watkins Platform, Sharavathi Valley Viewpoint, Linganamakki Dam",
                "Visit during or immediately after the monsoon for maximum volume; descending the 1,400 steps is strenuous.", "6:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "₹15 per person; ₹50 for car parking",
                new BigDecimal("14.2282"), new BigDecimal("74.8122"), 6, 2024, 1100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jog_falls,_Karnataka.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Badami Cave Temples", "badami-cave-temples", "Bagalkot",
                "The Badami Cave Temples are four exquisite 6th-century rock-cut sanctuaries carved into reddish-sandstone cliffs overlooking the historic Agastya Lake.",
                "Representing an early epoch of Indian temple architecture, this ensemble comprises four rock-cut caves dedicated respectively to Shiva, Vishnu (Caves 2 and 3), and Jain Tirthankaras (Cave 4). Cave 1 features a celebrated relief of an eighteen-armed dancing Shiva Nataraja portraying 81 classical dance mudras. The elevated location provides spectacular views over the turquoise waters of Agastya Lake and the sandstone Badami Fort.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Badami_cave_temples_karnataka.jpg",
                "October to March", "3 to 4 hours", "Carved during the Early Chalukya dynasty when Badami (ancient Vatapi) served as the imperial capital from 540 to 757 CE, with Cave 3 bearing an inscription dating directly to 578 CE under King Mangalesha.", "18-armed Nataraja carving in Cave 1, Trivikrama and Varaha reliefs in Cave 2, Vishnu Anantasayana in Cave 3, Agastya Lake, Bhutanatha Temples",
                "Watch out for monkeys around the stone stairways; visit in late afternoon for golden hour photography on red sandstone.", "9:00 AM – 5:30 PM daily", "₹25 for Indians, SAARC and BIMSTEC visitors; ₹300 for foreign tourists; Free under 15",
                new BigDecimal("15.9189"), new BigDecimal("75.6766"), 7, 2024, 650000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Badami_cave_temples_karnataka.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Pattadakal", "pattadakal", "Bagalkot",
                "Pattadakal is a UNESCO World Heritage complex on the Malaprabha River showcasing a harmonious synthesis of northern Nagara and southern Dravida architecture.",
                "Pattadakal served as the ceremonial coronation site for the Badami Chalukya kings in the 7th and 8th centuries CE. The riverside complex brings together nine Hindu temples and one Jain temple that represent the pinnacle of eclectic Chalukyan structural design. The monumental Virupaksha Temple, commissioned by Queen Lokamahadevi around 745 CE, stands as a triumph of Dravidian design and later inspired the Kailash Temple at Ellora.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Virupaksha_Temple,_Pattadakal,_Karnataka.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, Pattadakal represents high Chalukyan art where regional southern vimana designs and northern shikhara forms were developed side-by-side.", "Virupaksha Temple, Mallikarjuna Temple, Sangameshwara Temple, Papanatha Temple, Galaganatha Temple, Jain Narayana Temple",
                "Combine your visit with nearby Aihole and Badami to see the full evolution of Chalukya architecture; hire an ASI guide to decode friezes.", "6:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "₹40 for Indians, SAARC and BIMSTEC visitors; ₹600 for foreign tourists; Free under 15",
                new BigDecimal("15.949"), new BigDecimal("75.816"), 8, 2024, 420000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Virupaksha_Temple,_Pattadakal,_Karnataka.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Aihole", "aihole", "Bagalkot",
                "Revered as the cradle of Hindu temple architecture, Aihole is an open-air historical complex hosting over 120 stone temples dating from the 6th to 12th centuries.",
                "Aihole was a laboratory where ancient Chalukyan master artisans and guilds experimented with structural engineering, floor plans, and decorative carving styles. The showpiece of the complex is the apsidal 8th-century Durga Temple, featuring a distinctive rounded sanctum resembling a Buddhist chaitya and an exquisite pillared colonnade. Nearby structures like the Lad Khan Temple and Ravana Phadi cave highlight early developmental stages of Indian religious architecture.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Durga_Temple_of_Aihole.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Mentioned as Aryapura in medieval epigraphs, Aihole was the headquarters of the influential 500-member Ayyavole merchant guild and served as the testing ground where northern and southern temple designs were pioneered.", "Durga Temple Complex, Lad Khan Temple, Ravana Phadi Cave Temple, Huchappayya Matha, Meguti Jain Temple and Inscription",
                "Climb up to the Meguti Jain Temple hillock to view the famous 634 CE Aihole inscription; visit the on-site ASI Archaeological Museum.", "9:00 AM – 5:00 PM daily", "₹25 for Indians, SAARC and BIMSTEC visitors; ₹300 for foreign tourists; Free under 15",
                new BigDecimal("16.0194"), new BigDecimal("75.8828"), 9, 2024, 380000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Durga_Temple_of_Aihole.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Belur Chennakeshava Temple", "belur-chennakeshava-temple", "Hassan",
                "A UNESCO World Heritage marvel, the Chennakeshava Temple at Belur is a 12th-century Hoysala masterpiece celebrated for its intricate soapstone sculptures.",
                "Commissioned by King Vishnuvardhana in 1117 CE to commemorate his victory over the Cholas, this Vaishnavite sanctum took over a century to complete. Carved from soft chloritic schist, the temple sits on a raised star-shaped platform and features 42 exquisitely detailed bracket figures (Madanikas) depicting dancing celestial maidens. The walls, ceiling, and pillars display unsurpassed microscopic filigree craftsmanship detailing episodes from Hindu epics.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chennakeshava_Temple_at_Belur.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Inscribed as part of the UNESCO World Heritage Site 'Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas' in 2023, Belur served as the first capital of the Hoysala Empire.", "Madanika (Bracket Maidens) Carvings, Narasimha Pillar, Gravity Pillar (Deepa Stambha), Star-shaped Jagati Platform",
                "Carry a small torch to inspect the filigree carvings inside the navaranga hall; pair your trip with nearby Halebidu (16 km away).", "7:30 AM – 7:30 PM daily (Sanctum closes 1:00 PM – 2:30 PM)", "Free entry to the temple; Camera fees may apply for commercial equipment",
                new BigDecimal("13.1627"), new BigDecimal("75.8596"), 10, 2024, 750000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chennakeshava_Temple_at_Belur.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Halebidu Hoysaleswara Temple", "halebidu-hoysaleswara-temple", "Hassan",
                "Halebidu Hoysaleswara Temple is a 12th-century twin-shrine Hoysala marvel renowned for its relief friezes of thousands of unique elephants, lions, and deities.",
                "Built around 1121 CE under King Vishnuvardhana by master architect Ketamalla, this twin Shaivite temple stands as one of the apex achievements of medieval Indian stone art. The exterior base is ringed by eight continuous horizontal friezes featuring over 1,200 unique intricately sculpted elephants, followed by lions, horses, and floral scrolls. Two massive open-air pavilions house exceptionally polished monolithic statues of Nandi, the sacred bull.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Hoysaleswara_temple,_Halebidu.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Formerly named Dwarasamudra, Halebidu was the bustling capital of the Hoysalas before suffering multiple invasions by the Delhi Sultanate in the 14th century, earning UNESCO World Heritage status in 2023.", "Twin Sanctums (Hoysaleswara and Shantaleswara), Monolithic Nandi Pavilions, Relief frieze of 1,200 elephants, Dancing Ganesha carving",
                "Study the outer base friezes closely—no two elephants among the hundreds carved share the same posture; visit the adjacent ASI museum.", "6:30 AM – 6:30 PM daily", "Free entry to temple; ₹5 for ASI Museum; Free under 15",
                new BigDecimal("13.2169"), new BigDecimal("75.9942"), 11, 2024, 680000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hoysaleswara_temple,_Halebidu.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Murudeshwar Temple", "murudeshwar-temple", "Uttara Kannada",
                "Perched on Kanduka Hill surrounded by the Arabian Sea, Murudeshwar Temple is famed for the world's second-tallest Shiva statue and a towering 20-storey Raja Gopura.",
                "Dominating the coastal skyline of coastal Karnataka, Murudeshwar features a magnificent 123-foot (37 m) colossal seated statue of Lord Shiva glittering in gold and bronze hues against the ocean backdrop. The temple entrance is marked by a majestic 249-foot, 20-storey Raja Gopura equipped with a high-speed elevator taking visitors to an observation floor for bird's-eye views. The sanctum preserves a piece of the sacred Atmalinga associated with the legend of Ravana.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Lord_Shiva_statue_at_Murudeshwara.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "According to Hindu legend, when Ravana attempted to uproot the Atmalinga from Gokarna in anger, the linga cloth covering fell onto Kanduka Hill, sanctifying the spot as Murudeshwar.", "123-foot Lord Shiva Statue, 20-storey Raja Gopura with lift to 18th floor, Ancient Shiva Sanctum, Geetopadesha sculptures, Murudeshwar Beach",
                "Take the lift inside Raja Gopura to the 18th floor for panoramic ocean vistas; early morning and sunset offer cooler temperatures.", "6:00 AM – 1:00 PM, 3:00 PM – 8:30 PM daily (Raja Gopura lift: 7:00 AM – 8:00 PM)", "Free temple entry; ₹20 per person for Raja Gopura lift",
                new BigDecimal("14.0942"), new BigDecimal("74.4899"), 12, 2024, 1900000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lord_Shiva_statue_at_Murudeshwara.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Udupi Sri Krishna Temple", "udupi-sri-krishna-temple", "Udupi",
                "Udupi Sri Krishna Temple is a revered 13th-century Dvaita Vedanta pilgrimage center where the deity is worshipped through the sacred Kanakana Kindi window.",
                "Founded by the great philosopher saint Jagadguru Sri Madhvacharya in the 13th century, the Udupi Krishna Matha is one of South India's holiest Vaishnavite shrines. Uniquely, devotees do not view Lord Krishna directly, but through a nine-hole silver window known as the Navagraha Kindi, or the sacred Kanakana Kindi where Krishna miraculously turned around to bless the lower-caste saint Kanakadasa. The shrine is maintained on a rotational basis by eight monastic orders known as the Ashta Mathas.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sri_Krishna_Mutt,_Udupi.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Established in the 13th century as the foundational seat of Dvaita (dualistic) philosophy by Madhvacharya, who recovered the Balakrishna idol embedded in gopichandana clay from a shipwreck off Malpe.", "Kanakana Kindi (Window of Kanaka), Madhwa Sarovara (Holy Tank), Golden Chariot (Suvarna Ratha), Goshala, Anantheshwara Temple",
                "Men must remove their shirts and vests before entering the sanctum; partake in the temple's daily traditional satvik lunch (Maha Prasada).", "5:00 AM – 9:30 PM daily", "Free general entry; Special darshan tickets available for ₹100–₹500",
                new BigDecimal("13.3409"), new BigDecimal("74.7525"), 13, 2024, 2400000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sri_Krishna_Mutt,_Udupi.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Bandipur National Park", "bandipur-national-park", "Chamarajanagar",
                "Part of the Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve, Bandipur National Park is a premier tiger reserve celebrated for dense populations of wild elephants, gaurs, and tigers.",
                "Encompassing 874 square kilometers of dry deciduous scrub and moist teak forests, Bandipur forms one of India's most critical contiguous tiger habitats alongside Nagarhole, Mudumalai, and Wayanad. The park supports high densities of Bengal tigers, Indian leopards, Asian elephants, dholes, and gaurs. Regulated forest department bus and jeep safaris guide visitors through scenic trails where herds of spotted deer and wild tuskers frequently cross the forest tracks.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Elephant_family_bandipur_national_park,_india.jpg",
                "October to May", "1 to 2 days", "Originally established in 1931 as the Venugopala Wildlife Park by the Maharaja of Mysore, it was expanded and designated among India's first nine Project Tiger reserves in 1973.", "Forest Department Wildlife Safari, Gopalaswamy Betta Peak, Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve corridor, Kabini and Moyar river valleys",
                "Book morning (6:00–9:00 AM) or afternoon (3:30–6:00 PM) safaris online; night traffic on NH 766 is closed 9:00 PM to 6:00 AM.", "Safaris: 6:00 AM – 9:00 AM, 3:30 PM – 6:00 PM daily", "Bus Safari: ₹350–₹500; Jeep Safari: ₹3,000–₹5,000 per vehicle",
                new BigDecimal("11.6664"), new BigDecimal("76.6293"), 14, 2024, 320000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elephant_family_bandipur_national_park,_india.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Nagarhole National Park", "nagarhole-national-park", "Kodagu and Mysuru",
                "Also known as Rajiv Gandhi National Park, Nagarhole is a lush wildlife sanctuary renowned for exceptional sightings of Bengal tigers, leopards, and wild elephants.",
                "Derived from Kannada words for 'serpent river' due to its meandering streams, Nagarhole encompasses over 640 square kilometers of thick teak canopy, rosewood trees, and bamboo thickets. The reserve boasts one of the highest predator densities in Asia, featuring healthy populations of tigers, black panthers, leopards, and wild dogs (dholes). Safaris through the lush jungle provide unparalleled opportunities to encounter herds of elephants, sambar deer, and rare birdlife.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tiger_from_Nagarhole_Tiger_Reserve,_Karnataka.jpg",
                "October to May", "1 to 2 days", "Once an exclusive private hunting preserve of the Wadiyar rulers of Mysore, it was declared a wildlife sanctuary in 1955 and recognized as a Project Tiger Reserve in 1999.", "Tiger and Leopard Safaris, Kabini River Backwaters, Elephant Herds, Taraka Dam, Balle Elephant Camp",
                "Book safaris directly through Karnataka Forest Department or Jungle Lodges & Resorts; dress in muted earth tones.", "Safaris: 6:00 AM – 9:00 AM, 3:00 PM – 6:00 PM daily", "Bus Safari: ₹300–₹400; Jeep Safari: ₹3,500–₹5,500 per vehicle",
                new BigDecimal("12.0311"), new BigDecimal("76.1557"), 15, 2024, 280000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiger_from_Nagarhole_Tiger_Reserve,_Karnataka.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Kabini", "kabini", "Mysuru",
                "Kabini is an iconic eco-tourism haven along the Kabini River reservoir, world-famous for boat safaris, Asiatic elephant congregations, and melanistic black panthers.",
                "Bordering the southern perimeter of Nagarhole, Kabini centers around the reservoir formed by the dam across the sacred Kapila (Kabini) River. During summer months, receding waters create lush green pasturelands that draw the world's largest gathering of Asiatic wild elephants. Boat safaris on the tranquil backwaters offer serene encounters with marsh crocodiles, herds of elephants bathing along the shores, and elusive big cats including the renowned black leopard.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kabini_reservoir.jpg",
                "October to May (Best elephant gatherings March to May)", "2 days", "Historically a legendary hunting reserve famous for the 'Khedda' system developed in colonial times to capture wild elephants for royal and imperial service.", "Kabini River Boat Safari, Backwater Sunset Cruise, Elephant Congregations, Kabini Dam",
                "Opt for both boat safari on the reservoir and land jeep safari into Nagarhole; book eco-lodges months in advance.", "Safaris: 6:30 AM – 9:30 AM, 3:30 PM – 6:30 PM daily", "Boat Safari: ₹2,000–₹3,500 per person (often included in resort packages)",
                new BigDecimal("11.9261"), new BigDecimal("76.3512"), 16, 2024, 210000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kabini_reservoir.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Dandeli Wildlife Sanctuary", "dandeli-wildlife-sanctuary", "Uttara Kannada",
                "Dandeli Wildlife Sanctuary is an adventure and ecotourism haven in the Western Ghats known for Kali River whitewater rafting and rich birdlife including hornbills.",
                "Part of the Kali Tiger Reserve, Dandeli encompasses dense moist deciduous forests and bamboo canopies along the rapids of the Kali River. It is celebrated as a prime adventure sports destination offering thrilling Grade 2 and 3 whitewater rafting, jungle kayaking, and canyoning. The forest is also a biodiversity hotspot supporting Malabar pied hornbills, black panthers, sloth bears, and unique limestone rock formations at Syntheri Rocks.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Hornbill_at_Dandeli.jpg",
                "October to May", "2 to 3 days", "Declared a wildlife sanctuary in 1956 and later combined with Anshi National Park in 2007 to form the Kali Tiger Reserve, Dandeli preserves crucial Western Ghats evergreen wildlife corridors.", "Kali River Whitewater Rafting, Hornbill Birdwatching Trails, Syntheri Rocks, Shiroli Peak Viewpoint, Kavala Caves",
                "Confirm water release from Supa Dam in advance for rafting; carry mosquito repellent and quick-dry apparel.", "Sanctuary: 6:00 AM – 6:00 PM; Rafting sessions: 8:00 AM – 3:00 PM", "₹40 per person; Whitewater rafting: ₹600–₹1,500 per person",
                new BigDecimal("15.2417"), new BigDecimal("74.6289"), 17, 2024, 350000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hornbill_at_Dandeli.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Gol Gumbaz", "gol-gumbaz", "Vijayapura",
                "Gol Gumbaz is the 17th-century mausoleum of Mohammed Adil Shah in Vijayapura, famous for possessing the second-largest unsupported dome in the world.",
                "Completed in 1656 by architect Yaqut of Dabul, this massive Indo-Islamic tomb is crowned by a colossal dome measuring 44 meters in external diameter that stands without pillars or central supports. Inside the dome runs the legendary Whispering Gallery, where even the softest whisper echoes eleven times across the vast acoustic chamber. Four soaring seven-storey octagonal minarets flank the corners of the cubic tomb, offering sweeping city vistas from their upper tiers.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gol_Gumbaz_Landscape.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Built as the royal mausoleum of Sultan Mohammed Adil Shah, the seventh ruler of the Adil Shahi dynasty that ruled Bijapur from 1489 to 1686 until conquest by Mughal Emperor Aurangzeb.", "Whispering Gallery, Colossal Unsupported Dome, Cenotaph Chamber, Seven-Storey Minarets, Adil Shahi Museum",
                "Arrive at 6:00 AM when gates open to experience Whispering Gallery acoustics before crowds arrive; climb the corner minaret staircases.", "6:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "₹25 for Indians, SAARC and BIMSTEC visitors; ₹300 for foreigners; Free under 15",
                new BigDecimal("16.8306"), new BigDecimal("75.736"), 18, 2024, 820000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gol_Gumbaz_Landscape.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "Bengaluru Palace", "bengaluru-palace", "Bengaluru Urban",
                "Bengaluru Palace is a 19th-century royal mansion modeled after England's Windsor Castle, featuring Tudor-style battlements, turrets, and woodcarvings.",
                "Constructed in 1878 by Chamarajendra Wadiyar X, Bengaluru Palace showcases fortified towers, Gothic battlements, ivy-draped granite walls, and manicured grounds. The palace interior is resplendent with Victorian floral motifs, Portuguese tiles, hunting trophies, and classic oil paintings by Raja Ravi Varma. A grand courtyard flanked by Spanish bench mosaics and wood-carved spiral staircases transports visitors into the lavish lifestyle of the Wadiyar royals.",
                "Palace", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bengaluru_Palace_Front_part.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Acquired and expanded by the Wadiyar maharajas in 1884 to provide a grand residence during royal stays in Bangalore, embodying colonial Tudor Revival architecture.", "Durbar Hall with stained glass, Raja Ravi Varma paintings, Spanish tile courtyard, Wadiyar Royal Memorabilia",
                "Audio guide devices in multiple languages are included with foreign admission and recommended; allow time to photograph ivy facade.", "10:00 AM – 5:30 PM daily", "₹250 for Indians, ₹500 for foreigners; Camera fee: ₹300 for mobile, ₹1,000 for DSLR",
                new BigDecimal("12.9988"), new BigDecimal("77.5921"), 19, 2024, 920000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bengaluru_Palace_Front_part.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(karnataka, "St. Mary's Island", "st-marys-island", "Udupi",
                "St. Mary's Island is a National Geological Monument off Malpe, famed for its extraordinary hexagonal columnar basaltic rock formations formed millions of years ago.",
                "Formed during sub-aerial volcanic activity when Madagascar broke away from the Indian plate roughly 88 million years ago, this group of four small islands features rare vertical columnar basalt rocks. Surrounded by azure waters of the Arabian Sea and white seashell shores, the island is recognized as a prime Geo-Heritage site in India. Visitors take scenic ferry rides from Malpe Beach to explore the pristine rocky terrain and gaze upon unique natural stone pillars.",
                "Island", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Basaltic_rock_formation_in_St_Mary's_Island,_Karnataka.jpg",
                "October to May (Closed during monsoons)", "2 to 3 hours", "Designated a National Geological Monument by the Geological Survey of India in 2001, folklore holds that Portuguese explorer Vasco da Gama stepped ashore here in 1498 and erected a cross naming the isle El Padrón de Santa María.", "Columnar Hexagonal Basalt Formations, Shell Beach, Coconut Palm Groves, Ferry Ride from Malpe Harbor, Malpe Sea Walk",
                "Ferries do not operate in monsoon (June to September); swimming is strictly prohibited due to sharp basalt rocks and currents.", "9:00 AM – 5:00 PM daily (Ferry services: 9:30 AM – 4:30 PM)", "Island entry: ₹40; Ferry ticket: ₹300–₹400 per person round-trip from Malpe Beach",
                new BigDecimal("13.3808"), new BigDecimal("74.6719"), 20, 2024, 480000L, "TOTAL",
                "Karnataka Tourism (KSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basaltic_rock_formation_in_St_Mary%27s_Island,_Karnataka.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        // --- 11. KERALA ---
        State kerala = createState("Kerala", "kerala", "STATE", "Southern", "Thiruvananthapuram", "God's Own Country — emerald backwaters, palm beaches, and Ayurvedic wellness.",
                "Kerala is world-famous for its tranquil Alappuzha backwaters, tea-laden hills of Munnar, wildlife in Periyar, and Kathakali culture.",
                "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tea_Gardens_in_Munnar.jpg", "September to March");

        createDestination(kerala, "Munnar", "munnar", "Idukki",
                "Munnar is a picturesque hill station nestled in the Western Ghats of Kerala, renowned for its rolling tea plantations, mist-blanketed hills, and cool climate.",
                "Perched at an elevation of around 1,600 meters in the Idukki district, Munnar was once the summer resort of the British administration in South India. It is famed for expansive tea estates, cascading waterfalls, and the endangered Nilgiri tahr inhabiting Eravikulam National Park. Visitors flock here for scenic treks, tea tasting experiences, and peaceful high-altitude vistas.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tea_Gardens_in_Munnar.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 days", "Developed as a major tea plantation hub during the British Raj in the late 19th century, featuring the historic Kannan Devan Hills estate.", "Eravikulam National Park, Mattupetty Dam, Anamudi Peak, Tata Tea Museum, Top Station",
                "Carry warm clothing for chilly evenings, book Eravikulam National Park passes online in advance, and hire an experienced driver for winding roads.", "Open 24 hours (Attractions typically 9:00 AM – 5:00 PM)", "Free entry to town; Eravikulam National Park: ₹200 for Indians",
                new BigDecimal("10.0889"), new BigDecimal("77.0595"), 1, 2024, 2200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tea_Gardens_in_Munnar.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Alappuzha Backwaters", "alappuzha-backwaters", "Alappuzha",
                "Alappuzha Backwaters, famously dubbed the 'Venice of the East', is an intricate network of serene lagoons, canals, and lakes fringed by coconut palms.",
                "World-renowned for overnight cruises in traditional thatched houseboats (kettuvallams), the backwaters offer an intimate glimpse into tranquil rural life along the waterways. The region is also celebrated for the annual Nehru Trophy Boat Race held on Punnamada Lake. Gliding past paddy fields lying below sea level provides one of Kerala's most iconic travel experiences.",
                "Backwaters", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Make_Way_For_The_Houseboat!_-_Backwaters_of_Alappuzha.jpg",
                "November to February", "1 to 2 days", "Historically a vital maritime trading center founded by Raja Kesavadas in 1762, famous for spice, coir, and coconut oil trade.", "Punnamada Lake, Houseboat Cruises, Kuttanad Below-Sea-Level Paddy Fields, Alappuzha Beach & Lighthouse, Pathiramanal Island",
                "Book government-approved DTPC houseboats to avoid touts; opt for day cruises or overnight stays; sample authentic Karimeen Pollichathu.", "Open 24 hours (Houseboat check-in typically 12:00 PM to 9:00 AM next day)", "Canals free; Shikara boats ₹600–₹1,000/hr; Houseboats ₹7,000–₹20,000/night",
                new BigDecimal("9.4981"), new BigDecimal("76.3388"), 2, 2024, 2500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Make_Way_For_The_Houseboat!_-_Backwaters_of_Alappuzha.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Fort Kochi", "fort-kochi", "Ernakulam",
                "Fort Kochi is a charming seaside heritage precinct steeped in colonial history, featuring monumental Chinese fishing nets, colonial architecture, and a thriving art culture.",
                "Fort Kochi showcases a unique tapestry of Portuguese, Dutch, and British cultural influences dating back to the 16th century. It is home to India's oldest European church, St. Francis Church, where Vasco da Gama was originally buried. Today, its cobblestone streets, vibrant cafes, and prestigious Kochi-Muziris Biennale attract travelers and culture enthusiasts from across the globe.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chinese_fishing_nets_in_Fort_Kochi.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 days", "First European colonial settlement in India, ceded to the Portuguese in 1503, later captured by the Dutch in 1663 and the British in 1795.", "Chinese Fishing Nets, St. Francis Church, Santa Cruz Cathedral Basilica, Fort Kochi Beach, Jew Town and Paradesi Synagogue",
                "Explore the neighborhood on rented bicycles or on foot; visit fishing nets at sunset; attend a traditional Kathakali performance.", "Open 24 hours (Churches and museums typically 9:00 AM – 5:00 PM)", "Free entry to historic precinct; nominal museum fees ₹10–₹50 where applicable",
                new BigDecimal("9.9658"), new BigDecimal("76.2421"), 3, 2024, 2100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinese_fishing_nets_in_Fort_Kochi.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Wayanad", "wayanad", "Wayanad",
                "Wayanad is a verdant highland district blessed with mist-laden peaks, dense rainforests, spice plantations, and ancient prehistoric rock art.",
                "Set along the southern tip of the Deccan plateau in the Western Ghats, Wayanad is celebrated for its pristine biodiversity and tribal heritage. Major highlights include the Neolithic carvings of Edakkal Caves and Banasura Sagar Dam, India's largest earthen dam. Adventure seekers and nature lovers frequent its wildlife sanctuaries, sprawling spice plantations, and misty trekking trails.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Banasura_sagar_Dam.jpg",
                "October to May", "2 to 3 days", "Home to prehistoric Neolithic petroglyphs at Edakkal Caves dating back to 6000 BCE, and the historic guerrilla battlegrounds of Pazhassi Raja against the British.", "Banasura Sagar Dam, Edakkal Caves, Chembra Peak, Wayanad Wildlife Sanctuary, Soochipara Falls",
                "Trekking Chembra Peak requires forest department permission; carry leech repellents during monsoon; purchase local wild honey.", "Open 24 hours (Attractions generally 9:00 AM – 5:00 PM)", "Free entry to district; individual attraction fees range ₹30 to ₹150",
                new BigDecimal("11.6854"), new BigDecimal("76.132"), 4, 2024, 1400000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banasura_sagar_Dam.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Thekkady", "thekkady", "Idukki",
                "Thekkady is an enchanting forest retreat renowned as the spice capital of Kerala and the lively tourist gateway to Periyar Tiger Reserve.",
                "Surrounded by aromatic plantations of cardamom, pepper, and cinnamon, Thekkady offers immersive ecotourism activities ranging from bamboo rafting to spice plantation walks. The town acts as the primary staging area for lake safaris across Periyar Lake, where wild animals congregate along the banks. It is also an acclaimed center for authentic Kalaripayattu martial arts and Kathakali cultural performances.",
                "Nature & Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Thekkady_Lake.jpg",
                "September to April", "1 to 2 days", "Historic spice route crossroads connecting Tamil Nadu and Travancore; conserved as a game sanctuary in 1934 by the Maharaja of Travancore.", "Periyar Lake Boating, Kumily Spice Markets, Kadathanadan Kalari Centre, Murikkady Spice Plantations, Pandikuzhi Viewpoint",
                "Book KTDC Periyar lake boat safari tickets online days ahead; purchase authentic spices from cooperative stores in Kumily.", "Open 24 hours (Plantation tours and shows 9:00 AM – 8:00 PM)", "Free entry to town; Spice tours ₹100–₹200; Cultural shows ₹200–₹300",
                new BigDecimal("9.6031"), new BigDecimal("77.1615"), 5, 2024, 1100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thekkady_Lake.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Periyar National Park", "periyar-national-park", "Idukki",
                "Periyar National Park is one of India's premier tiger and elephant reserves, set around a scenic artificial lake deep within the Cardamom Hills.",
                "Encompassing an expansive 925 square kilometers of tropical evergreen and moist deciduous forests, Periyar is celebrated for its rich populations of Asian elephants, Bengal tigers, and rare lion-tailed macaques. A signature experience is taking a boat safari on Periyar Lake to observe herds of wild elephants bathing along the water's edge. Guided jungle treks, bamboo rafting, and night patrols led by local tribal wardens offer thrilling conservation-focused exploration.",
                "Wildlife Sanctuary", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Periyar_National_Park_01.jpg",
                "October to March", "Full Day (6-8 Hours)", "Established in 1895 with the construction of Mullaperiyar Dam, designated as Nellikkampatty Game Sanctuary in 1934, and declared a Project Tiger Reserve in 1978.", "Periyar Lake Boat Safari, Bamboo Rafting, Border Hiking, Tiger Trail Trekking, Elephant Herds and Bird Watching",
                "Early morning boat safaris (7:30 AM) offer the best chances for wildlife sightings; wear neutral forest colors.", "6:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "Adults: ₹45 (Indians), ₹500 (Foreigners); Boating: ₹255–₹300 extra",
                new BigDecimal("9.4679"), new BigDecimal("77.1425"), 6, 2024, 650000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Periyar_National_Park_01.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Varkala Cliff", "varkala-cliff", "Thiruvananthapuram",
                "Varkala Cliff is a dramatic geological formation where striking red laterite cliffs stand adjacent to the azure waters of the Arabian Sea.",
                "Recognized as a geological monument by the Geological Survey of India, the Varkala Formation hosts a vibrant cliff-top promenade lined with bohemian cafes, yoga studios, and handicraft boutiques. Below lies Papanasam Beach, famed for its golden sands and sacred waters believed to wash away sins. Sunsets viewed from the edge of the cliff are considered among the most breathtaking spectacles in South India.",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Varkala_Cliff.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 days", "Declared a National Geological Monument; adjacent to the 2,000-year-old Janardhanaswamy Temple, an ancient Vaishnavite pilgrimage site.", "North Cliff Promenade, Papanasam Beach, Janardhanaswamy Temple, Varkala Helipad, Sivagiri Mutt",
                "Take care along unfenced cliff edges; try water sports like surfing and paragliding during season; relax in open-air seafood cafes.", "Open 24 hours daily", "Free entry to cliff and public beach",
                new BigDecimal("8.7339"), new BigDecimal("76.7032"), 7, 2024, 1300000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Varkala_Cliff.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Kovalam Beach", "kovalam-beach", "Thiruvananthapuram",
                "Kovalam Beach is an internationally acclaimed coastal paradise characterized by three crescent-shaped golden beaches separated by rocky outcroppings.",
                "Placed firmly on the international tourism map in the 1970s, Kovalam is anchored by Lighthouse Beach with its iconic striped Vizhinjam Lighthouse dating back to 1972. The calm, shallow waters of Hawah Beach and Samudra Beach make it ideal for swimming, catamaran cruising, and sunbathing. The beachside promenade is filled with world-class Ayurvedic rejuvenation resorts, seafood restaurants, and surf clubs.",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kovalam_Beach.JPG",
                "September to March", "1 to 2 days", "Brought to prominence by Regent Maharani Sethu Lakshmi Bayi of Travancore in the 1920s, and later popular on the 1970s Hippie Trail.", "Vizhinjam Lighthouse, Lighthouse Beach, Hawah Beach, Samudra Beach, Ayurvedic Massage Centres",
                "Climb to the top of Vizhinjam Lighthouse for panoramic ocean views; swim only within flagged safe zones.", "Open 24 hours (Lighthouse: 10:00 AM – 12:45 PM, 2:00 PM – 5:45 PM, closed Mondays)", "Beach access free; Lighthouse entry ₹20 for Indians, ₹50 for Foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("8.4004"), new BigDecimal("76.9787"), 8, 2024, 1800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kovalam_Beach.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Athirappilly Waterfalls", "athirappilly-waterfalls", "Thrissur",
                "Athirappilly Waterfalls, celebrated as the 'Niagara of South India', is an 80-foot-tall, 330-foot-wide cascade roaring through dense Vazhachal rainforests.",
                "Originating from the upper reaches of the Western Ghats along the Chalakudy River, Athirappilly is Kerala's largest and most majestic waterfall. The falls are cradled by one of the state's richest riparian biodiversity reserves, home to endangered hornbills and elephants. A stone path leads visitors through lush greenery right to the base of the thundering gorge, offering awe-inspiring spray and cinematic views.",
                "Waterfall", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Athirappilly_waterfalls.jpg",
                "July to November", "3 to 5 hours", "Celebrated filming location for major Indian blockbusters (e.g., Baahubali, Dil Se, Raavan) and part of ancient riparian forest ecosystems.", "Athirappilly Waterfall Base Trek, Vazhachal Falls, Charpa Falls, Chalakudy River Riparian Ecosystem",
                "Wear shoes with good grip for the slippery trek to the base; keep rainproof covers for electronics; beware of wild monkeys.", "8:00 AM – 5:00 PM daily", "₹50 for Indian adults, ₹10 for children, ₹150 for Foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("10.2851"), new BigDecimal("76.5698"), 9, 2024, 1500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athirappilly_waterfalls.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Kumarakom", "kumarakom", "Kottayam",
                "Kumarakom is an enchanting cluster of tranquil islands on Vembanad Lake, famed for luxury eco-resorts, backwater lagoons, and a prolific bird sanctuary.",
                "Set against the backdrop of India's longest lake, Kumarakom is a premier destination for responsible tourism and slow-paced wellness travel. The Kumarakom Bird Sanctuary spreads across 14 acres, attracting migratory birds such as Siberian storks, egrets, and darters. Travelers can embark on peaceful canoe rides through narrow mangrove canals, indulge in traditional Ayurvedic therapies, and enjoy exquisite freshwater delicacies.",
                "Backwaters", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kumarakom.JPG",
                "September to March (Migratory birds: Nov–Feb)", "1 to 2 days", "Developed from reclaimed wetlands in the 19th century by Alfred George Baker, an English missionary whose historic bungalow remains a heritage icon.", "Kumarakom Bird Sanctuary, Vembanad Lake, Aruvikkuzhi Waterfall, Bay Island Driftwood Museum, Canal Canoe Rides",
                "Visit bird sanctuary at sunrise (6:00 AM) for prime birdwatching; rent non-motorized wooden canoes for village canals.", "Open 24 hours (Bird Sanctuary: 6:00 AM – 6:00 PM)", "Town free; Bird Sanctuary ₹50 for Indians, ₹100 for Foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("9.6175"), new BigDecimal("76.4301"), 10, 2024, 850000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kumarakom.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Bekal Fort", "bekal-fort", "Kasaragod",
                "Bekal Fort is the largest and best-preserved historic coastal fortress in Kerala, standing proudly atop a headland over the rolling waves of the Arabian Sea.",
                "Sprawled over 40 majestic seaside acres in Kasaragod district, Bekal Fort was built primarily for coastal defense rather than administrative residence. Its unique architectural hallmarks include a towering central observation tower with sweeping views of the coastline, underground water tanks, and zigzag entry gateways. The fort gained iconic pop-culture fame as the scenic setting for Mani Ratnam's movie song 'Uyire' / 'Tu Hi Re'.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bekal_Fort,_Kerala.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Built around 1650 CE by Shivappa Nayaka of the Keladi Nayakas, later captured by Hyder Ali, Tipu Sultan, and eventually the British East India Company.", "Central Observation Tower, Seaside Ramparts, Magazine and Water Reservoir, Mukhyaprana Hanuman Temple, Bekal Beach Walkway",
                "Visit during late afternoon to catch the sunset over the sea and avoid midday heat; wear comfortable walking shoes.", "8:00 AM – 5:30 PM daily", "₹25 for Indians, ₹300 for Foreigners; Free for children under 15",
                new BigDecimal("12.3926"), new BigDecimal("75.0315"), 11, 2024, 720000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bekal_Fort,_Kerala.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Guruvayur Temple", "guruvayur-temple", "Thrissur",
                "Guruvayur Temple, known as the 'Bhuloka Vaikunta' (Earthly Abode of Vishnu), is one of the most revered and historic Krishna temples in India.",
                "Dedicated to Guruvayurappan (Lord Krishna in his infant form with four arms), this ancient shrine attracts millions of devotees seeking blessings, wedding ceremonies, and child-naming rites. The temple complex features exquisite mural paintings, an imposing deepastambham (pillar of lamps), and traditional temple music. Nearby Punnathur Kotta houses more than 40 temple elephants in an expansive historic palace compound.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Guruvayoor_Temple_1.jpg",
                "October to March", "3 to 5 hours", "Mentioned in 14th-century Tamil texts; idol legendarily installed by Brihaspati (Guru) and Vayu after the submersion of Dwarka.", "Guruvayurappan Sanctum Sanctorum, Punnathur Kotta Elephant Sanctuary, Deepastambham, Mammiyoor Shiva Temple",
                "Strict traditional dress code applies (mundu/dhoti for men, bare chest; saree/salwar for women); only Hindus permitted inside.", "3:00 AM – 1:30 PM, 4:30 PM – 9:15 PM daily", "Free general entry; special darshan passes available from ₹1,000 for fast track",
                new BigDecimal("10.5946"), new BigDecimal("76.0407"), 12, 2024, 4800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guruvayoor_Temple_1.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Sree Padmanabhaswamy Temple", "sree-padmanabhaswamy-temple", "Thiruvananthapuram",
                "Sree Padmanabhaswamy Temple is an awe-inspiring architectural marvel blending Chera and Dravidian styles, celebrated as the world's richest religious shrine.",
                "Situated in the heart of Kerala's capital, the temple is dedicated to Lord Vishnu reclining in the divine Anantha Shayanam posture upon the serpent Adishesha. Renovated in its monumental present form by Maharaja Marthanda Varma in the 18th century, it is famous for its 100-foot-tall, seven-tier sculpted gopuram and corridor of 365 carved granite pillars. In 2011, vast subterranean vaults revealed historic treasures of gold, jewels, and antique artifacts worth billions of dollars.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sree_Padmanabhaswamy_Temple.jpg",
                "October to February", "2 to 3 hours", "Celebrated in ancient Sangam literature and Divya Desams; in 1750, Maharaja Marthanda Varma consecrated his entire kingdom to the deity through the historic Thrippadidanam.", "16th-Century Gopuram, Anantha Shayanam Sanctum, Padmatheertham Sacred Tank, Ottakkal Mandapam, Kuthira Malika Palace",
                "Strict dress code enforced (dhoti/mundu for men; sarees for women); mobile phones and leather items must be deposited in lockers.", "3:30 AM – 12:00 PM, 5:00 PM – 8:30 PM (sessions vary)", "Free general entry; Special darshan tickets ₹150 to ₹250",
                new BigDecimal("8.4828"), new BigDecimal("76.9436"), 13, 2024, 3900000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sree_Padmanabhaswamy_Temple.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Silent Valley National Park", "silent-valley-national-park", "Palakkad",
                "Silent Valley National Park is a pristine, undisturbed expanse of tropical rainforest in the Kundali Hills, renowned for its rare lion-tailed macaque population.",
                "Nestled within the Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve, Silent Valley represents one of the last undisturbed tracts of South Western Ghats montane rain forests. The park earned its name from the intriguing absence of buzzing cicadas typically heard in dense jungle foliage. It is bisected by the crystal-clear Kunthi River, enveloped in mist, and celebrated worldwide as the triumphant focal point of India's pioneering 'Save Silent Valley' ecological movement.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Silent_Valley_National_Park.jpg",
                "November to April", "5 to 6 hours", "Epicenter of the landmark 1970s 'Save Silent Valley' movement which halted a hydroelectric dam to protect virgin biodiversity, declared a national park by Indira Gandhi in 1984.", "Kunthi River Suspension Bridge, Watchtower Views of Valley Canopy, Lion-Tailed Macaque Habitats, Sairandhri Forest Trail",
                "Entry requires forest department safari jeeps booked from Mukkali base camp; private vehicles restricted; plastic banned.", "8:00 AM – 1:00 PM (safari entry window; park closes by 5:00 PM)", "₹50 entry per person; Forest Safari Jeep costs approx. ₹2,000–₹2,500 per vehicle",
                new BigDecimal("11.1333"), new BigDecimal("76.4333"), 14, 2024, 110000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silent_Valley_National_Park.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Marari Beach", "marari-beach", "Alappuzha",
                "Marari Beach is a peaceful, secluded paradise of soft white sands and swaying coconut groves, preserving the authentic charm of a traditional fishing village.",
                "Located near Mararikulam, this tranquil alternative to bustling commercial beaches offers miles of clean coast where visitors can witness local fishermen setting out in colorful traditional wooden boats. It is acclaimed for eco-conscious boutique beach resorts, ayurvedic wellness retreats, and hammock-strewn relaxation under coconut canopies. The shallow shoreline makes it an idyllic sanctuary for peaceful walks and dramatic Arabian Sea sunsets.",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Marari_Beach_Shoreline.JPG",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 days", "Centuries-old artisanal fishing hamlet of Mararikulam, embodying traditional coir making, canoe fishing, and coastal lifestyle heritage.", "Marari Fishermen Village, Palm-Fringed Shoreline, Ayurvedic Beach Wellness Centers, Sunset Beach Walks",
                "Ideal for a quiet digital detox; avoid high midday sun; respect the local fishing community's nets and boats.", "Open 24 hours daily", "Free entry to the public beach",
                new BigDecimal("9.6006"), new BigDecimal("76.2974"), 15, 2024, 580000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marari_Beach_Shoreline.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Vagamon", "vagamon", "Idukki",
                "Vagamon is a tranquil hill station celebrated for velvety rolling green meadows, aromatic pine forests, misty tea valleys, and paragliding adventures.",
                "Tucked away on the Kottayam-Idukki border at an altitude of 1,100 meters, Vagamon offers unhurried serenity away from commercial tourist crowds. The hill station is characterized by three distinct peaks—Thangal Para, Murugan Para, and Kurisumala—signifying communal harmony among Islam, Hinduism, and Christianity. Thrill-seekers flock to Vagamon for international paragliding fests, off-road jeep safaris, and nature walks through whisper-quiet pine valleys.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Vagamon.JPG",
                "September to May", "1 to 2 days", "Originally surveyed and developed as tea estates by British planters in the early 20th century, later made famous as a spiritual retreat by Kurisumala Ashram.", "Vagamon Pine Forest, Vagamon Rolling Meadows (Motta Kunnu), Kurisumala Ashram, Thangal Para, Vagamon Lake",
                "Features India's longest cantilever glass bridge at Vagamon Adventure Park; carry light jackets for sudden mist and fog.", "Open 24 hours (Pine forest & parks: 8:30 AM – 6:00 PM)", "Free entry to town; Pine Forest & Meadows ₹25–₹50; Glass Bridge ₹200",
                new BigDecimal("9.6892"), new BigDecimal("76.9056"), 16, 2024, 640000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vagamon.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Ponmudi", "ponmudi", "Thiruvananthapuram",
                "Ponmudi, or the 'Golden Peak', is an enchanting hill station in the Western Ghats famous for its cool climate, misty ridges, and 22 thrilling hairpin bends.",
                "Perched at an elevation of 1,100 meters, Ponmudi is a favorite weekend getaway located just 55 kilometers north-east of Thiruvananthapuram. The winding drive up through rubber and tea plantations features dramatic panoramic valley views and lush forest corridors rich in endemic bird species. The crest of the hills offers breezy grassland walks, natural spring-fed streams, and access to the serene Peppara Wildlife Sanctuary.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ponmudi_hills_1.jpg",
                "October to March", "4 to 6 hours", "Ancient tribal territory of the Kani tribe and established as a high-altitude tea plantation retreat during the colonial British era.", "Ponmudi Upper Sanatorium Crest, 22 Hairpin Bends Road, Kallar River & Meenmutty Waterfalls, Peppara Sanctuary",
                "The road closes to entry by late afternoon; fill your fuel tank before beginning the ascent as there are no fuel stations at the summit.", "8:00 AM – 4:00 PM (Checkpoint entry hours)", "₹30–₹40 per person; Vehicle parking fees ₹20–₹50",
                new BigDecimal("8.7601"), new BigDecimal("77.1167"), 17, 2024, 450000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ponmudi_hills_1.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Kappad Beach", "kappad-beach", "Kozhikode",
                "Kappad Beach is a scenic, rock-studded historic shoreline where Portuguese navigator Vasco da Gama first set foot in India in 1498.",
                "Situated 16 kilometers from Kozhikode, Kappad (historically Kappakkadavu) is a monumental landmark that reshaped world maritime trade routes by inaugurating the sea route from Europe to India. A stone monument commemorates da Gama's historic landing alongside an 800-year-old rock-perched temple overlooking the sea. The beach has received the prestigious international Blue Flag certification for its spotless sands, eco-friendly amenities, and immaculate promenade.",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kappad_beach_kerala.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Landing site of Vasco da Gama on 20 May 1498 with 170 men in three ships, marking the start of direct European contact and the Age of Discovery in India.", "Vasco da Gama Monument, Blue Flag Certified Eco-Beach Promenade, Ancient Rock-top Temple, Tide Pools",
                "One of Kerala's cleanest beaches; visit in late afternoon to explore rocks and monument; sample Malabar street snacks nearby.", "6:00 AM – 7:00 PM daily", "Free public entry; Blue Flag designated amenity area charges ₹20–₹25",
                new BigDecimal("11.3853"), new BigDecimal("75.7197"), 18, 2024, 520000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kappad_beach_kerala.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Thusharagiri Waterfalls", "thusharagiri-waterfalls", "Kozhikode",
                "Thusharagiri Waterfalls, poetically translating to 'Snow-capped Mountain', is a cascading trio of forest falls famous for scenic trekking and whitewater rafting.",
                "Formed by the gushing tributaries of the Chalippuzha River as it descends the Western Ghats, Thusharagiri is surrounded by lush spice plantations of rubber, arecanut, and ginger. The waterfall complex consists of Erattumukku, Mazhavil Chatam, and Thumbitullum Para, creating a perpetual mist that gives the hills their snowy moniker. It serves as a favored destination for outdoor enthusiasts seeking rainforest treks, suspension bridge walks, and rock climbing.",
                "Waterfall", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Thusharagiri_Falls.jpg",
                "September to February", "3 to 4 hours", "Longstanding traditional gateway for high-range spice cultivation and part of ancient indigenous tribal trails traversing the Malabar rainforest.", "Erattumukku Falls, Mazhavil Chatam (Rainbow Falls), Thumbitullum Para, Hanging Suspension Bridge, Chalippuzha River Trek",
                "Hire a registered local guide if attempting the full 5-km trek through the forest to the highest falls; carry sturdy water shoes.", "8:00 AM – 5:00 PM daily", "₹30 for adults, ₹15 for children; camera charges extra",
                new BigDecimal("11.4728"), new BigDecimal("76.0531"), 19, 2024, 340000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thusharagiri_Falls.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(kerala, "Jatayu Earth's Center", "jatayu-earths-center", "Kollam",
                "Jatayu Earth's Center is home to the world's largest bird sculpture, an architectural wonder honoring the mythical bird Jatayu atop a soaring rocky hill.",
                "Perched at 1,000 feet above sea level in Chadayamangalam, this 65-acre ecotourism marvel was conceived and sculpted by renowned artist and filmmaker Rajiv Anchal. The colossal concrete sculpture measures 200 feet long, 150 feet wide, and 70 feet high, symbolizing women's safety, chivalry, and ecological conservation. Visitors access the hilltop via a state-of-the-art Swiss cable car (ropeway) or scenic walkway, and can explore a multi-dimensional museum inside the bird's body.",
                "Monument", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jatayu_Earth_Centre.jpg",
                "September to March", "3 to 4 hours", "According to the epic Ramayana, Jatayu fell on this massive rock (Jatayupara) after valiantly fighting the demon king Ravana to rescue Goddess Sita.", "World's Largest Bird Sculpture, Scenic Cable Car / Ropeway Ride, Jatayu Audio-Visual Museum, Hilltop Viewpoint",
                "Book combo tickets (Cable Car + Sculpture entry) online to skip ticketing queues; midday can be very sunny on the rock summit.", "10:00 AM – 5:30 PM (Weekdays), 10:00 AM – 6:00 PM (Weekends)", "₹450–₹500 + GST for Cable Car and Jatayu Sculpture entry; Adventure activities extra",
                new BigDecimal("8.8681"), new BigDecimal("76.8679"), 20, 2024, 980000L, "TOTAL",
                "Kerala Tourism (KTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jatayu_Earth_Centre.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        // --- 12. MADHYA PRADESH ---
        State mp = createState("Madhya Pradesh", "madhya-pradesh", "STATE", "Central", "Bhopal", "Heart of Incredible India — tiger reserves, UNESCO Khajuraho, and Sanchi Stupa.",
                "Madhya Pradesh features Khajuraho temples, Bandhavgarh & Kanha tiger reserves, and Sanchi Stupa.",
                "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kandariya_Mahadev_Temple_Khajuraho.jpg", "October to March");

        createDestination(mp, "Khajuraho Temples", "khajuraho-temples", "Chhatarpur",
                "A UNESCO World Heritage Site renowned for nagara-style Hindu and Jain temples built between 950 and 1050 CE by the Chandela dynasty, celebrated for intricate erotic sculptures and architectural magnificence.",
                "The Khajuraho Group of Monuments represents one of the pinnacle achievements of medieval Indian temple architecture. Built during the Chandela dynasty, these temples celebrate various aspects of human existence, spirituality, and cosmic rhythm across three distinct geographic clusters.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kandariya_Mahadev_Temple_Khajuraho.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 days", "Capital and spiritual center of the Chandela dynasty between the 10th and 11th centuries; survived destruction during medieval invasions due to remoteness.", "Kandariya Mahadeva Temple, Lakshmana Temple, Vishvanatha Temple, Western Group of Temples, Matangeshwar Temple, Raneh Falls",
                "Rent an audio guide or hire a government-licensed guide at the Western Group; attend evening sound and light show; dress modestly.", "Sunrise to Sunset (approx. 6:00 AM – 6:00 PM)", "Indians ₹40; Foreigners ₹600; Children under 15 free (Western Group)",
                new BigDecimal("24.8524"), new BigDecimal("79.9199"), 1, 2024, 1400000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kandariya_Mahadev_Temple_Khajuraho.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Sanchi Stupa", "sanchi-stupa", "Raisen",
                "One of the oldest stone structures in India and a UNESCO World Heritage Site, originally commissioned by Emperor Ashoka in the 3rd century BCE to enshrine Buddhist relics.",
                "The Great Stupa at Sanchi is an ancient Buddhist complex that serves as a supreme testament to Mauryan and Satavahana art. Its hemispherical dome, crowned by the chhatri and surrounded by four grand toranas (ceremonial gateways) carved with Jataka tales, illustrates Buddhist philosophy and historical narratives.",
                "Archaeological", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sanchi_Stupa,_Sanchi.jpg",
                "October to March", "3 to 4 hours", "Commissioned by Emperor Ashoka the Great; expanded under the Shunga and Satavahana dynasties; one of India's best-preserved early Buddhist sanctuaries.", "Great Stupa (Stupa 1), Toranas (ornate gateways), Ashoka Pillar, Stupa 2 and 3, Archaeological Museum Sanchi, Buddhist viharas",
                "Visit early morning or late afternoon to walk barefoot on stone pathways; hire an ASI guide to decode torana carvings; museum closed on Fridays.", "6:30 AM to 6:30 PM (Daily)", "Indians ₹40; Foreigners ₹600; Children under 15 free",
                new BigDecimal("23.4794"), new BigDecimal("77.7397"), 2, 2024, 580000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sanchi_Stupa,_Sanchi.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Bhimbetka Rock Shelters", "bhimbetka-rock-shelters", "Raisen",
                "A UNESCO World Heritage Site featuring prehistoric rock shelters with cave paintings spanning Upper Paleolithic to medieval eras, depicting animals, hunts, and daily prehistoric life.",
                "Situated in the foothills of the Vindhyan Mountains, Bhimbetka exhibits the earliest traces of human life on the Indian subcontinent. The natural sandstone rock shelters house vibrant rock paintings in red and white mineral pigments spanning over 30,000 years, providing exceptional anthropological insights.",
                "Archaeological", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Rock_Shelter_15,_Bhimbetka_02.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Discovered in 1957 by archaeologist V. S. Wakankar; contains over 750 rock shelters spanning from the Paleolithic and Mesolithic ages through historical periods.", "Auditorium Cave, Zoo Rock (Shelter 15), Boar Rock, Turtle Rock, ancient rock art galleries",
                "Wear sturdy walking shoes for rocky pathways; carry drinking water; combine with Bhojpur Temple on a day trip from Bhopal.", "7:00 AM to 6:00 PM (Daily)", "Indians ₹25; Foreigners ₹500; Light vehicles ₹100",
                new BigDecimal("22.9372"), new BigDecimal("77.6127"), 3, 2024, 420000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rock_Shelter_15,_Bhimbetka_02.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Kanha National Park", "kanha-national-park", "Mandla and Balaghat",
                "One of India's premier tiger reserves and the largest national park in Madhya Pradesh, famous for its lush sal forests, vast meadows, and successful conservation of the swamp deer (Barasingha).",
                "Kanha National Park served as the primary natural inspiration for Rudyard Kipling's The Jungle Book. Spanning expansive sal and bamboo forests punctuated by sweeping grasslands, the reserve provides critical sanctuary for the Royal Bengal tiger, Indian leopard, sloth bear, and the hard-ground barasingha.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tiger_Munna_-_The_Fame_of_Kanha.jpg",
                "October to June (Peak sightings March to May)", "2 to 3 days", "Created in 1955 and made a tiger reserve under Project Tiger in 1973; world-renowned for rescuing the endangered hard-ground barasingha from near extinction.", "Kanha Zone, Kisli Zone, Mukki Zone, Sarhi Zone, Bamni Dadar (Sunset Point), Kanha Museum, Barasingha breeding enclosures",
                "Book safari permits well in advance via MP Forest Department portal; carry warm clothing for winter mornings; park closed Wednesday afternoons.", "Morning: 6:00 AM – 11:00 AM; Afternoon: 3:00 PM – 6:00 PM", "Safari permits ₹1,500 – ₹2,400 per vehicle plus gypsy & guide charges",
                new BigDecimal("22.3345"), new BigDecimal("80.6115"), 4, 2024, 290000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiger_Munna_-_The_Fame_of_Kanha.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Bandhavgarh National Park", "bandhavgarh-national-park", "Umaria",
                "World-renowned tiger reserve boasting one of the highest densities of Royal Bengal tigers in India, framed around an ancient hilltop fort with legendary mythological connections.",
                "Nestled in the Vindhya Hills of Umaria district, Bandhavgarh National Park encompasses rugged rocky terrain, steep cliffs, and thick sal forests. Dominating the landscape is the 2,000-year-old Bandhavgarh Fort, believed to have been gifted by Lord Rama to his brother Lakshmana, while the forest floor is ruled by tigers and rich wildlife.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bandhavgarh_Tiger_(3503896744).jpg",
                "October to June (Peak sightings April to June)", "2 to 3 days", "Former hunting reserve of the Maharajas of Rewa, where the first white tiger was captured in 1951; fort contains 10th-century Kalchuri rock sculptures including Shesh Shaiya.", "Tala Zone, Magadhi Zone, Khitauli Zone, Shesh Shaiya (reclining Vishnu statue), Bandhavgarh Fort, Chakradhara meadow",
                "Tala zone offers highest scenic value and tiger density; reserve safaris up to 120 days ahead; closed Wednesday afternoons.", "Morning: 6:00 AM – 10:30 AM; Evening: 3:00 PM – 6:00 PM", "Safari permit ₹1,500 – ₹2,400 plus Gypsy hire & guide fee",
                new BigDecimal("23.7225"), new BigDecimal("81.0264"), 5, 2024, 310000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandhavgarh_Tiger_(3503896744).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Pench National Park", "pench-national-park", "Seoni and Chhindwara",
                "Named after the pristine Pench River that flows through it, this picturesque tiger reserve inspired the classic landscape and animal characters of Rudyard Kipling's The Jungle Book.",
                "Pench National Park straddles the southern border of Madhya Pradesh and Maharashtra, characterized by teak forests, rolling hills, and meandering waterways. It is home to healthy populations of Bengal tigers, Indian wild dogs (dholes), leopards, and over 260 species of resident and migratory birds.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Collarwali_Tigress_of_Pench.jpg",
                "October to June (Winter for birding, Summer for tigers)", "2 to 3 days", "Celebrated as the original setting for Kipling's The Jungle Book; declared a national park in 1983 and a Project Tiger reserve in 1992.", "Turia Gate, Karmajhiri Gate, Jamtara Gate, Pench River reservoir, Kohka Lake, Potter's Village of Pachdhar",
                "Turia is the most popular gate with maximum sightings; opt for both morning and afternoon safaris; winter mornings require warm woolens.", "Morning: 6:00 AM – 11:00 AM; Afternoon: 3:00 PM – 6:00 PM", "Safari permit ₹1,500 – ₹2,400 plus Gypsy and guide fees",
                new BigDecimal("21.7583"), new BigDecimal("79.3"), 6, 2024, 260000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Collarwali_Tigress_of_Pench.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Panna National Park", "panna-national-park", "Panna and Chhatarpur",
                "A UNESCO Biosphere Reserve celebrated for its dramatic gorges along the Ken River, thriving tiger reintroduction success, and breathtaking waterfalls like Pandav and Raneh Falls.",
                "Situated in northern Madhya Pradesh along the Vindhya ranges, Panna National Park features deep gorges, cascading waterfalls, and dry deciduous forests. After completely losing its tiger population in 2009, Panna executed one of the world's most successful tiger reintroduction programs, restoring an abundant population alongside leopards, sloth bears, and vultures.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Panna_national_park.jpg",
                "October to April", "1 to 2 days", "Renowned globally for the historic 2009 tiger reintroduction and rehabilitation project; added to UNESCO's World Network of Biosphere Reserves in 2020.", "Madla Gate, Hinauta Gate, Ken River boat safari, Pandav Falls, Raneh Falls and Ken Gharial Sanctuary, Vulture Point",
                "Take the Ken River boat ride to spot crocodiles; located just 30 km from Khajuraho; binoculars are essential for vulture nesting cliffs.", "Morning: 6:30 AM – 11:00 AM; Evening: 2:30 PM – 5:30 PM", "Park entry permit ₹1,500 – ₹2,400 plus Gypsy & guide costs",
                new BigDecimal("24.6637"), new BigDecimal("80.0034"), 7, 2024, 210000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panna_national_park.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Ujjain Mahakaleshwar Temple", "ujjain-mahakaleshwar-temple", "Ujjain",
                "One of the twelve sacred Jyotirlingas in India, revered for its south-facing (dakshinmukhi) Shiva idol and the legendary pre-dawn Bhasma Aarti ritual on the banks of the Shipra River.",
                "Situated in the ancient holy city of Ujjain, Mahakaleshwar is among the most revered Hindu pilgrimage centers in India. The three-tiered temple complex features the Swayambhu Mahakal Jyotirlinga, Omkareshwar Shiva in the middle tier, and Nagchandreshwar on the top tier, which opens to devotees only once a year on Nag Panchami.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mahakaleshwar_Temple,_Ujjain.jpg",
                "October to March (Also during Mahashivratri and Shravan)", "Half day to 1 day", "Ancient seat of timekeeping (Greenwich of ancient India); destroyed by Iltutmish in 1234 CE and rebuilt by Maratha general Ranoji Shinde in the 18th century.", "Mahakaleshwar Garbhagriha, Bhasma Aarti, Shri Mahakal Lok corridor, Kotitirth Kund, Omkareshwar shrine, Shipra Ram Ghat",
                "Advance online booking required for 4:00 AM Bhasma Aarti; traditional dress code is mandatory (dhoti for men, saree for women) in inner sanctum.", "4:00 AM to 11:00 PM (Daily)", "Free general entry; VIP darshan ₹250; Bhasma Aarti booking ₹200",
                new BigDecimal("23.1827"), new BigDecimal("75.7682"), 8, 2024, 6500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahakaleshwar_Temple,_Ujjain.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Omkareshwar Temple", "omkareshwar-temple", "Khandwa",
                "A sacred island Jyotirlinga temple situated on Mandhata island in the holy Narmada River, shaped naturally in the sacred Hindu symbol 'Om' (ॐ).",
                "Omkareshwar is one of the 12 revered Jyotirlinga shrines of Lord Shiva, perched on an island in the Narmada River formed by two hills. Pilgrims venerate both the Omkareshwar temple on Mandhata island and the Mamleshwar (Amareshwar) temple on the mainland, completing the holy parikrama across the sacred waters.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Omkareshwar_Temple_05.jpg",
                "October to March (and during Shravan month)", "1 day", "Mentioned in the Skanda Purana and Shiva Purana; historically patronized by the Paramaras, Chauhans, and later restored by the Holkars of Indore under Maharani Ahilyabai.", "Shri Omkar Mandhata Temple, Mamleshwar Temple, Parikrama Path, Narmada Ghats, Gomukh Ghat, Suspension Bridge, Statue of Oneness",
                "Take a local boat ride along the Narmada River for scenic views; walk or take an e-rickshaw across suspension bridge; visit both shrines.", "5:00 AM to 9:30 PM (Daily)", "Free general darshan; Special VIP darshan tickets ₹300",
                new BigDecimal("22.2464"), new BigDecimal("76.1506"), 9, 2024, 3200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Omkareshwar_Temple_05.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Maheshwar Fort", "maheshwar-fort", "Khargone",
                "An imposing 18th-century riverside fort and palace built by Queen Ahilyabai Holkar on the serene northern bank of the holy Narmada River, famed for heritage architecture and Maheshwari weaves.",
                "Maheshwar Fort, also known as Ahilya Fort, rises dramatically above the broad stone ghats of the Narmada River. It served as the capital of the Holkar dynasty under the visionary benevolent ruler Rajmata Ahilyabai Holkar, featuring exquisitely carved sandstone cenotaphs (chhatris), royal courtyards, and traditional handloom workshops.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The_Grand_Maheshwar_Fort.jpg",
                "October to March", "Half day to 1 day", "Mentioned in the epics Ramayana and Mahabharata as ancient Mahishmati; transformed into an enlightened administrative capital by Queen Ahilyabai Holkar from 1767 to 1795.", "Ahilya Fort complex, Ahilyeshwar Shivalaya chhatri, Narmada Ghats, Rajwada, Rehwa Society Maheshwari handlooms, sunset boating",
                "Walk the ghats early morning or at sunset; visit Rehwa Society within the fort to buy authentic GI-tagged Maheshwari sarees.", "7:00 AM to 7:00 PM (Ghats open 24 hours)", "Free entry to fort and ghats; Ahilya palace museum ₹50",
                new BigDecimal("22.1764"), new BigDecimal("75.5828"), 10, 2024, 1100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Grand_Maheshwar_Fort.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Mandu", "mandu", "Dhar",
                "An enchanting fortress city of romance and medieval Afghan architecture, celebrated for Jahaz Mahal (Ship Palace), Roopmati's Pavilion, and the legendary love story of Baz Bahadur and Rani Roopmati.",
                "Perched atop a plateau of the Vindhya Range, Mandu—historically known as Shadiabad (the City of Joy)—is India's largest fortified ruined city. The romantic hill capital showcases exquisite Islamic architecture characterized by horseshoe arches, sprawling pleasure pavilions, bathhouses, and water reservoirs spanning over 20 square kilometers.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jahaz_Mahal-Mandu.JPG",
                "July to March (Monsoon brings lush landscapes)", "1 to 2 days", "Capital of the Malwa Sultanate in the 15th and 16th centuries; celebrated in folklore for the passionate tragic love between ruler Baz Bahadur and songstress Rani Roopmati.", "Jahaz Mahal, Hindola Mahal, Rani Roopmati Pavilion, Baz Bahadur Palace, Hoshang Shah's Tomb, Jami Masjid, Rewa Kund",
                "Rent a bicycle or auto to traverse the vast 20 sq km complex; visit in monsoon or winter; try local baobab fruit (khorasani imli).", "Sunrise to Sunset (approx. 6:00 AM – 6:30 PM)", "Indians ₹25; Foreigners ₹300 (covers Central & Royal Enclaves)",
                new BigDecimal("22.366"), new BigDecimal("75.3424"), 11, 2024, 750000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jahaz_Mahal-Mandu.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Gwalior Fort", "gwalior-fort", "Gwalior",
                "Described by Mughal Emperor Babur as 'the pearl in the necklace of forts of Hind', this majestic 8th-century hilltop fortress dominates the skyline of Gwalior with vibrant turquoise-tiled palace walls.",
                "Rising 100 meters above Gwalior on a sheer sandstone ridge, Gwalior Fort is one of India's most impregnable and historically pivotal citadels. Built through centuries by Tomars, Mughals, and Scindias, the complex contains stunning monuments including Man Mandir Palace, Sas Bahu Temples, Teli Ka Mandir, and monumental rock-cut Jain tirthankaras.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gwalior-Fort.jpg",
                "October to March", "Half day to 1 full day", "Strategic northern fort held by Hunas, Tomars, Mughals, Marathas, and British; site of the heroic stand of Rani Lakshmibai during the 1857 rebellion.", "Man Mandir Palace, Gujari Mahal Archaeological Museum, Sas Bahu Temples, Teli Ka Mandir, Gopachal Parvat Jain rock sculptures, Urwahi Gate",
                "Drive up to Urwahi Gate to admire colossal Jain monoliths; attend the evening sound and light show at Man Mandir amphitheater.", "6:00 AM to 5:30 PM (Daily)", "Indians ₹25; Foreigners ₹300; Sound and Light show ₹100–₹250",
                new BigDecimal("26.2307"), new BigDecimal("78.1695"), 12, 2024, 1600000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gwalior-Fort.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Orchha", "orchha", "Niwari",
                "A timeless Bundela capital on the banks of the Betwa River, famous for its magnificent fort complex, soaring Chaturbhuj Temple, riverside royal chhatris, and Ram Raja Temple.",
                "Founded in the 16th century by Bundela chieftain Rudra Pratap Singh, Orchha preserves an astonishing ensemble of palaces and multi-tiered temples frozen in time. The medieval citadel on an island of the Betwa River features Jahangir Mahal, Raja Mahal with vibrant murals, and Ram Raja Temple—the only shrine where Lord Rama is officially worshipped as a reigning monarch.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chaturbhuj_Temple,_Orchha.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 days", "Capital of the Bundela Rajput kingdom from 1531 to 1783; celebrated for Bundela architecture fusing Rajput and Mughal elements, surviving untouched.", "Orchha Fort complex (Jahangir Mahal & Raja Mahal), Chaturbhuj Temple, Ram Raja Temple, Chhatris on Betwa riverbank, Laxmi Narayan Temple",
                "Climb upper terraces of Chaturbhuj Temple for panoramic views; attend evening aarti at Ram Raja Temple; watch sunset by the Royal Chhatris.", "Fort: 8:00 AM – 5:30 PM; Temples: 8:00 AM – 12:30 PM & 5:00 PM – 9:30 PM", "Indians ₹10; Foreigners ₹250 (Composite ticket for fort & monuments)",
                new BigDecimal("25.3516"), new BigDecimal("78.6416"), 13, 2024, 890000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chaturbhuj_Temple,_Orchha.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Bhedaghat Marble Rocks", "bhedaghat-marble-rocks", "Jabalpur",
                "A spectacular 3-kilometer gorge carved by the Narmada River through towering cliffs of pure white, cream, and magnesium marble, creating an ethereal landscape best explored by boat.",
                "Located near Jabalpur, Bhedaghat Marble Rocks feature dramatic hundred-foot marble cliffs that rise vertically on either side of the crystal-clear Narmada River. The river narrows as it flows through these reflective stone canyons, creating a world-famous natural spectacle that shifts colors under sunlight and appears surreal under full-moon skies.",
                "Nature", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Marble_Rocks_-_Jabalpur.jpg",
                "October to May (Full moon nights are famous)", "3 to 5 hours", "Natural geological formation of national geo-heritage significance; site featured in ancient lore and numerous classic Indian cinema film sequences.", "Narmada River canyon boat ride, Bandar Kudani (narrow gorge), Chausath Yogini Temple, marble handicraft shopping, full moon boating",
                "Hire a rowboat with local boatmen; boating is strictly closed during monsoon season (July to September).", "7:00 AM to 7:00 PM (Boating operates until late on full-moon nights)", "Free entry to viewpoint; Boating shared ₹100–₹150; Private boat ₹800–₹1200",
                new BigDecimal("23.1311"), new BigDecimal("79.8006"), 14, 2024, 1200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_Rocks_-_Jabalpur.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Dhuandhar Falls", "dhuandhar-falls", "Jabalpur",
                "A roaring 30-meter high waterfall on the Narmada River where massive torrents crash into marble bedrock, creating an enchanting mist that resembles a column of smoke.",
                "Deriving its name from 'Dhuan' (smoke) and 'Dhar' (flow), the Dhuandhar Falls is one of the most powerful and mesmerizing waterfalls in central India. The Narmada River plunges with tremendous acoustic force over white marble rocks, throwing up a perpetual smoky vapor cloud visible and audible from a substantial distance.",
                "Waterfall", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dhuandhar_Waterfall.jpg",
                "September to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Integral part of the holy Narmada River pilgrimage and geo-heritage landscape at Bhedaghat.", "Dhuandhar viewpoint platforms, Bhedaghat ropeway (cable car ride crossing gorge), Narmada River ghats, marble souvenir shops",
                "Ride the cable car across the gorge for an incredible bird's-eye perspective; avoid slippery edges near spray rocks.", "6:00 AM to 8:00 PM (Ropeway operational 10:00 AM – 6:00 PM)", "Free entry; Ropeway cable car ₹120–₹150 round-trip per person",
                new BigDecimal("23.1293"), new BigDecimal("79.8094"), 15, 2024, 1100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dhuandhar_Waterfall.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Pachmarhi", "pachmarhi", "Narmadapuram",
                "Known as the 'Queen of the Satpuras' (Satpura ki Rani), Pachmarhi is the only hill station in Madhya Pradesh, blessed with evergreen forests, cascading waterfalls, and ancient caves.",
                "Situated at an altitude of 1,067 meters in the Satpura Range within a UNESCO Biosphere Reserve, Pachmarhi offers a tranquil retreat of pine glades, ravines, and perennial streams. The plateau features rock shelters with prehistoric paintings, colonial-era bungalows, and the highest peak in Madhya Pradesh, Dhupgarh.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mount_Dhupgarh,_Pachmarhi.jpg",
                "Throughout the year (October to June for sightseeing)", "2 to 3 days", "Legend holds the Pandavas carved the five caves (Pandav Caves) here during exile; introduced as a British hill sanatorium by Captain James Forsyth in 1857.", "Mount Dhupgarh, Bee Falls, Duchess Falls, Pandav Caves, Jata Shankar Cave, Reechgarh, Handi Khoh gorge, Chauragarh Temple trek",
                "Rent a local 4x4 Gypsy with mandatory forest guide for visiting core biosphere spots; wear comfortable walking shoes for Bee Falls descent.", "Viewpoints open 6:00 AM to 6:00 PM (Forest areas close by 5:30 PM)", "Forest entry permit ₹1,000–₹1,500 per gypsy plus guide fee (₹500)",
                new BigDecimal("22.4674"), new BigDecimal("78.4346"), 16, 2024, 920000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Dhupgarh,_Pachmarhi.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Chitrakoot", "chitrakoot", "Satna",
                "A tranquil pilgrimage town steeped in epic mythology, where Lord Rama, Sita, and Lakshmana spent eleven of their fourteen years of forest exile according to the Ramayana.",
                "Straddling the border of Madhya Pradesh and Uttar Pradesh amidst the northern Vindhyan forests, Chitrakoot (the 'Hill of Many Wonders') is revered for its deeply spiritual atmosphere. The sacred Mandakini River is lined with vibrant bathing ghats, ashrams, and shrines where saints including Goswami Tulsidas meditated and composed hymns.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ramghat_at_chitrakoot.jpg",
                "October to March (and during Deepotsav / Diwali)", "1 to 2 days", "Celebrated in the Valmiki Ramayana as the forest retreat of Lord Rama; sanctuary of Sage Atri and Anasuya; site of the Bharat Milap meeting.", "Ramghat on Mandakini River, Kamadgiri Temple & Parikrama, Bharat Milap Temple, Gupt Godavari caves, Hanuman Dhara, Sati Anasuya Ashram",
                "Attend evening Mandakini river aarti at Ramghat on a paddle boat; take ropeway to Hanuman Dhara; carry a torch for Gupt Godavari caves.", "Ghats open 24 hours; Temples typically 5:00 AM to 9:00 PM", "Free temple entry; Hanuman Dhara ropeway ₹100–₹150 round trip",
                new BigDecimal("25.1764"), new BigDecimal("80.8678"), 17, 2024, 1800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramghat_at_chitrakoot.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Bhojpur Temple", "bhojpur-temple", "Raisen",
                "Often called the 'Somnath of the East', this colossal 11th-century unfinished Shiva temple founded by King Bhoja houses one of the largest monolithic stone Shivlingas in India.",
                "The Bhojeshwar Temple at Bhojpur is an architectural marvel of the Paramara dynasty situated on a rocky ridge along the Betwa River near Bhopal. The temple sanctum houses a gigantic 7.5-foot-tall lingam on a 20-foot-square base carved from single stones, accompanied by rare medieval architectural blueprints etched permanently into surrounding quarry rocks.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Shiva_Temple,_Bhojpur_01.jpg",
                "October to March (Grandeur during Mahashivratri)", "1 to 2 hours", "Built by polymath monarch Raja Bhoja of Dhar (r. 1010–1055 CE); uncompleted for unknown reasons; features intact ancient construction ramps.", "Monolithic Shiva Lingam, massive sanctum dome and corbelled ceiling, 11th-century architectural rock engravings, Betwa riverbank",
                "Easily combined with Bhimbetka Rock Shelters on a single-day circuit from Bhopal; inspect the bedrock near temple to see mason drawings.", "6:00 AM to 6:30 PM (Daily)", "Indians ₹25; Foreigners ₹300; Children under 15 free",
                new BigDecimal("23.1001"), new BigDecimal("77.5843"), 18, 2024, 680000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shiva_Temple,_Bhojpur_01.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Burhanpur", "burhanpur", "Burhanpur",
                "A historic Mughal river fortress city on the Tapti River, where Empress Mumtaz Mahal originally died and was first laid to rest, famed for Shahi Qila, Asirgarh Fort, and ancient water systems.",
                "Established in 1400 CE by the Faruqi Sultanate and later made the premier Deccan headquarters of the Mughal Empire, Burhanpur is a treasure trove of medieval architecture. The city houses the grand Shahi Qila with its decorated royal bath (hammam), Asirgarh Fort ('Key to the Deccan'), Dargah-e-Hakimi, and the ingenious Kundi Bhandara underground water system.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Shahi_Qila,Burhanpur.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 days", "Headquarters of Shah Jahan during his Deccan campaigns; original resting place of Mumtaz Mahal before Taj Mahal was built; Asirgarh was key to the Deccan.", "Shahi Qila and Royal Hammam, Asirgarh Fort, Kundi Bhandara (underground water system), Dargah-e-Hakimi, Jama Masjid",
                "Obtain permission to take the elevator into 400-year-old Kundi Bhandara water system; hire a local heritage guide for Mughal hammam paintings.", "8:00 AM to 6:00 PM (Monuments); Dargah: 6:00 AM to 9:00 PM", "Shahi Qila: Indians ₹25, Foreigners ₹300; Asirgarh: ₹25; Kundi Bhandara: ₹20",
                new BigDecimal("21.3129"), new BigDecimal("76.2298"), 19, 2024, 350000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shahi_Qila,Burhanpur.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(mp, "Kuno National Park", "kuno-national-park", "Sheopur and Morena",
                "The historic home of 'Project Cheetah' in India, this expansive open grassland and dry deciduous park was chosen to reintroduce wild cheetahs to the subcontinent after 70 years of extinction.",
                "Situated in the Vindhyan hills along the meandering Kuno River in northern Madhya Pradesh, Kuno National Park features an open savannah-like landscape of grasslands and Kardhai trees. In September 2022, it stepped onto the global wildlife conservation stage by welcoming the first translocated wild African cheetahs back into India.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kuno_river.jpg",
                "October to March (Avoid high heat in late summer)", "1 to 2 days", "Chosen as the introduction site for the world's first intercontinental large carnivore translocation project (Project Cheetah); declared a national park in 2018.", "Cheetah tourism zones, Kuno River valley, Tiktoli Gate, Ahera Gate, Peepalbawdi Gate, Dev Kho gorge, chital herds, leopards",
                "Book safari slots in advance via MP Forest Department portal; choose Tiktoli gate for prime circuits; carry telephoto lens and binoculars.", "Morning: 6:30 AM – 10:30 AM; Evening: 3:00 PM – 6:00 PM", "Safari permit ₹1,500 – ₹2,400 plus Gypsy hire and guide fees",
                new BigDecimal("25.6833"), new BigDecimal("77.2667"), 20, 2024, 140000L, "TOTAL",
                "Madhya Pradesh Tourism (MPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kuno_river.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        // --- 13. MAHARASHTRA ---
        State maharashtra = createState("Maharashtra", "maharashtra", "STATE", "Western", "Mumbai", "Financial gateway of India, home to Ajanta-Ellora caves, Shivaji forts, and Bollywood.",
                "Maharashtra encompasses financial capital Mumbai, UNESCO cave art at Ajanta & Ellora, Sahyadri hill stations, and Konkan beaches.",
                "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Courtyard_and_Mahabharata_Reliefs_at_the_Kailasa_Temple,_Ellora_01.jpg", "October to March");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Ajanta and Ellora Caves", "ajanta-ellora-caves", "Chhatrapati Sambhaji Nagar",
                "UNESCO World Heritage rock-cut cave monuments showcasing Buddhist, Hindu, and Jain cave art.",
                "Ellora features Cave 16 (Kailasa Temple), the world's largest monolithic rock-cut structure carved out of a single basalt rock face.",
                "Historical", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Courtyard_and_Mahabharata_Reliefs_at_the_Kailasa_Temple,_Ellora_01.jpg",
                "October to March", "1-2 Days", "Carved between 2nd century BCE and 10th century CE.", "Kailasa Temple (Ellora Cave 16), Ajanta Painting Caves 1 & 2",
                "Ajanta caves are closed on Mondays; Ellora caves are closed on Tuesdays.", "9:00 AM - 5:30 PM", "Rs 40 Indian, Rs 600 Foreigner",
                new BigDecimal("20.0268"), new BigDecimal("75.1779"), 1, 2024, 1950000L, "TOTAL",
                "ASI Aurangabad Circle", "Cave Footfall Records 2024", "https://maharashtratourism.gov.in", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Gateway of India", "gateway-of-india", "Mumbai",
                "Iconic waterfront basalt arch monument overlooking the Arabian Sea.",
                "Built to commemorate the visit of King George V and Queen Mary to Mumbai in 1911, standing at Apollo Bunder.",
                "Monument", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Panoramic_view_of_Taj_Palace_Hotel_and_Taj_Tower_with_the_iconic_Gateway_of_India_in_the_background.jpg",
                "October to March", "1-2 Hours", "Designed by George Wittet and completed in 1924.", "Taj Mahal Palace Hotel, Elephanta Caves Ferry",
                "Take a ferry ride from Gateway of India to Elephanta Caves island.", "Open 24 Hours", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("18.9220"), new BigDecimal("72.8347"), 2, 2024, 9500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corp (MTDC)", "Mumbai Sightseeing Data", "https://maharashtratourism.gov.in", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Terminus (CSMT)", "chhatrapati-shivaji-maharaj-terminus-mumbai", "Mumbai",
                "UNESCO World Heritage Victorian Gothic Revival railway headquarters in Mumbai, blending high domes and gargoyles with Indian palace stone carvings.",
                "Designed by British architect Frederick William Stevens and completed in 1888 over ten years, Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Terminus (formerly Victoria Terminus) is a living architectural masterpiece that serves over three million commuters daily. Its central octagonal ribbed dome—crowned by a 16-foot statue of Progress—combines Italianate Victorian Gothic arches with traditional Indian stone screenwork, peacocks, and sculpted flora carved by students of the Sir J.J. School of Art.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mumbai_-_Chhatrapati_Shivaji_Terminus_Tracks.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 hours", "Opened in 1887 to mark Queen Victoria's Golden Jubilee on the site of Bori Bunder (where India's first train departed for Thane in 1853); inscribed by UNESCO in 2004.", "Octagonal Central Dome & Statue of Progress, Star Chamber Booking Hall, Heritage Rail Museum & Cantilevered Grand Staircase, Illuminated Evening Façade",
                "Book the weekday afternoon CSMT Heritage Walk to see the grand cantilevered staircase and railway museum, and photograph the exterior lighting after 7:00 PM.", "Open 24 Hours (Heritage Museum Tour 3:00 PM – 5:00 PM Mon–Fri)", "Exterior & Concourse Free (Heritage Tour ₹200)",
                new BigDecimal("18.9398"), new BigDecimal("72.8355"), 3, 2024, 3200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corporation (MTDC)", "Maharashtra Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mumbai_-_Chhatrapati_Shivaji_Terminus_Tracks.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Shirdi Sai Baba Samadhi Mandir", "shirdi-sai-baba-temple", "Ahmednagar",
                "Revered pilgrimage shrine in Ahmednagar district housing the sacred Samadhi and Italian marble idol of Saint Sai Baba of Shirdi.",
                "One of the most visited spiritual centers in the world, the Shirdi Sai Baba Sansthan complex centers around the Samadhi Mandir (originally constructed by Nagpur millionaire Gopalrao Booty) where Sai Baba attained Mahasamadhi on October 15, 1918. Surrounding the main sanctum are Dwarkamai—where the sacred eternal fire (Dhuni) still burns—Chavadi, Gurusthan beneath the sacred neem tree, and Lendi Baug.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Shirdi_Sai_Baba_Samadhi.jpg",
                "Year-round (October to March & Guru Purnima)", "3 to 5 hours", "Sai Baba lived in Shirdi from the mid-19th century until 1918, preaching universal unity (\"Sabka Malik Ek\"); the famous lifelike marble statue was sculpted by Balaji Vasant Talim in 1954.", "Samadhi Mandir & Marble Murti, Dwarkamai & Sacred Dhuni, Chavadi Procession Hall, Gurusthan Neem Tree, Sai Prasadalaya (Solar-Powered Kitchen)",
                "Book timed Darshan and Kakad/Shej Aarti passes online via online.sai.org.in before arrival; phones and cameras must be deposited at the gate lockers.", "4:00 AM – 11:15 PM daily", "Free Sarva Darshan (VIP Timed Pass ₹200)",
                new BigDecimal("19.7662"), new BigDecimal("74.4762"), 4, 2024, 8500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corporation (MTDC)", "Maharashtra Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shirdi_Sai_Baba_Samadhi.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Elephanta Caves", "elephanta-caves-mumbai", "Mumbai Harbour",
                "UNESCO World Heritage 6th-century rock-cut basalt cave temples on Gharapuri Island in Mumbai Harbour, home to the colossal 20-foot Trimurti Sadashiva.",
                "Reached by a scenic one-hour boat voyage across Mumbai Harbour from the Gateway of India, Elephanta Island (Gharapuri) contains seven rock-cut caves carved out of solid Deccan trap basalt between the 5th and 7th centuries CE. Cave 1 (the Great Cave) is a pillared mandapa housing monumental relief panels of Nataraja, Ardhanarishvara, Gangadhara, and the iconic 6-meter three-headed Maheshmurti (Trimurti) depicting Shiva as Creator, Preserver, and Destroyer.",
                "Caves", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Elephanta_-_Mahesh_Murti.jpg",
                "November to March", "Half Day (4 to 5 hours including ferry)", "Carved under Konkan Maurya, Kalachuri, and Chalukya patronage; named \"Elephanta\" by 16th-century Portuguese sailors after a stone elephant statue found near the landing; inscribed by UNESCO in 1987.", "20-Foot Trimurti Sadashiva Sculpture (Cave 1), Eastern Wing Linga Shrine, Cannon Hill Viewpoint, Toy Train on Pier, Gateway-to-Elephanta Harbour Cruise",
                "Board the first ferry from Gateway of India at 9:00 AM to beat midday heat and tour groups; the caves are closed on Mondays.", "9:00 AM – 5:30 PM (Closed Mondays)", "₹40 Indian nationals; ₹600 Foreigners (Ferry ₹260 return)",
                new BigDecimal("18.9633"), new BigDecimal("72.9315"), 5, 2024, 1450000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corporation (MTDC)", "Maharashtra Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elephanta_-_Mahesh_Murti.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Marine Drive (Queen’s Necklace)", "marine-drive-mumbai", "Mumbai",
                "Iconic 3.6-km C-shaped Arabian Sea boulevard from Nariman Point to Malabar Hill, lined with UNESCO Art Deco buildings.",
                "Officially named Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose Road, Marine Drive curves gracefully along Back Bay from Nariman Point to Girgaum Chowpatty and the foot of Malabar Hill. Lined with tetrapod sea-breakers, swaying palms, and the world's second-largest collection of 1930s Art Deco residential buildings (inscribed by UNESCO in 2018 alongside Mumbai's Victorian Gothic ensemble), its streetlights form a glittering \"Queen's Necklace\" after dark.",
                "Nature", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mumbai_03-2016_27_skyline_at_Marine_Drive.jpg",
                "October to March (and Monsoon high-tide)", "1 to 2 hours", "Constructed on reclaimed Back Bay land in the 1920s–1930s; inscribed as part of the Victorian Gothic and Art Deco Ensembles of Mumbai UNESCO Site in 2018.", "3.6-Km Seafront Promenade, UNESCO Art Deco Apartment Façades, Girgaum Chowpatty Beach, Nariman Point Sunset Skyline",
                "Stroll from Nariman Point toward Chowpatty around 5:45 PM for sunset, followed by Mumbai street snacks at Girgaum Chowpatty.", "Open 24 Hours", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("18.9432"), new BigDecimal("72.8235"), 6, 2024, 6500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corporation (MTDC)", "Maharashtra Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mumbai_03-2016_27_skyline_at_Marine_Drive.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Shaniwar Wada", "shaniwar-wada-pune", "Pune",
                "18th-century fortified palace of the Peshwas of the Maratha Empire in the heart of Pune, famous for the spike-studded Dilli Darwaza.",
                "Built in 1732 by Peshwa Baji Rao I as the seat of the Peshwa prime ministers of the Maratha Empire, Shaniwar Wada served as the political nerve center of 18th-century India until the British takeover in 1818. Although an 1828 fire destroyed its seven-storey teakwood palaces, the massive stone ramparts, five grand gateways including the 21-foot elephant-proof Dilli Darwaza, Mastani Darwaza, and the sixteen-petal Hazari Karanje lotus fountain foundations remain intact.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Shaniwar_wada_(pune).jpg",
                "October to March", "1.5 to 2 hours", "Foundation laid on Saturday (Shaniwar), January 10, 1730, by Peshwa Baji Rao I and completed in 1732 at a cost of 16,110 rupees.", "Dilli Darwaza with 72 Iron Spikes, Mastani Darwaza, Hazari Karanje (16-Petal Lotus Fountain), Palace Plinths & Maratha History Light Show",
                "Pair with the nearby Lal Mahal (100 meters away) and Dagdusheth Halwai Ganpati Temple in the Kasba Peth heritage precinct.", "8:00 AM – 6:30 PM daily", "₹25 Indian nationals; ₹300 Foreign nationals",
                new BigDecimal("18.5195"), new BigDecimal("73.8553"), 7, 2024, 1620000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corporation (MTDC)", "Maharashtra Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shaniwar_wada_(pune).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Mahabaleshwar Hill Station", "mahabaleshwar-hill-station", "Satara",
                "Premier Sahyadri hill station at 1,353 meters in Satara district, famed for Arthur's Seat canyon views, Venna Lake, and strawberry farms.",
                "Crowning a vast evergreen plateau in the Western Ghats, Mahabaleshwar is the source of five sacred rivers including the Krishna and served as the summer capital of the Bombay Presidency. Its dramatic basalt escarpments—such as Arthur's Seat, Elephant's Head Point, and Kate's Point—plunge nearly 1,000 meters into the Konkan and Savitri valleys, while surrounding orchards produce over 80% of India's strawberries.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Arthur%27s_seat,_Mahabaleshwar.jpg",
                "October to June", "2 to 3 Days", "Home to the 13th-century Panchganga and Mahabaleshwar Shiva temples built by the Yadava kings; developed as a hill resort by Sir John Malcolm in 1828.", "Arthur's Seat & Elephant's Head Point, Venna Lake Boating, Old Mahabaleshwar Panchganga Temple, Mapro Strawberry Gardens, Pratapgad Fort Excursion",
                "Visit between December and March for fresh strawberry picking and crisp, fog-free canyon vistas at Arthur's Seat.", "Viewpoints 6:00 AM – 6:30 PM daily", "₹50 Municipal Eco-Entry Tax",
                new BigDecimal("17.9237"), new BigDecimal("73.6586"), 8, 2024, 2200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corporation (MTDC)", "Maharashtra Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arthur%27s_seat,_Mahabaleshwar.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Lonavala & Karla Caves", "lonavala-and-khandala", "Pune District",
                "Twin Sahyadri hill resorts on the Mumbai-Pune expressway paired with the 2nd-century BCE Karla and Bhaja Buddhist rock-cut Chaitya caves.",
                "Perched atop the Bhor Ghat pass at 624 meters between Mumbai and Pune, Lonavala and Khandala transform into an emerald wonderland of waterfalls, mist-shrouded valleys, and Maratha hill forts during the monsoon and winter. Just 11 km east at Karli stands the Great Chaitya of Karla Caves—the largest and best-preserved Hinayana Buddhist rock-cut prayer hall in India, still retaining its 2,000-year-old wooden ceiling ribs.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Karle_caves_(_Lonavala).png",
                "July to March", "1 to 2 Days", "Karla and Bhaja Caves were excavated along the ancient Konkan-Deccan trade route between 160 BCE and 2nd century CE; Lonavala was developed in 1871 by Lord Elphinstone.", "Karla & Bhaja Rock-Cut Buddhist Caves, Tiger's Leap & Lion's Point, Bhushi Dam, Lohagad & Visapur Hill Forts, Rajmachi Viewpoint",
                "Climb to Karla Caves at 8:30 AM before crowds arrive, and pick up freshly made Lonavala almond and walnut chikki in town.", "Karla Caves 9:00 AM – 5:30 PM daily", "Karla Caves ₹25 Indians; ₹300 Foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("18.7557"), new BigDecimal("73.4091"), 9, 2024, 3100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corporation (MTDC)", "Maharashtra Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karle_caves_(_Lonavala).png", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Trimbakeshwar Shiva Temple", "trimbakeshwar-jyotirlinga-nashik", "Nashik",
                "Sacred Jyotirlinga shrine in black basalt at the foot of Brahmagiri Hill near Nashik, marking the origin of the Godavari River.",
                "Located 28 km from Nashik at the source of the sacred Godavari (Gautami Ganga) River, Trimbakeshwar is one of the twelve revered Jyotirlingas of India. Uniquely, its sanctum houses a three-faced Linga embodying Brahma, Vishnu, and Rudra (Mahesh). The current Hemadpanthi-Nagara temple in gleaming black basalt was commissioned in 1755 by Peshwa Balaji Baji Rao (Nana Saheb).",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Trimbakeshwar_Shiva_Temple,_Trimbak,_Nashik_District,_Maharastra.jpg",
                "October to March & Shravan Month", "2 to 3 hours", "Reconstructed between 1755 and 1786 by Peshwa Balaji Baji Rao on the ancient site associated with Sage Gautama and the Simhastha Kumbh Mela.", "Three-Faced Jyotirlinga Sanctum, Kushavarta Kund (Sacred Stepped Tank), Brahmagiri Hill Trek (Origin of Godavari), Anjaneri Hills nearby",
                "Arrive at 5:30 AM for early morning darshan; traditional dhoti/sovale dress code applies only if entering the inner garbhagriha for special puja.", "5:30 AM – 9:00 PM daily", "Free general darshan (VIP Pass ₹200)",
                new BigDecimal("19.9322"), new BigDecimal("73.5307"), 10, 2024, 3600000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corporation (MTDC)", "Maharashtra Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trimbakeshwar_Shiva_Temple,_Trimbak,_Nashik_District,_Maharastra.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Raigad Fort", "raigad-fort", "Raigad",
                "Impregnable mountain capital of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj at 820 meters in the Sahyadris, site of his 1674 coronation as founder of the Maratha Empire.",
                "Rising sheer on three sides from the Sahyadri ranges, Raigad (originally Rairi) was captured by Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj in 1656 and fortified by chief architect Hiroji Indulkar as the sovereign capital of Hindavi Swarajya. Visitors can climb 1,737 rock-cut steps through the curved Maha Darwaza or ride the Raigad Ropeway to the Raj Bhavan, Nagarkhana acoustic arch, Jagdishwar Temple, and the Samadhi of Shivaji Maharaj and his loyal dog Waghya.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Shivaji_Maharaj_Raigad.jpg",
                "October to March", "Half Day (4 to 5 hours)", "On June 6, 1674 (Jyeshtha Shuddha Trayodashi), Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj was crowned here in a grand Vedic coronation ceremony; inscribed as part of the Maratha Military Landscapes.", "Rajya Sabha (Coronation Court & Acoustic Nagarkhana), Maha Darwaza, Jagdishwar Temple & Samadhi of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj, Takmak Tok Cliff, Raigad Aerial Ropeway",
                "Take the Raigad Ropeway up in the morning to spend 3 hours exploring the vast plateau while temperatures are cool.", "8:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "₹25 ASI Entry (Raigad Ropeway ₹355 return)",
                new BigDecimal("18.2346"), new BigDecimal("73.4464"), 11, 2024, 1150000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corporation (MTDC)", "Maharashtra Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shivaji_Maharaj_Raigad.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Tadoba Andhari Tiger Reserve", "tadoba-andhari-tiger-reserve", "Chandrapur",
                "Maharashtra's oldest and largest national park in Chandrapur district, famed for bamboo-teak forests and one of India's highest Royal Bengal Tiger densities.",
                "Spanning 1,727 sq km across the Chimur Hills of eastern Vidarbha, Tadoba Andhari Tiger Reserve combines Tadoba National Park (created in 1955) and Andhari Wildlife Sanctuary. Centered around the perennial Tadoba Lake and Telia Dam, its dry deciduous teak and bamboo corridors support over 115 Royal Bengal Tigers, Indian leopards, sloth bears, dholes (wild dogs), gaur, and marsh crocodiles.",
                "National Park", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bamboo-Lined_Forest_Trail_in_Tadoba_Andhari_National_Park.jpg",
                "October to May", "2 to 3 Days", "Named after the tribal deity \"Taru\" (Tadoba) worshipped by local Gond communities and the meandering Andhari River; declared a Project Tiger Reserve in 1995.", "Moharli, Kolara, Khutwanda & Navegaon Core Jeep Safaris, Tadoba Lake & Telia Dam Waterholes, Irai Backwaters Birdwatching",
                "Book core-zone Gypsy safaris (especially Moharli and Kolara gates) 60 to 120 days in advance on mytadoba.mahaforest.gov.in; core zones close on Tuesdays.", "Morning Safari 6:00 AM – 10:00 AM; Afternoon Safari 2:30 PM – 6:30 PM", "Safari Permit + Gypsy + Guide ~₹4,500–₹6,000 per vehicle",
                new BigDecimal("20.2408"), new BigDecimal("79.3606"), 12, 2024, 340000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corporation (MTDC)", "Maharashtra Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bamboo-Lined_Forest_Trail_in_Tadoba_Andhari_National_Park.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Bibi Ka Maqbara", "bibi-ka-maqbara-aurangabad", "Chhatrapati Sambhaji Nagar",
                "17th-century Mughal garden mausoleum known as the \"Taj of the Deccan,\" designed by Ata-ullah, son of the Taj Mahal's chief architect.",
                "Set inside a classic four-quartered Charbagh garden with axial reflection pools and dark basalt hill backdrops in Chhatrapati Sambhaji Nagar (Aurangabad), Bibi Ka Maqbara was commissioned in 1660 CE to enshrine Dilras Banu Begum (Rabia-ud-Daurani). Its onion dome is clad in white marble quarried near Jaipur, while the lower façades feature fine burnished stucco plaster, brass-inlaid doors, and octagonal corner minarets.",
                "Monument", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bibi_ka_Maqbara,_Aurangabad,_Maharashtra_02.jpg",
                "October to March", "1.5 hours", "Built between 1660 and 1668 CE by Prince Azam Shah and Emperor Aurangzeb, designed by architect Ata-ullah (son of Ustad Ahmad Lahori, architect of the Taj Mahal).", "Marble-Crowned Central Mausoleum, Octagonal Pierced Marble Screen, Charbagh Reflecting Pools, Archaeological Museum in the Rear Pavilion",
                "Visit around 4:30 PM for warm golden light on the dome and pair with nearby Panchakki (17th-century water mill) and Aurangabad Caves.", "8:00 AM – 8:00 PM daily", "₹25 Indian nationals; ₹300 Foreign nationals",
                new BigDecimal("19.9015"), new BigDecimal("75.3202"), 13, 2024, 1280000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corporation (MTDC)", "Maharashtra Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bibi_ka_Maqbara,_Aurangabad,_Maharashtra_02.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Pratapgad Fort", "pratapgad-fort-satara", "Satara",
                "Historic Sahyadri hill fort 24 km below Mahabaleshwar, site of the decisive 1659 Battle of Pratapgad between Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj and Afzal Khan.",
                "Commanding the strategic Par pass between the Deccan plateau and the Konkan coast at 1,080 meters, Pratapgad Fort was commissioned by Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj in 1656 and completed by Moropant Trimbak Pingle. Divided into an Upper Fort and Lower Fort with double-walled bastions, it houses the Tulja Bhavani Temple, Mahadev Temple, and a 17-foot bronze equestrian statue of Shivaji Maharaj unveiled by Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru in 1957.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Pratapgad_fort_front_view.jpg",
                "October to March & Post-Monsoon", "2 to 3 hours", "On November 10, 1659, the Battle of Pratapgad fought on these slopes established Maratha military supremacy across the Western Ghats.", "17-Foot Bronze Equestrian Statue of Shivaji Maharaj, Tulja Bhavani Temple, Tehalni Buruj Watchtower, 450-Step Fortified Ascent",
                "Easily visited as a half-day excursion from Mahabaleshwar (45-minute drive down the scenic Fitzgerald Ghat road).", "6:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "Free entry",
                new BigDecimal("17.9339"), new BigDecimal("73.5806"), 14, 2024, 940000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corporation (MTDC)", "Maharashtra Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pratapgad_fort_front_view.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Sindhudurg Sea Fort", "sindhudurg-sea-fort-malvan", "Sindhudurg",
                "48-acre island sea fortress built by Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj in 1664–1667 off the Malvan coast using molten lead foundations.",
                "Erected on the rocky Kurte islet half a kilometer off Malvan in the Arabian Sea, Sindhudurg (\"Fort of the Ocean\") was engineered under Hiroji Indulkar to serve as the naval headquarters of the Maratha Empire. Over 70,000 kg of molten lead were poured to anchor its 30-foot-high, 12-foot-thick zigzag basalt walls against pounding waves. Inside stand freshwater wells (Dudhbaav, Dahiyaav, Sakharbaav), a concealed entrance (Dindi Darwaza), and the historic Shivrajeshwar Temple.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Leaving_Malvan_jetty_for_Sindhudurg_fort_(5363541711).jpg",
                "October to May", "3 hours", "Foundation stone laid by Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj on November 25, 1664; preserves his authentic handprint and footprint cast in lime mortar.", "Preserved Handprint & Footprint of Shivaji Maharaj, Shivrajeshwar Temple, Concealed Dindi Darwaza, Three Freshwater Wells Inside a Sea Island, Malvan Scuba & Snorkeling nearby",
                "Take the morning ferry from Malvan Jetty and try coral snorkeling around the outer fort reef afterward.", "8:00 AM – 5:30 PM (Closed during peak monsoon rough seas)", "₹50 Entry (Boat ferry ₹110 return from Malvan Jetty)",
                new BigDecimal("16.0425"), new BigDecimal("73.4597"), 15, 2024, 680000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corporation (MTDC)", "Maharashtra Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leaving_Malvan_jetty_for_Sindhudurg_fort_(5363541711).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Murud-Janjira Sea Fort", "murud-janjira-sea-fort", "Raigad",
                "Unconquered 17th-century oval marine fortress rising 40 feet straight out of the Arabian Sea off Rajapuri near Alibaug and Murud.",
                "Approached by traditional wooden sailboats from Rajapuri jetty, Murud-Janjira is renowned as one of India's strongest marine forts, having withstood repeated naval sieges by the Portuguese, British, and Marathas. Its 22 rounded bastions still mount gigantic cannons—including Kalalbangdi, Chavri, and Landa Kasam—while its interior preserves multi-storey palace ruins and two large freshwater lakes in the middle of the saltwater bay.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Murud_Janjira_Fort_top_view,_Maharashtra.jpg",
                "October to May", "2 to 3 hours", "Originally a 15th-century wooden stockade built by Koli fishermen, expanded into an impregnable basalt fortress between 1567 and 1676 by Siddi Amber and the Siddis of Janjira.", "22 Intact Sea Bastions, Kalalbangdi Giant Cannon (3rd largest in India), Two Freshwater Lakes Inside the Sea Fort, Sailboat Transfer from Rajapuri",
                "At the fort gate, the sailboat only pauses briefly—listen carefully to the local boatmen's historical commentary inside the fort.", "7:30 AM – 5:00 PM daily", "₹25 ASI Entry (Sailboat transfer ₹80–₹100 per person)",
                new BigDecimal("18.2997"), new BigDecimal("72.9644"), 16, 2024, 610000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corporation (MTDC)", "Maharashtra Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Murud_Janjira_Fort_top_view,_Maharashtra.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Ganpatipule Beach & Swayambhu Temple", "ganpatipule-temple-and-beach", "Ratnagiri",
                "400-year-old beachfront Swayambhu Ganpati Temple nestled at the base of a sacred hill along Ratnagiri's golden Konkan coastline.",
                "One of the rarest coastal temples in India, the Swayambhu Ganpati Temple at Ganpatipule enshrines a naturally formed monolithic sandstone idol of Lord Ganesha facing west toward the Arabian Sea. Pilgrims perform a 1-km pradakshina (circumambulation) around the entire Ganesha-shaped coastal hill before walking directly onto the pristine 2-km golden beach flanked by mango, betel nut, and casuarina groves.",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ganpatipule_-_panoramio.jpg",
                "October to March", "Half Day to 2 Days", "Venerated for over 400 years as the Paschim Dwarpalak (Western Sentinel Deity) of India.", "Swayambhu West-Facing Ganesha Sanctum, 1-Km Hill Pradakshina Path, Ganpatipule Golden Beach, Prachin Konkan Open-Air Heritage Museum, Aare-Ware Twin Beaches Drive",
                "Drive the breathtaking coastal cliff road between Ganpatipule and Ratnagiri via Aare-Ware Twin Beaches and sample Ratnagiri Alphonso products and Solkadhi.", "Temple 5:00 AM – 9:00 PM daily; Beach Open 24 Hours", "Free temple & beach entry (Prachin Konkan Museum ₹80)",
                new BigDecimal("17.1447"), new BigDecimal("73.2667"), 17, 2024, 890000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corporation (MTDC)", "Maharashtra Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ganpatipule_-_panoramio.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Matheran Automobile-Free Hill Station", "matheran-eco-hill-station", "Raigad",
                "Asia's only automobile-free hill station at 800 meters in the Sahyadris, reached by the 1907 UNESCO-tentative Neral-Matheran narrow-gauge Toy Train.",
                "Declared an Eco-Sensitive Zone by the Ministry of Environment, Matheran (\"Forest on the Forehead\") bans all private motor vehicles beyond Dasturi Naka, preserving its red laterite forest trails, colonial Parsi and British heritage bungalows, and 38 cliffside viewpoints. Visitors arrive either via the historic 21-km Neral-Matheran Light Railway (built by Sir Adamjee Peerbhoy in 1907) or on foot along shaded canopy paths to Panorama Point, Louisa Point, and Charlotte Lake.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Toy_train_of_Matheran_(51980234322).jpg",
                "October to May", "1 to 2 Days", "Discovered in May 1850 by Hugh Poyntz Malet, Collector of Thane; the 2-foot narrow-gauge toy train opened in 1907.", "Neral-Matheran Heritage Toy Train, Charlotte Lake & Pisarnath Temple, Louisa Point & Echo Point, Panorama Sunrise Point, Red Laterite Forest Trails",
                "Park your vehicle at Dasturi Car Park (2.5 km before town) and walk or take the shuttle toy train from Aman Lodge into Matheran bazaar.", "Open 24 Hours (Toy Train scheduled departures)", "₹50 Municipal Eco-Capitation Tax; Toy Train ₹45–₹300",
                new BigDecimal("18.9848"), new BigDecimal("73.2712"), 18, 2024, 980000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corporation (MTDC)", "Maharashtra Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toy_train_of_Matheran_(51980234322).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Lonar Crater Lake", "lonar-meteorite-crater-lake", "Buldhana",
                "50,000-year-old hypervelocity meteorite impact crater in basaltic Deccan Trap rock—both saline and alkaline—ringed by medieval Hemadpanthi temples.",
                "Formed during the Pleistocene epoch when a 2-million-ton meteor slammed into the basalt volcanic plateau of Buldhana at 90,000 km/h, Lonar Crater spans 1.8 km in diameter and plunges 137 meters deep. Designated a National Geo-Heritage Monument and Ramsar Wetland, its emerald lake water is uniquely both saline and alkaline, supporting rare halo-alkaliphilic microbes, flamingos, and over a dozen 12th-century Yadava-era temples along its forested inner rim.",
                "Lake", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sunset_over_Lonar_Crater_Lake,_Maharashtra,_India.jpg",
                "October to March", "Half Day", "Mentioned in the Skanda Purana and Ain-i-Akbari; scientifically confirmed as a hypervelocity impact crater with maskelynite impact glass in the 20th century; Ramsar site since 2020.", "Rim Viewpoint & Gomukh Temple (Perennial Freshwater Spring), 12th-Century Daitya Sudan Temple in Lonar Town, Kamalja Devi Temple on the Crater Floor",
                "Start at the Gomukh Temple rim viewpoint before trekking down the stone steps through the forest canopy to the crater lake shore.", "6:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "₹25 Sanctuary & Trail Entry",
                new BigDecimal("19.9767"), new BigDecimal("76.5083"), 19, 2024, 265000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corporation (MTDC)", "Maharashtra Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sunset_over_Lonar_Crater_Lake,_Maharashtra,_India.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(maharashtra, "Kaas Plateau UNESCO Biodiversity Site", "kaas-plateau-valley-of-flowers", "Satara",
                "UNESCO World Natural Heritage volcanic laterite plateau at 1,200 meters near Satara, blooming with over 850 species of endemic wildflowers.",
                "Known as Maharashtra's Valley of Flowers (Kaas Pathar), this high-altitude basaltic laterite tableland 25 km west of Satara is part of the Western Ghats UNESCO World Heritage Site. Every late monsoon (August to October), its thin soil transforms into shifting carpets of purple, yellow, and pink blossoms—including Smithia, Utricularia (insectivorous bladderworts), Eriocaulon, and the rare Karvi shrub that blooms once every seven years.",
                "Nature", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Satara_to_Kaas_plateau_road_views_-_vrvbsatara102k23iph_(28).jpg",
                "Late August to October (Bloom Season) & Winter", "Half Day", "Inscribed as part of the Western Ghats UNESCO World Natural Heritage Site in 2012.", "Seasonal Wildflower Carpets (850+ Species), Kumudini Lake (Water Lily Pond), Kaas Lake, Vajrai Waterfall (Bambavli) nearby",
                "During the September–October bloom season, daily visitor numbers are capped to protect the fragile flora—book your slot online at kas.ind.in.", "7:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily (3-hour slots during bloom season)", "₹150 Eco-Conservation Entry",
                new BigDecimal("17.7214"), new BigDecimal("73.8222"), 20, 2024, 375000L, "TOTAL",
                "Maharashtra Tourism Development Corporation (MTDC)", "Maharashtra Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Satara_to_Kaas_plateau_road_views_-_vrvbsatara102k23iph_(28).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        // --- 14. ODISHA ---
        State odisha = createState("Odisha", "odisha", "STATE", "Eastern", "Bhubaneswar", "Land of Lord Jagannath, Konark Sun Temple, and Chilika Lake.",
                "Odisha features the UNESCO Konark Sun Temple, Jagannath Temple in Puri, and Chilika Lake, Asia's largest brackish water lagoon.",
                "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Lord_Jagannath_temple_at_night.jpg", "October to March");

        createDestination(odisha, "Jagannath Temple, Puri", "jagannath-temple-puri", "Puri",
                "The Jagannath Temple in Puri is a sacred 12th-century Char Dham pilgrimage shrine dedicated to Lord Jagannath, famous worldwide for the grand Ratha Yatra.",
                "Built by King Anantavarman Chodaganga Deva in the 12th century, this colossal Kalinga-style temple rises 65 meters above the sacred city of Puri. Revering the holy trinity of Lord Jagannath, Balabhadra, and Subhadra, the temple complex features the world's largest kitchen (Ananda Bazar) cooking Mahaprasad for thousands daily, and culminates in the epic annual Ratha Yatra chariot procession.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Lord_Jagannath_temple_at_night.jpg",
                "October to March (Ratha Yatra in June/July)", "3 to 4 hours", "One of the supreme Char Dham pilgrimage sites of Hinduism, an enduring symbol of Odia religious life for nearly a millennium.", "Bada Deula (Main Sanctum), Aruna Stambha, Ananda Bazar (Mahaprasad Kitchen), Mukti Mandapa, Meghanada Pacheri walls",
                "Non-Hindus are not permitted inside the inner sanctum but can view the temple from the Raghunandan Library roof; dress traditionally.", "5:00 AM – 10:00 PM daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("19.8049"), new BigDecimal("85.8179"), 1, 2024, 15000000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lord_Jagannath_temple_at_night.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Konark Sun Temple", "konark-sun-temple", "Puri",
                "UNESCO World Heritage Site designed as a monumental 24-wheeled stone chariot of the Sun God pulled by seven horses, a peak of Kalinga architecture.",
                "Constructed around 1250 CE by King Narasimhadeva I of the Eastern Ganga Dynasty, Konark Sun Temple is an engineering triumph carved in stone. The entire temple is shaped as a colossal celestial chariot with 24 intricately sculpted sundial wheels that tell time accurately to the minute, adorned with erotic sculptures and mythical beasts.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Wheel_engraved_in_the_13th_century_built_Konark_Sun_Temple_in_Orissa,_India.jpg",
                "September to March", "2 to 3 hours", "A masterpiece of 13th-century Kalinga architecture, described by Rabindranath Tagore as a place where 'the language of stone surpasses the language of human.'", "Intricately Carved Sundial Wheels, Natya Mandap (Dance Hall), Horse Sculptures, Light & Sound Show, Archaeological Museum",
                "Hire an ASI-certified guide to decode the astronomical sundials; attend the mesmerizing evening light and sound show.", "6:00 AM – 8:00 PM daily", "₹40 for Indians, SAARC & BIMSTEC; ₹600 for foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("19.8876"), new BigDecimal("86.0945"), 2, 2024, 2800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wheel_engraved_in_the_13th_century_built_Konark_Sun_Temple_in_Orissa,_India.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Chilika Lake", "chilika-lake", "Puri / Khordha / Ganjam",
                "Chilika Lake is Asia's largest brackish water lagoon, a Ramsar wetland sheltering endangered Irrawaddy dolphins and millions of migratory winter birds.",
                "Stretching over 1,100 square kilometers along the Bay of Bengal, Chilika Lake is a dynamic estuarine ecosystem dotted with islands. It is one of only two lagoons on earth harboring the rare Irrawaddy dolphin, best observed near Satapada. Every winter, over a million migratory birds fly from Siberia, the Caspian Sea, and Central Asia to Nalabana Bird Sanctuary.",
                "Lake", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chilika_Lake_or_Chilka_in_Odisha_08.jpg",
                "November to February", "Half Day to Full Day", "Mentioned by the 4th-century poet Kalidasa and Ptolemy; ancient Odia merchants (Sadhabas) set sail from Chilika ports to Southeast Asia on Boita Bandana.", "Satapada Dolphin Watching, Nalabana Bird Sanctuary, Kalijai Temple Island, Mangalajodi Birding Wetland, Honeymoon Island",
                "Take early morning wooden boat tours at Mangalajodi for close-up bird photography; OTDC operates regulated boat trips from Satapada.", "6:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily (Boating operating hours)", "Free entry; Motorized boats ₹1200–₹2500 per boat",
                new BigDecimal("19.7167"), new BigDecimal("85.3167"), 3, 2024, 2100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chilika_Lake_or_Chilka_in_Odisha_08.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Lingaraj Temple", "lingaraj-temple", "Bhubaneswar",
                "Lingaraj Temple is an 11th-century architectural crown jewel of Bhubaneswar, rising 55 meters in quintessential Kalinga deula spire honoring Harihara.",
                "Representing the zenith of Kalinga architectural genius, Lingaraj Temple dominates the skyline of the 'Temple City' of Bhubaneswar. Built by the Somavamsi dynasty, the sanctum enshrines Lord Shiva worshipped in the syncretic form of Harihara (half Shiva, half Vishnu). The walled complex encompasses over 150 smaller subsidiary shrines and the sacred Bindusagar Lake.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Lingaraja_Temple_01.jpg",
                "October to March (Shivaratri in Feb/March)", "1 to 2 hours", "Constructed during the 11th century CE by King Jajati Keshari, crystallizing the synthesis of Shaivism and Vaishnavism in classical Odisha.", "55m Deula Tower, Jagamohana (Assembly Hall), Natamandira (Dance Pavilion), Bindusagar Lake, Parvati Temple",
                "Non-Hindus can view the magnificent temple complex from a dedicated viewing platform erected by Lord Curzon outside the northern wall.", "6:00 AM – 9:00 PM daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("20.2381"), new BigDecimal("85.8336"), 4, 2024, 3500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lingaraja_Temple_01.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Udayagiri and Khandagiri Caves", "udayagiri-and-khandagiri-caves", "Bhubaneswar",
                "Ancient rock-cut cave dwellings carved into twin hills during the 2nd century BCE for Jain ascetics, featuring King Kharavela's famous Hathigumpha inscription.",
                "Perched on the outskirts of Bhubaneswar, Udayagiri (Sunrise Hill, 18 caves) and Khandagiri (Broken Hill, 15 caves) were chiseled out of sandstone cliffs under the patronage of King Kharavela of the Mahameghavahana dynasty. Notable caves include the double-storeyed Rani Gumpha with exquisite relief friezes, and Hathigumpha with its 17-line Brahmi inscription recording Kharavela's military conquests.",
                "Caves", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Udayagiri_and_Khandagiri_Caves_2024_11.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Dates back to the 2nd–1st century BCE, home to the primary historical epigraph documenting the reign and achievements of Emperor Kharavela.", "Rani Gumpha (Queen's Cave), Hathigumpha (Elephant Cave), Ganesha Gumpha, Navamuni Cave, Jain Tirthankara reliefs",
                "Visit in the early morning to avoid the heat and monkeys; explore both hills connected by an easy walking path.", "9:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "₹25 for Indians, SAARC & BIMSTEC; ₹300 for foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("20.2575"), new BigDecimal("85.7864"), 5, 2024, 1200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Udayagiri_and_Khandagiri_Caves_2024_11.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Dhauli Shanti Stupa", "dhauli-shanti-stupa", "Bhubaneswar",
                "Dhauli Shanti Stupa is an iconic white peace pagoda standing atop the hill overlooking the Daya River, where Emperor Ashoka embraced Buddhism after the Kalinga War.",
                "Erected jointly in 1972 by the Kalinga Nippon Buddha Sangha and the Government of Odisha, the dazzling white Shanti Stupa crowns Dhauli Hill. Below the stupa lie the 3rd-century BCE Major Rock Edicts of Ashoka and a monumental rock-cut elephant head, commemorating Ashoka's profound spiritual transformation from Chandashoka to Dharmashoka.",
                "Buddhist Stupa", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Shanti_Stupa%2C_Dhauli_01.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 hours", "Fought here in 261 BCE, the bloody Kalinga War resulted in immense carnage that caused Emperor Ashoka to renounce violence and adopt non-violence (Ahimsa).", "White Peace Pagoda, 3rd Century BCE Ashokan Rock Edicts, Rock-Cut Elephant, Daya River Valley View, Evening Light and Sound Show",
                "Attend the evening sound and light show narrating the story of Emperor Ashoka; sunset views over the Daya River are breathtaking.", "6:00 AM – 8:00 PM daily", "Free entry; Light & Sound show ₹25 per person",
                new BigDecimal("20.1925"), new BigDecimal("85.8394"), 6, 2024, 1600000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shanti_Stupa,_Dhauli_01.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Similipal National Park", "similipal-national-park", "Mayurbhanj",
                "Similipal is a vast UNESCO Biosphere Reserve and Tiger Reserve famed for melanistic (black) tigers, roaring waterfalls, and dense red silk cotton forests.",
                "Covering 2,750 square kilometers in Mayurbhanj District, Similipal derives its name from 'Simul' (red silk cotton tree). It is globally unique as the world's only natural habitat for wild pseudo-melanistic (black) Royal Bengal tigers. The reserve features spectacular waterfalls like Joranda and Barehipani, rolling sal forests, and wild elephant herds.",
                "National Park", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Palpala_River_near_lulung%2C_Similipal_National_Park.jpg",
                "November to June", "2 to 3 days", "Once the exclusive royal hunting grounds of the Mayurbhanj Maharajas, notified under Project Tiger in 1973 and UNESCO Biosphere Reserve in 2009.", "Barehipani Falls (399m), Joranda Falls, Melanistic Tiger Habitat, Chahala Wildlife Watchtower, Lulung Valley",
                "Entry permits must be obtained at Pithabata or Jashipur entry gates; only 4WD vehicles are allowed inside the park.", "6:00 AM – 9:00 AM (Entry gates open for day visitors)", "₹100 for Indians; ₹1000 for foreigners; Vehicle permit ₹200",
                new BigDecimal("21.8667"), new BigDecimal("86.3333"), 7, 2024, 180000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palpala_River_near_lulung,_Similipal_National_Park.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Bhitarkanika National Park", "bhitarkanika-national-park", "Kendrapara",
                "Bhitarkanika is India's second-largest mangrove ecosystem, celebrated for giant saltwater crocodiles, King cobras, and the olive ridley turtle nesting beach.",
                "Interlaced by estuarine rivers, creeks, and mudflats, Bhitarkanika National Park shelters one of the richest mangrove forests on the subcontinent. The park holds the Guinness World Record for the largest recorded saltwater crocodiles (over 23 feet). Neighboring Gahirmatha Beach is the world's most critical mass nesting rookery (arribada) for endangered Olive Ridley sea turtles.",
                "National Park", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bhitarkanika_Mangroves_Flora_and_Fauna_04.JPG",
                "October to March (Turtle nesting: Jan to March)", "1 to 2 days", "Former royal hunting preserve of the Kanika Rajas, declared a Ramsar Wetland of International Importance in 2002.", "Saltwater Crocodile Boat Safari, Gahirmatha Turtle Rookery, Dangmal Crocodile Breeding Center, Bagagahana Heronry",
                "Board a boat from Khola or Gupti entry points; keep arms inside the boats as giant crocodiles sunbathe along the mudbanks.", "7:00 AM – 5:00 PM daily", "₹40 for Indians; ₹500 for foreigners; Boat hire charges extra",
                new BigDecimal("20.7333"), new BigDecimal("86.8667"), 8, 2024, 220000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bhitarkanika_Mangroves_Flora_and_Fauna_04.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Hirakud Dam", "hirakud-dam", "Sambalpur",
                "Hirakud Dam on the Mahanadi River is one of the world's longest earthen dams, stretching 25.8 km and forming an inland freshwater sea.",
                "Constructed across the mighty Mahanadi River near Sambalpur, Hirakud Dam is an engineering wonder of post-independence India. Inaugurated by Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru in 1957, the main dam and dykes extend for nearly 26 kilometers. Visitors ascend the Gandhi Minar and Nehru Minar observation towers for sweeping views across the vast artificial reservoir.",
                "Dam", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Hirakud_Dam_Viewpoint%2C_Sambalpur.jpg",
                "September to March", "2 to 3 hours", "One of the earliest major multipurpose river valley projects launched after Indian independence to control devastating Mahanadi floods and generate power.", "Gandhi Minar Observation Tower, Nehru Minar, Hirakud Reservoir Boating, Debrigarh Wildlife Sanctuary fringes",
                "Gandhi Minar has a revolving restaurant structure offering 360-degree views; visit in late monsoon to witness water gushing through sluice gates.", "6:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "Free (Nominal fee of ₹10–₹20 at observation towers)",
                new BigDecimal("21.57"), new BigDecimal("83.87"), 9, 2024, 650000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hirakud_Dam_Viewpoint,_Sambalpur.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Chandipur Beach", "chandipur-beach", "Balasore",
                "Chandipur Beach is famous for its unique 'vanishing sea' phenomenon where the tide recedes up to 5 kilometers twice a day, letting visitors walk on the seabed.",
                "Located 16 km from Balasore, Chandipur is unlike any typical beach in the world. Due to unique coastal bathymetry, the sea recedes up to 5 kilometers into the horizon during low tide every day and rushes back during high tide. The vast exposed wet sands allow travelers to stroll on the seafloor, discovering red crabs, horseshoe crabs, and seashells.",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chandipur_sea_beach_Baleswar.jpg",
                "October to March", "Half Day", "Adjacent to the Integrated Test Range (ITR) at Abdul Kalam Island, where India successfully tests Agni and Prithvi ballistic missiles.", "Vanishing Tide Phenomenon, Walking on the Seabed, Horseshoe Crab Sightings, Casuarina Grove Sunsets",
                "Check local tide tables before planning your walk; make sure to return to the shoreline well before the incoming high tide begins.", "Open 24 hours", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("21.4719"), new BigDecimal("87.0222"), 10, 2024, 550000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chandipur_sea_beach_Baleswar.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Gopalpur Beach", "gopalpur-beach", "Ganjam",
                "Gopalpur-on-Sea is a historic port town featuring golden sandy beaches, coconut groves, a heritage lighthouse, and calm maritime ambiance.",
                "Once a bustling commercial seaport for ancient maritime trade with Burma during British rule, Gopalpur-on-Sea is now one of Odisha's most serene coastal retreats. Fringed by casuarina groves and coconut palms, the beach offers soft golden sands, horse rides, and sweeping views of the Bay of Bengal from its red-and-white colonial lighthouse.",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gopalpur_%27s_beach%2C_the_ocean.JPG",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 days", "Thrived as an important maritime port during the British East India Company era, trading rice and silks with Rangoon (Yangon).", "Gopalpur Lighthouse, Heritage Pier Ruins, Beach Promenade, Seafood Shacks, Tampara Lake Excursion",
                "Climb the lighthouse between 3:30 PM and 5:30 PM for a spectacular bird's-eye view of the coastline and breaking surf.", "Open 24 hours (Lighthouse: 3:30 PM – 5:30 PM)", "Free (Lighthouse entry ₹20)",
                new BigDecimal("19.26"), new BigDecimal("84.91"), 11, 2024, 700000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gopalpur_'s_beach,_the_ocean.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Raghurajpur Heritage Village", "raghurajpur-heritage-village", "Puri",
                "Raghurajpur is an internationally acclaimed heritage crafts village where every family practices traditional Pattachitra scroll painting and Gotipua dance.",
                "Nestled among coconut, betel nut, and mango groves on the banks of the Bhargabi River near Puri, Raghurajpur is an open-air art gallery. All 140 households in the village are master Chitrakars (artists) specializing in intricate Pattachitra cloth scrolls, palm leaf engravings, and wooden toys. It is also the birthplace of legendary Odissi maestro Guru Kelucharan Mohapatra.",
                "Heritage Village", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Raghurajpur_-_Decorated_Village_House.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 4 hours", "Preserves the ancient artistic tradition of creating replacement deities (Anasara Patti) during the annual seclusion of Lord Jagannath.", "Mural-Decorated Houses, Pattachitra Art Studios, Palm Leaf Inscriptions (Talapatra Chitra), Gotipua Folk Dance Academy",
                "Engage with artists inside their verandas to watch raw mineral pigments being prepared and buy original art directly from creators.", "8:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("19.8667"), new BigDecimal("85.8333"), 12, 2024, 300000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raghurajpur_-_Decorated_Village_House.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Ratnagiri Buddhist Site", "ratnagiri-buddhist-site", "Jajpur",
                "Ratnagiri is the most excavated site of the 'Diamond Triangle' of Buddhism in Odisha, famed for a carved green chlorite monastery doorway and colossal Buddha heads.",
                "Flourishing from the 5th to the 12th century CE as a premier center of Tantric Buddhism (Vajrayana), Ratnagiri rivaled Nalanda in intellectual prestige. Excavations have revealed a magnificent central stupa, multi-storeyed monasteries, thousands of votive stupas, and a famously carved green chlorite portal leading to the sanctum.",
                "Buddhist Site", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ratnagiri_-_Odisha_-_001.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Mentioned in Tibetan texts as a major cradle of the Kalachakra Tantra school, excavated systematically by the Archaeological Survey of India.", "Carved Chlorite Monastery Doorway, Colossal Seated Buddha Statues, Monastic Courtyard, ASI Archaeological Museum, Mahastupa",
                "Pair with nearby Lalitgiri and Udayagiri to experience the complete Diamond Triangle circuit of ancient Buddhist Odisha.", "9:00 AM – 5:00 PM daily (Museum closed Fridays)", "₹25 for Indians, SAARC & BIMSTEC; ₹300 for foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("20.64"), new BigDecimal("86.3333"), 13, 2024, 150000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ratnagiri_-_Odisha_-_001.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Lalitgiri Buddhist Site", "lalitgiri-buddhist-site", "Cuttack",
                "Lalitgiri is the oldest Buddhist complex in Odisha dating back to the 1st century BCE, where sacred bone relics believed to be of Gautama Buddha were unearthed.",
                "Occupying the lush slopes of the Parabhadi and Landa hills, Lalitgiri is the earliest documented Buddhist establishment in Odisha. The excavation of a large hemispherical stupa yielded three nested casket reliquaries made of khondalite, steatite, silver, and gold containing sacred bone relics. The site also contains an apsidal brick chaityagriha and a modern ASI museum.",
                "Buddhist Site", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Lalitgiri_-_Odisha_-_001.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Continuous Buddhist occupation from the 3rd century BCE to the 13th century CE, confirmed by the discovery of authentic caskets containing Buddha relics.", "Mahastupa Relic Chamber, Apsidal Chaityagriha, Monastic Quadrangle, ASI Sculpture Museum, Buddha Statues",
                "The state-of-the-art ASI museum houses the holy relics; climb the hill summit stupa for peaceful rural panoramas.", "9:00 AM – 5:00 PM daily (Museum closed Fridays)", "₹25 for Indians; ₹300 for foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("20.5892"), new BigDecimal("86.2514"), 14, 2024, 140000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lalitgiri_-_Odisha_-_001.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Satkosia Gorge", "satkosia-gorge", "Angul / Nayagarh",
                "Satkosia is a 22 km scenic gorge carved through the Eastern Ghats by the Mahanadi River, sheltering breeding gharials, mugger crocodiles, and tigers.",
                "Where the mighty Mahanadi River slices through the rugged hills of the Eastern Ghats over a length of 'Sat Kos' (approx. 22 km), it forms the spectacular Satkosia Gorge. The sanctuary protects a unique confluence of moist peninsular sal forests and riverine canyons inhabited by elephants, leopards, and thriving populations of freshwater Gharial crocodiles.",
                "Wildlife Sanctuary", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Satkosia_gorge.jpg",
                "November to April", "1 to 2 days", "Notified as a tiger reserve in 2007, pioneer of the pioneering Gharial Research and Conservation Unit founded at Tikarpada in 1975.", "Mahanadi River Canyon Boat Safari, Tikarpada Gharial Sanctuary, Nature Camps at Chhotkei and Purunakote, Jungle Treks",
                "Stay in OTDC luxury eco-tents right on the sandy riverbanks of the Mahanadi; boat rides through the canyon offer breathtaking cliff photography.", "6:00 AM – 5:00 PM daily", "₹50 entry fee; Boat safari ₹500–₹1000",
                new BigDecimal("20.5833"), new BigDecimal("84.8667"), 15, 2024, 110000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Satkosia_gorge.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Deomali Hills", "deomali-hills", "Koraput",
                "Deomali is the highest mountain peak in Odisha at 1,672 meters, celebrated for rolling cloud-capped meadows, tribal hamlets, and paragliding.",
                "Crowning the Chandragiri-Pottangi sub-range in Koraput District, Deomali is the highest mountain peak in the state. The hill features emerald undulating slopes reminiscent of rolling European downs, surrounded by rich tribal villages of the Kandha and Paraja communities. It has become a premier hotspot for hiking, stargazing, and paragliding.",
                "Peak", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Deomali_by_Santanu_Mahapatra%2C_Koraput_-_panoramio.jpg",
                "September to March", "Half Day to Full Day", "Highest geographic peak of Odisha, celebrated in regional folk songs and tribal folklore as the crown of the Eastern Ghats.", "1,672m Summit Observation Points, Rolling Grassland Downs, Sunset Viewpoints, Indigenous Tribal Markets, Paragliding Launch",
                "Drive up from Damanjodi or Semiliguda; morning sunrise sees a spectacular sea of clouds swirling around the hilltops.", "Open 24 hours (Daylight visits recommended)", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("18.6667"), new BigDecimal("82.9833"), 16, 2024, 190000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deomali_by_Santanu_Mahapatra,_Koraput_-_panoramio.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Taratarini Temple", "taratarini-temple", "Ganjam",
                "Taratarini Temple is a revered ancient Shakti Peetha perched on the crest of Purnagiri Hill overlooking the Rushikulya River near Brahmapur.",
                "Perched dramatically atop Purnagiri Hill at an elevation of 708 feet, Taratarini is revered as the Sthana Peetha (breast shrine) among the four ancient Adi Shakti Peethas. The twin goddesses Tara and Tarini are worshipped as the presiding deities of ancient seafarers. Devotees reach the reconstructed Kalinga-style temple via 999 stone steps or a scenic modern ropeway.",
                "Temple", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Taratarini_Temple%2C_Odisha.jpg",
                "October to April (Chaitra Mela in March/April)", "2 to 3 hours", "Historically venerated as the patron deity of ancient Kalinga sailors traveling to Sri Lanka, Java, and Bali across the Bay of Bengal.", "Hilltop Kalinga Sanctum, Rushikulya River Valley Panorama, 999 Holy Steps, Cable Car Ropeway, Chaitra Yatra",
                "Take the cable car ropeway for thrilling aerial views of the Rushikulya River; Tuesday visits are particularly sacred.", "5:00 AM – 9:00 PM daily", "Free (Ropeway ticket ₹60 round-trip)",
                new BigDecimal("19.4936"), new BigDecimal("84.8967"), 17, 2024, 2200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taratarini_Temple,_Odisha.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Barabati Fort", "barabati-fort", "Cuttack",
                "Barabati Fort is a 14th-century citadel in Cuttack, celebrated for its stone moat, grand arched gateway, and ruins of a nine-storeyed Ganga dynasty palace.",
                "Situated along the Mahanadi River in Cuttack, Barabati Fort was built by the Eastern Ganga Dynasty around the 14th century. The fortress featured a deep protective moat, high earthen ramparts, and a legendary nine-storey palace described by Mughal and British chroniclers. Today, its carved arched gateway and excavated central mound stand as majestic reminders of Odisha's military past.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Barabati_Fort_%2C_Cuttack_01.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 hours", "Seat of imperial power for the Ganga, Gajapati, and Maratha rulers before falling to the British East India Company in 1803.", "Historic Arched Gateway, Nine-Storeyed Palace Mound Excavations, Ancient Stone Moat, Adjacent Barabati Stadium",
                "Combine with a visit to the nearby Cuttack Netaji Birthplace Museum and Odisha Maritime Museum.", "8:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "₹20 for Indians; ₹100 for foreign visitors",
                new BigDecimal("20.4853"), new BigDecimal("85.8672"), 18, 2024, 450000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barabati_Fort_,_Cuttack_01.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Nandankanan Zoological Park", "nandankanan-zoological-park", "Bhubaneswar",
                "Nandankanan ('Garden of Heavens') is a premier 437-hectare zoo and botanical garden famous as the world's first captive breeding center for white tigers.",
                "Nestled in the lush Chandaka forest beside Kanjia Lake, Nandankanan Zoological Park is internationally renowned for wildlife conservation. It was the world's first zoo where white tigers were born from normal-colored parents in captivity. The zoo features an expansive lion safari, white tiger safari, herbivore safari, ropeway, and open-air natural moats.",
                "Zoo", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nandankanan%2C_Bhubaneswar%2C_Odisha.jpg",
                "October to March", "Half Day to Full Day", "Inaugurated in 1960, Nandankanan became a pioneer in white tiger breeding, gharial crocodile breeding, and captive pangolin conservation.", "White Tiger & Lion Safaris, Kanjia Lake Ropeway, Toy Train, Reptile Park, Nocturnal Animal House, Botanical Garden",
                "Rent a battery-operated cart if visiting with seniors or kids; book safari tickets early at the main ticketing counters.", "7:30 AM – 5:30 PM (April–Sep); 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM (Oct–March; Closed Mondays)", "₹50 for adults; ₹10 for children; Safari combo ₹60 extra",
                new BigDecimal("20.3956"), new BigDecimal("85.8267"), 19, 2024, 3400000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nandankanan,_Bhubaneswar,_Odisha.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(odisha, "Khandadhar Waterfall", "khandadhar-waterfall", "Sundargarh / Kendujhar",
                "Khandadhar Waterfall plunges 244 meters like a gleaming sword edge through dense virgin hills, making it the second-highest waterfall in Odisha.",
                "Dropping in a sheer, unbroken column of mist and spray, Khandadhar gets its name from its resemblance to a gleaming sword ('Khanda' meaning sword, 'Dhar' meaning blade). Surrounded by untouched deciduous forests and mineral-rich hills, the cascade creates a perpetual rainbow in the sunlight and offers nature trails to its foaming plunge pool.",
                "Waterfall", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Khandadhar_Waterfall_Kendujhar_01.jpg",
                "October to March", "Half Day", "Held sacred by indigenous Paudi Bhuyan tribal communities who revere the waterfall and its surrounding sacred mountain groves.", "244-meter Single-Drop Cascade, Foaming Plunge Basin, Forest Hiking Trails, Picnic Lawns",
                "Wear comfortable walking shoes with good traction for the descent to the waterfall basin; carry snacks as food stalls are limited.", "7:00 AM – 5:30 PM daily", "₹20 entry fee",
                new BigDecimal("21.75"), new BigDecimal("85.1167"), 20, 2024, 280000L, "TOTAL",
                "Odisha Tourism (OTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khandadhar_Waterfall_Kendujhar_01.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        // --- 15. RAJASTHAN ---
        State rajasthan = createState("Rajasthan", "rajasthan", "STATE", "Western", "Jaipur", "Land of Kings — majestic forts, royal palaces, Thar desert, and rich folk music.",
                "India's iconic royal desert state featuring Pink City Jaipur, Blue City Jodhpur, Golden City Jaisalmer, and Lake City Udaipur.",
                "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jaipur_03-2016_02_Amber_Fort.jpg", "October to March");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Amber Fort", "amber-fort", "Jaipur",
                "UNESCO World Heritage hill fortress overlooking Maota Lake, famous for Rajput-Mughal architectural harmony, ornate courtyards, and the glittering Sheesh Mahal.",
                "Perched high on the rugged Aravalli hills in Amer, 11 km from Jaipur, Amber Fort was constructed in red sandstone and yellow marble by Raja Man Singh I in 1592. The fortress encompasses magnificent ceremonial courtyards, subterranean escape tunnels connecting to Jaigarh Fort, and the world-famous Sheesh Mahal (Mirror Palace) where candlelight mirrors infinitely across thousands of convex glass inlays.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jaipur_03-2016_02_Amber_Fort.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "The royal seat of the Kachwaha Rajputs from the 11th century until Sawai Jai Singh II founded Jaipur in 1727; inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site.", "Sheesh Mahal (Mirror Palace), Diwan-e-Aam, Diwan-e-Khas, Ganesh Pol gateway, Maota Lake, Kesar Kyari gardens",
                "Arrive early in the morning to beat the afternoon desert heat and tour crowds; attend the evening sound and light show.", "8:00 AM – 5:30 PM, 6:30 PM – 9:15 PM daily", "₹100 for Indians; ₹500 for foreigners; Composite tickets available",
                new BigDecimal("26.9855"), new BigDecimal("75.8513"), 1, 2024, 2800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaipur_03-2016_02_Amber_Fort.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Hawa Mahal", "hawa-mahal", "Jaipur",
                "Hawa Mahal ('Palace of Winds') is an iconic 5-storey pink sandstone facade featuring 953 jharokha honeycomb windows designed for royal women to observe city festivals.",
                "Built in 1799 by Maharaja Sawai Pratap Singh and designed by Lal Chand Ustad, the Hawa Mahal resembles the crown of Lord Krishna. Its pyramid-shaped five-storey facade contains 953 finely latticed casements that allow continuous cool breezes to circulate through the structure, enabling royal purdah women to observe bustling street life and religious processions unseen from outside.",
                "Palace", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/20191218%2C_Hawa_Mahal_(The_Palace_of_Winds)_in_Jaipur%2C_1128_9119.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 hours", "An architectural wonder of Rajput royal architecture, built to observe the strict purdah customs while keeping royal women engaged with city life.", "953 Honeycomb Jharokhas, Stained Glass Windows, Panoramic City Views toward Jantar Mantar, Heritage Museum",
                "Early morning is the best time for photography as golden morning sunlight illuminates the red and pink sandstone facade from the street opposite.", "9:00 AM – 5:00 PM daily", "₹50 for Indians; ₹200 for foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("26.9239"), new BigDecimal("75.8267"), 2, 2024, 2200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20191218,_Hawa_Mahal_(The_Palace_of_Winds)_in_Jaipur,_1128_9119.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "City Palace, Jaipur", "city-palace-jaipur", "Jaipur",
                "The City Palace is the sprawling royal residence of the Jaipur Maharaja, blending Rajput, Mughal, and European architecture with grand courtyards and museums.",
                "Established by Maharaja Sawai Jai Singh II along with the planned city of Jaipur in 1727, the City Palace occupies a seventh of the walled city. The complex encompasses vast public and private durbars, including the Peacock Gate in Pritam Niwas Chowk, the Mubarak Mahal textiles museum, and the private Chandra Mahal where the titular royal family still resides.",
                "Palace", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jaipur_03-2016_20_City_Palace_complex.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "The administrative and residential seat of Jaipur's Kachwaha dynasty since the foundation of the Pink City in 1727.", "Chandra Mahal, Mubarak Mahal, Pritam Niwas Chowk (Peacock Gate), Giant Sterling Silver Urns (Gangajalis), Royal Armoury",
                "Don't miss the two colossal sterling silver vessels in Diwan-e-Khas, recorded in Guinness World Records as the world's largest sterling silver objects.", "9:30 AM – 5:00 PM, 7:00 PM – 9:30 PM (Night tour) daily", "₹75 for Indians; ₹300 for foreigners (Special royal rooms tour passes extra)",
                new BigDecimal("26.9256"), new BigDecimal("75.8236"), 3, 2024, 1900000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaipur_03-2016_20_City_Palace_complex.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Jaisalmer Fort", "jaisalmer-fort", "Jaisalmer",
                "UNESCO World Heritage 'Sonar Qila' (Golden Fort) is one of the world's few living forts, rising from the Thar Desert with thousands of residents inside its walls.",
                "Constructed in 1156 CE by Rajput ruler Rawal Jaisal atop the Trikuta Hill, Jaisalmer Fort is a golden sandstone marvel that seems to emerge organically from the Thar Desert sands. Unlike most forts, it is a bustling living city where one-fourth of Jaisalmer's population lives within its 99 defensive bastions, surrounded by medieval havelis, Jain temples, and rooftop eateries.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jaisalmer_forteresse.jpg",
                "October to March", "3 to 4 hours", "A critical caravan crossroads along the ancient Silk Road connecting India with Persia, Arabia, and Central Asia through the Thar Desert.", "Raj Mahal (Palace of the Maharawal), 7 Medieval Jain Temples, Laxminath Temple, 99 Sandstone Bastions, Desert Viewpoints",
                "Explore the labyrinthine alleys on foot; catch sunset from Cannon Point or a rooftop cafe as the yellow sandstone glows deep amber.", "Open 24 hours (Palace museum: 9:00 AM – 6:00 PM)", "Free entry to fort; ₹100 for palace museum (Indians), ₹250 (foreigners)",
                new BigDecimal("26.9128"), new BigDecimal("70.9125"), 4, 2024, 1400000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaisalmer_forteresse.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Mehrangarh Fort", "mehrangarh-fort", "Jodhpur",
                "Mehrangarh Fort is a colossal 15th-century citadel perched atop a 400-foot cliff over Jodhpur's blue city, described by Rudyard Kipling as 'the work of giants'.",
                "Founded in 1459 by Rao Jodha, Mehrangarh Fort stands imposingly 125 meters above Jodhpur's skyline. Its formidable defensive battlements conceal some of India's most opulent palace courtyards: the Sheesh Mahal, Phool Mahal, and Moti Mahal. The fort museum houses an unmatched collection of royal palanquins, turbans, howdahs, and Rajput armory.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mehrangarh_Fort_2%2C_Jodhpur%2C_Rajasthan%2C_India.jpg",
                "October to March", "3 to 4 hours", "Impregnable fortress home of the Rathore clan of Marwar, never captured by assault throughout centuries of Mughal and regional warfare.", "Sheesh Mahal, Phool Mahal (Flower Palace), Howdah and Palanquin Museum, Chamunda Devi Temple, Rampart Cannons",
                "Take the audio guide narrated by the Maharaja of Jodhpur; zipline with Flying Fox across the fort's battlements and desert lakes.", "9:00 AM – 5:00 PM daily", "₹100 for Indians; ₹600 for foreigners (Audio guide extra)",
                new BigDecimal("26.2978"), new BigDecimal("73.0186"), 5, 2024, 1700000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mehrangarh_Fort_2,_Jodhpur,_Rajasthan,_India.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Udaipur City Palace", "udaipur-city-palace", "Udaipur",
                "Rajasthan's largest royal palace complex, perched gracefully on the eastern banks of Lake Pichola with ornate marble balconies, towers, and glass mosaics.",
                "Begun in 1559 by Maharana Udai Singh II after the fall of Chittorgarh, Udaipur City Palace grew under 22 successive Maharanas into an enormous 244-meter-long architectural masterpiece. Overlooking Lake Pichola, the palace seamlessly synthesizes Mewari and Mughal aesthetics, housing the celebrated Mor Chowk (Peacock Courtyard) with vivid glass mosaic peacocks and the Crystal Gallery.",
                "Palace", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/City_Palace_Udaipur_Rajasthan_India.JPG",
                "October to March", "3 to 4 hours", "The majestic administrative seat of the Suryavanshi Maharanas of Mewar, who maintained fierce independence against Mughal subjugation.", "Mor Chowk (Peacock Courtyard), Manak Mahal, Zenana Mahal, Crystal Gallery, Vintage Car Museum, Lake Pichola Promenade",
                "Combine with an evening boat cruise from the palace jetty to Jagmandir Island; wear comfortable shoes for walking up palace ramps.", "9:00 AM – 5:30 PM daily", "₹300 for adults; ₹100 for children; Camera fee ₹200",
                new BigDecimal("24.5764"), new BigDecimal("73.6836"), 6, 2024, 1800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:City_Palace_Udaipur_Rajasthan_India.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Lake Pichola", "lake-pichola", "Udaipur",
                "Lake Pichola is an enchanting 14th-century artificial freshwater lake famous for the gleaming white Taj Lake Palace and romantic sunset boat cruises.",
                "Created in 1362 by a Banjara gypsy grain merchant and later expanded by Maharana Udai Singh II, Lake Pichola is the crown jewel of Udaipur's romantic cityscape. The lake's calm waters mirror the Aravalli hills, the City Palace, and four picturesque islands: Jag Niwas (site of the floating Lake Palace), Jag Mandir, Mohan Mandir, and Arsi Vilas.",
                "Lake", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Pichola_lake_sunset.JPG",
                "September to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Prince Khurram (later Emperor Shah Jahan) took refuge on Jag Mandir island in 1623, drawing architectural inspiration for the Taj Mahal dome.", "Sunset Boat Cruises, Taj Lake Palace (Jag Niwas), Jag Mandir Island Palace, Gangaur Ghat, Ambrai Ghat",
                "Book the golden-hour sunset boat ride from Rameshwar Ghat or Dudh Talai for unforgettable views of the glowing marble palaces.", "Open 24 hours (Boating: 9:00 AM – 6:00 PM)", "Free lake access; Boat rides ₹400–₹800 per person",
                new BigDecimal("24.57"), new BigDecimal("73.675"), 7, 2024, 2100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pichola_lake_sunset.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Chittorgarh Fort", "chittorgarh-fort", "Chittorgarh",
                "UNESCO World Heritage Site and India's largest fort, spanning 700 acres atop a 590-foot hill, embodying the chivalry, sacrifice, and Jauhar of Mewar.",
                "Spreading across a 700-acre plateau, Chittorgarh Fort is the eternal symbol of Rajput pride and martial valour. The fort withstood three legendary sieges—by Alauddin Khalji in 1303, Bahadur Shah in 1535, and Akbar in 1567—each culminating in the defiant sacrifice of Jauhar by royal women. It contains 65 historic structures, including the iconic Vijay Stambh and Kirti Stambh.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chittorgarh_fort.JPG",
                "October to March", "Half Day to Full Day", "The ancient capital of Mewar, immortalized by Rani Padmini, Maharana Kumbha, and Meera Bai's devotional poetry.", "Vijay Stambh (Tower of Victory), Kirti Stambh, Padmini's Palace, Gaumukh Reservoir, Meera Temple, Rana Kumbha Palace",
                "Hire an auto-rickshaw or taxi inside the sprawling fort complex as monuments are kilometers apart; climb Vijay Stambh for panoramic views.", "9:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "₹40 for Indians; ₹600 for foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("24.8878"), new BigDecimal("74.6453"), 8, 2024, 1100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chittorgarh_fort.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Kumbhalgarh Fort", "kumbhalgarh-fort", "Rajsamand",
                "UNESCO World Heritage fort famous for the second-longest continuous wall in the world after the Great Wall of China, stretching 36 kilometers across hills.",
                "Constructed during the 15th century by Maharana Kumbha on a high ridge of the western Aravalli hills, Kumbhalgarh was the most formidable refuge of the Mewar rulers. The fort's perimeter wall extends over 36 kilometers with a width allowing eight horses to gallop abreast. Inside lie over 360 temples, the Badal Mahal (Cloud Palace), and the birthplace of Maharana Pratap.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kumbhalgarh_Fort_viewed_at_Sunset.JPG",
                "October to March", "3 to 4 hours", "Birthplace of the legendary warrior king Maharana Pratap in 1540; fell only once in its history due to water poisoning by combined armies.", "36-km Great Wall of Kumbhalgarh, Badal Mahal (Cloud Palace), Neelkanth Mahadev Temple, Vedi Temple, Sound & Light Show",
                "Combine with nearby Ranakpur Jain Temple on a day trip from Udaipur; stay for the illuminated evening light and sound show.", "9:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "₹40 for Indians; ₹600 for foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("25.1528"), new BigDecimal("73.5872"), 9, 2024, 750000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kumbhalgarh_Fort_viewed_at_Sunset.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Ranthambore National Park", "ranthambore-national-park", "Sawai Madhopur",
                "One of India's premier Project Tiger reserves, celebrated for wild Royal Bengal tiger sightings amidst ancient banyan trees, lakes, and a 10th-century fort.",
                "Spanning 1,334 sq km where the Aravalli and Vindhya ranges converge, Ranthambore is internationally acclaimed as one of the best locations in the world to observe wild Bengal tigers in daylight. The dramatic landscape features dry deciduous forests, ruined pavilions, and the imposing 10th-century Ranthambore Fort looming over Padam Talao and Rajbagh lakes.",
                "National Park", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/A_tiger_in_Ranthambore_National_Park_(2016).jpg",
                "October to June (Park closed July–September)", "1 to 2 days", "Former royal hunting ground of the Maharajas of Jaipur, declared a national park in 1980 and a cornerstone of Project Tiger conservation.", "Tiger Safaris (Zones 1–10), Ranthambore Fort, Padam Talao, Jogi Mahal, Trinetra Ganesha Temple, Crocodile Sightings",
                "Book safari permits (gypsy or canter) months in advance online via the Rajasthan forest portal; Zones 1 through 5 have higher tiger activity.", "Morning: 6:00 AM – 9:30 AM; Afternoon: 2:30 PM – 6:00 PM (seasonal variations)", "Canter Safari ~₹600–₹900; Gypsy Safari ~₹1200–₹1800 per person",
                new BigDecimal("26.0173"), new BigDecimal("76.5026"), 10, 2024, 480000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_tiger_in_Ranthambore_National_Park_(2016).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Pushkar Lake", "pushkar-lake", "Ajmer",
                "Pushkar Lake is a sacred water body surrounded by 52 ghats, home to the rare Brahma Temple and the world-famous annual Pushkar Camel Fair.",
                "Regarded in Hindu mythology as the 'Tirtha Raj' (King of all Pilgrimage Sites), Pushkar Lake was created where Lord Brahma dropped a lotus flower to earth. Encircled by 52 bathing ghats and over 500 whitewashed temples, it is home to one of the world's very few shrines dedicated to Lord Brahma. Every Kartik Poornima, the town hosts the vibrant Pushkar Camel Fair.",
                "Lake", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/20191214_Ghats_in_Pushkar_1713_8593.jpg",
                "October to March (Pushkar Fair in October/November)", "Half Day to Full Day", "Mentioned in the Ramayana, Mahabharata, and Buddhist texts; the sole premier theological seat in India dedicated to Lord Brahma.", "52 Sacred Bathing Ghats, Jagatpita Brahma Mandir, Evening Maha Aarti at Varaha Ghat, Savitri Devi Temple Ropeway, Pushkar Camel Fair",
                "Remove footwear before stepping onto the ghats; take the ropeway to Savitri Temple on Ratnagiri hill for sunrise panoramas of the desert oasis.", "Open 24 hours daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("26.4897"), new BigDecimal("74.5511"), 11, 2024, 2600000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20191214_Ghats_in_Pushkar_1713_8593.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Ajmer Sharif Dargah", "ajmer-sharif-dargah", "Ajmer",
                "The holy Sufi shrine of Khwaja Moinuddin Chishti in Ajmer, visited by millions of devotees of all faiths seeking spiritual solace and blessing.",
                "Located at the foot of the Taragarh hill, the Dargah Sharif is one of the most revered Islamic pilgrimage destinations in South Asia. It contains the mortal remains of the revered 13th-century Persian Sufi mystic and philosopher Khwaja Moinuddin Chishti (Gharib Nawaz). The complex features grand silver doors, Mughal courtyards donated by Akbar and Shah Jahan, and two colossal cauldrons (Degs).",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Night_View_of_Ajmer_Sharif_Dargah.jpg",
                "October to March (Annual Urs Festival)", "2 to 3 hours", "Emperor Akbar made regular annual pilgrimages from Agra on foot to pray at the shrine; symbol of interfaith harmony for eight centuries.", "Sanctum of Khwaja Gharib Nawaz, Buland Darwaza, Akbari & Shah Jahani Mosques, Badi Deg & Chhoti Deg, Soul-stirring Qawwali sessions",
                "Cover your head with a cap or scarf; experience the mystical evening Qawwali performances inside the courtyard after evening prayers.", "5:00 AM – 9:00 PM daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("26.4564"), new BigDecimal("74.6281"), 12, 2024, 12000000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Night_View_of_Ajmer_Sharif_Dargah.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Mount Abu", "mount-abu", "Sirohi",
                "Rajasthan's only hill station, set at 1,220 meters on an Aravalli granite plateau, famous for serene Nakki Lake, Toad Rock, and panoramic sunset viewpoints.",
                "Rising like an emerald green island above the arid plains of southwestern Rajasthan, Mount Abu provides a cool mountain haven. Centered around the picturesque Nakki Lake—mythologically scooped out by gods using their fingernails (nakh)—the hill town features forested wildlife sanctuaries, Toad Rock cliff, and Guru Shikhar, the highest peak in the Aravalli Range at 1,722 meters.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nakki_Lake%2C_Mount_Abu%2C_Rajasthan%2C_610.jpg",
                "October to April", "2 days", "Ancient mountain retreat mentioned in the Puranas as Arbuda Mountain, where Sage Vashistha performed the sacrificial fire (Agnikula) rituals.", "Nakki Lake Boating, Guru Shikhar Peak (1,722m), Sunset Point, Toad Rock, Mount Abu Wildlife Sanctuary, Achalgarh Fort",
                "Rent a paddleboat on Nakki Lake at dusk; drive up to Guru Shikhar early in the morning for crisp clear vistas extending into Gujarat.", "Open 24 hours (Boating: 8:00 AM – 6:00 PM)", "Free entry to town; Boating ₹100–₹200",
                new BigDecimal("24.5925"), new BigDecimal("72.7156"), 13, 2024, 1500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nakki_Lake,_Mount_Abu,_Rajasthan,_610.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Dilwara Temples", "dilwara-temples", "Sirohi",
                "World-renowned complex of five Jain temples carved from pure white marble between the 11th and 13th centuries, celebrated for unmatched stone carving perfection.",
                "Located 2.5 km from Mount Abu, the Dilwara Temples represent the pinnacle of marble carving craftsmanship anywhere in the world. Constructed by Solanki-era ministers Vimal Shah and the brothers Tejpal and Vastupal, the temples feature translucent marble ceilings carved with minute filigree lotuses, ornate doorways, and mandapa pillars so delicate they resemble ivory lace.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dilwara_temple_at_mount_abu.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 hours", "Supreme masterpieces of Maru-Gurjara architectural style built between 1031 and 1230 CE, dedicating each shrine to Jain Tirthankaras.", "Vimal Vasahi Temple, Luna Vasahi Temple, Pittalhar Temple, Parshvanatha Temple, Mahavir Swami Temple, Translucent Marble Ceilings",
                "Strict temple dress code enforced; leather items, footwear, and cameras/phones are strictly prohibited inside the complex.", "12:00 PM – 5:00 PM for tourists (Open earlier for Jain devotees)", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("24.6067"), new BigDecimal("72.7228"), 14, 2024, 1100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dilwara_temple_at_mount_abu.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Jantar Mantar, Jaipur", "jantar-mantar-jaipur", "Jaipur",
                "UNESCO World Heritage astronomical observatory completed in 1734, housing 19 stone architectural instruments including the world's largest stone sundial.",
                "Commissioned by astronomer-king Maharaja Sawai Jai Singh II and completed in 1734, Jantar Mantar is the largest and best-preserved of five historic observatories built in northern India. Constructed from local stone and marble, its 19 monumental geometric instruments measure local time to within two seconds, predict eclipses, and track celestial orbits with astounding accuracy.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jaipur_Jantar_Mantar_panorama_2011.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 hours", "Inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site as an outstanding masterpiece of late medieval astronomical and mathematical genius.", "Vrihat Samrat Yantra (27m stone sundial), Jai Prakash Yantra, Rama Yantra, Chakra Yantra, Misra Yantra",
                "Hire an authorized ASI guide to explain the mathematical working of each instrument; mid-day is the best time to see sundials in action.", "9:00 AM – 5:00 PM daily", "₹50 for Indians; ₹200 for foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("26.9247"), new BigDecimal("75.8246"), 15, 2024, 1300000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaipur_Jantar_Mantar_panorama_2011.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Junagarh Fort, Bikaner", "junagarh-fort-bikaner", "Bikaner",
                "An unconquered 16th-century desert plains fortress in Bikaner, celebrated for lavishly painted gold-leaf ceilings, glass inlays, and red sandstone architecture.",
                "Constructed in 1589 by Raja Rai Singh, general in Akbar's imperial army, Junagarh Fort is uniquely built on the flat desert plains rather than on a hilltop. Surrounded by a deep moat and red Dulmera sandstone ramparts, the fortress interior features stunningly decorated royal halls, including the Anup Mahal with gold leaf relief work, Badal Mahal painted with monsoon clouds, and a WWI de Havilland biplane.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/20191211_Junagarh_Fort%2C_Bikaner%2C_India_1547_8077_DxO.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "One of the few major Rajput fortresses never built on a hill, remaining virtually unbreached throughout its four-century history.", "Anup Mahal (Gold-embossed Durbar), Badal Mahal (Cloud Room), Chandra Mahal, Phool Mahal, Fort Museum with DH-9 World War I bomber",
                "The guided tour provides deep insight into Bikaneri lacquerwork and glass inlays; visit the adjacent Prachina Museum for textiles.", "10:00 AM – 4:30 PM daily", "₹50 for Indians; ₹300 for foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("28.0219"), new BigDecimal("73.3175"), 16, 2024, 650000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20191211_Junagarh_Fort,_Bikaner,_India_1547_8077_DxO.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Sam Sand Dunes", "sam-sand-dunes", "Jaisalmer",
                "Sweeping 30-meter golden sand dunes in the Thar Desert 40 km from Jaisalmer, famous for camel safaris, 4x4 dune bashing, and desert folk music camps.",
                "Located 42 km west of Jaisalmer near the Desert National Park, the Sam Sand Dunes offer an authentic vast expanse of sweeping, wind-sculpted sand ripples up to 30 meters high. As the sun dips into the desert horizon, visitors enjoy camel safaris, quad biking, and dune-bashing jeeps, followed by vibrant Kalbelia gypsy dances and Rajasthani cuisine under starry desert skies.",
                "Desert", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sam_dunes_(Jaisalmer).jpg",
                "October to March", "Overnight to 1 day", "The heart of the great Indian Thar Desert, along the historic camel caravan paths between Rajasthan and Sindh.", "Golden Sand Dunes Sunset, Camel Safaris, 4x4 Jeep Dune Bashing, Desert Camps with Kalbelia Folk Dance, Stargazing",
                "Book a desert camp stay for cultural folk performances and overnight stargazing; sunset camel rides should be arranged an hour before sundown.", "Open 24 hours daily (Desert activities: 6:00 AM – 10:00 PM)", "Free access to dunes; Camel rides ₹200–₹500; Jeep safaris ₹1000–₹1500",
                new BigDecimal("26.8333"), new BigDecimal("70.5"), 17, 2024, 950000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sam_dunes_(Jaisalmer).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Keoladeo National Park", "keoladeo-national-park", "Bharatpur",
                "UNESCO World Heritage wetland and bird sanctuary in Bharatpur, hosting over 370 bird species including thousands of wintering migratory waterfowl.",
                "Formerly known as the Bharatpur Bird Sanctuary, Keoladeo is a 29-square-kilometer man-made wetland reserve created 250 years ago by the Maharaja of Bharatpur. A designated Ramsar site and UNESCO World Heritage sanctuary, it serves as a critical wintering refuge for migratory water birds from Afghanistan, Turkmenistan, China, and Siberia, best explored by bicycle or cycle rickshaws guided by trained naturalists.",
                "National Park", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Keoladeo_National_Park_-_Bharatpur_029_(409155591).jpg",
                "October to March", "Half Day to Full Day", "Established as a duck-hunting reserve by Maharaja Suraj Mal in the 1850s, protected as a national park in 1982 and UNESCO site in 1985.", "Painted Stork Heronries, Sarus Crane Pairs, Pelican Flocks, Keoladeo Shiva Temple, Bicycle and Rickshaw Birding Trails",
                "Hire a cycle-rickshaw puller who has been trained by the forest department in bird identification and carries spotting scopes.", "6:00 AM – 6:00 PM daily", "₹75 for Indians; ₹500 for foreigners; Rickshaw hire ₹150/hr",
                new BigDecimal("27.16"), new BigDecimal("77.52"), 18, 2024, 320000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Keoladeo_National_Park_-_Bharatpur_029_(409155591).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Taragarh Fort, Bundi", "taragarh-fort-bundi", "Bundi",
                "The 'Star Fort' of Bundi, built in 1354 atop a steep 1,400-foot hill, celebrated for monumental battlements, deep stepwells, and Garh Palace murals.",
                "Constructed in 1354 by Rao Raja Bar Singh on a steep crag overlooking the blue town of Bundi, Taragarh Fort is one of the most rugged and atmospheric hilltop fortifications in Rajasthan. Below the wild ramparts lies the Garh Palace, famous worldwide for the Chitrashala (Picture Gallery) filled with miniature turquoise and gold murals depicting Krishna legends and courtly processions.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bundi_Taragarh_Fort_06.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Seat of the Hada Chauhans of Bundi, famed for repelling multiple imperial assaults by Sultanate and Mughal armies.", "Chitrashala Bundi Miniature Murals, Bhim Burj Cannon Mount (Garbh Gunjam), Miran Saheb Dargah, Stepwell Reservoirs",
                "Carry a walking stick to ward off curious monkeys on the steep stone path up to the fort; visit the Chitrashala murals in morning light.", "8:00 AM – 5:00 PM daily", "₹100 for Indians; ₹500 for foreigners (includes Garh Palace & Chitrashala)",
                new BigDecimal("25.4419"), new BigDecimal("75.6367"), 19, 2024, 280000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundi_Taragarh_Fort_06.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(rajasthan, "Ranakpur Jain Temple", "ranakpur-jain-temple", "Pali",
                "A 15th-century marble temple dedicated to Tirthankara Adinatha, famous for 1,444 individually carved non-identical marble pillars that shift color with the sun.",
                "Nestled in a tranquil valley of the western Aravalli hills, the Chaumukha (Four-Faced) temple of Ranakpur was commissioned in 1437 by wealthy Jain merchant Dharna Shah under the patronage of Maharana Kumbha. Crafted from light-colored marble, the temple rises across three storeys, supported by 1,444 uniquely carved pillars where no two pillars share the same carving.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jain_Temple_Ranakpur.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Constructed over 50 years in the 15th century, representing the zenith of Jain stone craftsmanship and spiritual devotion.", "1,444 Unique Marble Pillars, Four-Faced Adinatha Idol, Kalpavriksha (Wish-Fulfilling Tree) Leaf Carving, Suparshvanatha Dome",
                "Audio guides in multiple languages provide wonderful architectural explanations; strict dress code enforced (shoulders and knees covered).", "12:00 PM – 5:00 PM for tourists (Open earlier for worshippers)", "Free entry; Audio guide ₹200; Camera fee ₹100",
                new BigDecimal("25.1186"), new BigDecimal("73.4736"), 20, 2024, 890000L, "TOTAL",
                "Rajasthan Tourism (RTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jain_Temple_Ranakpur.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        // --- 16. TAMIL NADU ---
        State tn = createState("Tamil Nadu", "tamil-nadu", "STATE", "Southern", "Chennai", "Enchanted land of Dravidian stone temples, Nilgiri hills, and cultural heritage.",
                "Tamil Nadu leads India in domestic tourism arrivals, home to the UNESCO Great Living Chola Temples, Meenakshi Temple, and Ooty hill station.",
                "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Meenakshi_Amman_Temple_Tank.jpg", "October to March");

        createDestination(tn, "Meenakshi Amman Temple, Madurai", "meenakshi-temple", "Madurai",
                "Historic Dravidian temple complex dedicated to Goddess Meenakshi and Lord Sundareswarar, celebrated for its 14 towering gopurams encrusted with thousands of painted sculptures.",
                "Located at the heart of the ancient city of Madurai on the banks of the Vaigai River, Meenakshi Amman Temple is a masterpiece of late Pandyan and Nayak architecture. The sprawling 14-acre complex features the sacred Golden Lotus Tank (Pottramarai Kulam) and the celebrated Hall of Thousand Pillars (Aayiram Kaal Mandapam) displaying intricate musical stone pillars. The temple is one of the premier living spiritual traditions of Tamil culture.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Meenakshi_Amman_Temple_Tank.jpg",
                "October to March (pleasant weather; Chithirai Festival in April/May)", "3 to 4 hours", "Mentioned in 6th-century Sangam literature; rebuilt and extensively expanded by Vishwanatha Nayak and Thirumalai Nayak in the 16th and 17th centuries.", "Golden Lotus Tank, Hall of Thousand Pillars, Musical Pillars, Ashta Shakthi Mandapam, Night Palki Ceremony",
                "Strict traditional South Indian dress code enforced; mobile phones and electronic devices are prohibited inside the sanctum inner complex.", "05:00 AM – 12:30 PM, 04:00 PM – 10:00 PM daily", "Free general darshan; ₹50 Special Darshan; ₹100 Quick Darshan; ₹50 Museum entry",
                new BigDecimal("9.9195"), new BigDecimal("78.1193"), 1, 2024, 9500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meenakshi_Amman_Temple_Tank.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Brihadeeswarar Temple, Thanjavur", "brihadeeswarar-temple", "Thanjavur",
                "UNESCO World Heritage 'Big Temple' built by Emperor Raja Raja Chola I in 1010 CE, crowned by a 66-meter granite vimana and an 80-tonne monolithic stone cupola.",
                "Brihadeeswarar Temple (Peruvudaiyar Kovil) is one of India's grandest architectural triumphs, built entirely of granite in an alluvial plain with no natural stone quarries nearby. Dedicated to Lord Shiva, the complex is renowned for its 216-foot vimana tower whose shadow never touches the outer grounds at noon, a colossal 20-tonne monolithic Nandi statue, and ancient Chola fresco paintings depicting royal and mythological scenes.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Brihadisvara_Temple_during_Maha_Shivaratri-WUS03611_(edit).jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Consecrated in 1010 CE to celebrate Chola imperial power, representing the architectural pinnacle of the Great Living Chola Temples UNESCO World Heritage site.", "66-meter Granite Vimana, Monolithic 20-tonne Nandi Bull, Chola Frescoes, Subrahmanya Shrine, Intricate Tamil Epigraphical Inscriptions",
                "Visit in the early morning or golden late afternoon to experience the granite structure glowing in natural light; ASI guides are recommended.", "06:00 AM – 12:30 PM, 04:00 PM – 08:30 PM daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("10.7828"), new BigDecimal("79.1318"), 2, 2024, 5200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brihadisvara_Temple_during_Maha_Shivaratri-WUS03611_(edit).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Shore Temple, Mahabalipuram", "shore-temple", "Chengalpattu",
                "Eighth-century structural stone temple perched directly on the Bay of Bengal coastline, representing the crowning architectural achievement of the Pallava Dynasty.",
                "Constructed under Pallava King Narasimhavarman II (Rajasimha) between 700 and 728 CE, the Shore Temple is one of the earliest structural stone temples in Southern India. Built from finely cut granite blocks to overlook the ancient bustling seaport of Mamallapuram, it houses three shrines dedicated to Shiva and Vishnu and is encircled by rows of sculpted Nandi bulls weathering the sea breezes.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mamallapuram,_Shore_Temple,_India.jpg",
                "November to February", "1 to 2 hours", "Inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage list in 1984; served as an ancient maritime navigation landmark for seafaring merchant ships sailing to Southeast Asia.", "Main Shiva Temple Vimana, Reclining Vishnu Shrine (Anantashayana), Perimeter Nandi Sculptures, Coastal Seawall Gardens",
                "Arrive at sunrise for breathtaking coastal views as morning light strikes the granite spires; single composite ASI ticket covers all Mahabalipuram monuments.", "06:00 AM – 06:00 PM daily", "₹40 for Indians; ₹600 for foreigners (combined ASI ticket for Mahabalipuram)",
                new BigDecimal("12.6167"), new BigDecimal("80.1983"), 3, 2024, 3100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mamallapuram,_Shore_Temple,_India.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Group of Monuments, Mahabalipuram", "group-of-monuments-mahabalipuram", "Chengalpattu",
                "UNESCO World Heritage sanctuary featuring monolithic rock-cut Pancha Rathas, the monumental bas-relief 'Descent of the Ganges', and Krishna's Butterball.",
                "The Group of Monuments at Mahabalipuram constitutes one of the world's greatest open-air rock-cut sculptural galleries, carved during the 7th and 8th centuries by Pallava monarchs. The site features the Five Rathas (monolithic chariots carved each from single granite boulders), cave sanctuaries (Mandapas) depicting Mahishasuramardini, and the monumental 96-foot bas-relief panel depicting Arjuna's Penance and the Descent of the Ganges.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Info_Five_Rathas_Mahabalipuram_Sep22_A7C_02536.jpg",
                "November to February", "3 to 4 hours", "Served as the premier experimental ground for South Indian Dravidian stone architecture, transitioning from rock-cut caves to free-standing structural temples.", "Pancha Rathas (Five Chariots), Descent of the Ganges (Arjuna's Penance), Krishna's Butterball, Mahishasuramardini Cave Temple, Varaha Cave",
                "Wear comfortable walking shoes and a sun hat; hire an authorized ASI guide to decode the rich mythological iconography carved into the cliff faces.", "06:00 AM – 06:00 PM daily", "₹40 for Indians; ₹600 for foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("12.62"), new BigDecimal("80.1933"), 4, 2024, 2800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Info_Five_Rathas_Mahabalipuram_Sep22_A7C_02536.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Ramanathaswamy Temple, Rameswaram", "ramanathaswamy-temple", "Ramanathapuram",
                "Sacred Jyotirlinga pilgrimage shrine situated on Pamban Island, famous for possessing the world's longest pillared temple corridors and 22 holy bathing teerthams.",
                "Ramanathaswamy Temple is one of the four cardinal Char Dham pilgrimage destinations and one of the 12 sacred Jyotirlinga shrines of Lord Shiva. According to the Ramayana, Lord Rama consecrated the Shiva lingam here to seek absolution after defeating Ravana. The temple is celebrated worldwide for its magnificent 1,200-meter outer corridor containing over 1,200 elaborately sculpted sandstone pillars.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ramanathaswamy_Temple_corridor.jpg",
                "October to April", "3 to 4 hours", "Expanded over centuries by the Jaffna kings of the Sethupathy dynasty; pivotal center of pan-Indian Vaishnavite and Shaivite synthesis.", "World's Longest Outer Corridor (Third Prakaram), 22 Sacred Theerthams (Bathing Wells), Ramalingam Sanctum, Agni Theertham beach",
                "Pilgrims traditionally bathe at Agni Theertham sea first, followed by bathing at all 22 inner temple wells; carry dry clothes for darshan after holy baths.", "05:00 AM – 01:00 PM, 03:00 PM – 09:00 PM daily", "Free entry; ₹25 well-bathing fee; ₹50/₹100 Special Darshan passes available",
                new BigDecimal("9.2881"), new BigDecimal("79.3174"), 5, 2024, 8400000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramanathaswamy_Temple_corridor.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Kanyakumari and Vivekananda Rock Memorial", "vivekananda-rock-memorial-kanyakumari", "Kanyakumari",
                "Iconic offshore rock monument erected in 1970 where Swami Vivekananda meditated in 1892, set at the confluence of the Arabian Sea, Indian Ocean, and Bay of Bengal.",
                "Located 500 meters offshore on a granite islet at India's southernmost mainland tip, the Vivekananda Rock Memorial commemorates the spiritual enlightenment experienced by Swami Vivekananda prior to his historic 1893 Chicago address. The monument features the Vivekananda Mandapam and Dhyana Mandapam meditation hall, set alongside the colossal 133-foot stone statue of Tamil saint-poet Thiruvalluvar.",
                "Monument", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Vivekananda_Rock_Memorial,_Kanyakumari,_India_2.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Erected through a nationwide grassroots movement led by Eknath Ranade in 1970; revered as the sacred footprint site (Sripada Parai) of Goddess Kanya Kumari.", "Vivekananda Mandapam, Dhyana Mandapam (Meditation Hall), Thiruvalluvar Statue, Triveni Sangam Confluence, Sunrise and Sunset points",
                "Ferries run from the mainland jetty every 15 minutes; arrive early morning to witness the sun rising out of the ocean and avoid ferry ticket queues.", "08:00 AM – 04:00 PM daily (Ferry services subject to sea conditions)", "₹50 Ferry ticket; ₹20 Memorial entry fee",
                new BigDecimal("8.0781"), new BigDecimal("77.5553"), 6, 2024, 4500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vivekananda_Rock_Memorial,_Kanyakumari,_India_2.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Ooty Botanical Gardens", "ooty-botanical-gardens", "Nilgiris",
                "Sprawling 55-acre botanical reserve established in 1848 on the slopes of Doddabetta Peak, cultivating over a thousand species of exotic flora and a 20-million-year-old fossil tree trunk.",
                "Designed in 1848 by British botanist William Graham McIvor, the Government Botanical Garden in Udhagamandalam (Ooty) is divided into six distinct terraces featuring lush terraced lawns, Italian glasshouses, conservatory gardens, and fern houses. The garden is world-famous for its annual May Flower Show and displays an authentic 20-million-year-old petrified tree trunk.",
                "Nature", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Front_Gate_House_Botanical_Garden_Ooty_Aug26_A7CR_12481-2_Pano.jpg",
                "October to June (Annual Flower Show in May)", "2 to 3 hours", "One of the oldest botanical gardens in India, established during British colonial times to acclimatize temperate vegetables and exotic ornamental plants in the Nilgiri hills.", "Lower Lawn, Italian Garden, Conservatory Glasshouse, 20-Million-Year-Old Fossil Tree, Toda Hillock, Japanese Garden section",
                "Carry a light jacket as Nilgiri mountain mist rolls in quickly; combine with visits to nearby Ooty Lake and Doddabetta Peak.", "07:00 AM – 06:30 PM daily", "₹40 for adults; ₹20 for children; ₹50 for still camera",
                new BigDecimal("11.4183"), new BigDecimal("76.7119"), 7, 2024, 3200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Front_Gate_House_Botanical_Garden_Ooty_Aug26_A7CR_12481-2_Pano.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Kodaikanal Lake", "kodaikanal-lake", "Dindigul",
                "Picturesque 60-acre star-shaped artificial lake created in 1863 in the Palani Hills, renowned for leisurely pedal boating, cycling, and equestrian perimeter paths.",
                "Nestled at 2,285 meters in the misty hill station of Kodaikanal ('Princess of Hill Stations'), this star-shaped lake was engineered in 1863 by Sir Vere Henry Levinge, Collector of Madurai. Surrounded by eucalyptus woods and lush slopes, the 5-kilometer perimeter path is lined with cycle rentals, horse riders, and chocolate shops, while the lake waters host rowing and paddle boats.",
                "Lake", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kodaikanal_Lake_2.jpg",
                "September to May", "2 to 3 hours", "Built as a centerpiece for the hill station by American missionaries and British administrators seeking refuge from the plains' summer heat.", "Star-Shaped Lake Boating (Kodaikanal Boat Club), 5-km Perimeter Cycling Trail, Bryant Park, Coaker's Walk scenic viewpoints nearby",
                "Rent a tandem bicycle to cycle the 5 km loop around the lake; early mornings offer peaceful waters and picturesque mountain mist.", "06:00 AM – 06:00 PM daily (Boating: 08:30 AM – 05:30 PM)", "Free access to lake promenade; Boating ₹150–₹350; Bicycle rental ₹50/hour",
                new BigDecimal("10.2333"), new BigDecimal("77.4833"), 8, 2024, 2900000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kodaikanal_Lake_2.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Nilgiri Mountain Railway", "nilgiri-mountain-railway", "Nilgiris",
                "UNESCO World Heritage metre-gauge rack-and-pinion steam railway opened in 1908, scaling 46 km through 208 curves, 16 tunnels, and 250 bridges from Mettupalayam to Ooty.",
                "Inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage list as an outstanding engineering feat, the Nilgiri Mountain Railway operates the steepest track in Asia, utilizing the unique Swiss Abt rack-and-pinion system. Powered by vintage 'X' Class steam locomotives, the 5-hour journey traverses scenic pine forests, tea estates, misty mountain gorges, and heritage stations like Coonoor.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nilgiri_Mountain_Railway_on_Bridge,_May_2010.JPG",
                "October to May", "Half Day (journey duration ~4.5 to 5 hours)", "Completed in 1908 after decades of complex civil engineering; declared a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2005 alongside the Darjeeling Himalayan Railway.", "Abt Rack-and-Pinion System, Vintage Steam Locomotives, 16 Tunnels & 250 Bridges, Coonoor Heritage Station, Emerald Tea Plantation Panoramas",
                "Book tickets months in advance on the IRCTC portal as seats sell out very quickly; sit on the right-hand side when travelling uphill for the best valley views.", "Scheduled train timings: Depart Mettupalayam ~07:10 AM, Arrive Ooty ~12:00 PM", "Second Class ~₹85; First Class ~₹300–₹600 (subject to IRCTC fare schedules)",
                new BigDecimal("11.3486"), new BigDecimal("76.7972"), 9, 2024, 1100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nilgiri_Mountain_Railway_on_Bridge,_May_2010.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Dhanushkodi", "dhanushkodi", "Ramanathapuram",
                "The 'Ghost Town' of southern India, swept away by the 1964 cyclone, featuring haunting ruined churches and the narrow sandspit of Ram Setu pointing towards Sri Lanka.",
                "Located at the south-eastern tip of Pamban Island just 24 kilometers from Talaimannar in Sri Lanka, Dhanushkodi was a bustling port town until it was tragically devastated by a colossal super-cyclone in December 1964. Today, visitors explore the windswept ruins of the railway station, church, and temple amidst shimmering white sandbanks where the Bay of Bengal (Mahodadhi) meets the Indian Ocean (Ratnakara).",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dhanushkodi_church_ruin.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Mythologically revered as the site where Lord Rama marked the spot with his bow (Dhanush) to build Ram Setu (Adam's Bridge) to Lanka.", "Ruined Portuguese Church, Destroyed Railway Station Water Tower, Arichal Munai (India's Land's End viewpoint), Pristine Sand Dunes & Confluence of Seas",
                "Travel via the newly constructed metalled road to Arichal Munai; carry plenty of drinking water and sun protection as there is virtually no shade on the sandspit.", "06:00 AM – 05:00 PM daily (No overnight stays permitted; checkpost closes at dusk)", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("9.1783"), new BigDecimal("79.4183"), 10, 2024, 1850000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dhanushkodi_church_ruin.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Gingee Fort", "gingee-fort", "Viluppuram",
                "Impregnable hilltop fortress complex celebrated as the 'Troy of the East' by the British, spanning three rugged granite citadels connected by massive stone ramparts.",
                "Originally built by the Chola dynasty in the 9th century and dramatically fortified by the Vijayanagara Nayaks, Marathas under Chhatrapati Shivaji, and the Mughals, Gingee Fort is one of the most formidable citadels in South India. The fortress encompasses Rajagiri (King's Hill), Krishnagiri (Queen's Hill), and Chandrayandurg, housing the eight-storey Kalyana Mahal palace, royal granaries, and cliffside drawbridges.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gingee_Fort_panorama.jpg",
                "November to February", "3 to 4 hours", "Chhatrapati Shivaji acclaimed it as 'the most impregnable fortress in India' in 1677; witnessed a heroic seven-year Mughal siege under General Zulfiqar Khan.", "Rajagiri Citadel Peak (800 feet), Kalyana Mahal (Wedding Hall Palace), Multi-storeyed Granaries, Venkataramana Temple, Natural Moat & Drawbridge",
                "Climbing to Rajagiri requires ascending over 800 stone steps; start early in the morning before midday heat and carry ample water and snacks.", "09:00 AM – 05:30 PM daily", "₹25 for Indians; ₹300 for foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("12.2536"), new BigDecimal("79.4181"), 11, 2024, 920000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gingee_Fort_panorama.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Chidambaram Nataraja Temple", "chidambaram-nataraja-temple", "Cuddalore",
                "Ancient temple dedicated to Lord Shiva as Nataraja performing the cosmic dance of bliss (Ananda Tandava), home to the golden roofed Kanaka Sabha and the mystical 'Chidambara Rahasya'.",
                "Chidambaram Nataraja Temple (Thillai Nataraja) is one of the five holiest Pancha Bhoota Sthalas, representing the element of Space (Akasha). Spread across 40 acres, the temple features four monumental 140-foot gopurams decorated with all 108 postures of Bharatanatyam dance carved in stone. The sanctum holds the sacred Chidambara Rahasya, symbolising God as formless, infinite space.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Thillai_Nataraja_Temple_(Chidambaram_Nataraja_Temple).jpg",
                "October to March (Natyanjali Dance Festival in Feb/March)", "2 to 3 hours", "Spiritual headquarters of the Chola empire; managed for millennia by an ancient hereditary guild of Shaivite priests known as the Dikshitars.", "Golden Roof of Kanaka Sabha, 108 Bharatanatyam Sculptures on Gopurams, Chidambara Rahasya Sanctum, Sivaganga Holy Tank, Thousand Pillar Hall",
                "Attend the daily afternoon and evening abhishekams of the Sphatika (Ruby & Crystal) Lingam; photography inside the inner mandapas is strictly forbidden.", "06:00 AM – 12:00 PM, 05:00 PM – 10:00 PM daily", "Free entry; special darshan tickets ₹50–₹100",
                new BigDecimal("11.3992"), new BigDecimal("79.6933"), 12, 2024, 4100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thillai_Nataraja_Temple_(Chidambaram_Nataraja_Temple).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Airavatesvara Temple, Darasuram", "airavatesvara-temple-darasuram", "Thanjavur",
                "UNESCO World Heritage Chola temple constructed in the 12th century by Rajaraja Chola II, renowned for its chariot-shaped mandapa and stone musical steps.",
                "Located 4 km from Kumbakonam, the Airavatesvara Temple is part of the UNESCO Great Living Chola Temples trilogy. Built with exquisite jewel-like precision, its front mandapa (Agra Mandapa) is designed as a colossal stone chariot drawn by galloping horses and elephants. The temple is famed for its intricate micro-relief stone carvings depicting dancers, musicians, and Saiva saints.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Darasuram,_Airavatesvara_Temple,_Mandapa_at_night_4,_India.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 hours", "Constructed during the late Chola golden era between 1146 and 1173 CE; named after Airavata, the white elephant of Indra who worshiped Shiva here.", "Stone Chariot Mandapa with Wheels and Horses, Musical Steps (Saptaswara Steps), Exquisite Miniature Relief Friezes, Periya Nayaki Amman Temple",
                "Examine the miniature carvings around the pillar bases depicting everyday 12th-century life and performing arts; visit together with nearby Gangaikonda Cholapuram.", "06:00 AM – 12:30 PM, 04:00 PM – 08:30 PM daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("10.95"), new BigDecimal("79.3556"), 13, 2024, 1650000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darasuram,_Airavatesvara_Temple,_Mandapa_at_night_4,_India.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Chettinad Heritage Mansions, Kanadukathan", "chettinad-heritage-mansions", "Sivaganga",
                "Palatial 19th-century merchant mansions built by wealthy Nagarathar bankers, famed for Burmese teak woodwork, Italian marble, Athangudi handmade tiles, and fiery Chettinad cuisine.",
                "The heritage village of Kanadukathan in the Chettinad region is home to majestic architectural wonders constructed between the 1850s and 1930s by affluent Nattukottai Chettiars. These massive mansions feature grand colonnaded courtyards (Valavu), intricately carved Burmese teak beams, Belgian mirrors, handmade terracotta Athangudi tiles, and lime-plastered walls finished with egg-white glaze.",
                "Cultural", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chettinad_Mansion_Kanadukathan.jpg",
                "October to March", "Half Day to 1 Day", "Showcases the transnational merchant heritage of the Chettiar community whose mercantile trading empires spanned Burma, Ceylon, Vietnam, and Malaysia.", "Chettinad Palace, Kanadukathan Heritage Street, Athangudi Tile Workshops, Traditional Chettinad Feast Dining, Antique Market in Karaikudi",
                "Several heritage mansions have been converted into boutique hotels and museums; do not miss sampling authentic spicy Chettinad cuisine served on banana leaves.", "Mansion visits typically 09:00 AM – 05:00 PM daily", "₹50–₹100 for private mansion museum entry",
                new BigDecimal("10.1583"), new BigDecimal("78.7833"), 14, 2024, 750000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chettinad_Mansion_Kanadukathan.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Hogenakkal Falls", "hogenakkal-falls", "Dharmapuri",
                "Spectacular cascade on the Kaveri River celebrated as the 'Niagara of India', known for circular coracle boat rides, herbal oil massages, and medicinal waters.",
                "Located where the Kaveri River enters Tamil Nadu through deep carbonatite rock gorges, Hogenakkal ('Smoky Rocks') derives its name from the spray of water appearing like smoke rising from the churning rocks. The falls feature multiple cascading channels dropping into whirlpool gorges, best experienced through traditional round wicker coracle (parisal) boat rides.",
                "Waterfall", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Hogenakkal_Falls_Close.jpg",
                "August to February (post-monsoon water volumes are most dramatic)", "3 to 4 hours", "Geologically famous for containing some of the oldest carbonatite rock formations in South Asia, dating back over 2 billion years.", "Coracle Boat Rides in Kaveri Gorge, Hanging Bridge Viewpoint, Fresh River Fish Fry, Traditional Herbal Oil Massages",
                "Always wear safety lifejackets during coracle rides; government authorized boat operators are located near the main ticket counter.", "08:00 AM – 05:30 PM daily (Boating depends on river water release from dams)", "Free entry to viewing areas; Coracle ride ~₹750 per boat (regulated rates)",
                new BigDecimal("12.1167"), new BigDecimal("77.7667"), 15, 2024, 1950000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hogenakkal_Falls_Close.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Mudumalai National Park", "mudumalai-national-park", "Nilgiris",
                "UNESCO Nilgiri Biosphere tiger reserve spanning 321 sq km in the Western Ghats, home to the Theppakadu Elephant Camp, Bengal tigers, and Asian elephants.",
                "Situated at the tri-junction of Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, and Kerala, Mudumalai National Park protects dry deciduous and moist teak forests harboring India's highest tiger density in the Western Ghats complex. Established in 1940 as South India's first wildlife sanctuary, it is celebrated for the historic Theppakadu Elephant Camp (Asia's oldest captive elephant camp) featured in the Oscar-winning documentary 'The Elephant Whisperers'.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Oriental_honey_buzzard_Mudumalai_Mar21_DSC01405.jpg",
                "October to May", "1 to 2 days", "Declared a tiger reserve in 2007; core component of the UNESCO World Heritage Western Ghats cluster.", "Forest Department Jeep and Bus Safaris, Theppakadu Elephant Camp, Moyar River Gorge, Birdwatching (over 260 avian species)",
                "Book early morning (06:00 AM) or late afternoon (03:30 PM) safari slots online via the Tamil Nadu forest portal; stay in government forest cottages.", "06:00 AM – 10:00 AM, 02:00 PM – 06:00 PM daily", "Safari bus ~₹350 per person; Jeep safari ~₹2,500; Camera fees apply",
                new BigDecimal("11.5833"), new BigDecimal("76.5333"), 16, 2024, 480000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oriental_honey_buzzard_Mudumalai_Mar21_DSC01405.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Courtallam Waterfalls", "courtallam-waterfalls", "Tenkasi",
                "Renowned 'Spa of South India' featuring nine natural cascading waterfalls across the Western Ghats, celebrated for their therapeutic and mineral-rich herbal waters.",
                "Fed by the Chittar River tumbling down the dense forested slopes of the Western Ghats, Courtallam (Kutralam) is famed for waters that flow over hundreds of medicinal herbs before plunging into scenic pools. The town hosts nine waterfalls including the Main Falls (Peraruvi), Five Falls (Aintharuvi), Old Courtallam Falls, and Tiger Falls, drawing millions during the refreshing southwest monsoon season.",
                "Waterfall", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Courtallam_Main_waterfall_(_%E0%B4%95%E0%B5%81%E0%B4%B1%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B1%E0%B4%BE%E0%B4%B2%E0%B4%82_%E0%B4%B5%E0%B5%86%E0%B4%B3%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B3%E0%B4%9A%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%9A%E0%B4%BE%E0%B4%9F%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%9F%E0%B4%82)_(70448).jpg",
                "June to September (Peak 'Saral' monsoon season)", "Half Day to Full Day", "Celebrated in ancient Tamil Sangam poetry and the 18th-century musical drama 'Kutrala Kuravanji' by Thirikooda Rasappa Kavirayar.", "Main Falls (Peraruvi), Five Falls (Aintharuvi), Shenbaga Devi Falls, Old Courtallam Falls, Historic Kutralanathar Shiva Temple",
                "The 'Saral' season (fine drizzle with cool winds) from June to September is the most festive time to visit; slippery rocks require careful footing.", "Open 24 hours daily (Bathing access monitored during flash floods)", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("8.93"), new BigDecimal("77.2683"), 17, 2024, 3800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Courtallam_Main_waterfall_(_%E0%B4%95%E0%B5%81%E0%B4%B1%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B1%E0%B4%BE%E0%B4%B2%E0%B4%82_%E0%B4%B5%E0%B5%86%E0%B4%B3%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B3%E0%B4%9A%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%9A%E0%B4%BE%E0%B4%9F%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%9F%E0%B4%82)_(70448).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Yercaud Lake", "yercaud-lake", "Salem",
                "The picturesque 'Jewel of the South' lake located at 1,515 meters in the Shevaroy Hills, bordered by landscaped Deer Park gardens, coffee estates, and scenic boating.",
                "Yercaud Lake (Emerald Lake) forms the tranquil centerpiece of the Shevaroy Hills in the Eastern Ghats. Surrounded by cloud-topped mountain slopes, orange groves, and spice plantations, this natural mountain lake features a picturesque fountain, island bridge, and landscaped gardens, offering pedal and row boating in crisp hill station air.",
                "Hill Station", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Yercaud_lake_view.jpg",
                "October to June", "2 to 3 hours", "Developed in the 1840s by Sir Thomas Strange and MD Cockburn as a quiet coffee-growing hill station in the Madras Presidency.", "Boating on Emerald Lake, Anna Park & Deer Park, Lady's Seat Cliff Viewpoint, Pagoda Point, Shevaroy Temple Peak (1,620m)",
                "Drive up the 20 hairpin bends from Salem for panoramic valley vistas; visit Lady's Seat in the evening to view the illuminated Salem city lights below.", "08:30 AM – 05:30 PM daily for boating", "Free lake entry; Boating ₹120–₹250; Parking fees apply",
                new BigDecimal("11.7761"), new BigDecimal("78.2094"), 18, 2024, 1700000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yercaud_lake_view.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Tiruvannamalai Arunachaleswarar Temple", "tiruvannamalai-arunachaleswarar-temple", "Tiruvannamalai",
                "Monumental 25-acre temple representing the Fire (Agni) element of the Pancha Bhoota Sthalas at the base of sacred Mount Arunachala, famed for the Karthigai Deepam festival.",
                "Arunachaleswarar Temple is one of the largest temple complexes in India, spanning 25 acres with four imposing gopurams, including the 66-meter eastern Rajagopuram built by King Krishnadevaraya. Dedicated to Lord Shiva as an eternal column of fire (Agni Lingam), the temple is internationally celebrated for the 14-km barefoot circular pilgrimage (Girivalam) around Mount Arunachala on every full moon night.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tiruvannamalai_temple_gopuram.jpg",
                "October to March (Karthigai Deepam festival in Nov/Dec)", "3 to 4 hours", "Praised by Tamil Saiva Nayanars in the 7th-century Thevaram; nearby Sri Ramana Maharshi Ashram drew global seekers of Advaita Vedanta philosophy.", "66-meter Eastern Rajagopuram, Thousand-Pillared Hall, Shivaganga Holy Tank, 14-km Sacred Girivalam Path, Sri Ramana Ashram nearby",
                "Participate in the 14 km Girivalam barefoot pradakshina during Chitra Pournami or regular full moon nights; dress respectfully in traditional attire.", "05:30 AM – 12:30 PM, 03:30 PM – 09:30 PM daily", "Free general entry; ₹50/₹100 Special Darshan passes available",
                new BigDecimal("12.2319"), new BigDecimal("79.0678"), 19, 2024, 7200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiruvannamalai_temple_gopuram.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(tn, "Fort St. George, Chennai", "fort-st-george-chennai", "Chennai",
                "The first British colonial fortress in India, founded in 1644 on the Coromandel Coast, housing the Fort Museum and St. Mary's Church, the oldest Anglican church in Asia.",
                "Erected in 1644 by the British East India Company, Fort St. George served as the administrative crucible for the British presence in Southern India and led to the modern founding of the city of Madras (Chennai). Within its historic moat and bastion walls are the Tamil Nadu Legislative Assembly secretariat, the Fort Museum showcasing colonial weaponry and coins, and St. Mary's Church.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fort_St._George,_Chennai.jpg",
                "November to February", "2 to 3 hours", "First British fortress in India (founded on St. George's Day, 1644); home to St. Mary's Church where Robert Clive married in 1753.", "Fort Museum (3 Floors of Artifacts, Uniforms & Letters), St. Mary's Church (consecrated 1680), 150-foot Heritage Flagstaff, Clive's Corner",
                "Carry a government photo ID for security screening as the fort contains state government administrative offices; museum is closed on Fridays.", "09:00 AM – 05:00 PM (Museum closed on Fridays)", "₹25 for Indians; ₹300 for foreigners (Fort Museum)",
                new BigDecimal("13.08"), new BigDecimal("80.2872"), 20, 2024, 850000L, "TOTAL",
                "Tamil Nadu Tourism Development Corporation (TTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_St._George_Chennai.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        // --- 17. UTTAR PRADESH ---
        State up = createState("Uttar Pradesh", "uttar-pradesh", "STATE", "Northern", "Lucknow", "Heartland of Indian civilization, home to the Taj Mahal, Varanasi Ghats, and Ayodhya.",
                "Uttar Pradesh is India's most populous state and top cultural destination, housing the Taj Mahal in Agra, sacred Varanasi, Ayodhya, and Mathura.",
                "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Taj_Mahal%2C_Agra%2C_India_edit2.jpg", "October to March");

        createDestination(up, "Taj Mahal, Agra", "taj-mahal", "Agra",
                "UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of the New 7 Wonders of the World, an ivory-white marble mausoleum commissioned in 1631 by Mughal Emperor Shah Jahan.",
                "Standing majestically on the south bank of the Yamuna River in Agra, the Taj Mahal is internationally acclaimed as the jewel of Muslim art in India and a supreme masterpiece of world heritage. Commissioned by Emperor Shah Jahan to house the tomb of his beloved wife Mumtaz Mahal, the monument features pristine white Makrana marble inlaid with semi-precious pietra dura stones, a 73-meter central dome, four minarets, and classical Charbagh Mughal gardens.",
                "Monument", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Taj_Mahal%2C_Agra%2C_India_edit2.jpg",
                "October to March (Pleasant weather; Taj Mahotsav festival in February)", "3 to 4 hours", "Constructed between 1631 and 1653 CE by over 20,000 artisans under chief architect Ustad Ahmad Lahori; inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1983.", "Main Marble Mausoleum, Pietra Dura Inlay Cenotaphs, Yamuna Riverfront Promenade, Charbagh Gardens, Red Sandstone Mosque and Jawab",
                "Taj Mahal is strictly CLOSED on Fridays. Arrive at sunrise (opens 30 min before sunrise) to experience golden morning light and smaller crowds.", "Sunrise to Sunset daily (Closed on Fridays); Night viewing on Full Moon nights", "₹50 for Indians (+ ₹200 for Main Mausoleum); ₹1,100 for Foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("27.1751"), new BigDecimal("78.0421"), 1, 2024, 7800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taj_Mahal,_Agra,_India_edit2.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Agra Fort", "agra-fort", "Agra",
                "Colossal 16th-century UNESCO World Heritage red sandstone fortress on the Yamuna River, serving as the imperial seat of Mughal emperors from Akbar to Aurangzeb.",
                "Spanning 94 acres along the Yamuna River 2.5 km northwest of the Taj Mahal, Agra Fort was transformed by Emperor Akbar from a ruined brick fortress into a massive red sandstone citadel with 70-foot walls. Within its imposing ramparts lie magnificent royal palaces, including the Jahangiri Mahal, Khas Mahal, Diwan-i-Aam, and Musamman Burj, the octagonal marble tower where Shah Jahan spent his final years gazing at the Taj Mahal.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jahangiri_Mahal%2C_Agra_Fort.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Main imperial residence of the Mughal emperors until 1648; inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1983.", "Jahangiri Mahal, Musamman Burj (Shah Jahan's prison balcony), Sheesh Mahal (Mirror Palace), Diwan-i-Aam, Diwan-i-Khas, Amar Singh Gate",
                "Visit in the morning to avoid midday heat; the view of the Taj Mahal across the Yamuna River from Musamman Burj is extraordinary.", "Sunrise to Sunset daily", "₹50 for Indians; ₹650 for Foreigners (Discount with same-day Taj Mahal ticket)",
                new BigDecimal("27.1795"), new BigDecimal("78.0211"), 2, 2024, 3600000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jahangiri_Mahal,_Agra_Fort.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Fatehpur Sikri", "fatehpur-sikri", "Agra District",
                "UNESCO World Heritage Mughal capital city built by Emperor Akbar in 1571, featuring the monumental 54-meter Buland Darwaza and the white marble Tomb of Salim Chishti.",
                "Constructed on a rocky ridge 37 km southwest of Agra, Fatehpur Sikri ('City of Victory') was founded by Emperor Akbar in honour of Sufi saint Sheikh Salim Chishti. Served as the capital of the Mughal Empire from 1571 to 1585, the remarkably preserved red sandstone ghost city features monumental courtyards, the 54-meter Buland Darwaza (Gate of Magnificence), Panch Mahal, Diwan-i-Khas, and Jodha Bai's Palace.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Buland_Darwaza_Fatehpur_Sikri.jpg",
                "October to March", "3 to 4 hours", "Planned imperial capital of the Mughal Empire; UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1986; exemplary fusion of Hindu, Persian, and Islamic architecture.", "Buland Darwaza (54-meter Victory Gate), Tomb of Sheikh Salim Chishti, Panch Mahal (Five-Storey Pavilion), Diwan-i-Khas with Central Pillar, Jama Masjid",
                "Tie a thread on the marble lattice screens of Salim Chishti's tomb for wishes; take the government shuttle bus from the parking area to the hilltop monument.", "Sunrise to Sunset daily", "₹50 for Indians; ₹610 for Foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("27.0944"), new BigDecimal("77.6683"), 3, 2024, 2100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buland_Darwaza_Fatehpur_Sikri.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Varanasi Ghats", "varanasi-ghats", "Varanasi",
                "The timeless 84 stone steps lining the sacred River Ganges in one of the world's oldest continuously inhabited cities, renowned for the mesmerizing evening Ganga Aarti.",
                "Stretching along a 7-kilometer crescent bend of the holy Ganges River, the 84 ghats of Varanasi form the spiritual heart of Hinduism. From Dashashwamedh Ghat, where the world-famous choreographed Maha Ganga Aarti is performed with brass oil lamps every evening, to Assi Ghat at the southern confluence and the historic cremation ghats of Manikarnika and Harishchandra, life, devotion, and eternity unfold along these sacred waters.",
                "Cultural", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dashashwamedh_Ghat%27s_View_Varanasi.jpg",
                "October to March (Dev Deepawali on Kartik Poornima is spectacular)", "1 to 2 days", "Continuous spiritual and cultural center for over 3,000 years; rebuilt primarily in the 18th century by Maratha rulers including Ahilyabai Holkar and the Scindias.", "Dashashwamedh Ghat Evening Ganga Aarti, Sunrise Ganges Boat Cruise, Assi Ghat Morning Yoga, Manikarnika Ghat, Panchganga Ghat",
                "Take a hand-rowed wooden boat ride at 05:30 AM to witness thousands of pilgrims bathing and meditating as the sun rises over the eastern bank.", "Open 24 hours daily (Evening Ganga Aarti: ~06:30 PM in winter, ~07:00 PM in summer)", "Free access; Morning boat ride ~₹300–₹600 per person",
                new BigDecimal("25.3089"), new BigDecimal("83.0089"), 4, 2024, 12500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dashashwamedh_Ghat%27s_View_Varanasi.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Kashi Vishwanath Temple, Varanasi", "kashi-vishwanath-temple", "Varanasi",
                "Supreme Jyotirlinga shrine dedicated to Lord Shiva as Vishveshwara, crowned by an 800-kg gold spire and connected directly to the Ganges via the Kashi Vishwanath Corridor.",
                "Located on the western bank of the sacred Ganges, Kashi Vishwanath Temple is one of the most revered of the 12 Jyotirlingas, symbolizing Lord Shiva as the Ruler of the Universe. Originally consecrated millennia ago and rebuilt in 1780 by Queen Ahilyabai Holkar of Indore, its spires were gilded with pure gold by Maharaja Ranjit Singh in 1835. The magnificent 2021 Kashi Vishwanath Corridor provides direct pilgrim access from the river ghats.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Shri_Kashi_Vishwanath_Temple_2.jpg",
                "October to March (Maha Shivratri and Shravan month)", "2 to 3 hours", "Rebuilt by Maharani Ahilyabai Holkar in 1780; gold leaf donated by Maharaja Ranjit Singh in 1835; rejuvenated by the 2021 Vishwanath Dham Corridor.", "Golden Spire (Swarna Shikhar), Kashi Vishwanath Jyotirlinga Sanctum, Vishwanath Dham Corridor, Gyanvapi Well, Ganga Viewpoint Gallery",
                "Book Sugam Darshan tickets online via the official temple portal to skip long general queues; mobile phones and bags must be deposited in digital lockers.", "03:00 AM – 11:00 PM daily (Mangala Aarti at 03:00 AM)", "Free general darshan; ₹300 Sugam Darshan pass; special aarti tickets ₹500–₹1,500",
                new BigDecimal("25.3109"), new BigDecimal("83.0107"), 5, 2024, 14200000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shri_Kashi_Vishwanath_Temple_2.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Sarnath", "sarnath", "Varanasi District",
                "Sacred Buddhist pilgrimage site 10 km from Varanasi where Lord Buddha preached his first sermon, home to the colossal Dhamek Stupa and the Ashoka Lion Capital.",
                "Located in the ancient Deer Park (Isipatana) just 10 kilometers north of Varanasi, Sarnath is one of the four principal pilgrimage destinations of Buddhism. Here, after attaining enlightenment at Bodh Gaya, Gautama Buddha delivered his first sermon (Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta) and set in motion the Wheel of Law. The site is anchored by the 43.6-meter Dhamek Stupa and the Archaeological Museum housing India's national emblem, the Ashoka Lion Capital.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The_Dhamek_Stupa_,_500_CE_,Sarnath,_Varanasi_Uttar_Pradesh.jpg",
                "October to March (Buddha Purnima in May)", "3 to 4 hours", "Cradle of the Buddhist Sangha founded in the 6th century BCE; monumentally developed by Emperor Ashoka in the 3rd century BCE.", "Dhamek Stupa (43.6 meters high), Chaukhandi Stupa, Ashoka Pillar Edict Ruins, Sarnath Archaeological Museum (Lion Capital), Mulagandha Kuti Vihar",
                "Visit the Sarnath Archaeological Museum (closed on Fridays) to see the original 3rd-century BCE polished sandstone Lion Capital of Ashoka.", "06:00 AM – 06:00 PM daily (Museum: 09:00 AM – 05:00 PM, closed Fridays)", "₹25 for Indians; ₹300 for Foreigners (Museum entry ₹5 extra)",
                new BigDecimal("25.3811"), new BigDecimal("83.0228"), 6, 2024, 1750000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Dhamek_Stupa_,_500_CE_,Sarnath,_Varanasi_Uttar_Pradesh.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Ayodhya Ram Mandir", "ayodhya-ram-mandir", "Ayodhya",
                "Monumental pink sandstone temple consecrated in 2024 at Ram Janmabhoomi, built in classical Nagara architectural style with five mandapas and a 161-foot spire.",
                "Erected at the revered birthplace of Lord Rama on the banks of the sacred Saryu River in Ayodhya, the Ram Janmabhoomi Mandir is one of India's largest Hindu shrines. Designed by the renowned Sompura family in classical Nagara temple style, the complex is built from pink Bansi Paharpur sandstone without using structural steel. It houses the sacred idol of Ram Lalla and features 392 intricately carved pillars and 44 teakwood doors.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ayodhya_Ram_Mandir_Inauguration_Day_Picture.jpg",
                "October to March (Ram Navami and Deepotsav festival in October/November)", "3 to 4 hours", "Revered as the sacred birth ground of Lord Rama; consecrated in a historic Pran Pratishtha ceremony on January 22, 2024.", "Ram Lalla Sanctum, 161-foot Central Shikhar, Five Mandapas (Nritya, Rang, Sabha, Prarthana, Kirtan), Ramkot Park, Saryu River Aarti at Ram Ki Paidi nearby",
                "Book Aarti passes online via the Shri Ram Janmbhoomi Teerth Kshetra portal; locker facilities are available for electronics and footwear before entry.", "06:30 AM – 12:00 PM, 02:00 PM – 10:00 PM daily", "Free general darshan; VIP and Aarti passes online via trust portal",
                new BigDecimal("26.7956"), new BigDecimal("82.1944"), 7, 2024, 24000000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ayodhya_Ram_Mandir_Inauguration_Day_Picture.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Bara Imambara, Lucknow", "bara-imambara-lucknow", "Lucknow",
                "Monumental 18th-century Awadhi architectural complex constructed by Nawab Asaf-ud-Daula, celebrated for possessing the world's largest unsupported vaulted hall and the Bhool Bhulaiya labyrinth.",
                "Constructed in 1784 as a famine-relief public works project by Nawab Asaf-ud-Daula, the Bara Imambara (Asafi Imambara) is a triumph of Mughal-Awadhi engineering. The central arched hall measures 50 meters in length and 15 meters in height without a single supporting pillar or wooden beam. Above the hall sits the celebrated Bhool Bhulaiya, an intricate labyrinth of over 1,000 interlocking passageways with panoramic rooftop vistas.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bara_Imambara_Lucknow.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Built during the devastating 1784 famine to provide dignified employment to nobility and commoners alike; symbol of Awadhi communal solidarity.", "Pillarless Central Vaulted Hall, Bhool Bhulaiya (The Labyrinth), Asafi Mosque, Shahi Baoli (Five-Storey Stepwell), Panoramic Terrace Views",
                "Hire an authorized ASI guide to explore the Bhool Bhulaiya labyrinth to avoid losing your way; composite ticket includes Chota Imambara and Picture Gallery.", "06:00 AM – 05:00 PM daily", "₹50 for Indians; ₹500 for Foreigners (composite ticket)",
                new BigDecimal("26.8694"), new BigDecimal("80.9128"), 8, 2024, 1950000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bara_Imambara_Lucknow.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Chota Imambara, Lucknow", "chota-imambara-lucknow", "Lucknow",
                "Exquisite 19th-century mausoleum built by Nawab Muhammad Ali Shah, celebrated as the 'Palace of Lights' for its opulent Belgian crystal chandeliers and gilded domes.",
                "Located west of the Bara Imambara in Old Lucknow, the Chota Imambara (Imambara Husainabad Mubarak) was built in 1838 as a mausoleum for Nawab Muhammad Ali Shah and his mother. Renowned for its gilded main dome and white and turquoise facades adorned with Islamic calligraphy, its interior dazzles with hundreds of Belgian crystal chandeliers, stained-glass lanterns, and ornate silver mimbars.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chhota_imambara_Lucknow.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 hours", "Completed in 1838 as the final resting place of Nawab Muhammad Ali Shah; famously illuminated during the mourning month of Muharram.", "Gilded Dome and Calligraphic Facades, Belgian Glass and Crystal Chandelier Hall, Taj Mahal Replica (Tomb of Princess Zinat Asiya), Husainabad Watchtower",
                "Dress modestly covering shoulders and knees; footwear must be removed before entering the inner mausoleum courtyards.", "06:00 AM – 05:00 PM daily", "Included in composite Bara Imambara ticket (₹50 Indians; ₹500 Foreigners)",
                new BigDecimal("26.8739"), new BigDecimal("80.9042"), 9, 2024, 1420000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chhota_imambara_Lucknow.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Rumi Darwaza, Lucknow", "rumi-darwaza-lucknow", "Lucknow",
                "Monumental 60-foot ornamental gateway built in 1784 by Nawab Asaf-ud-Daula, celebrated as the 'Turkish Gate' and iconic architectural symbol of Lucknow.",
                "Standing 60 feet tall between the Bara Imambara and Chota Imambara in Old Lucknow, Rumi Darwaza is the quintessential architectural emblem of the city of Nawabs. Commissioned by Nawab Asaf-ud-Daula in 1784 to replicate the ancient imperial gateways of Constantinople (Istanbul), the gateway features an enormous arched portal crowned by carved floral petals and an ornate octagonal lantern dome.",
                "Monument", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/CST_-_Rumi_Darwaza,_Lucknow.jpg",
                "October to March", "30 minutes to 1 hour", "Served as the main grand entrance to the historic walled city of old Lucknow; prime example of innovative Awadhi brick-and-stucco craftsmanship.", "60-foot Ornamental Archway, Chhatri Lantern Tower, Heritage Street Lighting, Clock Tower (Ghanta Ghar) nearby, Picture Gallery nearby",
                "Visit in the evening when the gateway and the surrounding heritage boulevard are illuminated by golden floodlights; fantastic photography spot.", "Open 24 hours (External monument on public boulevard)", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("26.8714"), new BigDecimal("80.9119"), 10, 2024, 2800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CST_-_Rumi_Darwaza,_Lucknow.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Krishna Janmabhoomi, Mathura", "krishna-janmabhoomi-mathura", "Mathura",
                "Revered Hindu temple complex built around the subterranean prison cell (Garbha Griha) where Lord Krishna was born to Devaki and Vasudeva over 5,000 years ago.",
                "Situated in the ancient city of Mathura on the banks of the Yamuna River, Sri Krishna Janmasthan is one of Hinduism's most sacred pilgrimage destinations. The complex encompasses the Garbha Griha cell marking the exact subterranean birth chamber, the magnificent Keshavdeva Temple, the ornate Bhagavat Bhavan adorned with frescoes of Krishna's life, and the sacred Potra Kund stepwell.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mathura_Temple-Mathura-India0002.JPG",
                "August to March (Janmashtami and Holi festivals are grand spectacles)", "2 to 3 hours", "Ancient archaeological site occupied continuously since the 1st millennium BCE; patronized by Gupta emperors, King Chandragupta II, and Raja Veer Singh Bundela.", "Garbha Griha (Birth Cell Sanctum), Keshavdeva Temple, Bhagavat Bhavan Frescoes & Shrines, Potra Kund (Sacred Tank where infant Krishna's clothes were washed)",
                "Strict multi-tier security enforced; mobile phones, smartwatches, cameras, and leather items must be deposited in electronic lockers outside.", "05:00 AM – 12:00 PM, 04:00 PM – 09:30 PM daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("27.5053"), new BigDecimal("77.6694"), 11, 2024, 11000000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mathura_Temple-Mathura-India0002.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Banke Bihari Temple, Vrindavan", "banke-bihari-temple-vrindavan", "Mathura (Vrindavan)",
                "Renowned temple in the heart of Vrindavan established in 1864 by Swami Haridas, enshrining Lord Krishna in the playful, bent 'Tribhanga' posture.",
                "Dedicated to Banke Bihari ('Bent in Three Places'), this temple in the sacred town of Vrindavan is one of the most vibrant centers of Krishna devotion in India. The black marble deity was manifested through the deep musical devotion of 16th-century saint Swami Haridas, the guru of Tansen. Uniquely, the curtain in front of the deity is periodically drawn and opened every few minutes to prevent devotees from losing consciousness under the spell of divine love.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Radha_Syamasundar_Vrindavan_Radhastami_2004.jpg",
                "October to March (Holi and Janmashtami are celebrated with immense fervor)", "2 to 3 hours", "Manifested in the 16th century at Nidhivan by classical saint-musician Swami Haridas; present temple constructed in 1864.", "Tribhanga Posture Banke Bihari Idol, Frequent Parda (Curtain) Darshan Ceremony, Phoolon Ki Holi (Flower Holi), Samaj Gayan Devotional Chants",
                "Be vigilant of eyeglasses and food items as Vrindavan monkeys are notorious; attend morning Shringar Darshan or evening Shayan Darshan.", "Winter: 08:30 AM – 01:00 PM, 04:30 PM – 08:30 PM; Summer: 07:45 AM – 12:00 PM, 05:30 PM – 09:30 PM", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("27.5817"), new BigDecimal("77.7019"), 12, 2024, 9800000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Radha_Syamasundar_Vrindavan_Radhastami_2004.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Prem Mandir, Vrindavan", "prem-mandir-vrindavan", "Mathura (Vrindavan)",
                "Magnificent 54-acre temple complex carved from pure Italian Carrara marble, celebrated for its spectacular night lighting and life-sized dioramas of Krishna's pastimes.",
                "Constructed over 11 years by spiritual master Jagadguru Kripalu Maharaj and opened in 2012, Prem Mandir ('Temple of Divine Love') is a modern architectural masterpiece. Built from 30,000 tons of Italian white Carrara marble by 1,000 sculptors, the two-storey complex features shrines dedicated to Radha Krishna and Sita Rama, encircled by beautifully landscaped gardens with life-sized tableaux depicting Krishna lifting Govardhan Hill and dancing with the Gopis.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Vrindavan_Prem_Mandir_4.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Inaugurated in February 2012 as an architectural testament to eternal divine love, attracting millions of international and domestic visitors.", "Pure Italian White Marble Sanctum, Evening Color-Changing LED Illuminations, Govardhan Leela & Ras Leela Dioramas, Musical Fountain Show",
                "Arrive around 05:30 PM to view the pristine white marble in late daylight and stay for the breathtaking color-changing LED illuminations and musical fountain show.", "05:30 AM – 12:00 PM, 04:30 PM – 08:30 PM daily (Musical Fountain: 07:30 PM)", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("27.5722"), new BigDecimal("77.6744"), 13, 2024, 7600000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vrindavan_Prem_Mandir_4.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Triveni Sangam, Prayagraj", "triveni-sangam-prayagraj", "Prayagraj",
                "Sacred confluence of the Ganges, Yamuna, and mystical underground Saraswati rivers, host to the colossal Maha Kumbh Mela, the largest gathering of humanity on earth.",
                "Regarded as the 'King of all Pilgrimage Centers' (Teerthraj), Triveni Sangam is where the greenish clear waters of the Yamuna meet the pale brownish waters of the Ganges, alongside the subterranean Saraswati River. Millions of pilgrims take holy dips at this sacred confluence to wash away lifetimes of sins, reaching extraordinary spiritual peaks every 12 years during the world-renowned Kumbh Mela.",
                "Cultural", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Boat_Pilgrims_Triveni_Sangam_Allahabad_Jan24_A7C_08513.jpg",
                "October to March (Magh Mela in Jan/Feb; Kumbh Mela periodically)", "2 to 3 hours", "Revered in the Rigveda and Mahabharata; host to the UNESCO-inscribed Kumbh Mela, the largest peaceful gathering of humankind on Earth.", "Sangam Boat Ride to the Holy Water Confluence, Patalpuri Underground Temple & Immortal Banyan Tree (Akshayavat), Historic Akbar Fort Ramparts",
                "Hire an authorized boat from Qila Ghat or VIP Ghat to reach the middle confluence platform; early morning visits offer mist, holy chants, and Siberian migratory gulls.", "Open 24 hours daily (Boat services operation: 05:30 AM – 06:30 PM)", "Free access to ghats; Government boat fare ~₹80–₹120 per person",
                new BigDecimal("25.4267"), new BigDecimal("81.8833"), 14, 2024, 18500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boat_Pilgrims_Triveni_Sangam_Allahabad_Jan24_A7C_08513.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Jhansi Fort", "jhansi-fort", "Jhansi",
                "Historic 17th-century hilltop fortress famed as the citadel of Rani Lakshmibai, the heroic 'Rani of Jhansi' who led the fierce 1857 First War of Indian Independence.",
                "Perched dramatically on the rocky granite hill of Bangra in the Bundelkhand region, Jhansi Fort was constructed in 1613 by Orchha ruler Raja Bir Singh Deo and subsequently expanded by Maratha subedars. The fortress became immortalized in world history during the 1857 rebellion, when the legendary 22-year-old warrior queen Rani Lakshmibai defended its ramparts against General Rose's British forces before leaping from its walls on horseback.",
                "Fort", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Inside_the_Rani_Laxmibai%27s_Jhansi_Fort_in_Jhansi,_Uttar_Pradesh_04.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Epicenter of the 1857 First War of Indian Independence; home to Rani Lakshmibai, one of India's greatest freedom symbols.", "Karak Bijli Cannon, Jump Point of Rani Lakshmibai (Kudan Sthal), Panch Mahal Palace, Shiva Temple, Rani Mahal Museum nearby",
                "Attend the evening sound and light show inside the fort complex narrating Rani Lakshmibai's heroic defense; carry water for walking the ramparts.", "06:00 AM – 06:00 PM daily (Sound and Light Show: 07:00 PM in Hindi/English)", "₹25 for Indians; ₹300 for Foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("25.4578"), new BigDecimal("78.5778"), 15, 2024, 1100000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inside_the_Rani_Laxmibai%27s_Jhansi_Fort_in_Jhansi,_Uttar_Pradesh_04.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Dudhwa National Park", "dudhwa-national-park", "Lakhimpur Kheri",
                "Dense Terai wildlife reserve along the Indo-Nepal border spanning 490 sq km, famous for protecting Bengal tigers, the one-horned rhinoceros, and swamp deer (Barasingha).",
                "Located in the sub-Himalayan Terai floodplains of northern Uttar Pradesh, Dudhwa National Park preserves magnificent primeval sal forests, extensive alluvial grasslands, and marshes. Re-established by famed conservationist Billy Arjan Singh, the park is home to surviving populations of the endangered swamp deer (Barasingha), reintroduced Indian one-horned rhinos, Bengal tigers, leopards, and over 400 species of birds.",
                "Wildlife", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gate_of_Dudhwa_National_Park.jpg",
                "November to April (Park closed during monsoon from June 15 to Nov 15)", "1 to 2 days", "Declared a national park in 1977 through the untiring conservation advocacy of 'Tiger Man' Billy Arjan Singh; core of the Dudhwa Tiger Reserve.", "Sal Forest Jeep Safaris, Elephant Back Rhino Tracking, Bankey Tal Bird Sanctuary, Endangered Swamp Deer (Barasingha) Herds",
                "Book forest rest houses inside the park (like Dudhwa, Sathiana, and Sonaripur) and safari jeeps online via the UP Forest Ecotourism portal well in advance.", "Morning Safari: 06:00 AM – 09:30 AM; Afternoon Safari: 02:30 PM – 05:30 PM", "₹100 for Indians; ₹500 for Foreigners; Jeep safari hire ~₹2,500–₹3,500",
                new BigDecimal("28.5167"), new BigDecimal("80.65"), 16, 2024, 95000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gate_of_Dudhwa_National_Park.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Kushinagar Mahaparinirvana Temple", "kushinagar-mahaparinirvana-temple", "Kushinagar",
                "Sacred international Buddhist pilgrimage site enshrining a 6.1-meter monolithic reclining sandstone statue of Gautama Buddha at the location of his Mahaparinirvana.",
                "Kushinagar is one of the four holiest pilgrimage places of Buddhism, marking the sacred grove of Sal trees where Gautama Buddha passed away and entered Mahaparinirvana in 483 BCE at the age of 80. The Mahaparinirvana Temple houses an extraordinary 6.1-meter monolith reclining statue of the dying Buddha carved from Chunar sandstone in the 5th century, flanked by the 2.7-meter Parinirvana Stupa and the Ramabhar cremation stupa.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mahaparinirvana_Temple_in_Kushinagar_U.P._India.jpg",
                "October to March (Buddha Purnima in May)", "2 to 3 hours", "Ancient capital of the Mallas; site of Buddha's death and cremation; extensively excavated and restored by ASI archaeologist A.C. Carlleyle in 1876.", "6.1-meter Reclining Buddha Statue, Parinirvana Stupa (relic mound), Ramabhar Stupa (Cremation site), Matha Kuar Shrine, International Monasteries (Thai, Tibetan, Japanese)",
                "Maintain solemn silence inside the sanctum where Buddhist monks from across the world chant sutras and offer golden silk robes to the reclining Buddha.", "06:00 AM – 06:00 PM daily", "₹25 for Indians; ₹300 for Foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("26.7408"), new BigDecimal("83.8906"), 17, 2024, 890000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahaparinirvana_Temple_in_Kushinagar_U.P._India.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Deogarh Dashavatara Temple, Lalitpur district", "deogarh-dashavatara-temple", "Lalitpur",
                "Sixth-century Gupta-period stone temple, celebrated as the earliest surviving Panchayatana Hindu temple in northern India with peerless carvings of Sheshashayi Vishnu.",
                "Perched near the Betwa River in the Lalitpur district of southwestern Uttar Pradesh, the Dashavatara Temple of Deogarh was constructed during the golden age of the Gupta Empire in the early 6th century CE. It is famous as the earliest known Hindu stone temple featuring a spire (shikhara) and four secondary corner shrines. Its exterior niches contain supreme masterpieces of classical Indian sculpture, including Sheshashayi Vishnu reclining on the serpent Shesha.",
                "Archaeological", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Remains_of_the_Dashavatara_Temple_in_Deogarh,_Lalitpur.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Built circa 500 CE; earliest surviving structural stone temple of classical Gupta architectural and sculptural perfection in India; ASI protected monument.", "Masterpiece Relief of Sheshashayi Vishnu, Gajendramoksha Relief, Nara-Narayana Penance Relief, Carved Doorway with Ganga and Yamuna, Betwa River Valley",
                "Combine with visits to the 31 medieval Jain rock-cut temples located within the nearby Deogarh Fort cliff complex along the Betwa River gorges.", "06:00 AM – 06:00 PM daily", "₹25 for Indians; ₹300 for Foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("24.5244"), new BigDecimal("78.2536"), 18, 2024, 180000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_the_Dashavatara_Temple_in_Deogarh,_Lalitpur.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Vindhyachal Dham, Mirzapur", "vindhyachal-dham-mirzapur", "Mirzapur",
                "Sacred Shakti Peetha perched on the banks of the Ganges in the Vindhya mountains, revered as the eternal abode of Goddess Vindhyavasini and the Trikona Yatra.",
                "Located 70 km from Varanasi where the Vindhya mountain range touches the sacred Ganges River, Vindhyachal Dham is one of the premier Shakta pilgrimage destinations in India. Unlike other Shakti Peethas where limbs of Sati fell, Vindhyachal is believed to be the permanent residence chosen by Goddess Durga after slaying Mahishasura. Pilgrims perform the sacred triangular pilgrimage (Trikona Yatra) covering Vindhyavasini, Kali Khoh, and Ashtabhuja temples.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mirzapur_from_the_Ganges.JPG",
                "October to March (Chaitra and Ashwin Navratri fairs draw millions)", "3 to 4 hours", "Mentioned in the Markandeya Purana and Durga Saptashati; celebrated for centuries as the abode of the primordial protector goddess of the Vindhyas.", "Maa Vindhyavasini Temple Sanctum, Kali Khoh Hill Cave Temple, Ashtabhuja Temple & Ropeway, Ganga River Ghats, Trikona Yatra Circuit",
                "Take the newly built aerial passenger ropeway to visit Kali Khoh and Ashtabhuja temples on the hilltop; avoid visiting during peak noon hours during Navratri.", "05:00 AM – 10:30 PM daily", "Free entry; Ropeway ticket ~₹80 round trip",
                new BigDecimal("25.1611"), new BigDecimal("82.5028"), 19, 2024, 6500000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mirzapur_from_the_Ganges.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(up, "Sravasti Buddhist Archaeological Sites", "sravasti-buddhist-archaeological-sites", "Sravasti District",
                "Ancient capital of Kosala where Lord Buddha spent 24 monsoon retreats (vassas) and performed the Twin Miracle, home to the Jetavana Monastery and Anathapindika Stupa.",
                "Situated along the Rapti River in eastern Uttar Pradesh, Sravasti (Sahet-Mahet) is one of the most prominent pilgrimage centers associated with the life of Gautama Buddha. Here, wealthy merchant Anathapindika purchased the Jetavana park by paving the ground with gold coins and donated it to the Buddha. The site preserves ancient monastic viharas, the Anandabodhi Tree, the Twin Miracle site, and the towering stupa of reformed robber Angulimala.",
                "Archaeological", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Anathapindika_stupa_(5703626234).jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Ancient capital of Kosala Kingdom under King Pasenadi; site where Buddha delivered the majority of his discourses and sutras over 24 monsoon retreats.", "Jetavana Monastery Complex, Anathapindika Stupa (Kachchi Kuti), Angulimala Stupa (Pakki Kuti), Sacred Anandabodhi Tree, International Buddhist Monasteries",
                "Sit in meditation under the Anandabodhi Tree; visit international temples constructed by Sri Lankan, Burmese, Thai, Korean, and Chinese Buddhist pilgrims.", "06:00 AM – 06:00 PM daily", "₹25 for Indians; ₹300 for Foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("27.5167"), new BigDecimal("82.05"), 20, 2024, 720000L, "TOTAL",
                "Uttar Pradesh Tourism (UPSTDC)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anathapindika_stupa_(5703626234).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        // --- 18. WEST BENGAL ---
        State wb = createState("West Bengal", "west-bengal", "STATE", "Eastern", "Kolkata", "Cultural heartland of literature, Darjeeling tea, Sundarbans mangrove tigers, and Durga Puja.",
                "West Bengal spans from the high Himalayas in Darjeeling down to the Sundarbans mangrove forests on the Bay of Bengal.",
                "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Victoria_Memorial_situated_in_Kolkata.jpg", "October to March");

        createDestination(wb, "Victoria Memorial, Kolkata", "victoria-memorial-kolkata", "Kolkata",
                "Iconic white Makrana marble monument and museum built between 1906 and 1921, blending Indo-Saracenic and British neoclassical architecture on the Kolkata Maidan.",
                "Dominating the southern end of the Kolkata Maidan, the Victoria Memorial is the defining architectural symbol of Kolkata. Constructed from gleaming white Makrana marble (the same marble used in the Taj Mahal), the imposing monument was conceptualized by Lord Curzon. It features 25 galleries housing over 28,000 historical artifacts, royal paintings by Thomas Daniell, rare manuscripts, and 64 acres of landscaped gardens with reflecting pools.",
                "Monument", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Victoria_Memorial_situated_in_Kolkata.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Dedicated to Queen Victoria and inaugurated in 1921 by the Prince of Wales, designed by architect William Emerson.", "Central Dome with Angel of Victory Statue, Royal Gallery, Calcutta Gallery, 64-acre Landscaped Gardens, Evening Light and Sound Show",
                "Purchase tickets online to skip queues; separate garden-only and museum tickets available; evening light and sound show runs in English and Bengali.", "Gardens: 05:30 AM – 06:00 PM; Museum: 10:00 AM – 06:00 PM (Closed Mondays)", "Garden ₹50; Museum ₹50 for Indians, ₹500 for foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("22.5448"), new BigDecimal("88.3426"), 1, 2024, 4500000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Victoria_Memorial_situated_in_Kolkata.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Howrah Bridge, Kolkata", "howrah-bridge-kolkata", "Kolkata",
                "Colossal balanced cantilever bridge spanning 705 meters over the Hooghly River without any nuts or bolts, connecting Kolkata and Howrah.",
                "Officially named Rabindra Setu, the Howrah Bridge is one of the busiest cantilever bridges in the world, carrying over 100,000 vehicles and 150,000 pedestrians daily. Commissioned in 1943 during World War II, the iconic steel structure spans 1,500 feet between towers without a single pylon in the riverbed. Constructed entirely with high-tensile alloy steel supplied by Tata Steel, the bridge is held together purely by rivets.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Howrah_Bridge%2C_Foggy.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 hour", "Replaced a pontoon bridge in 1943; recognized worldwide as an engineering marvel of 20th-century steel construction.", "Pedestrian Walkway over Hooghly River, Views of Kolkata Flower Market (Mullik Ghat), Riverboat views from Ferries, Evening Illumination",
                "Take a commuter ferry from Howrah Ghat to Babughat for panoramic views of the entire bridge; photography on the bridge itself is restricted for security.", "Open 24 hours daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("22.5851"), new BigDecimal("88.3468"), 2, 2024, 12000000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Howrah_Bridge,_Foggy.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Dakshineswar Kali Temple", "dakshineswar-kali-temple", "Kolkata",
                "Historic 19th-century Navaratna-style Kali temple on the eastern bank of the Hooghly River, consecrated by Rani Rashmoni and sanctified by Sri Ramakrishna Paramahamsa.",
                "Located at Dakshineswar on the Hooghly River just north of Kolkata, this grand temple was built in 1855 by philanthropist Rani Rashmoni. The three-storey main temple features nine spires (Navaratna style) dedicated to Goddess Bhavatarini (an aspect of Kali). Flanking the central courtyard along the river are twelve identical terracotta Shiva temples, a Radha-Krishna shrine, and the preserved room where 19th-century mystic Sri Ramakrishna lived and taught.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Dakshineswar_Kali_temple%2C_Kolkata%2C_West_Bengal%2C_India.JPG",
                "October to March (special celebrations during Kali Puja in Oct/Nov)", "2 to 3 hours", "Founded in 1855, serving as the spiritual center where Sri Ramakrishna Paramahamsa attained divine spiritual realization and formulated his universal philosophy of religious harmony.", "Navaratna Kali Sanctum, Twelve Identical Shiva Temples along River Ghat, Sri Ramakrishna Room and Panchavati Garden, Radha-Kanta Temple",
                "Connected directly to Kolkata via Metro Line 2 (Green line) and local ferries from Belur Math; footwear and electronics must be deposited in cloakrooms before entry.", "06:00 AM – 12:30 PM, 03:30 PM – 09:00 PM daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("22.6534"), new BigDecimal("88.3575"), 3, 2024, 8200000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dakshineswar_Kali_temple,_Kolkata,_West_Bengal,_India.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Kalighat Kali Temple", "kalighat-kali-temple", "Kolkata",
                "Ancient Shakti Peetha on the banks of Adi Ganga in southern Kolkata, revered as the sacred site where the right toes of Goddess Sati fell.",
                "Kalighat Kali Temple is one of the 51 Shakti Peethas and one of the oldest spiritual pilgrimage sites in Eastern India. The present brick temple, built in 1809 by the Sabarna Roy Choudhury family, displays traditional Bengali Chala architecture. The unique idol of Goddess Kali features a touchstone face with three huge eyes, a long protruding tongue made of pure gold, and four golden hands holding a scimitar and severed head.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kalighat_Kali_Temple.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 hours", "Associated with the origin of the city's name (Kolkata is widely believed to be derived from Kalighat); referenced in 15th-century Mansar Bhasan and Chandi Mangal texts.", "Main Kali Sanctum with Golden Tongue, Natmandir Hall, Kundupukur Sacred Tank, Radha Krishna Temple, Sosthi Tala",
                "Be vigilant of aggressive local pandas offering VIP darshan; buy standard puja offerings only from authorized stalls; easily reachable via Kalighat Metro Station.", "05:00 AM – 02:00 PM, 05:00 PM – 10:30 PM daily", "Free general darshan; VIP queue ₹100",
                new BigDecimal("22.52"), new BigDecimal("88.3425"), 4, 2024, 7800000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalighat_Kali_Temple.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Sundarbans National Park", "sundarbans-national-park", "South 24 Parganas",
                "UNESCO World Heritage mangrove delta on the Bay of Bengal, home to the world's largest tidal halophytic mangrove forest and the legendary swimming Royal Bengal Tigers.",
                "Formed at the confluence of the Ganges, Brahmaputra, and Meghna rivers, the Sundarbans is a vast delta spanning 10,000 square kilometers across India and Bangladesh. The Indian national park covers 1,330 square kilometers of tidal creeks, mudflats, and dense sundari mangrove trees. It is world-renowned for its swimming Royal Bengal tigers that have adapted to drinking saline water, along with estuarine crocodiles, Gangetic dolphins, and olive ridley sea turtles.",
                "National Park", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sundarbans_National_Park.jpg",
                "November to March (pleasant weather and highest wildlife sightings)", "2 to 3 days", "Inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987 and a Ramsar Wetland of International Importance in 2019; designated a Project Tiger reserve in 1973.", "Motorized Boat Safaris through Mangrove Creeks, Sajnekhali Bird Sanctuary & Watchtower, Sudhanyakhali Tiger Watchtower, Dobanki Canopy Walkway",
                "Boat safaris depart from Godkhali jetty (3 hours drive from Kolkata); forest permits required; all tourism is conducted from licensed riverboats as land walking is strictly prohibited.", "07:00 AM – 05:00 PM (Day safaris only; boats must anchor before sunset)", "Park entry ₹60 for Indians, ₹200 for foreigners; Boat permit ₹400 – ₹800 per day",
                new BigDecimal("21.9497"), new BigDecimal("88.8986"), 5, 2024, 350000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sundarbans_National_Park.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Darjeeling Himalayan Railway", "darjeeling-hills", "Darjeeling",
                "UNESCO World Heritage 2-foot narrow-gauge 'Toy Train' engineered between 1879 and 1881, scaling over 2,000 meters into the Eastern Himalayas using loops and reverses.",
                "Operating over 88 kilometers from New Jalpaiguri in the plains to Darjeeling at 2,073 meters, the Darjeeling Himalayan Railway (DHR) is an iconic triumph of 19th-century railway engineering. Famous for its vintage British steam engines puffing through mist-covered tea gardens and mountain market streets, the line employs innovative loops and Z-reverses to conquer steep gradients. Its heritage steam joyrides between Darjeeling and Ghum (India's highest railway station at 2,258m) remain a signature Indian travel experience.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Darjeeling_Himalayan_Railway%2Ctoy_train_%281%29.jpg",
                "October to May", "2 hours (joyride) or full day", "Inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage list in 1999 as an outstanding example of bold engineering that solved the problem of establishing rail communication over a rugged mountain terrain.", "Steam Engine Joyride to Ghum, Ghum Railway Museum (India's highest station), Batasia Loop Panoramic Spiral, Hillside Market Street Running",
                "Book steam joyride tickets months in advance via IRCTC, especially during peak seasons (Oct–Nov and Apr–May); window seats on the right provide best valley views towards Darjeeling.", "Joyride services operate 09:00 AM – 04:30 PM daily", "Steam Joyride ₹1,500; Diesel Joyride ₹1,000; Regular transit fare varies",
                new BigDecimal("27.041"), new BigDecimal("88.2663"), 6, 2024, 1400000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darjeeling_Himalayan_Railway,toy_train_(1).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Tiger Hill, Darjeeling", "tiger-hill-darjeeling", "Darjeeling",
                "Celebrated summit at 2,590 meters providing the world's most famous sunrise vista over Mount Kangchenjunga and glimpses of Mount Everest.",
                "Perched 11 kilometers from Darjeeling town at an altitude of 2,590 meters (8,497 feet), Tiger Hill is legendary for its sunrise spectacles over the Eastern Himalayas. As dawn breaks, the sun's first rays illuminate the colossal snow-covered summit of Mount Kangchenjunga (the world's third-highest peak) in shades of pink, gold, and incandescent orange before daylight reaches the valleys. On clear mornings, Mount Everest and Mount Makalu are also visible on the distant horizon.",
                "Nature", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kanchenjunga.JPG",
                "October to December and March to April (clearest skies)", "2 hours", "Served as the primary Great Trigonometrical Survey observation station used by George Everest and Radhanath Sikdar to calculate the height of Mount Everest in the 1850s.", "Sunrise over Mount Kangchenjunga, Three-tiered Glass Observatory Deck, Distant Views of Everest and Makalu, Senchal Wildlife Sanctuary surroundings",
                "Wake up by 3:30 AM to beat mountain traffic; pre-book a local shared jeep the previous evening; wear heavy woolens and gloves as morning temperatures are near freezing.", "04:00 AM – 06:30 AM (Peak sunrise viewing)", "Observatory deck ₹40 – ₹80 depending on tier level; Vehicle permit ₹50",
                new BigDecimal("27.0142"), new BigDecimal("88.2861"), 7, 2024, 980000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kanchenjunga.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Batasia Loop, Darjeeling", "batasia-loop-darjeeling", "Darjeeling",
                "A circular spiral railway loop engineered to lower train gradient, featuring manicured landscaped flower gardens and a poignant Gorkha War Memorial.",
                "Located 5 kilometers from Darjeeling just below Ghum, Batasia Loop is an engineering marvel constructed in 1919 where the Toy Train track loops around itself through a 360-degree circle to drop 1,000 feet of gradient. The center of the loop is transformed into a manicured landscaped alpine garden with flowerbeds, pathways, and the Gorkha War Memorial honoring soldiers of the Indian Army. Visitors enjoy a 360-degree panoramic vista of Darjeeling town and Kangchenjunga.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Batasia_Loop_of_Darjeeling_Himalayan_Railway.jpg",
                "October to May", "1 hour", "Engineered in 1919 by British railway builders to navigate the steep descent from Ghum; War Memorial erected in 1995 to honor brave Gorkha soldiers.", "360-degree Toy Train Track Spiral, Gorkha War Memorial Cenotaph, Kangchenjunga Mountain Viewpoint, Landscaped Eco-Garden",
                "Included on the standard Toy Train steam joyride with a 10-minute photo stop; can also be easily visited by taxi from town; local traditional dress photo rentals available.", "05:00 AM – 08:00 PM daily", "₹20 for adults",
                new BigDecimal("27.0175"), new BigDecimal("88.2519"), 8, 2024, 1100000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batasia_Loop_of_Darjeeling_Himalayan_Railway.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Mirik Lake", "mirik-lake", "Darjeeling",
                "A serene natural hill station lake surrounded by undulating tea gardens and Japanese cryptomeria pine groves, spanned by the footbridge Indreni Pul.",
                "Nestled at an altitude of 1,495 meters in the hills southwest of Darjeeling, Mirik Lake (officially Sumendu Lake) is the centerpiece of a tranquil hill resort. The 1.25-kilometer long lake is bordered on one side by terraced gardens and on the other by a dense forest of tall cryptomeria pine trees. An 80-foot arched rainbow footbridge (Indreni Pul) spans the lake, connecting the lakeside promenades where visitors enjoy paddle boating, pony rides, and walks through cardamom and tea plantations.",
                "Lake", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mirik_Sumendu_Lake.jpg",
                "March to June and September to December", "3 to 4 hours", "Developed by the West Bengal tourism department in the 1970s as a serene hill alternative to Darjeeling along the historic trade route to Nepal.", "Sumendu Lake Boating, Indreni Pul Rainbow Bridge, Pine Forest Lakeside Walk, Rameetay Viewpoint, Bokar Monastery, Tingling Tea Garden",
                "Located just 49 km from Siliguri on the road to Darjeeling; ideal peaceful day trip; horse rides around the pine forest periphery are popular with families.", "Open 24 hours daily; Boating 08:30 AM – 05:00 PM", "Free lake entry; Pedal boat ₹100 – ₹200 for 30 minutes",
                new BigDecimal("26.8889"), new BigDecimal("88.175"), 9, 2024, 620000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mirik_Sumendu_Lake.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Dooars and Gorumara National Park", "dooars-and-gorumara-national-park", "Jalpaiguri",
                "Lush Terai-Dooars floodplain forest along the Murti and Raidak rivers, celebrated for breeding populations of the Indian one-horned rhinoceros and Asian elephants.",
                "Situated in the Dooars floodplains of the sub-Himalayan Terai belt in Jalpaiguri district, Gorumara National Park covers 80 square kilometers of moist deciduous sal forests, tall elephant grass savannahs, and riverine woodlands. Located primarily on the floodplains of the Murti and Jaldhaka rivers, the park is home to the majestic one-horned rhinoceros, Asian elephants, gaur (Indian bison), leopards, and hornbills. Open-top jeep safaris and strategically located watchtowers offer spectacular wildlife observation.",
                "National Park", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gorumara_Gateway_Arnab_Dutta.JPG",
                "November to April", "1 to 2 days", "Declared a wildlife sanctuary in 1949 and elevated to a national park in 1994; recognized for exemplary habitat recovery for the one-horned rhino.", "Jatraprasad Watchtower (named after legendary forest elephant), Rhino Observation Point, Medla Watchtower with Bullock Cart ride, Murti Riverbank",
                "Park is strictly closed during monsoon season (June 16 to September 15); safaris depart from Lataguri ticket counters; carry binoculars for canopy birdwatching.", "06:00 AM – 09:30 AM, 02:00 PM – 05:30 PM (Closed mid-Jun to mid-Sep)", "Jeep safari ₹1,800 – ₹2,500 per vehicle including guide and permit fees",
                new BigDecimal("26.7583"), new BigDecimal("88.8"), 10, 2024, 290000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gorumara_Gateway_Arnab_Dutta.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Jaldapara National Park", "jaldapara-national-park", "Alipurduar",
                "A vast 216-square-kilometer wildlife sanctuary on the banks of the Torsa River, harboring Bengal's largest population of the great Indian one-horned rhinoceros.",
                "Located at the foothills of the Eastern Himalayas along the banks of the Torsa River in Alipurduar, Jaldapara National Park is a biodiversity stronghold spanning 216 square kilometers. It holds the second-largest population of the vulnerable Indian one-horned rhinoceros in India after Kaziranga. The park features expansive savannah grasslands, riverine forests, and provides sanctuary to elephants, leopards, sambar, hog deer, and the critically endangered Bengal florican.",
                "National Park", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Facing_a_rhino_at_Jaldapara_national_park.jpg",
                "October to May", "1 to 2 days", "Established as a wildlife sanctuary in 1941 to protect the endangered rhino population and upgraded to a national park in May 2012.", "One-horned Rhinoceros Safaris, Morning Elephant Safari, Hollong Watchtower, Torsa Riverbed Scenic Views, Chilapata Forest Elephant Corridor",
                "Elephant safaris must be booked through the West Bengal Tourism Hollong / Madarihat lodges; park is closed from June 16 to September 15 for monsoon breeding season.", "06:00 AM – 09:30 AM, 02:30 PM – 05:30 PM daily (Closed mid-June to mid-Sept)", "Jeep safari ₹2,000 – ₹3,000 per vehicle; Elephant ride ₹800 – ₹1,000 per person",
                new BigDecimal("26.6833"), new BigDecimal("89.3"), 11, 2024, 310000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facing_a_rhino_at_Jaldapara_national_park.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Bishnupur Terracotta", "bishnupur-terracotta", "Bankura",
                "Historic capital of the Malla kings renowned for 17th-century terracotta temples embellished with intricately sculpted brick relief plaques.",
                "Bishnupur in Bankura district is celebrated worldwide for its distinctive terracotta temples constructed by the Malla kings between the 17th and 18th centuries. In an alluvial region lacking stone quarries, artisans used locally dug clay baked into fired terracotta plaques to adorn temple walls with intricate scenes from the Ramayana, Mahabharata, and Krishna Lila. Outstanding monuments include the pyramidal Rasmancha, the five-pinnacled Shyam Rai Temple, and the ornate Jor Bangla temple.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Madana_Mohana_temple_of_Bishnupur_in_Bankura_district_37.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 to 2 days", "flourished under the Vaishnava Malla dynasty (especially King Bir Hambir), who made it a thriving capital of classical music (Bishnupur Gharana) and Baluchari silk weaving.", "Rasmancha (oldest brick temple), Shyam Rai Temple, Jor Bangla Temple, Madan Mohan Temple, Dalmadal Great Iron Cannon, Baluchari Silk Handlooms",
                "Single composite ASI ticket covers entry to all major protected terracotta monuments in town; explore local weaver artisan workshops for authentic GI-tagged Baluchari saris.", "06:00 AM – 06:00 PM daily", "₹25 for Indians, ₹300 for foreigners (combined ASI ticket for all monuments)",
                new BigDecimal("23.0681"), new BigDecimal("87.3197"), 12, 2024, 650000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madana_Mohana_temple_of_Bishnupur_in_Bankura_district_37.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Hazarduari Palace, Murshidabad", "hazarduari-palace-murshidabad", "Murshidabad",
                "A grand neoclassical Greek-Doric royal palace with 1,000 doors (900 false) built on the banks of the Bhagirathi River for Nawab Nazim Humayun Jah.",
                "Located on the banks of the Bhagirathi River in the historic former capital of Bengal, Hazarduari Palace ('Palace of a Thousand Doors') was built between 1829 and 1837 by architect Duncan Macleod for Nawab Nazim Humayun Jah. Built in Italian neoclassical and Greek-Doric style, the palace boasts 1,000 doors (of which 900 are ornamental false doors designed to deter intruders). Now an ASI museum, it displays priceless Mughal armory, the silver throne of the Nawabs, rare ivory palanquins, and historic paintings.",
                "Palace", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Hazarduari_Palace_%28Murshidabad%29.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 to 3 hours", "Symbolizes the grandeur and eventual decline of the Nawabs of Bengal following the Battle of Plassey; now preserved as an ASI national site museum.", "Nawab's Silver Throne & Crystal Chandelier, Royal Armory with Siraj-ud-Daulah's sword, Nizamat Imambara opposite, Madina Mosque, Clock Tower",
                "Located 10 km from Berhampore and reachable by express train from Kolkata (Sealdah); electronic gadgets must be checked in at the cloakroom.", "09:00 AM – 05:00 PM (Closed Fridays)", "₹25 for Indians, ₹300 for foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("24.1853"), new BigDecimal("88.2689"), 13, 2024, 920000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hazarduari_Palace_(Murshidabad).jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Cooch Behar Palace", "cooch-behar-palace", "Cooch Behar",
                "An opulent Italian Renaissance brick palace modeled on Buckingham Palace, built in 1887 under the reign of Maharaja Nripendra Narayan of the Koch dynasty.",
                "Also known as Victor Jubilee Palace, Cooch Behar Palace is a grand monument of brick and stone designed in classical Italian Renaissance style. Commissioned in 1887 by the visionary Maharaja Nripendra Narayan, the two-storey palace spans 51,000 square feet and features a central copper-domed Durbar Hall with Renaissance Corinthian columns, stepped balconies, and Italian fresco paintings. Now maintained by the ASI, the palace museum houses the royal artifacts, armory, and oil paintings of the Koch royalty.",
                "Palace", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Cooch_Behar_Palace_alias_Victor_Jubilee_Palace_of_Cooch_Behar_Town_at_Cooch_Behar_district_in_West_Bengal_25.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 hours", "Seat of the Koch princely state of Cooch Behar, closely associated with Maharani Gayatri Devi of Jaipur, who was born a princess of Cooch Behar.", "Corinthian Domed Durbar Hall, Royal Dining and Billiard Rooms, Palace Museum Gallery, Manicured Italianate Gardens, Madan Mohan Temple nearby",
                "Well-connected by rail to New Cooch Behar station; hire an ASI guide to learn about Koch royal history; visit the iconic Madan Mohan Temple in the evening.", "10:00 AM – 05:00 PM (Closed Fridays)", "₹25 for Indians, ₹300 for foreigners",
                new BigDecimal("26.3267"), new BigDecimal("89.4447"), 14, 2024, 480000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cooch_Behar_Palace_alias_Victor_Jubilee_Palace_of_Cooch_Behar_Town_at_Cooch_Behar_district_in_West_Bengal_25.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Shantiniketan", "shantiniketan", "Birbhum",
                "UNESCO World Heritage cultural sanctuary and open-air university founded by Nobel Laureate Rabindranath Tagore, embodying an idyllic union of art, nature, and humanist education.",
                "Located 160 kilometers northwest of Kolkata in rural Birbhum, Santiniketan ('Abode of Peace') was founded in 1901 by visionary poet and polymath Rabindranath Tagore as an experimental open-air school that grew into Visva-Bharati University. The campus is renowned for its open-air classrooms under spreading banyan trees, the stained-glass Upasana Griha (prayer hall), the Uttarayan complex of five residences where Tagore lived, and murals by pioneering modern artists like Nandalal Bose and Ramkinkar Baij.",
                "Heritage", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Visva_Bharati_University_Shantiniketan.jpg",
                "October to March (famous Poush Mela in late December & Basanta Utsav in spring)", "1 to 2 days", "Inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage list in September 2023; represented Tagore's global vision of education uniting Eastern philosophy and international humanism.", "Uttarayan Complex (Udayan, Konarka, Shyamali residences), Rabindra Bhavana Museum, Upasana Griha (Glass Temple), Kala Bhavana Art Murals, Amar Kutir",
                "Explore the campus quietly on foot or by local electric toto; visit on Saturdays to experience the vibrant Khoai Sanibarer Haat open-air handicraft market.", "10:00 AM – 04:30 PM (Museum and Uttarayan closed Wednesdays)", "Uttarayan and Museum ₹70 for adults; campus grounds are open to public",
                new BigDecimal("23.6797"), new BigDecimal("87.6853"), 15, 2024, 1850000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Visva_Bharati_University_Shantiniketan.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Digha Beach", "digha-beach", "Purba Medinipur",
                "West Bengal's most popular seaside resort town on the Bay of Bengal, featuring a flat, seven-kilometer hard-sand beach and gentle shallow surf.",
                "Located 185 kilometers southwest of Kolkata along the border with Odisha, Digha is Bengal's premier coastal vacation spot. Known for its wide, hard-sand shoreline fringed by casuarina (jhau) plantations, the town is divided into Old Digha (famous for its reinforced sea wall, breakers, and evening fish markets) and New Digha (featuring a flat, expansive swimming beach, children's parks, and modern promenade). The calm shallow waters make it an ideal family swimming beach.",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Beach_Ghat_-_New_Digha_-_East_Midnapore_2015-05-03_9779.JPG",
                "October to March (pleasant coastal breeze)", "2 days", "Originally discovered by British diplomat Warren Hastings in the 18th century as 'Brighton of the East'; developed by West Bengal Chief Minister Dr. B.C. Roy in the 1950s.", "New Digha Swimming Beach, Old Digha Sea Wall Promenade, Marine Aquarium and Regional Centre, Digha Science Centre, Amarabati Park",
                "Well-connected by daily express trains from Howrah (around 3 hours); sample freshly fried coastal pomfret, hilsa, and prawns from local beachside stalls.", "Open 24 hours daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("21.6267"), new BigDecimal("87.5078"), 16, 2024, 4800000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beach_Ghat_-_New_Digha_-_East_Midnapore_2015-05-03_9779.JPG", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Mandarmani Beach", "mandarmani-beach", "Purba Medinipur",
                "A tranquil 13-kilometer uninterrupted coastal beach resort known for red ghost crabs, water sports, and beachside luxury resorts.",
                "Situated along the Bay of Bengal 180 kilometers from Kolkata, Mandarmani is celebrated for its pristine 13-kilometer long, wide hard-sand shoreline. The beach is famous for millions of crawling red ghost crabs that carpet the sand during low tide like a scarlet floral bloom (earning it the name Mandarmani, meaning 'flower of red coral'). Unlike Digha, Mandarmani offers an exclusive, relaxed resort experience with beachside cottages, jet-skiing, quad biking, and paramotoring.",
                "Beach", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fishing_Boat_in_Mandarmani_beach_02.jpg",
                "October to March", "2 days", "Emerged in the early 2000s as a modern eco-resort destination, transforming from a secluded coastal fishing hamlet into a major seaside retreat.", "13-km Expansive Hard-Sand Beach, Red Ghost Crab Colonies at Mohana (river mouth), Jet Skiing and ATV Rides, Coastal Fishing Boats",
                "Private vehicle driving on the beach sand is now strictly prohibited by court order for environmental protection; explore the scenic Mohana delta on foot.", "Open 24 hours daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("21.6667"), new BigDecimal("87.7"), 17, 2024, 1600000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fishing_Boat_in_Mandarmani_beach_02.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Mukutmanipur Dam", "mukutmanipur-dam", "Bankura",
                "India's second-largest earthen dam built at the confluence of the Kangsabati and Kumari rivers, framed by picturesque forested hills and deer park islands.",
                "Located in the southwestern corner of Bankura district, Mukutmanipur is a tranquil ecotourism haven built around the massive Kangsabati-Kumari Earthen Dam. Stretches over 11 kilometers in length, it is one of the longest earthen dams in the world. The expansive blue water reservoir is dotted with green islets, including Bonpukuria Deer Park accessible by motorboat. The surrounding rolling hills, tribal Santhal hamlets, and verdant sal forests make it a favorite winter picnic retreat.",
                "Dam", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/%E0%A6%95%E0%A6%82%E0%A6%B8%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%AC%E0%A6%A4%E0%A7%80_%E0%A6%9C%E0%A6%B2%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%A7%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%B0%2C_%E0%A6%AA%E0%A6%B6%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%9a%E0%A6%BF%E0%A6%AE%E0%A6%AC%E0%A6%99%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%97%2C_%E0%A6%AD%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%B0%E0%A6%A4%E0%A7%87_Kangsavati_Reservoir%2C_West_Bengal%2C_India.jpg",
                "October to March", "1 day", "Constructed in 1956 under the planning of Chief Minister Dr. B.C. Roy to provide irrigation and flood control to three drought-prone districts of Bengal.", "11-km Earthen Dam Promenade, Motorboat Rides to Bonpukuria Deer Park Island, Pareshnath Jain Temple Ruins, Musafirana Viewpoint",
                "Hire a boat from the jetty to reach the deer park island; rent a local bicycle or toto to ride along the scenic crest of the earthen dam.", "Open 24 hours daily; Boating 08:00 AM – 05:00 PM", "Free dam entry; Motorboat to Deer Park ₹300 – ₹600 per boat",
                new BigDecimal("22.9667"), new BigDecimal("86.7833"), 18, 2024, 540000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:কংসাবতী_জলাধার,_পশ্চিমবঙ্গ,_ভারত।_Kangsavati_Reservoir,_West_Bengal,_India.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Tarapith Temple", "tarapith-temple", "Birbhum",
                "Famed Tantric Shakti Peetha on the banks of the Dwarka River, dedicated to Goddess Tara and celebrated for the cremation-ground sadhana of Saint Bamakhepa.",
                "Situated along the Dwarka River in the Birbhum district, Tarapith is one of Bengal's most important Tantric pilgrimage shrines. Dedicated to the fierce goddess Tara (a second Mahavidya aspect of Kali), the temple features a four-roofed terracotta Bengali Chala structure. Adjacent to the temple is the revered Mahasmashan (cremation ground) where the legendary 19th-century avadhuta mystic Bamakhepa practiced esoteric sadhana and attained spiritual enlightenment.",
                "Religious", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Tarapith_Kali_temple_at_Tarapith_04.jpg",
                "October to March (Kaushiki Amavasya festival in August/September)", "2 to 3 hours", "Revered in Shakti tradition as the site where Goddess Sati's eyeball (tara) fell; sanctified by sage Vashistha and the mystic Saint Bamakhepa.", "Goddess Tara Sanctum, Sacred Mahasmashan (Cremation Grounds), Bamakhepa Samadhi Ashram, Dwarka River Holy Ghats",
                "Trek early in the morning to avoid long pilgrim darshan lines; beware of local touts offering quick entry; reachable via Rampurhat railway junction (12 km away).", "06:00 AM – 09:00 PM daily", "Free general darshan; Special darshan tickets available",
                new BigDecimal("24.1147"), new BigDecimal("87.8019"), 19, 2024, 3200000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarapith_Kali_temple_at_Tarapith_04.jpg", "VERIFIED");

        createDestination(wb, "Kalimpong Durpin Monastery", "kalimpong-durpin-monastery", "Kalimpong",
                "A magnificent Tibetan Buddhist monastery consecrated by the Dalai Lama atop Durpin Hill, housing rare Kangyur scriptures and 3D geometric mandalas.",
                "Perched dramatically at 1,372 meters atop Durpin Dara hill overlooking Kalimpong, Zang Dhok Palri Phodang (popularly Durpin Monastery) is the largest monastery in Kalimpong. Consecrated by the 14th Dalai Lama in 1976, the three-storey complex belongs to the Yellow Hat (Gelugpa) sect of Tibetan Buddhism. The main prayer hall features rare Buddhist scriptures (Kangyur in 108 volumes brought out of Tibet) and a breathtaking three-dimensional painted wooden mandala, with sweeping views of the Teesta River and Mount Kangchenjunga.",
                "Monastery", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Durpin_Dara_Monastery_or_Zang_Dhok_Palri_Phodang%2C_Kalimpong_02.jpg",
                "October to May", "1 to 2 hours", "Built in 1972 by Dudjom Rinpoche and consecrated by the Dalai Lama in 1976; serves as an important preservation center for rare Tibetan scriptures.", "3D Wooden Mandala, 108-volume Kangyur Scripture Reliquary, Wall Murals and Golden Buddha Statues, Durpin Dara 360-degree Viewpoint",
                "Maintain respectful silence in the prayer hall; visit during monk prayer and chanting sessions at 6:00 AM or 3:00 PM; easily accessible by taxi from Kalimpong town.", "08:00 AM – 05:00 PM daily", "Free",
                new BigDecimal("27.05"), new BigDecimal("88.4667"), 20, 2024, 410000L, "TOTAL",
                "West Bengal Tourism Development Corporation (WBTDCL)", "State Tourism Statistical Report 2024", "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Durpin_Dara_Monastery_or_Zang_Dhok_Palri_Phodang,_Kalimpong_02.jpg", "VERIFIED");
